INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (48547,'en-US','mixed-content-blocker-firefox-android','Mixed content blocker in Firefox for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/mixed-content-blocker-firefox-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/mixed-content-blocker-firefox-android/revision/48547',NULL,'2013-10-23T08:05:25-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','DO NOT APPROVE - WORK IN PROGRESS! - added green and gray padlock',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','{for not m25}
[[T:ApplyToFx|channel=Beta|slug=beta]]
{/for}
When you see the shield icon in the address bar, it means that Firefox for Android has blocked content that is insecure on the page you''re visiting. We''ll explain what that means and what options you have.<br><br>
[[Image:firefox has blocked content that is not secure]]
=What is mixed content?=
When you visit a page served over HTTP, your connection is open for eavesdropping and [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man-in-the-middle_attack man-in-the-middle] attacks. Most websites are served over HTTP because they don''t involve passing sensitive information back and forth and do not need to be secured. When you visit a page fully served over HTTPS (you see a gray padlock <br /><br />[[Image:gray-padlock-jellybean]]<br /><br />or you see a green padlock<br /><br />[[Image:green-padlock-jellybean]]<br /><br />in the address bar), like your bank, your connection is authenticated and encrypted and hence safeguarded from eavesdroppers and man-in-the-middle attacks.

However, if the HTTPS page you visit includes HTTP content, the HTTP portion can be read or modified by attackers, even though the main page is served over HTTPS.  When an HTTPS page has HTTP content, we call that content “mixed”. The page you are visiting is only partially encrypted and even though it appears to be [[How do I tell if my connection to a website is secure?|secure]], it isn''t.
:;[[Image:Mixed Content Requests]]

The Mixed Content Blocker blocks potentially harmful HTTP content on HTTPS pages.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' For more information about Mixed Content (active and passive), see [https://blog.mozilla.org/tanvi/2013/04/10/mixed-content-blocking-enabled-in-firefox-23 this blog post].{/note}','<div class="for" data-for="not m25" style="display: none;"><p>The template "ApplyToFx" does not exist or has no approved revision.
</p></div><p>When you see the shield icon in the address bar, it means that Firefox for Android has blocked content that is insecure on the page you''re visiting. We''ll explain what that means and what options you have.<br><br>
<img alt="firefox has blocked content that is not secure" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-10-23-14-32-15-bd4746.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-is-mixed-content">What is mixed content?</h1>
<p>When you visit a page served over HTTP, your connection is open for eavesdropping and <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man-in-the-middle_attack">man-in-the-middle</a> attacks. Most websites are served over HTTP because they don''t involve passing sensitive information back and forth and do not need to be secured. When you visit a page fully served over HTTPS (you see a gray padlock <br><br><img alt="gray-padlock-jellybean" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-10-23-15-00-09-196afb.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br><br>or you see a green padlock<br><br><img alt="green-padlock-jellybean" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-10-23-15-03-08-03ff24.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br><br>in the address bar), like your bank, your connection is authenticated and encrypted and hence safeguarded from eavesdroppers and man-in-the-middle attacks.
</p><p>However, if the HTTPS page you visit includes HTTP content, the HTTP portion can be read or modified by attackers, even though the main page is served over HTTPS.  When an HTTPS page has HTTP content, we call that content “mixed”. The page you are visiting is only partially encrypted and even though it appears to be <a href="/en-US/kb/how-do-i-tell-if-my-connection-is-secure">secure</a>, it isn''t.
</p>
<dl><dd><dl><dt><img alt="Mixed Content Requests" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-07-16-03-20-10-da0c66.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</dd></dl>
<p>The Mixed Content Blocker blocks potentially harmful HTTP content on HTTPS pages.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> For more information about Mixed Content (active and passive), see <a href="https://blog.mozilla.org/tanvi/2013/04/10/mixed-content-blocking-enabled-in-firefox-23">this blog post</a>.</div>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "48547", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 23, 2013, 8:05:25 AM", "datetime": "2013-10-23T08:05:25-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "DO NOT APPROVE - WORK IN PROGRESS! - added green and gray padlock", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:46:11+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (48548,'en-US','mixed-content-blocker-firefox-android','Mixed content blocker in Firefox for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/mixed-content-blocker-firefox-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/mixed-content-blocker-firefox-android/revision/48548',NULL,'2013-10-23T08:16:20-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','DO NOT APPROVE - WORK IN PROGRESS!',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','{for not m25}
[[T:ApplyToFx|channel=Beta|slug=beta]]
{/for}
When you see the shield icon in the address bar, it means that Firefox for Android has blocked content that is insecure on the page you''re visiting. We''ll explain what that means and what options you have.<br><br>
[[Image:firefox has blocked content that is not secure]]
=What is mixed content?=
When you visit a page served over [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext_Transfer_Protocol HTTP], your connection is open for eavesdropping and [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man-in-the-middle_attack man-in-the-middle] attacks. Most websites are served over HTTP because they don''t involve passing sensitive information back and forth and do not need to be secured. When you visit a page fully served over [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_Secure HTTPS] (you see a gray padlock <br /><br />[[Image:gray-padlock-jellybean]]<br /><br />or you see a green padlock<br /><br />[[Image:green-padlock-jellybean]]<br /><br />in the address bar), like your bank, your connection is authenticated and encrypted and hence safeguarded from eavesdroppers and man-in-the-middle attacks.

However, if the HTTPS page you visit includes HTTP content, the HTTP portion can be read or modified by attackers, even though the main page is served over HTTPS.  When an HTTPS page has HTTP content, we call that content “mixed”. The page you are visiting is only partially encrypted and even though it appears to be secure, it isn''t.
:;[[Image:Mixed Content Requests]]

The Mixed Content Blocker blocks potentially harmful HTTP content on HTTPS pages.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' For more information about Mixed Content (active and passive), see [https://blog.mozilla.org/tanvi/2013/04/10/mixed-content-blocking-enabled-in-firefox-23 this blog post].{/note}','<div class="for" data-for="not m25" style="display: none;"><p>The template "ApplyToFx" does not exist or has no approved revision.
