<p>This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which will become  available in Thunderbird 115.0. Until version 115 is released, please refer to this article, the <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/notes/beta">beta release notes</a>, and <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-beta">the beta KB article</a>.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_new-unified-toolbar"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">New Unified Toolbar</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_simplified-application-menu"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Simplified Application Menu</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_improved-message-list"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Improved Message List</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_calendar-improvements"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Calendar Improvements</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_look-and-feel-improvements"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Look and Feel Improvements</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_more-coming"><span class="tocnumber">6</span> <span class="toctext">More Coming!</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_new-unified-toolbar">New Unified Toolbar</h1>
<p>There is now a single “Unified Toolbar” on top of the tabs, which is fully screenreader and keyboard accessible.  This toolbar is used for all  “Spaces”, which are  Mail, Address Book, Calendar, Tasks, Chat and Settings. The toolbar is customizable by right clicking (control click on macOS) &gt; <span class="button">Customize...</span>.
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-unified-toolbar-customize" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-24-15-35-00-da152a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p><span class="button">Customize...</span> offers a common set of  buttons available for all “Spaces”, which correspond to the <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar">Spaces Toolbar Introduced in 102</a>. Also available are Spaces-specific buttons for each Space. 
</p><p>Full keyboard navigation includes <span class="key">Tab</span> and arrow keys to navigate, <span class="key">Enter</span> to add a button to the toolbar, <span class="key">Delete</span> to remove it, and <span class="key">Alt</span> with arrow keys to move buttons around on the toolbar, in addition to mouse actions such as drag and drop. 
</p><p><br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-unified-toolbar-buttons-customize-screen" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-24-15-46-56-cb80ef.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>We are working on improving the other toolbars (e.g. composer, message window, event editor, etc.) for a future release.
</p>
<h1 id="w_simplified-application-menu">Simplified Application Menu</h1>
<p>The Application Menu (also known as appmenu or “Thunderbird Menu”), <span class="menu">≡</span>, has been simplified and reduced to make it easier to navigate. Only global context items are listed, which do not need to be updated or changed based on which Thunderbird “Space” you are using. There are now at most two layers of submenus so you no longer need to dig around in multiple sublevels to find what you need.
</p><p>Of course, you can still find other menu items in the Thunderbird menu bar by pressing the <span class="key">ALT</span> key on Windows and Linux (and in the macOS Thunderbird menu bar). 
</p>
<h1 id="w_improved-message-list">Improved Message List</h1>
<p>The message list now uses modern HTML technology which is faster and has a whole host of behind the scenes improvements to make improving it in the future much easier.
</p><p>A new message list header shows the current folder name, number of messages, Quick Filter, and “Message list display options” icon.
</p><p>The Vertical View Layout now defaults to a new two line “card view” which is more compact and better suited to the Vertical Layout.  With the customization options you can change the Vertical View back to the version 102 “table view”.
</p>
<h1 id="w_calendar-improvements">Calendar Improvements</h1>
<p>To summarize, there is an improved “mini-month” layout, a unified toolbar for the Calendar “Space” on top of the tabs, improvements to the day/week/month grid, and some other minor changes.
</p><p>For the mini-month:
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-calendar-minimonth" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-31-21-10-53-c1c4fd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<ul><li> [a] The Today icon in the “mini-month” has been changed from an <span class="button">◯</span> button to a graphical icon on the <strong>left</strong>. 
</li><li> Dates with an event or task are now marked with a blue dot to allow easier scanning without relying on color.
</li><li> [b,c] New buttons are placed in a more contextually relevant place: <span class="button">+ New Event</span>, <span class="button">New Calendar</span>.
</li></ul>
<p>For the day/week/month grid:
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-calendar-day-week-month-grid" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-31-21-19-08-93ac56.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<ul><li> [a] The <span class="button">Today</span> navigation button has been changed to the same Today icon used in the mini-month.
</li><li> [b] The “Day, Week, Multiweek, Month” tab bar was changed to radio style buttons to indicate you can only select one.
</li><li> [c] There is a new Settings icon to the <strong>right</strong> of the “Day, Week, Multiweek, Month” again in a more contextually relevant place.
</li></ul>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_look-and-feel-improvements">Look and Feel Improvements</h1>
<p>All the buttons in the Folder Pane and Unified Toolbars have been revamped using modern CSS and HTML Technology. The buttons are now more accessible and customizable. 
</p><p>Many menus and buttons have been moved and changed to be closer to where they are more relevant in the context of Thunderbird. One example is the new <span class="menu">Folder Modes</span> sub menu which is documented below.
</p><p>Miscellaneous but important improvements:
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-new-message-get-messages-folder-pane-options" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-25-21-21-06-24e829.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<ul><li> 1. There is a new <span class="button">+ New Message</span> button to compose a new email, chat message, etc. that replaces the old <span class="button">Write</span> button.
</li><li> 2. There is a new "Get messages" icon to the <strong>left</strong> of <span class="button">+ New Message</span>. This new icon replaces the old <span class="button">Get Messages</span> button.
</li><li> 3. There is a new <span class="menu">Folder pane options</span> menu also known as a "meatball" or "three horizonal dots" menu, <span class="menu">⋯</span>, to the <strong>right</strong> of   <span class="button">+ New Message</span>. This menu has a <span class="menu">Folder Modes</span> sub menu which replaces the 102 <span class="menu">⋯</span> menu at the top of the "Folder Pane" toolbar.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_more-coming">More Coming!</h1>
<p>In future versions of this article, we will document more improvements and link to more articles that explain the changes in more detail.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "261464", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "May 31, 2023, 2:24:33 PM", "datetime": "2023-05-31T14:24:33-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Work In Progress. PLEASE do NOT localize! until 3 weeks before TB115 Supernova. Needs to be shortened and screenshots moved to separate more detailed articles.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 2, 2023, 1:46:22 AM", "datetime": "2023-06-02T01:46:22-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:38:52+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (261850,'en-US','remote-content-in-messages','Remote Content in Messages','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/remote-content-in-messages','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/remote-content-in-messages/revision/261850',127133,'2023-06-07T01:40:48-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','clarify permanent versus temporary',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','Email messages can contain remote content such as images or stylesheets. To protect your privacy, Thunderbird does not load remote content automatically, but instead shows a notification bar to indicate that it blocked remote content.

__TOC__

= What is remote content and why is it blocked? =

Remote content are parts of a message (such as images, stylesheets, or videos) which are not included in the message itself, but are downloaded from the Internet when you view the message.

Remote content is a privacy concern because it allows the message sender to know: 

* each time you view the message
* rough details about what application and what platform you are using
* your current geographic location (a rough approximation by IP address)
* that your email address is actually used ("active")

This is almost the same information websites commonly collect about you during normal web surfing. While browsing has the same privacy implications, what makes remote content in messages different is that it is targeted at you initially, so you can assume your access is directly linkable to your email address. This is also why spam often contains remote images (also known as "web bugs") which allow the spammer to mark your address as valid if the image is ever loaded.

= How does Thunderbird protect my privacy? =

By default, Thunderbird blocks, and does not download, remote content so that the sender does not get any information about you. Instead, it displays a notification bar with the message ''''''To protect your privacy, Thunderbird has blocked remote content in this message''''''.

{for win}[[Image:Remote content blocked]]{/for}{for mac,linux}[[Image:Remote content blocked Lin]]{/for}

== Display remote content for a particular message, sender or website ==

If you trust the sender, you can allow remote content to be downloaded. Emails look much better with images and stylesheets turned on. To load remote content, click {button {for win}Options{/for}{for mac,linux}Preferences{/for}} in the notification bar and select one of the following options:

{for not tb45}{for win}[[Image:Remote content blocking options Win]]{/for}{for mac,linux}[[Image:Remote content blocking options]]{/for}{/for}
{for tb45}{for win}[[Image:Remote content blocking options Win TB45]]{/for}{for mac,linux}[[Image:Remote content blocking options TB45]]{/for}{/for}

* {menu Show remote content in this message} to temporarily show remote content just for this particular message;
* {menu Edit remote content {for win}options{/for}{for mac,linux}preferences{/for}…} to display remote content by default (see next section);
* {menu Allow remote content from <website>} to permanently show the remote content stored starting now on this site{for tb45} (to load remote content from all sites the message refers to, click {menu Allow remote content from all origins listed above}){/for};
* {menu Allow remote content from <email address>} to permanently show the remote content starting now for all messages from this sender.

{note}{menu Allow remote content from…} permanently adds the sender email address or sites (currently blocked in the message) to the remote content exceptions list.{/note}

Note however, that email addresses are easily spoofable in contrast to websites that serve remote content. As long as the network is not compromised, it is much more difficult to spoof content than email addresses.

== Display remote content by default ==

If you are willing to accept the privacy implications of displaying remote content for all messages, Thunderbird allows you to display remote content by default. To set it so:
#[[Template:optionspreferences TB]]
#Select the {menu Privacy} panel.
#Check the {menu Allow remote content in messages} checkbox to have all remote content loaded by default.
#*You can exclude specific sites from the rule. Click the {button Exceptions…} button, type the website or email address and click {button Block} or {button Allow}.','<p>Email messages can contain remote content such as images or stylesheets. To protect your privacy, Thunderbird does not load remote content automatically, but instead shows a notification bar to indicate that it blocked remote content.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_what-is-remote-content-and-why-is-it-blocked"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">What is remote content and why is it blocked?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_how-does-thunderbird-protect-my-privacy"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">How does Thunderbird protect my privacy?</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_display-remote-content-for-a-particular-message-sender-or-website"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Display remote content for a particular message, sender or website</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_display-remote-content-by-default"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Display remote content by default</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_what-is-remote-content-and-why-is-it-blocked">What is remote content and why is it blocked?</h1>
<p>Remote content are parts of a message (such as images, stylesheets, or videos) which are not included in the message itself, but are downloaded from the Internet when you view the message.
</p><p>Remote content is a privacy concern because it allows the message sender to know: 
</p>
<ul><li> each time you view the message
</li><li> rough details about what application and what platform you are using
</li><li> your current geographic location (a rough approximation by IP address)
</li><li> that your email address is actually used ("active")
</li></ul>
<p>This is almost the same information websites commonly collect about you during normal web surfing. While browsing has the same privacy implications, what makes remote content in messages different is that it is targeted at you initially, so you can assume your access is directly linkable to your email address. This is also why spam often contains remote images (also known as "web bugs") which allow the spammer to mark your address as valid if the image is ever loaded.
</p>
<h1 id="w_how-does-thunderbird-protect-my-privacy">How does Thunderbird protect my privacy?</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird blocks, and does not download, remote content so that the sender does not get any information about you. Instead, it displays a notification bar with the message <strong>To protect your privacy, Thunderbird has blocked remote content in this message</strong>.
</p><p><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="Remote content blocked" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2015-10-31-01-45-47-1fd2a5.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux"><img alt="Remote content blocked Lin" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-06-26-04-59-26-4e3374.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</p>
<h2 id="w_display-remote-content-for-a-particular-message-sender-or-website">Display remote content for a particular message, sender or website</h2>
<p>If you trust the sender, you can allow remote content to be downloaded. Emails look much better with images and stylesheets turned on. To load remote content, click <span class="button"><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Options</span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux">Preferences</span></span> in the notification bar and select one of the following options:
</p><p><span class="for" data-for="not tb45" style="display: none;"><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="Remote content blocking options Win" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2015-10-24-02-48-19-998820.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux"><img alt="Remote content blocking options" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-07-15-14-49-51-ca9091.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span></span>
<span class="for" data-for="tb45"><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="Remote content blocking options Win TB45" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-14-10-51-09-3e7d0b.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux"><img alt="Remote content blocking options TB45" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-06-25-09-51-48-ba4a40.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span></span>
</p>
<ul><li> <span class="menu">Show remote content in this message</span> to temporarily show remote content just for this particular message;
</li><li> <span class="menu">Edit remote content <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">options</span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux">preferences</span>…</span> to display remote content by default (see next section);
</li><li> <span class="menu">Allow remote content from &lt;website&gt;</span> to permanently show the remote content stored starting now on this site<span class="for" data-for="tb45"> (to load remote content from all sites the message refers to, click <span class="menu">Allow remote content from all origins listed above</span>)</span>;
</li><li> <span class="menu">Allow remote content from &lt;email address&gt;</span> to permanently show the remote content starting now for all messages from this sender.
</li></ul>
<div class="note"><span class="menu">Allow remote content from…</span> permanently adds the sender email address or sites (currently blocked in the message) to the remote content exceptions list.</div>
<p>Note however, that email addresses are easily spoofable in contrast to websites that serve remote content. As long as the network is not compromised, it is much more difficult to spoof content than email addresses.
</p>
<h2 id="w_display-remote-content-by-default">Display remote content by default</h2>
<p>If you are willing to accept the privacy implications of displaying remote content for all messages, Thunderbird allows you to display remote content by default. To set it so:
</p>
<ol><li>Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li>Select the <span class="menu">Privacy</span> panel.
</li><li>Check the <span class="menu">Allow remote content in messages</span> checkbox to have all remote content loaded by default.
<ul><li>You can exclude specific sites from the rule. Click the <span class="button">Exceptions…</span> button, type the website or email address and click <span class="button">Block</span> or <span class="button">Allow</span>.
</li></ul>
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "261850", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 7, 2023, 1:40:48 AM", "datetime": "2023-06-07T01:40:48-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "clarify permanent versus temporary", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:38:49+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (262926,'en-US','getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova','Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova/revision/262926',NULL,'2023-07-06T14:28:18-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP: Please do not approve, do not localize, ping roland with any questions',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','This article describes Thunderbird''s main window, as seen below. Refer also to the many [[keyboard shortcuts TB|keyboard shortcuts]] available in the main window to help you quickly perform functions, such as message actions and navigation, searching and calendar actions. 
[[Image:tb115-classic-table-view]] 

