<p><strong>Try it out:</strong> Experiment with different arrangements. You can always restore the default toolbar settings by clicking <span class="button">Restore default</span>. When you are finished customizing, click <span class="button">Save</span>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "263188", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 11:14:38 AM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T11:14:38-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP: Please do not localize, ping roland with any questions:fixed a typo", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 11:15:16 AM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T11:15:16-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:37:55+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (263189,'en-US','how-customize-toolbars','How to Customize Toolbars','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/how-customize-toolbars','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/how-customize-toolbars/revision/263189',263186,'2023-07-11T11:16:59-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','link to WIP Unified Toolbar article',1,'2023-07-11T11:17:27-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{note} For Thunderbird 115 Supernova and newer, please refer to: [[Unified Toolbar]].{/note}

Thunderbird''s toolbars provide easy access to common features. This article explains how you can customize toolbars to make Thunderbird work the way you do.

__TOC__

= How do I show and hide the different toolbars? =
Most toolbars can be shown or hidden depending on your needs.
To show or hide a toolbar, {for win,linux}right-click{/for}{for mac}control-click{/for} on an empty section of the Tab Strip at the top and check or uncheck it in the pop-up menu.
{for win}
*''''''Menu Bar:'''''' This toolbar at the top of the Thunderbird window contains the browser menus ({menu File}, {menu Edit}, {menu Help}, etc.). On Windows Vista and later versions, the Menu Bar is hidden by default, and its features are contained in the Thunderbird menu button, {menu ≡}. If the Menu Bar is hidden, you can temporarily show it by pressing the {key Alt} key.
{/for}
{for linux}
*''''''Menu Bar:'''''' This toolbar at the top of the Thunderbird window contains the Thunderbird menus ({menu File}, {menu Edit}, {menu Help}, etc.). 
{/for}
{for mac}
*''''''Menu Bar:'''''' This toolbar at the top of the Thunderbird window contains the Thunderbird menus ({menu File}, {menu Edit}, {menu Help}, etc.) and you can''t hide it on the Mac.
{/for}
*''''''Mail Toolbar'''''': This toolbar (which is underneath the Menu Bar) contains mail related buttons and menus such as  {button Get Messages}, {button Write}, {button Chat},  {button Tag}, {button Quick Filter}, etc.
*''''''Tab Strip:'''''' This is where your tabs such as the tabs for your inbox and for Thunderbird Add-ons are displayed. You can''t hide it.

[[Image:Thunderbird Toolbars - win]]
= How do I customize or rearrange toolbar items? =
# {for win,linux}Right click{/for}{for mac}Control click{/for} an empty section of the Tab Strip and select {menu Customize...}. <br />[[Image:Right Click Customize]]<br />The Customize Toolbar window opens. <br />[[Image:Thunderbird Customize Toolbar Window]]
# Change the toolbar items as necessary. 
#* To add an item, drag it from the Customize Toolbar window onto the toolbar where you want it to appear.
#* To remove an item, drag it to the Customize Toolbar window.
#* To rearrange an item, drag it to the spot where you want it.
# When finished, click {button Done}.

''''''Try it out:'''''' Experiment with different arrangements. You can always restore the default toolbar settings by clicking {button Restore Default Set} in the Customize Toolbar window.

= Toolbar Troubleshooting  =
See these toolbar troubleshooting articles:
*[[What happened to the Thunderbird File, Edit, and View menus?]]','<div class="note"> For Thunderbird 115 Supernova and newer, please refer to: <a href="/en-US/kb/unified-toolbar">Unified Toolbar</a>.</div>
<p>Thunderbird''s toolbars provide easy access to common features. This article explains how you can customize toolbars to make Thunderbird work the way you do.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_how-do-i-show-and-hide-the-different-toolbars"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">How do I show and hide the different toolbars?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_how-do-i-customize-or-rearrange-toolbar-items"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">How do I customize or rearrange toolbar items?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_toolbar-troubleshooting"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Toolbar Troubleshooting</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_how-do-i-show-and-hide-the-different-toolbars">How do I show and hide the different toolbars?</h1>
<p>Most toolbars can be shown or hidden depending on your needs.
To show or hide a toolbar, <span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">right-click</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">control-click</span> on an empty section of the Tab Strip at the top and check or uncheck it in the pop-up menu.
</p><div class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">
<ul><li><strong>Menu Bar:</strong> This toolbar at the top of the Thunderbird window contains the browser menus (<span class="menu">File</span>, <span class="menu">Edit</span>, <span class="menu">Help</span>, etc.). On Windows Vista and later versions, the Menu Bar is hidden by default, and its features are contained in the Thunderbird menu button, <span class="menu">≡</span>. If the Menu Bar is hidden, you can temporarily show it by pressing the <span class="key">Alt</span> key.
</li></ul>
</div><div class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">
<ul><li><strong>Menu Bar:</strong> This toolbar at the top of the Thunderbird window contains the Thunderbird menus (<span class="menu">File</span>, <span class="menu">Edit</span>, <span class="menu">Help</span>, etc.). 
</li></ul>
</div><div class="for" data-for="mac">
<ul><li><strong>Menu Bar:</strong> This toolbar at the top of the Thunderbird window contains the Thunderbird menus (<span class="menu">File</span>, <span class="menu">Edit</span>, <span class="menu">Help</span>, etc.) and you can''t hide it on the Mac.
</li></ul>
</div>
<ul><li><strong>Mail Toolbar</strong>: This toolbar (which is underneath the Menu Bar) contains mail related buttons and menus such as  <span class="button">Get Messages</span>, <span class="button">Write</span>, <span class="button">Chat</span>,  <span class="button">Tag</span>, <span class="button">Quick Filter</span>, etc.
</li><li><strong>Tab Strip:</strong> This is where your tabs such as the tabs for your inbox and for Thunderbird Add-ons are displayed. You can''t hide it.
</li></ul>
<p><img alt="Thunderbird Toolbars - win" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-11-20-10-43-19-d3036f.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<h1 id="w_how-do-i-customize-or-rearrange-toolbar-items">How do I customize or rearrange toolbar items?</h1>
<ol><li> <span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">Right click</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">Control click</span> an empty section of the Tab Strip and select <span class="menu">Customize...</span>. <br><img alt="Right Click Customize" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-11-20-10-53-08-4a1b83.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br>The Customize Toolbar window opens. <br><img alt="Thunderbird Customize Toolbar Window" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-11-20-10-55-08-b877e1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> Change the toolbar items as necessary. 
<ul><li> To add an item, drag it from the Customize Toolbar window onto the toolbar where you want it to appear.
</li><li> To remove an item, drag it to the Customize Toolbar window.
</li><li> To rearrange an item, drag it to the spot where you want it.
</li></ul>
</li><li> When finished, click <span class="button">Done</span>.
</li></ol>
<p><strong>Try it out:</strong> Experiment with different arrangements. You can always restore the default toolbar settings by clicking <span class="button">Restore Default Set</span> in the Customize Toolbar window.
</p>
<h1 id="w_toolbar-troubleshooting">Toolbar Troubleshooting</h1>
<p>See these toolbar troubleshooting articles:
</p>
<ul><li><a href="/en-US/kb/display-thunderbird-menus-and-toolbar">Display the Thunderbird menus and toolbar</a>
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "263189", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 11:16:59 AM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T11:16:59-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "link to WIP Unified Toolbar article", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 11:17:27 AM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T11:17:27-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:37:53+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (263191,'en-US','unified-toolbar','Unified Toolbar','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/unified-toolbar','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/unified-toolbar/revision/263191',263188,'2023-07-11T13:27:10-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fix some typos',1,'2023-07-11T13:27:25-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,1,'2024-01-31T20:18:10.079126-08:00','rtanglao','approved','{note} For versions of Thunderbird prior to Thunderbird 115 Supernova, please refer to: [[How to Customize Toolbars]].{/note}

For Thunderbird 115 Supernova and newer there is a “Unified Toolbar” on top of the Tabs Bar, which is fully screenreader and keyboard accessible.  

This toolbar is used for all  “Spaces”, which are  Mail, Address Book, Calendar, Tasks, Chat and Settings. The toolbar is customizable by right clicking (control click on macOS) > {button Customize...}.
<br />
<br />
[[Image:tb115-unified-toolbar-customize]]

{button Customize...} offers a common set of  buttons available for all “Spaces”, which correspond to the [[New in Thunderbird 102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar|Spaces Toolbar Introduced in 102]]. Also available are Spaces-specific buttons for each Space. 

Full keyboard navigation includes {key Tab} and arrow keys to navigate, {key Enter} to add a button to the toolbar, {key Delete} to remove it, and {key Alt} with arrow keys to move buttons around on the toolbar, in addition to mouse actions such as drag and drop. 

Please refer to the following screenshot:
[[Image:tb-115-customize-unified-toolbar-mail-space-selected]] 

* 1. To select a toolbar to customize, click on one of the buttons for the desired Space you wish to customize  i.e. {button Mail},  {button Address book}, {button Calendar},  etc.
* 2. This is the Universal Toolbar for the Space you have selected that you are customizing and its current buttons. Drag and Drop to move the buttons around, or use the Context Menu (control click on macOS, right click on Windows and Linux) to move the buttons. You can also use the keyboard: {key Tab} to select a button, {key Delete} to remove it, and {key Alt} with arrow keys to move buttons around on the toolbar, and {key Enter} to add a button to the toolbar.
* 3. These are the buttons that are available for the specific Space you have selected.
* 4. These are the buttons that are available for all the Spaces including the one that you have selected.
* 5. This is the Button Style. The default is {menu Icons beside text}

''''''Try it out:'''''' Experiment with some buttons and different arrangements. Try out the Context Menus on the buttons by right clicking (control click on macOS) You can always restore the default toolbar settings by clicking {button Restore default}. When you are finished customizing, click {button Save}.','<div class="note"> For versions of Thunderbird prior to Thunderbird 115 Supernova, please refer to: <a href="/en-US/kb/how-customize-toolbars">How to Customize Toolbars</a>.</div>
<p>For Thunderbird 115 Supernova and newer there is a “Unified Toolbar” on top of the Tabs Bar, which is fully screenreader and keyboard accessible.  
</p><p>This toolbar is used for all  “Spaces”, which are  Mail, Address Book, Calendar, Tasks, Chat and Settings. The toolbar is customizable by right clicking (control click on macOS) &gt; <span class="button">Customize...</span>.
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-unified-toolbar-customize" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-24-15-35-00-da152a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p><span class="button">Customize...</span> offers a common set of  buttons available for all “Spaces”, which correspond to the <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar">Spaces Toolbar Introduced in 102</a>. Also available are Spaces-specific buttons for each Space. 
</p><p>Full keyboard navigation includes <span class="key">Tab</span> and arrow keys to navigate, <span class="key">Enter</span> to add a button to the toolbar, <span class="key">Delete</span> to remove it, and <span class="key">Alt</span> with arrow keys to move buttons around on the toolbar, in addition to mouse actions such as drag and drop. 
</p><p>Please refer to the following screenshot:
<img alt="tb-115-customize-unified-toolbar-mail-space-selected" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-07-11-18-03-34-b603f8.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<ul><li> 1. To select a toolbar to customize, click on one of the buttons for the desired Space you wish to customize  i.e. <span class="button">Mail</span>,  <span class="button">Address book</span>, <span class="button">Calendar</span>,  etc.
</li><li> 2. This is the Universal Toolbar for the Space you have selected that you are customizing and its current buttons. Drag and Drop to move the buttons around, or use the Context Menu (control click on macOS, right click on Windows and Linux) to move the buttons. You can also use the keyboard: <span class="key">Tab</span> to select a button, <span class="key">Delete</span> to remove it, and <span class="key">Alt</span> with arrow keys to move buttons around on the toolbar, and <span class="key">Enter</span> to add a button to the toolbar.
</li><li> 3. These are the buttons that are available for the specific Space you have selected.
</li><li> 4. These are the buttons that are available for all the Spaces including the one that you have selected.
</li><li> 5. This is the Button Style. The default is <span class="menu">Icons beside text</span>
</li></ul>
<p><strong>Try it out:</strong> Experiment with some buttons and different arrangements. Try out the Context Menus on the buttons by right clicking (control click on macOS) You can always restore the default toolbar settings by clicking <span class="button">Restore default</span>. When you are finished customizing, click <span class="button">Save</span>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "263191", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 1:27:10 PM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T13:27:10-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fix some typos", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 1:27:25 PM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T13:27:25-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Jan 31, 2024, 8:18:10 PM", "datetime": "2024-01-31T20:18:10.079126-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:37:50+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (263192,'en-US','new-thunderbird-115-supernova','New in Thunderbird 115 Supernova','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-115-supernova','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-115-supernova/revision/263192',263095,'2023-07-11T13:32:14-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP: Please do not approve, do not localize, ping roland with any questions, link to [[Unified Toolbar]]',1,'2023-07-11T13:32:57-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{warning}This document is still under construction. If you have ideas to improve or items to add, please comment in the [https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-115-supernova/discuss discussion].{/warning}

This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which will become  available in Thunderbird 115.0. Until version 115 is released, please refer to this article, the [https://www.thunderbird.net/notes/beta beta release notes], and [[Thunderbird Beta|the beta KB article]].

For more details on the main window in TB115, please refer to: [[Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)]]

__TOC__

= New Unified Toolbar =

There is now a single “Unified Toolbar” on top of the Tabs Bar, which is fully screenreader and keyboard accessible.  This toolbar is used for all  “Spaces”, which are  Mail, Address Book, Calendar, Tasks, Chat and Settings. The toolbar is customizable by right clicking (control click on macOS) > {button Customize...}.
<br />
<br />
[[Image:tb115-unified-toolbar-customize]]

{button Customize...} offers a common set of  buttons available for all “Spaces”, which correspond to the [[New in Thunderbird 102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar|Spaces Toolbar Introduced in 102]]. Also available are Spaces-specific buttons for each Space. 

Full keyboard navigation includes {key Tab} and arrow keys to navigate, {key Enter} to add a button to the toolbar, {key Delete} to remove it, and {key Alt} with arrow keys to move buttons around on the toolbar, in addition to mouse actions such as drag and drop. 

Please refer to [[Unified Toolbar]] for more details on the Unified Toolbar and customizations.

We are working on improving the other toolbars (e.g. composer, message window, event editor, etc.) for a future release.

= Simplified Application Menu=

The Application Menu (also known as appmenu or “Thunderbird Menu”), {menu ≡}, has been simplified and reduced to make it easier to navigate. Only global context items are listed, which do not need to be updated or changed based on which Thunderbird “Space” you are using. There are now at most two layers of submenus so you no longer need to dig around in multiple sublevels to find what you need.

Of course, you can still find other menu items in the Thunderbird menu bar by pressing the {key ALT} key on Windows and Linux (and in the macOS Thunderbird menu bar). 

= Improved Message List =

The message list now uses modern HTML technology which is faster and has a whole host of behind the scenes improvements to make improving it in the future much easier.

A new Message List Header shows the current folder name, number of messages, Quick Filter, and new “Message list display options” icon, [Image:tb115-display-options-icon-offical-from-micah]] .

The Vertical View Layout now defaults to a new two line “Card View” which is more compact and better suited to the Vertical Layout.  With the customization options, you can change the Vertical View back to the version 102 “Table View”.

= Calendar Improvements = 

To summarize, there is an improved “mini-month” layout, a Unified Toolbar for the Calendar “Space” on top of the tabs, improvements to the day/week/month grid, and some other minor changes.

For the mini-month:
<br />
<br />
[[Image:tb115-calendar-minimonth]] 
* [a] The Today icon in the “mini-month” has been changed from an {button ◯} button to a graphical icon on the ''''''left''''''. 
* Dates with an event or task are now marked with a blue dot to allow easier scanning without relying on color.
* [b,c] New buttons are placed in a more contextually relevant place: {button + New Event}, {button New Calendar}.

For the day/week/month grid:
<br />
<br />
[[Image:tb115-calendar-day-week-month-grid]] 

* [a] The {button Today} navigation button has been changed to the same Today icon used in the mini-month.
* [b] The “Day, Week, Multiweek, Month” tab bar was changed to radio style buttons to indicate you can only select one.
* [c] There is a new Settings icon to the ''''''right'''''' of the “Day, Week, Multiweek, Month” again in a more contextually relevant place.


= Look and Feel Improvements: New Message Button, Get messages, Folder pane options meatball menu = 

All the buttons in the Folder Pane and Unified Toolbars have been revamped using modern CSS and HTML Technology. The buttons are now more accessible and customizable. 

Many menus and buttons have been moved and changed to be closer to where they are more relevant in the context of Thunderbird. One example is the new {menu Folder Modes} sub menu which is documented below.

Miscellaneous but important improvements:
<br />
<br />
[[Image:tb115-new-message-get-messages-folder-pane-options]]
* 1. There is a new {button + New Message} button to compose a new email, chat message, etc. that replaces the old {button Write} button.
* 2. There is a new "Get messages" icon to the ''''''left'''''' of {button + New Message}. This new icon replaces the old {button Get Messages} button.
* 3. There is a new {menu Folder pane options} menu also known as a "meatball" or "three horizontal dots" menu, {menu ⋯}, to the ''''''right'''''' of   {button + New Message}. This menu has options to show the Total message count, Folder Size and hide the "Get Messages" and "New Message" icons as well as hide the Local Folders  and Folder Pane Header. It also has a {menu Folder Modes} sub menu. This sub menu replaces the 102 {menu ⋯} menu at the top of the "Folder Pane" toolbar. 

= Other Look and Feel Improvements =
*  In the Mail Space, the "Quick Filter" icon that toggles between hiding or showing the Quick Filter Toolbar'' is always visible (in prior versions it became invisible when you removed it via customization).
* There is a new tabular view in Address Book.
* "Touch” has been removed from {menu ≡} > {button Density}. It’s now Compact, Default and Relaxed, before 115, it was Compact, Normal, Touch.
* A new Card View for messages can be turned on by clicking the new "Message list display options" icon (which is on the right of the "Quick Filter" icon): [[Image:tb115-display-options-icon-offical-from-micah]] > {menu Cards View} . Please see: [[Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)#w_vertical-view-layout-with-card-view]].','<div class="warning">This document is still under construction. If you have ideas to improve or items to add, please comment in the <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-115-supernova/discuss">discussion</a>.</div>
<p>This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which will become  available in Thunderbird 115.0. Until version 115 is released, please refer to this article, the <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/notes/beta">beta release notes</a>, and <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-beta">the beta KB article</a>.
</p><p>For more details on the main window in TB115, please refer to: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova">Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)</a>
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_new-unified-toolbar"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">New Unified Toolbar</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_simplified-application-menu"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Simplified Application Menu</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_improved-message-list"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Improved Message List</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_calendar-improvements"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Calendar Improvements</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_look-and-feel-improvements-new-message-button-get-messages-folder-pane-options-meatball-menu"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Look and Feel Improvements: New Message Button, Get messages, Folder pane options meatball menu</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_other-look-and-feel-improvements"><span class="tocnumber">6</span> <span class="toctext">Other Look and Feel Improvements</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_new-unified-toolbar">New Unified Toolbar</h1>
<p>There is now a single “Unified Toolbar” on top of the Tabs Bar, which is fully screenreader and keyboard accessible.  This toolbar is used for all  “Spaces”, which are  Mail, Address Book, Calendar, Tasks, Chat and Settings. The toolbar is customizable by right clicking (control click on macOS) &gt; <span class="button">Customize...</span>.
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-unified-toolbar-customize" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-24-15-35-00-da152a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p><span class="button">Customize...</span> offers a common set of  buttons available for all “Spaces”, which correspond to the <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar">Spaces Toolbar Introduced in 102</a>. Also available are Spaces-specific buttons for each Space. 
</p><p>Full keyboard navigation includes <span class="key">Tab</span> and arrow keys to navigate, <span class="key">Enter</span> to add a button to the toolbar, <span class="key">Delete</span> to remove it, and <span class="key">Alt</span> with arrow keys to move buttons around on the toolbar, in addition to mouse actions such as drag and drop. 
</p><p>Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/unified-toolbar">Unified Toolbar</a> for more details on the Unified Toolbar and customizations.
</p><p>We are working on improving the other toolbars (e.g. composer, message window, event editor, etc.) for a future release.
</p>
<h1 id="w_simplified-application-menu">Simplified Application Menu</h1>
<p>The Application Menu (also known as appmenu or “Thunderbird Menu”), <span class="menu">≡</span>, has been simplified and reduced to make it easier to navigate. Only global context items are listed, which do not need to be updated or changed based on which Thunderbird “Space” you are using. There are now at most two layers of submenus so you no longer need to dig around in multiple sublevels to find what you need.
</p><p>Of course, you can still find other menu items in the Thunderbird menu bar by pressing the <span class="key">ALT</span> key on Windows and Linux (and in the macOS Thunderbird menu bar). 
</p>
<h1 id="w_improved-message-list">Improved Message List</h1>
<p>The message list now uses modern HTML technology which is faster and has a whole host of behind the scenes improvements to make improving it in the future much easier.
</p><p>A new Message List Header shows the current folder name, number of messages, Quick Filter, and new “Message list display options” icon, [Image:tb115-display-options-icon-offical-from-micah]] .
</p><p>The Vertical View Layout now defaults to a new two line “Card View” which is more compact and better suited to the Vertical Layout.  With the customization options, you can change the Vertical View back to the version 102 “Table View”.
</p>
<h1 id="w_calendar-improvements">Calendar Improvements</h1>
<p>To summarize, there is an improved “mini-month” layout, a Unified Toolbar for the Calendar “Space” on top of the tabs, improvements to the day/week/month grid, and some other minor changes.
</p><p>For the mini-month:
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-calendar-minimonth" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-31-21-10-53-c1c4fd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<ul><li> [a] The Today icon in the “mini-month” has been changed from an <span class="button">◯</span> button to a graphical icon on the <strong>left</strong>. 
</li><li> Dates with an event or task are now marked with a blue dot to allow easier scanning without relying on color.
</li><li> [b,c] New buttons are placed in a more contextually relevant place: <span class="button">+ New Event</span>, <span class="button">New Calendar</span>.
</li></ul>
<p>For the day/week/month grid:
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-calendar-day-week-month-grid" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-31-21-19-08-93ac56.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<ul><li> [a] The <span class="button">Today</span> navigation button has been changed to the same Today icon used in the mini-month.
</li><li> [b] The “Day, Week, Multiweek, Month” tab bar was changed to radio style buttons to indicate you can only select one.
</li><li> [c] There is a new Settings icon to the <strong>right</strong> of the “Day, Week, Multiweek, Month” again in a more contextually relevant place.
</li></ul>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_look-and-feel-improvements-new-message-button-get-messages-folder-pane-options-meatball-menu">Look and Feel Improvements: New Message Button, Get messages, Folder pane options meatball menu</h1>
<p>All the buttons in the Folder Pane and Unified Toolbars have been revamped using modern CSS and HTML Technology. The buttons are now more accessible and customizable. 
</p><p>Many menus and buttons have been moved and changed to be closer to where they are more relevant in the context of Thunderbird. One example is the new <span class="menu">Folder Modes</span> sub menu which is documented below.
</p><p>Miscellaneous but important improvements:
<br>
<br>
<img alt="tb115-new-message-get-messages-folder-pane-options" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-05-25-21-21-06-24e829.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<ul><li> 1. There is a new <span class="button">+ New Message</span> button to compose a new email, chat message, etc. that replaces the old <span class="button">Write</span> button.
</li><li> 2. There is a new "Get messages" icon to the <strong>left</strong> of <span class="button">+ New Message</span>. This new icon replaces the old <span class="button">Get Messages</span> button.
</li><li> 3. There is a new <span class="menu">Folder pane options</span> menu also known as a "meatball" or "three horizontal dots" menu, <span class="menu">⋯</span>, to the <strong>right</strong> of   <span class="button">+ New Message</span>. This menu has options to show the Total message count, Folder Size and hide the "Get Messages" and "New Message" icons as well as hide the Local Folders  and Folder Pane Header. It also has a <span class="menu">Folder Modes</span> sub menu. This sub menu replaces the 102 <span class="menu">⋯</span> menu at the top of the "Folder Pane" toolbar. 
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_other-look-and-feel-improvements">Other Look and Feel Improvements</h1>
<ul><li>  In the Mail Space, the "Quick Filter" icon that toggles between hiding or showing the Quick Filter Toolbar'' is always visible (in prior versions it became invisible when you removed it via customization).
</li><li> There is a new tabular view in Address Book.
</li><li> "Touch” has been removed from <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="button">Density</span>. It’s now Compact, Default and Relaxed, before 115, it was Compact, Normal, Touch.
</li><li> A new Card View for messages can be turned on by clicking the new "Message list display options" icon (which is on the right of the "Quick Filter" icon): <img alt="tb115-display-options-icon-offical-from-micah" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-07-09-20-04-06-4a2c27.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> &gt; <span class="menu">Cards View</span> . Please see: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova#w_vertical-view-layout-with-card-view">Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "263192", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 1:32:14 PM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T13:32:14-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP: Please do not approve, do not localize, ping roland with any questions, link to [[Unified Toolbar]]", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jul 11, 2023, 1:32:57 PM", "datetime": "2023-07-11T13:32:57-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:37:48+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (263727,'en-US','userchromecss-js-usercontent-unsupported','userChrome.css and .js and userContent are unsupported','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/userchromecss-js-usercontent-unsupported','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/userchromecss-js-usercontent-unsupported/revision/263727',NULL,'2023-07-26T09:55:54-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','first draft, do not approve, WIP',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','userChrome.css is a CSS style sheet that allows anyone who uses Thunderbird to make changes to its user interface. The CSS rules in userChrome.css are applied to the user interface, and can override nearly every built-in style rule.  userChrome.js allows anyone to inject Javascript code that changes Thunderbird. userContent.css and userContent.js are similar features for websites.

userChrome.css, userChrome.js, userContent.css and userUContent.js are very powerful tools, intended for testing by technical users who are familiar with JS, CSS and HTML. The Thunderbird Project does not offer support for modifications to these files. This is because Thunderbird developers are constantly making improvements to the HTML elements which alters the CSS selectors as well as the underlying Javascript code. These changes mean that customizations based on userChrome.js, userChrome.css, userContent.js and userContent.css can break Thunderbird at any time and are very difficult to troubleshoot.','<p>userChrome.css is a CSS style sheet that allows anyone who uses Thunderbird to make changes to its user interface. The CSS rules in userChrome.css are applied to the user interface, and can override nearly every built-in style rule.  userChrome.js allows anyone to inject Javascript code that changes Thunderbird. userContent.css and userContent.js are similar features for websites.
</p><p>userChrome.css, userChrome.js, userContent.css and userUContent.js are very powerful tools, intended for testing by technical users who are familiar with JS, CSS and HTML. The Thunderbird Project does not offer support for modifications to these files. This is because Thunderbird developers are constantly making improvements to the HTML elements which alters the CSS selectors as well as the underlying Javascript code. These changes mean that customizations based on userChrome.js, userChrome.css, userContent.js and userContent.css can break Thunderbird at any time and are very difficult to troubleshoot.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "263727", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jul 26, 2023, 9:55:54 AM", "datetime": "2023-07-26T09:55:54-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "first draft, do not approve, WIP", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:37:45+00:00');
