</p>
<h3 id="w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</h3>
<ol><li> Please try signing in with a new Thunderbird profile (see <a href="/en-US/kb/profile-manager-create-and-remove-thunderbird-profiles">Profile Manager - Create and remove Thunderbird profiles</a> for instructions on how to create a new profile). If a new Thunderbird profile works, then for most people it is best to continue using the new profile. <strong>More technical folks with other config editor changes</strong>: Use the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the config editor (see <a href="/en-US/kb/config-editor">Config Editor</a>) to filter for <em>oauth2</em>, find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for <em>oauth2.issuer</em> and <em>auth2.scope</em>  
</li><li> Otherwise, <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask for support</a>.
</li></ol>
<h3 id="w_imappop3-work-but-smtp-does-not-work">IMAP/POP3 work, but SMTP does not work</h3>
<ul><li> If you have an Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP Authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled.  Microsoft has some instructions in their article: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission">Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online</a> 
</li><li> If you have a Microsoft 365/hotmail/etc <strong>personal</strong> account, use basic authentication (<a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040">Microsoft’s guide</a> on how to change this).
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_calendar-does-not-work">Calendar does not work</h3>
<ul><li> Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_where-to-get-help">Where to Get Help</h2>
<ul><li> If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance within your organization.
</li><li> If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft''s hosted services, <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask for support</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "275611", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 1, 2024, 12:01:41 PM", "datetime": "2024-02-01T12:01:41.305658-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "accept cookies has to be on in Thunderbird. NOT firefox. https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/37", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 1, 2024, 12:01:59 PM", "datetime": "2024-02-01T12:01:59.577084-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:34:28+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (275940,'en-US','microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202','Microsoft OAuth Authentication and Thunderbird in 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202/revision/275940',275730,'2024-02-06T20:55:37.495835-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','add Normal password error message and warning to update to current release of Thunderbird',1,'2024-02-07T03:09:56.820038-08:00','wsmwk',1,0,1,'2024-02-07T03:09:56.820038-08:00','wsmwk','approved','Microsoft has made some changes to authentication for their hosted email services for business and academic accounts. This article describes these changes and how to adjust to them.
{warning}As of January 2024, this information is still evolving and subject to change. Please upgrade to the current release of Thunderbird in order to have the best experience with Microsoft''s email services. Due to Microsoft''s ever-changing diversity of email servers and deployments, issues can emerge without warning that cause Thunderbird and other non Microsoft email clients to suddenly stop working after working for some time.{/warning}

__TOC__

== Changes to Authentication ==
Microsoft has instituted the following changes:
* [https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/deprecation-of-basic-authentication-exchange-online Deprecated basic authentication] (username/password), and is instead now requiring OAuth authentication.
* In some cases, SMTP authentication has been completely disabled. For new accounts, SMTP always starts disabled. In addition, there are some restrictions on SMTP that are not currently understood.
Microsoft have also changed the way they classify certain clients, and Thunderbird’s previous OAuth setup does not properly qualify as a desktop client. As a result, we have been forced to make configuration changes to Thunderbird, which may have side effects for users.

== Changes or Problems You May Encounter ==
For outlook&#46;com, hotmail&#46;com, Microsoft 365 (formerly known as Office 365 and often abbreviated as “O365”) or other Microsoft-hosted email services, you may see the following issues:

=== Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email ===
* Thunderbird might display an error message similar to: <code>Login to server outlook&#46;office365&#46;com with username youremail@hotmail&#46;com failed.</code>
* Solution: First, ensure that cookies for Microsoft''s Outlook or Hotmail websites (e.g. office365&#46;com) are enabled in Thunderbird (Click {menu ≡} > {menu Privacy & Security} > Tick ''''''Accept cookies from sites''''''), otherwise you will not be able to log in to your outlook&#46;com or hotmail&#46;com account using ''''OAuth2'''' authentication. Second, ensure two-step verification is turned on for your Microsoft account (see Microsoft''s Knowledge Base article: [https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/account-billing/how-to-use-two-step-verification-with-your-microsoft-account-c7910146-672f-01e9-50a0-93b4585e7eb4 How to use two-step verification with your Microsoft account]). Finally, change ''''Authentication method'''' to ''''''OAuth2'''''' from ''''Normal Password'''' for both, ''''IMAP'''' (or ''''POP'''') and ''''SMTP''''.
# IMAP (or POP): Click {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings} > On the left side, click {button Server Settings} for your outlook&#46;com or hotmail&#46;com account > select ''''Authentication method:'''' {menu OAuth2} (instead of ''''Normal password'''').
#; [[Image:tb115-server-settings]]
# SMTP:
## Click {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings} > On the left side, click on your outlook&#46;com or hotmail&#46;com account ''''e.g. thunderbirdrocks@hotmail.com'''' > {button Edit SMTP server…}
##; [[Image:tb115-smtp-settings]]
## Select ''''Authentication method:'''' {menu OAuth2} (instead of ''''Normal password'''') > {button OK}.
##; [[Image:tb115-smtp-oauth2-setting]]

=== A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app ===
* You must ask your administrator to authorize Thunderbird – approval must be done, but only once.
Per Microsoft documentation, administrators should visit ''''''<code>https://login.microsoftonline&#46;com/{tenant-id}/adminconsent?client_id=9e5f94bc-e8a4-4e73-b8be-63364c29d753</code>'''''' and grant the following permissions in order to authorize ''''Mozilla Thunderbird'''': ''''''IMAP.AccessAsUser.All'''''', ''''''POP&#46;AccessAsUser&#46;Al'''''', ''''''SMTP.Send'''''' and ''''''offline_access''''''

=== An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later ===
# Please try signing in with a new Thunderbird profile (see [[profile manager create and remove thunderbird profiles]] for instructions on how to create a new profile). If a new Thunderbird profile works, then for most people it is best to continue using the new profile. ''''''More technical folks with other config editor changes'''''': Use the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the config editor (see [[Config Editor]]) to filter for ''''''oauth2'''''', find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for ''''''oauth2.issuer'''''' and ''''''auth2.scope''''''
# Otherwise, [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form ask for support].

=== IMAP/POP3 work, but SMTP does not work ===
* If you have a Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled. Microsoft has some instructions in their article: [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online].
* If you have a Microsoft 365/Hotmail/etc. ''''''personal'''''' account, use basic authentication ([https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040 Microsoft’s guide] on how to change this).

=== Calendar does not work ===
* Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.

== Where to Get Help ==
* If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance within your organization.
* If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft''s hosted services, [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form ask for support].','<p>Microsoft has made some changes to authentication for their hosted email services for business and academic accounts. This article describes these changes and how to adjust to them.
</p>
<div class="warning">As of January 2024, this information is still evolving and subject to change. Please upgrade to the current release of Thunderbird in order to have the best experience with Microsoft''s email services. Due to Microsoft''s ever-changing diversity of email servers and deployments, issues can emerge without warning that cause Thunderbird and other non Microsoft email clients to suddenly stop working after working for some time.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changes-to-authentication"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Changes to Authentication</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changes-or-problems-you-may-encounter"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changes or Problems You May Encounter</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_your-outlook-or-hotmail-password-no-longer-works-with-thunderbird-and-you-cannot-send-or-receive-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_a-screen-that-indicates-it-administrator-approval-is-required-for-the-app"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later"><span class="tocnumber">2.3</span> <span class="toctext">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_imappop3-work-but-smtp-does-not-work"><span class="tocnumber">2.4</span> <span class="toctext">IMAP/POP3 work, but SMTP does not work</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_calendar-does-not-work"><span class="tocnumber">2.5</span> <span class="toctext">Calendar does not work</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_where-to-get-help"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Where to Get Help</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h2 id="w_changes-to-authentication">Changes to Authentication</h2>
<p>Microsoft has instituted the following changes:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/deprecation-of-basic-authentication-exchange-online">Deprecated basic authentication</a> (username/password), and is instead now requiring OAuth authentication.
</li><li> In some cases, SMTP authentication has been completely disabled. For new accounts, SMTP always starts disabled. In addition, there are some restrictions on SMTP that are not currently understood.
</li></ul>
<p>Microsoft have also changed the way they classify certain clients, and Thunderbird’s previous OAuth setup does not properly qualify as a desktop client. As a result, we have been forced to make configuration changes to Thunderbird, which may have side effects for users.
</p>
<h2 id="w_changes-or-problems-you-may-encounter">Changes or Problems You May Encounter</h2>
<p>For <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a>, <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a>, Microsoft 365 (formerly known as Office 365 and often abbreviated as “O365”) or other Microsoft-hosted email services, you may see the following issues:
</p>
<h3 id="w_your-outlook-or-hotmail-password-no-longer-works-with-thunderbird-and-you-cannot-send-or-receive-email">Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email</h3>
<ul><li> Thunderbird might display an error message similar to: <code>Login to server outlook.office365.com with username youremail@hotmail.com failed.</code>
</li><li> Solution: First, ensure that cookies for Microsoft''s Outlook or Hotmail websites (e.g. <a href="http://office365.com">office365.com</a>) are enabled in Thunderbird (Click <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> &gt; Tick <strong>Accept cookies from sites</strong>), otherwise you will not be able to log in to your <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> or <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a> account using <em>OAuth2</em> authentication. Second, ensure two-step verification is turned on for your Microsoft account (see Microsoft''s Knowledge Base article: <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/account-billing/how-to-use-two-step-verification-with-your-microsoft-account-c7910146-672f-01e9-50a0-93b4585e7eb4">How to use two-step verification with your Microsoft account</a>). Finally, change <em>Authentication method</em> to <strong>OAuth2</strong> from <em>Normal Password</em> for both, <em>IMAP</em> (or <em>POP</em>) and <em>SMTP</em>.
</li></ul>
<ol><li> IMAP (or POP): Click <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; On the left side, click <span class="button">Server Settings</span> for your <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> or <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a> account &gt; select <em>Authentication method:</em> <span class="menu">OAuth2</span> (instead of <em>Normal password</em>).
<dl><dt> <img alt="tb115-server-settings" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-01-29-20-58-01-57ec51.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> SMTP:
<ol><li> Click <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; On the left side, click on your <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> or <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a> account <em>e.g. <a href="mailto:thunderbirdrocks@hotmail.com">thunderbirdrocks@hotmail.com</a></em> &gt; <span class="button">Edit SMTP server…</span>
<dl><dt> <img alt="tb115-smtp-settings" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-01-29-21-24-25-454322.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Select <em>Authentication method:</em> <span class="menu">OAuth2</span> (instead of <em>Normal password</em>) &gt; <span class="button">OK</span>.
<dl><dt> <img alt="tb115-smtp-oauth2-setting" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-01-31-17-32-47-b38e5d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
</li></ol>
<h3 id="w_a-screen-that-indicates-it-administrator-approval-is-required-for-the-app">A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app</h3>
<ul><li> You must ask your administrator to authorize Thunderbird – approval must be done, but only once.
</li></ul>
<p>Per Microsoft documentation, administrators should visit <strong><code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/adminconsent?client_id=9e5f94bc-e8a4-4e73-b8be-63364c29d753</code></strong> and grant the following permissions in order to authorize <em>Mozilla Thunderbird</em>: <strong>IMAP.AccessAsUser.All</strong>, <strong><a href="http://POP.AccessAsUser.Al">POP.AccessAsUser.Al</a></strong>, <strong>SMTP.Send</strong> and <strong>offline_access</strong>
</p>
<h3 id="w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</h3>
<ol><li> Please try signing in with a new Thunderbird profile (see <a href="/en-US/kb/profile-manager-create-and-remove-thunderbird-profiles">Profile Manager - Create and remove Thunderbird profiles</a> for instructions on how to create a new profile). If a new Thunderbird profile works, then for most people it is best to continue using the new profile. <strong>More technical folks with other config editor changes</strong>: Use the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the config editor (see <a href="/en-US/kb/config-editor">Config Editor</a>) to filter for <strong>oauth2</strong>, find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for <strong>oauth2.issuer</strong> and <strong>auth2.scope</strong>
</li><li> Otherwise, <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask for support</a>.
</li></ol>
<h3 id="w_imappop3-work-but-smtp-does-not-work">IMAP/POP3 work, but SMTP does not work</h3>
<ul><li> If you have a Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled. Microsoft has some instructions in their article: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission">Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online</a>.
</li><li> If you have a Microsoft 365/Hotmail/etc. <strong>personal</strong> account, use basic authentication (<a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040">Microsoft’s guide</a> on how to change this).
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_calendar-does-not-work">Calendar does not work</h3>
<ul><li> Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_where-to-get-help">Where to Get Help</h2>
<ul><li> If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance within your organization.
</li><li> If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft''s hosted services, <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask for support</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "275940", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 6, 2024, 8:55:37 PM", "datetime": "2024-02-06T20:55:37.495835-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "add Normal password error message and warning to update to current release of Thunderbird", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 7, 2024, 3:09:56 AM", "datetime": "2024-02-07T03:09:56.820038-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 7, 2024, 3:09:56 AM", "datetime": "2024-02-07T03:09:56.820038-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:34:26+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (276408,'en-US','thunderbird-and-gmail','Thunderbird and Gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail/revision/276408',276003,'2024-02-15T10:46:19.562403-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fix some minor nits and broken link to tb57 image',1,'2024-02-15T10:46:54.820547-08:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s [https://www.google.com/gmail/about/ Gmail] service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
{warning}As of May 30, 2022, ''''''Google no longer allows [https://myaccount.google.com/lesssecureapps Less secure apps] access'''''' for Gmail accounts, thus ''''''OAuth is required''''''. If you are using Google supplied “app passwords“, Thunderbird version 91.8.0 and newer will [[Automatic Conversion of Google mail accounts to OAuth 2.0 Authentication|make that conversion]] for you. Further, ''''''Gmail accounts do not work with Thunderbird 31 and older'''''' because they do not support Google''s OAuth (implemented in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=849540 bug 849540]).{/warning}

__TOC__

= Configuring your Gmail account =
{for win}
[[Video:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JXs_YE9QyUQ]]
{/for}
To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail, first ensure IMAP is enabled on your Gmail account (the default for new Gmail accounts). See [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/7126229 Gmail''s instructions] for help.
{for not tb38}
Double check that “Access for less secure apps” is enabled. See [https://www.google.com/settings/security/lesssecureapps Google''s article on “less secure apps”] for help.
{/for}

Next, select {menu File} > {menu New} > {menu Existing Mail Account…} The ''''Mail Account Setup'''' dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information (real name, email address{for not tb38}, password{/for}), Thunderbird will automatically configure your Gmail connection. Click {button Done}{for tb38}, sign in with your Google account if prompted{/for}. Thunderbird will begin downloading your existing messages.

Lastly, review [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892#zippy=%2Cthunderbird Google''s recommended settings for Thunderbird] so that your local actions, such as labelling and deleting, correspond to those which Gmail takes and do not cause any conflicts.

For more detailed information, please refer to:
* [[Automatic Account Configuration]]
* [https://support.google.com/mail/topic/7280141 Gmail''s support articles on email clients]

= Gmail''s particularities =
== Archiving messages ==
In order to be consistent with Gmail''s behavior, Thunderbird archives your message in the ''''All Mail'''' folder, and not in an ''''Archive'''' folder as it does for other accounts (as described in the [[Archived Messages]] article).
;[[Image:Gmail Archive settings]]
{note}The {button Archive options…} button is disabled for Gmail accounts, as it is not necessary to define the archive granularity with Gmail.{/note}

== Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages ==
=== Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders ===
Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. In this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in Gmail, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.

If you copy a message to another folder, Gmail will still store only one message but apply both folder names as labels to it. Moving any copy of a message into the Trash will remove all labels from it, and it will no longer appear in any other folder.

In the example shown below, the folders at the same level as the ''''[Gmail]'''' folder (''''Personal'''', ''''Receipts'''', ''''Travel'''' and ''''Work'''') correspond to Gmail labels.
;[[Image:default gmail folders]]
There is also a set of special sub-folders for the ''''[Gmail]'''' folder:
* All Mail: contains all the messages of your Gmail account, including sent and archived messages. Any messages that you see in the inbox will also appear in the ''''[Gmail]/All Mail'''' folder.
* Drafts: contains draft messages.
* Sent Mail: contains sent messages.
* Spam: contains messages that were marked as spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Starred: contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Trash: contains deleted messages.
* Important: contains messages that Gmail has flagged as “important”. (See [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/186531 Priority Inbox overview] for more details about this Gmail feature.)

=== Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels) ===
You can configure which Gmail folders you want to display in Thunderbird. By default, all Gmail folders are displayed, as described above. To configure folder display, right-click the account name and select {menu Subscribe…}
;[[Image:subscribe gmail folders]]
Uncheck any folders that you don''t want to display in Thunderbird. Messages will still be downloaded and available through the ''''All Mail'''' folder. They will also be included in search results. However, the Gmail label (Thunderbird folder) will not be displayed.

=== Synchronizing folders (labels) ===
If you want to see all your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid all the messages being downloaded to your system, you may want to tweak the synchronization settings. This allows you to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don''t synchronize a folder, the message header will still be displayed in Thunderbird, but the message content will only be downloaded when you click the message header.

To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders. ({for win,mac}{menu Tools} > {menu Account Settings…}{/for}{for linux}{menu Edit} > {menu Account Settings…}{/for}) See [[IMAP Synchronization#w_synchronization]] for more information.

=== Thunderbird''s message lists differ from the web interface ===
If some messages are appearing in your Inbox that shouldn''t be, or aren''t appearing but should be, you may need to select the ''''Don''t override filters'''' option under {menu Inbox} > {menu Filtered mail} in Gmail''s settings.
The same can be achieved by turning off {menu General} > {menu Smart features and personalisation} in Gmail''s settings.

You can also change whether Gmail''s interface should group messages by conversation with the ''''''Conversation View'''''' setting in Gmail''s {menu General} settings page, to match your [[Message threading in Thunderbird - keep messages grouped by conversation|Thunderbird preference]].

See the support article on [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6562 how to change Gmail settings].

= Updating a Gmail account to use OAuth =
Thunderbird will normally set up or update your Gmail account to OAuth automatically. If something goes wrong, you can use the hints below to check and set up manually. For more details and how to resolve various OAuth issues, see [[Automatic Conversion of Google mail accounts to OAuth 2.0 Authentication]].

The following applies to both POP and IMAP mail accounts.
*Enable cookies in Thunderbird:
*#Click {menu ≡} > {menu Settings}.
*#Select {menu Privacy & Security}.
*#Under ''''Web Content'''' section, tick ''''''Accept cookies from sites''''''.
*You may need to set an exception:
*#Click {button Exceptions…} button.
*#Add an exception for ''''''https://accounts.google<!-- -->.com''''''
*#Click {button Allow}.
*#Click {button Save Changes}.
*Change the authentication method in ''''Account Settings'''':
*#Click {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings}, which opens in a new tab.
**Change the Outgoing SMTP authentication method:
**#In the left pane, select the POP/IMAP account name and look bottom right for ''''Outgoing Server (SMTP)''''.
**#Click {button Edit Server SMTP…} button.
**#Set ''''Authentication Method'''' to ''''''OAuth2''''''.
**#Click {button OK}.
**Change the Incoming authentication method:
**#In the left pane, select {menu Server Settings} for Gmail account.
**#Set ''''Authentication Method'''' to ''''''OAuth2''''''.

Exit Thunderbird and wait a few moments for background processes to finish. Then restart Thunderbird.

Gmail will prompt you to enter your Gmail email address and password, which is the same password you use to access Gmail webmail account. Follow the instructions to allow Thunderbird to access server.

An OAuth token will get saved in Thunderbird, in the same location as passwords.

= Useful Add-ons =
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/google-4-tbsync/ Google contact and contact group sync].
* If you want to sync your calendar between Thunderbird Calendar and Google Calendar, you should read [[Using Lightning with Google Calendar]].
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gtrash/ GTrash] to make true deletion more convenient when using Google''s recommended deletion settings.

= See also =
* [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892 Gmail''s support article on recommended settings].
* [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=402793 The ticket which tracks issues using Gmail with Thunderbird (Bug 402793)].','<p>Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s <a href="https://www.google.com/gmail/about/">Gmail</a> service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
</p>
<div class="warning">As of May 30, 2022, <strong>Google no longer allows <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/lesssecureapps">Less secure apps</a> access</strong> for Gmail accounts, thus <strong>OAuth is required</strong>. If you are using Google supplied “app passwords“, Thunderbird version 91.8.0 and newer will <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-conversion-google-mail-accounts-oauth20">make that conversion</a> for you. Further, <strong>Gmail accounts do not work with Thunderbird 31 and older</strong> because they do not support Google''s OAuth (implemented in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=849540">bug 849540</a>).</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-your-gmail-account"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring your Gmail account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_gmails-particularities"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Gmail''s particularities</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_archiving-messages"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Archiving messages</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_synchronizing-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.3</span> <span class="toctext">Synchronizing folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbirds-message-lists-differ-from-the-web-interface"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.4</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird''s message lists differ from the web interface</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_updating-a-gmail-account-to-use-oauth"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Updating a Gmail account to use OAuth</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_useful-add-ons"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Useful Add-ons</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_see-also"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">See also</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configuring-your-gmail-account">Configuring your Gmail account</h1>
<div class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><p></p><div class="youtube-embed">
  <iframe src="//www.youtube.com/embed/JXs_YE9QyUQ?wmode=transparent&amp;rel=0" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen=""></iframe>
</div>
<p></p></div><p>To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail, first ensure IMAP is enabled on your Gmail account (the default for new Gmail accounts). See <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/7126229">Gmail''s instructions</a> for help.
</p><div class="for" data-for="not tb38" style="display: none;"><p>Double check that “Access for less secure apps” is enabled. See <a href="https://www.google.com/settings/security/lesssecureapps">Google''s article on “less secure apps”</a> for help.
</p></div><p>Next, select <span class="menu">File</span> &gt; <span class="menu">New</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Existing Mail Account…</span> The <em>Mail Account Setup</em> dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information (real name, email address<span class="for" data-for="not tb38" style="display: none;">, password</span>), Thunderbird will automatically configure your Gmail connection. Click <span class="button">Done</span><span class="for" data-for="tb38">, sign in with your Google account if prompted</span>. Thunderbird will begin downloading your existing messages.
</p><p>Lastly, review <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892#zippy=%2Cthunderbird">Google''s recommended settings for Thunderbird</a> so that your local actions, such as labelling and deleting, correspond to those which Gmail takes and do not cause any conflicts.
</p><p>For more detailed information, please refer to:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration">Automatic Account Configuration</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/topic/7280141">Gmail''s support articles on email clients</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_gmails-particularities">Gmail''s particularities</h1>
<h2 id="w_archiving-messages">Archiving messages</h2>
<p>In order to be consistent with Gmail''s behavior, Thunderbird archives your message in the <em>All Mail</em> folder, and not in an <em>Archive</em> folder as it does for other accounts (as described in the <a href="/en-US/kb/archived-messages">Archived Messages</a> article).
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="Gmail Archive settings" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-17-04-13-09-41649e.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<div class="note">The <span class="button">Archive options…</span> button is disabled for Gmail accounts, as it is not necessary to define the archive granularity with Gmail.</div>
<h2 id="w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</h2>
<h3 id="w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</h3>
<p>Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. In this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in Gmail, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.
</p><p>If you copy a message to another folder, Gmail will still store only one message but apply both folder names as labels to it. Moving any copy of a message into the Trash will remove all labels from it, and it will no longer appear in any other folder.
</p><p>In the example shown below, the folders at the same level as the <em>[Gmail]</em> folder (<em>Personal</em>, <em>Receipts</em>, <em>Travel</em> and <em>Work</em>) correspond to Gmail labels.
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="default gmail folders" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-22-16-07-48-8304a9.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<p>There is also a set of special sub-folders for the <em>[Gmail]</em> folder:
</p>
<ul><li> All Mail: contains all the messages of your Gmail account, including sent and archived messages. Any messages that you see in the inbox will also appear in the <em>[Gmail]/All Mail</em> folder.
</li><li> Drafts: contains draft messages.
</li><li> Sent Mail: contains sent messages.
</li><li> Spam: contains messages that were marked as spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> Starred: contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> Trash: contains deleted messages.
</li><li> Important: contains messages that Gmail has flagged as “important”. (See <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/186531">Priority Inbox overview</a> for more details about this Gmail feature.)
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</h3>
<p>You can configure which Gmail folders you want to display in Thunderbird. By default, all Gmail folders are displayed, as described above. To configure folder display, right-click the account name and select <span class="menu">Subscribe…</span>
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="subscribe gmail folders" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-22-16-20-21-1f5531.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<p>Uncheck any folders that you don''t want to display in Thunderbird. Messages will still be downloaded and available through the <em>All Mail</em> folder. They will also be included in search results. However, the Gmail label (Thunderbird folder) will not be displayed.
</p>
<h3 id="w_synchronizing-folders-labels">Synchronizing folders (labels)</h3>
<p>If you want to see all your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid all the messages being downloaded to your system, you may want to tweak the synchronization settings. This allows you to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don''t synchronize a folder, the message header will still be displayed in Thunderbird, but the message content will only be downloaded when you click the message header.
</p><p>To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders. (<span class="for" data-for="win,mac"><span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings…</span></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><span class="menu">Edit</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings…</span></span>) See <a href="/en-US/kb/imap-synchronization#w_synchronization">IMAP Synchronization</a> for more information.
</p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbirds-message-lists-differ-from-the-web-interface">Thunderbird''s message lists differ from the web interface</h3>
<p>If some messages are appearing in your Inbox that shouldn''t be, or aren''t appearing but should be, you may need to select the <em>Don''t override filters</em> option under <span class="menu">Inbox</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Filtered mail</span> in Gmail''s settings.
The same can be achieved by turning off <span class="menu">General</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Smart features and personalisation</span> in Gmail''s settings.
</p><p>You can also change whether Gmail''s interface should group messages by conversation with the <strong>Conversation View</strong> setting in Gmail''s <span class="menu">General</span> settings page, to match your <a href="/en-US/kb/message-threading-thunderbird">Thunderbird preference</a>.
</p><p>See the support article on <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6562">how to change Gmail settings</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_updating-a-gmail-account-to-use-oauth">Updating a Gmail account to use OAuth</h1>
<p>Thunderbird will normally set up or update your Gmail account to OAuth automatically. If something goes wrong, you can use the hints below to check and set up manually. For more details and how to resolve various OAuth issues, see <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-conversion-google-mail-accounts-oauth20">Automatic Conversion of Google mail accounts to OAuth 2.0 Authentication</a>.
</p><p>The following applies to both POP and IMAP mail accounts.
</p>
<ul><li>Enable cookies in Thunderbird:
<ol><li>Click <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.
</li><li>Select <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span>.
</li><li>Under <em>Web Content</em> section, tick <strong>Accept cookies from sites</strong>.
</li></ol>
</li><li>You may need to set an exception:
<ol><li>Click <span class="button">Exceptions…</span> button.
</li><li>Add an exception for <strong><a href="https://accounts.google">https://accounts.google</a>.com</strong>
</li><li>Click <span class="button">Allow</span>.
</li><li>Click <span class="button">Save Changes</span>.
</li></ol>
</li><li>Change the authentication method in <em>Account Settings</em>:
<ol><li>Click <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span>, which opens in a new tab.
</li></ol>
<ul><li>Change the Outgoing SMTP authentication method:
<ol><li>In the left pane, select the POP/IMAP account name and look bottom right for <em>Outgoing Server (SMTP)</em>.
</li><li>Click <span class="button">Edit Server SMTP…</span> button.
</li><li>Set <em>Authentication Method</em> to <strong>OAuth2</strong>.
</li><li>Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
</li><li>Change the Incoming authentication method:
<ol><li>In the left pane, select <span class="menu">Server Settings</span> for Gmail account.
</li><li>Set <em>Authentication Method</em> to <strong>OAuth2</strong>.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
</li></ul>
<p>Exit Thunderbird and wait a few moments for background processes to finish. Then restart Thunderbird.
</p><p>Gmail will prompt you to enter your Gmail email address and password, which is the same password you use to access Gmail webmail account. Follow the instructions to allow Thunderbird to access server.
</p><p>An OAuth token will get saved in Thunderbird, in the same location as passwords.
</p>
<h1 id="w_useful-add-ons">Useful Add-ons</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/google-4-tbsync/">Google contact and contact group sync</a>.
</li><li> If you want to sync your calendar between Thunderbird Calendar and Google Calendar, you should read <a href="/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar">Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar</a>.
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gtrash/">GTrash</a> to make true deletion more convenient when using Google''s recommended deletion settings.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_see-also">See also</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892">Gmail''s support article on recommended settings</a>.
</li><li> <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=402793">The ticket which tracks issues using Gmail with Thunderbird (Bug 402793)</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "276408", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 15, 2024, 10:46:19 AM", "datetime": "2024-02-15T10:46:19.562403-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fix some minor nits and broken link to tb57 image", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 15, 2024, 10:46:54 AM", "datetime": "2024-02-15T10:46:54.820547-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:34:23+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (276412,'en-US','automatic-account-configuration','Automatic Account Configuration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration/revision/276412',264562,'2024-02-15T16:16:31.971951-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fix nits and now Thunderbird pops up a new TB window not a browser window',1,'2024-02-15T16:17:05.136946-08:00','rtanglao',1,0,1,'2024-02-15T16:17:05.136946-08:00','rtanglao','approved','{note}Important Note for those who have been redirected to this page by Yahoo or AOL website advice.
Please read information in the Help Article [[Thunderbird and Yahoo]] sub section ''Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice''.{/note}

=Getting Started:=

Adding an existing e-mail account from most providers is a snap in Thunderbird! To start, you will need your e-mail login credentials: username & password. Thunderbird will attempt to match your input with its internal database to properly configure the connection (protocol, server addresses, authentication) for your specific provider. If the profile is unavailable, no sweat! You can also configure manually!

==Step-By-Step Instructions==

{note}Your computer must be connected to the Internet for Thunderbird to create your account automatically.{/note}

===1. Access Account Setup===

You can initiate the process of adding a new account in one of two ways. For a new install, you will be automatically prompted to enter your existing e-mail account details upon first launching Thunderbird.

For an existing install, you can navigate to the setup screen via {menu ≡} > {menu New Account} > {menu Existing Email}.

[[Image:Existing E-mail Setup]] 

===2. Enter Account Details===

You can now populate your username and password in the setup screen. You will also be asked to include your name to associate with this specific account. 

Once finished, click {button Continue}.

===3. Validate Provider Profile Exists===

At this stage, Thunderbird will check if your webmail provider is available in the database and will provide available configuration options. IMAP (type of protocol) will be the preferred setting in most cases.

If Thunderbird was unable to retrieve your mail provider, you have the alternate option to configure manually. You will need to check with your provider for their specific email settings. You can visit this page for more help [[Manual Account Configuration]].

[[Image:Thunderbird Email Provider]] 

You have the option to choose either IMAP or POP as your incoming mail protocol. IMAP and POP are the standard protocols for retrieving emails. IMAP, the more modern protocol, allows you to store messages on the mail server and on your local machine at the same time. The POP protocol only allows messages to be stored locally. Email providers may provide access by either the IMAP or POP protocol, or may support both. (To get a better understanding of the technical details for both protocols, see [[Glossary of Terms including types of Accounts]] and [[IMAP Synchronization]].)

Once you have selected your protocol, click {button Done}.

===4. Granting Thunderbird Account Access===

Depending on your webmail provider, a new Thunderbird window may launch asking you to login via your provider''s website to grant access to Thunderbird. 

Enter your login credentials and click {button Allow} or equivalent.

[[Image:Thunderbird Provider Authentication]] 

===5. Finalizing Setup===

Thunderbird will now begin to retrieve your folders and messages from your provider. 

Please not this may take some time, a progress bar will be visible in the lower right of your screen. 

Congratulations on completing setup! 

[[Image:Thunderbird Interface Empty]] 

= Onboard your Webmail Provider / ISP to Thunderbird  =

The Thunderbird team wants to collect server information for as many ISP and webmail providers as possible. You can help! If your email server information is not found when you create your account, you must configure your account manually. However, please ask your email provider to submit their ISP connection information. For more detailed technical information about ISPDB and autoconfiguration, how to use it and add a record to it, see [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Autoconfiguration:ConfigFileFormat Autoconfiguration in Thunderbird] on the Mozilla Wiki.','<div class="note">Important Note for those who have been redirected to this page by Yahoo or AOL website advice.
Please read information in the Help Article <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-yahoo">OAuth2 Authentication for Yahoo, AOL and ATT</a> sub section ''Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice''.</div>
<h1 id="w_getting-started">Getting Started:</h1>
<p>Adding an existing e-mail account from most providers is a snap in Thunderbird! To start, you will need your e-mail login credentials: username &amp; password. Thunderbird will attempt to match your input with its internal database to properly configure the connection (protocol, server addresses, authentication) for your specific provider. If the profile is unavailable, no sweat! You can also configure manually!
</p>
<h2 id="w_step-by-step-instructions">Step-By-Step Instructions</h2>
<div class="note">Your computer must be connected to the Internet for Thunderbird to create your account automatically.</div>
<h3 id="w_1-access-account-setup">1. Access Account Setup</h3>
<p>You can initiate the process of adding a new account in one of two ways. For a new install, you will be automatically prompted to enter your existing e-mail account details upon first launching Thunderbird.
</p><p>For an existing install, you can navigate to the setup screen via <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Existing Email</span>.
</p><p><img alt="Existing E-mail Setup" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-08-16-19-48-31-c4ca42.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<h3 id="w_2-enter-account-details">2. Enter Account Details</h3>
<p>You can now populate your username and password in the setup screen. You will also be asked to include your name to associate with this specific account. 
</p><p>Once finished, click <span class="button">Continue</span>.
</p>
<h3 id="w_3-validate-provider-profile-exists">3. Validate Provider Profile Exists</h3>
<p>At this stage, Thunderbird will check if your webmail provider is available in the database and will provide available configuration options. IMAP (type of protocol) will be the preferred setting in most cases.
</p><p>If Thunderbird was unable to retrieve your mail provider, you have the alternate option to configure manually. You will need to check with your provider for their specific email settings. You can visit this page for more help <a href="/en-US/kb/manual-account-configuration">Manual Account Configuration</a>.
</p><p><img alt="Thunderbird Email Provider" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-08-16-20-06-33-949c27.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>You have the option to choose either IMAP or POP as your incoming mail protocol. IMAP and POP are the standard protocols for retrieving emails. IMAP, the more modern protocol, allows you to store messages on the mail server and on your local machine at the same time. The POP protocol only allows messages to be stored locally. Email providers may provide access by either the IMAP or POP protocol, or may support both. (To get a better understanding of the technical details for both protocols, see <a href="/en-US/kb/glossary-terms-including-types-accounts">Glossary of Terms including types of Accounts</a> and <a href="/en-US/kb/imap-synchronization">IMAP Synchronization</a>.)
</p><p>Once you have selected your protocol, click <span class="button">Done</span>.
</p>
<h3 id="w_4-granting-thunderbird-account-access">4. Granting Thunderbird Account Access</h3>
<p>Depending on your webmail provider, a new Thunderbird window may launch asking you to login via your provider''s website to grant access to Thunderbird. 
</p><p>Enter your login credentials and click <span class="button">Allow</span> or equivalent.
</p><p><img alt="Thunderbird Provider Authentication" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-08-16-20-21-10-a87ff6.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<h3 id="w_5-finalizing-setup">5. Finalizing Setup</h3>
<p>Thunderbird will now begin to retrieve your folders and messages from your provider. 
</p><p>Please not this may take some time, a progress bar will be visible in the lower right of your screen. 
</p><p>Congratulations on completing setup! 
</p><p><img alt="Thunderbird Interface Empty" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-08-16-20-31-42-e4da3b.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p>
<h1 id="w_onboard-your-webmail-provider-isp-to-thunderbird">Onboard your Webmail Provider / ISP to Thunderbird</h1>
<p>The Thunderbird team wants to collect server information for as many ISP and webmail providers as possible. You can help! If your email server information is not found when you create your account, you must configure your account manually. However, please ask your email provider to submit their ISP connection information. For more detailed technical information about ISPDB and autoconfiguration, how to use it and add a record to it, see <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Autoconfiguration:ConfigFileFormat">Autoconfiguration in Thunderbird</a> on the Mozilla Wiki.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "276412", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 15, 2024, 4:16:31 PM", "datetime": "2024-02-15T16:16:31.971951-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fix nits and now Thunderbird pops up a new TB window not a browser window", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 15, 2024, 4:17:05 PM", "datetime": "2024-02-15T16:17:05.136946-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 15, 2024, 4:17:05 PM", "datetime": "2024-02-15T16:17:05.136946-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:34:21+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (276912,'en-US','upgrading-older-version-thunderbird','Upgrading from an older version of Thunderbird','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/upgrading-older-version-thunderbird','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/upgrading-older-version-thunderbird/revision/276912',264698,'2024-02-25T22:53:03.958215-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','Add latest tested procedure. WIP: needs approval from Wayne or Danny or some other reviewer',1,'2024-02-27T06:57:59.080231-08:00','wsmwk',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__

Still on an older version of Thunderbird and looking to upgrade? Luckily, the process is fairly straight-forward.

= Before we begin =
Third-party add-ons may require an update, or may no longer work on newer versions of Thunderbird. During the upgrade process, Thunderbird may prompt you about any such add-ons.
{warning}''''''Important:'''''' The first thing you should always do before upgrading is to back up your profile. Please see [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data]] for more information.{/warning}
Once you have done that, ensure your computer is compatible with the [https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/115.1.1/system-requirements/ latest version of Thunderbird].

Next, you''ll want to check which version of Thunderbird you currently have installed. Please see [[Find what version of Thunderbird you are using]] for more information.

= If you are on version 60 or below =
Due to changes with how the address book is stored, you will have to manually upgrade to version 60 first. This will upgrade your address book to the newer format that later Thunderbird versions use.

== First, download and install 60.9.1 ==
{for mac}
* [https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/mac/en-US/Thunderbird%2060.9.1.dmg Direct Download (Intel)]
{/for}
{for win}
* [https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/win32/en-US/Thunderbird%20Setup%2060.9.1.exe Direct Download (32-bit)]
* [https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/win64/en-US/Thunderbird%20Setup%2060.9.1.exe Direct Download (64-bit)]
{/for}
{for linux}
* [https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/linux-x86_64/en-US/thunderbird-60.9.1.tar.bz2 Direct Download (64-bit)]
{/for}
{note}''''''Important:'''''' Once you have upgraded, open Thunderbird at least once before continuing.{/note}

== Second, upgrade to version 78 ==

Repeat the following steps twice (first upgrade to 68: 60.9.1 to 68.12.1 and second upgrade to 78: 68.12.1 to 78.14)  until you are at version 78 or above:
* Click {menu ≡}  > {menu Help}  > {menu About Thunderbird} ({menu About Mozilla Thunderbird} in TB60)
* Click {button Restart Thunderbird} ({button {Restart to update Thunderbird} in TB60)
* Wait for the newly upgraded Thunderbird to open

Now that you''re on version 78, you can follow the directions for the full upgrade below.

= If you are on version 78 or above =
You''re all set to upgrade to the latest version! Simply head on over to [https://www.thunderbird.net/ thunderbird.net], and click {button Download}, and install the latest version of Thunderbird over your Thunderbird 78 install once it is downloaded.

And that''s it, you''ve now upgraded to the latest version of Thunderbird! The interface may have changed a bit from the version you were previously on. We recommend spending a bit of time familiarizing yourself with any interface changes.

= Need help? =
Don''t hesitate to reach out if you need assistance. We have a fantastic [https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird/ Support Knowledge Base] with pre-existing topics that may be of use. Can''t find a topic for your particular need, [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/ try asking a question to our dedicated community].','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_before-we-begin"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Before we begin</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_if-you-are-on-version-60-or-below"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">If you are on version 60 or below</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_first-download-and-install-60-9-1"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">First, download and install 60.9.1</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_second-upgrade-to-version-78"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Second, upgrade to version 78</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_if-you-are-on-version-78-or-above"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">If you are on version 78 or above</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_need-help"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Need help?</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p>Still on an older version of Thunderbird and looking to upgrade? Luckily, the process is fairly straight-forward.
</p>
<h1 id="w_before-we-begin">Before we begin</h1>
<p>Third-party add-ons may require an update, or may no longer work on newer versions of Thunderbird. During the upgrade process, Thunderbird may prompt you about any such add-ons.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Important:</strong> The first thing you should always do before upgrading is to back up your profile. Please see <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data</a> for more information.</div>
<p>Once you have done that, ensure your computer is compatible with the <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/115.1.1/system-requirements/">latest version of Thunderbird</a>.
</p><p>Next, you''ll want to check which version of Thunderbird you currently have installed. Please see <a href="/en-US/kb/find-what-version-thunderbird-you-are-using">Find what version of Thunderbird you are using</a> for more information.
</p>
<h1 id="w_if-you-are-on-version-60-or-below">If you are on version 60 or below</h1>
<p>Due to changes with how the address book is stored, you will have to manually upgrade to version 60 first. This will upgrade your address book to the newer format that later Thunderbird versions use.
</p>
<h2 id="w_first-download-and-install-60-9-1">First, download and install 60.9.1</h2>
<div class="for" data-for="mac">
<ul><li> <a href="https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/mac/en-US/Thunderbird%2060.9.1.dmg">Direct Download (Intel)</a>
</li></ul>
</div><div class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">
<ul><li> <a href="https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/win32/en-US/Thunderbird%20Setup%2060.9.1.exe">Direct Download (32-bit)</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/win64/en-US/Thunderbird%20Setup%2060.9.1.exe">Direct Download (64-bit)</a>
</li></ul>
</div><div class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">
<ul><li> <a href="https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/thunderbird/releases/60.9.1/linux-x86_64/en-US/thunderbird-60.9.1.tar.bz2">Direct Download (64-bit)</a>
</li></ul>
</div>
<div class="note"><strong>Important:</strong> Once you have upgraded, open Thunderbird at least once before continuing.</div>
<h2 id="w_second-upgrade-to-version-78">Second, upgrade to version 78</h2>
<p>Repeat the following steps twice (first upgrade to 68: 60.9.1 to 68.12.1 and second upgrade to 78: 68.12.1 to 78.14)  until you are at version 78 or above:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">≡</span>  &gt; <span class="menu">Help</span>  &gt; <span class="menu">About Thunderbird</span> (<span class="menu">About Mozilla Thunderbird</span> in TB60)
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Restart Thunderbird</span> (<span class="button">{Restart to update Thunderbird</span> in TB60)
</li><li> Wait for the newly upgraded Thunderbird to open
</li></ul>
<p>Now that you''re on version 78, you can follow the directions for the full upgrade below.
</p>
<h1 id="w_if-you-are-on-version-78-or-above">If you are on version 78 or above</h1>
<p>You''re all set to upgrade to the latest version! Simply head on over to <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/">thunderbird.net</a>, and click <span class="button">Download</span>, and install the latest version of Thunderbird over your Thunderbird 78 install once it is downloaded.
</p><p>And that''s it, you''ve now upgraded to the latest version of Thunderbird! The interface may have changed a bit from the version you were previously on. We recommend spending a bit of time familiarizing yourself with any interface changes.
</p>
<h1 id="w_need-help">Need help?</h1>
<p>Don''t hesitate to reach out if you need assistance. We have a fantastic <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird/">Support Knowledge Base</a> with pre-existing topics that may be of use. Can''t find a topic for your particular need, <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/">try asking a question to our dedicated community</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "276912", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 25, 2024, 10:53:03 PM", "datetime": "2024-02-25T22:53:03.958215-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Add latest tested procedure. WIP: needs approval from Wayne or Danny or some other reviewer", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 27, 2024, 6:57:59 AM", "datetime": "2024-02-27T06:57:59.080231-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:34:19+00:00');
