</p>
<ul><li> You can click on the little icon in the "junk" column of the message list:
<dl><dt><img alt="TB91-thread-pane-junk-column" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-09-13-07-42-59-94994a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ul>
<ul><li> You can also click the <span class="button">Junk</span> button on the message header toolbar:
<dl><dt><img alt="TB91-message-pane-header-buttons-junk" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-09-13-08-23-20-eb9041.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ul>
<ul><li> Or you can press <span class="key">J</span> on your keyboard to mark one or more selected messages as junk.
</li></ul>
<div class="note">Thunderbird''s junk filter has been designed to learn from the training data which you provide. Marking more messages as <strong>junk</strong> or <strong>not junk</strong> (see below) will improve the accuracy of your junk filter by adding more training data.</div>
<h2 id="w_tell-thunderbird-what-is-not-junk">Tell Thunderbird what is NOT JUNK</h2>
<p>Sometimes Thunderbird''s junk filter might get it wrong and mark <em>good</em> messages as junk. Therefore, it is just as important to tell the filter which messages are <em>not</em> junk, especially on a new installation of Thunderbird.
</p>
