</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/organize-your-messages-using-filters">Organize your messages using filters</a>.
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/creating-new-calendars">Multiple calendar accounts</a> all in one place.
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/advanced-panel-settings-in-thunderbird">Offline</a> mode for reading emails and composing drafts while not connected to the internet.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_for-beginners-and-experts-alike-backups-the-main-window-and-more">For beginners and experts alike: Backups, the main window and more</h1>
<ul><li> Please ensure you back up your Thunderbird data periodically. See: <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-export">Export your Thunderbird Profile</a>
</li><li> There are a lot of features in Thunderbird''s main window that might improve your workflow. Full details: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova">Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_im-not-a-beginner-whats-changed">I''m not a beginner! What''s changed?</h1>
<p>Not new to Thunderbird? Perhaps checking it out after a few years? Then the following articles should be helpful in resuming your journey with Thunderbird:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-128-nebula">New in Thunderbird 128 Nebula</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-128-nebula-faq">Thunderbird 128 Nebula FAQ</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova">Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-115-supernova">New in Thunderbird 115 Supernova</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-115-supernova-faq">Thunderbird 115 Supernova FAQ</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_need-support-got-a-feature-request-want-to-contribute-to-thunderbird-or-mozilla">Need support? Got a Feature Request? Want to contribute to Thunderbird or Mozilla?</h1>
<ul><li> If the <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird">support articles for Thunderbird on this site</a> don''t solve your problem and you need support please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird">ask a question on our Thunderbird Forum</a>.
</li><li> Please suggest feature requests and other improvements on <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/">Mozilla Connect</a>.
</li><li> Did you know that our community of Thunderbird volunteers in addition to contributing sofware patches,  answers support questions, localizes support articles and more? <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/participate/">Join us</a>!
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "285136", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 16, 2024, 8:49:04 AM", "datetime": "2024-09-16T08:49:04.298659-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP: do not approve needs revisions and approval from Wayne", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 16, 2024, 9:56:29 AM", "datetime": "2024-09-16T09:56:29.660370-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 17, 2024, 6:37:05 AM", "datetime": "2024-09-17T06:37:05.660749-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:32:45+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (286063,'en-US','thunderbird-android-import','Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import/revision/286063',NULL,'2024-10-02T10:21:19.535331-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP: do not localize, needs content obviously (blocked on this feature being finished)',1,'2024-10-02T10:21:47.887070-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','Stub article only for now (in order to allow Engineering to link to it in the code).','<p>Stub article only for now (in order to allow Engineering to link to it in the code).
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "286063", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 2, 2024, 10:21:19 AM", "datetime": "2024-10-02T10:21:19.535331-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP: do not localize, needs content obviously (blocked on this feature being finished)", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 2, 2024, 10:21:47 AM", "datetime": "2024-10-02T10:21:47.887070-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:32:42+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (286343,'en-US','using-lightning-google-calendar','Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar/revision/286343',286326,'2024-10-09T18:06:46.962436-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','add sumo markup',1,'2024-10-09T18:07:39.371486-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','This article describes how to use Google calendars together with Thunderbird. You can use Google calendars in two ways. You can either have read-only access to public calendars or you can use a bi-directional calendar, with both read and write-access. 

__TOC__

= Read access =

If you only need read-access to a Google calendar, you can use the link of the public ICS file which is provided by Google, as indicated on this Google Help article: [http://www.google.com/support/calendar/bin/answer.py?hl=en&answer=37648 View from other applications]

= Read and write access =

== Configure Google account ==
Before you start the steps below, go to https://calendar.google.com/calendar/syncselect and select the calendars you want to synchronize.

== Add Calendars ==
To get read/write access to a calendar:
# Press {for mac,linux}{key Ctrl+3}{/for}{for win}{key Alt+3}{/for} to open the Calendar tab.
# On the bottom left, click {button New Calendar..} > {menu On the Network} > {button Next} and then enter your Google account address as username. This should start the Google sign-in flow. 

In case of errors, please retry at least once). After giving Thunderbird access to your calendars, you should be able to select any of the calendars you enabled in the previous step and add them to Thunderbird.','<p>This article describes how to use Google calendars together with Thunderbird. You can use Google calendars in two ways. You can either have read-only access to public calendars or you can use a bi-directional calendar, with both read and write-access. 
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_read-access"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Read access</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_read-and-write-access"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Read and write access</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configure-google-account"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Google account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_add-calendars"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Add Calendars</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_read-access">Read access</h1>
<p>If you only need read-access to a Google calendar, you can use the link of the public ICS file which is provided by Google, as indicated on this Google Help article: <a href="http://www.google.com/support/calendar/bin/answer.py?hl=en&amp;answer=37648">View from other applications</a>
</p>
<h1 id="w_read-and-write-access">Read and write access</h1>
<h2 id="w_configure-google-account">Configure Google account</h2>
<p>Before you start the steps below, go to <a href="https://calendar.google.com/calendar/syncselect">https://calendar.google.com/calendar/syncselect</a> and select the calendars you want to synchronize.
</p>
<h2 id="w_add-calendars">Add Calendars</h2>
<p>To get read/write access to a calendar:
</p>
<ol><li> Press <span class="for" data-for="mac,linux"><span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">3</span></span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><span class="key">Alt</span> + <span class="key">3</span></span> to open the Calendar tab.
</li><li> On the bottom left, click <span class="button">New Calendar..</span> &gt; <span class="menu">On the Network</span> &gt; <span class="button">Next</span> and then enter your Google account address as username. This should start the Google sign-in flow. 
</li></ol>
<p>In case of errors, please retry at least once). After giving Thunderbird access to your calendars, you should be able to select any of the calendars you enabled in the previous step and add them to Thunderbird.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "286343", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 9, 2024, 6:06:46 PM", "datetime": "2024-10-09T18:06:46.962436-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "add sumo markup", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 9, 2024, 6:07:39 PM", "datetime": "2024-10-09T18:07:39.371486-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:32:40+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (286345,'en-US','using-lightning-google-calendar','Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar/revision/286345',286343,'2024-10-09T20:57:38.081338-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP, do not translate, needs approval from Wayne et al',1,'2024-10-09T20:58:45.423573-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','This article describes how to use Google calendars together with Thunderbird. You can use Google calendars in two ways. You can either use a read/write calendar, with both read and write access or read only (also known as view only) access to public calendars. 

__TOC__

= How to add read/write Google calendars =

== First, select the Google calendars you want to synchronize with Thunderbird ==
Before you start the steps below, go to https://calendar.google.com/calendar/syncselect and select the calendars you want to synchronize. If you do not select them in this step, they will not appear in Thunderbird in the next step.

== Second, add your read/write calendars ==
# Click {button ≡} > {menu New Account} > {menu Calendar} > {menu On the Network} > {button Next} 
# Enter your Google account address in the ''''''Username'''''' field and then click {button Find Calendars}. This should start the Google sign-in flow and then present you with a list of calendars you can subcribe to.
# Tick the calendars you would like to synchronize and then click {button Subscribe}. 

In case of errors, please retry at least once. You should be able to select any of the calendars you enabled in the previous step and add them to Thunderbird.

= How to add read only Google calendars =

If you only need read-access  to a Google calendar, you can use the link of the public ICS file which is provided by Google, as indicated on this Google Help article: [http://www.google.com/support/calendar/bin/answer.py?hl=en&answer=37648 View from other applications]','<p>This article describes how to use Google calendars together with Thunderbird. You can use Google calendars in two ways. You can either use a read/write calendar, with both read and write access or read only (also known as view only) access to public calendars. 
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_how-to-add-readwrite-google-calendars"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">How to add read/write Google calendars</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_first-select-the-google-calendars-you-want-to-synchronize-with-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">First, select the Google calendars you want to synchronize with Thunderbird</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_second-add-your-readwrite-calendars"><span class="tocnumber">1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Second, add your read/write calendars</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_how-to-add-read-only-google-calendars"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">How to add read only Google calendars</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_how-to-add-readwrite-google-calendars">How to add read/write Google calendars</h1>
<h2 id="w_first-select-the-google-calendars-you-want-to-synchronize-with-thunderbird">First, select the Google calendars you want to synchronize with Thunderbird</h2>
<p>Before you start the steps below, go to <a href="https://calendar.google.com/calendar/syncselect">https://calendar.google.com/calendar/syncselect</a> and select the calendars you want to synchronize. If you do not select them in this step, they will not appear in Thunderbird in the next step.
</p>
<h2 id="w_second-add-your-readwrite-calendars">Second, add your read/write calendars</h2>
<ol><li> Click <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Calendar</span> &gt; <span class="menu">On the Network</span> &gt; <span class="button">Next</span> 
</li><li> Enter your Google account address in the <strong>Username</strong> field and then click <span class="button">Find Calendars</span>. This should start the Google sign-in flow and then present you with a list of calendars you can subcribe to.
</li><li> Tick the calendars you would like to synchronize and then click <span class="button">Subscribe</span>. 
</li></ol>
<p>In case of errors, please retry at least once. You should be able to select any of the calendars you enabled in the previous step and add them to Thunderbird.
</p>
<h1 id="w_how-to-add-read-only-google-calendars">How to add read only Google calendars</h1>
<p>If you only need read-access  to a Google calendar, you can use the link of the public ICS file which is provided by Google, as indicated on this Google Help article: <a href="http://www.google.com/support/calendar/bin/answer.py?hl=en&amp;answer=37648">View from other applications</a>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "286345", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 9, 2024, 8:57:38 PM", "datetime": "2024-10-09T20:57:38.081338-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP, do not translate, needs approval from Wayne et al", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 9, 2024, 8:58:45 PM", "datetime": "2024-10-09T20:58:45.423573-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:32:38+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (286782,'en-US','thunderbird-android-import','Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import/revision/286782',286063,'2024-10-15T17:31:01.310347-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP: do not localize, needs content obviously (blocked on this feature being finished)',1,'2024-10-15T17:31:19.083335-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','= How To Import from Thunderbird Desktop = 
== Export Thunderbird Desktop settings ==
# Click {button ≡} > {menu Tools} > {menu Export for Mobile}. 
# Tick the Email accounts you wish to export to Thunderbird for Android.
# If you have accounts that are not using OAuth and you wish to import your passwords, tick ''''''Tick all accounts passwords'''''' to export your passwords in the QR code.
# Click {button Export}. A QR code will be displayed. Follow the steps below on your Android phone or tablet to scan the QR code.
== Import the Desktop settings into Thunderbird for Android ==
# Open Thunderbird for Android on your phone or tablet.
## If you see a screen with two buttons: {button Start} and {button Import settings}, tap {button Import settings} > {button Scan QR code} and scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop.
## Otherwise, tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the [[Image:gear icon]] at the bottom left > tap {button Import settings} > {button Scan QR code} and scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop.
# Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all ticked. Tap {button Import}.
# If your account uses OAuth or you have chosen not to import your passwords, you will be prompted for your passwords.','<h1 id="w_how-to-import-from-thunderbird-desktop">How To Import from Thunderbird Desktop</h1>
<h2 id="w_export-thunderbird-desktop-settings">Export Thunderbird Desktop settings</h2>
<ol><li> Click <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Export for Mobile</span>. 
</li><li> Tick the Email accounts you wish to export to Thunderbird for Android.
</li><li> If you have accounts that are not using OAuth and you wish to import your passwords, tick <strong>Tick all accounts passwords</strong> to export your passwords in the QR code.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Export</span>. A QR code will be displayed. Follow the steps below on your Android phone or tablet to scan the QR code.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_import-the-desktop-settings-into-thunderbird-for-android">Import the Desktop settings into Thunderbird for Android</h2>
<ol><li> Open Thunderbird for Android on your phone or tablet.
<ol><li> If you see a screen with two buttons: <span class="button">Start</span> and <span class="button">Import settings</span>, tap <span class="button">Import settings</span> &gt; <span class="button">Scan QR code</span> and scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop.
</li><li> Otherwise, tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the <img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> at the bottom left &gt; tap <span class="button">Import settings</span> &gt; <span class="button">Scan QR code</span> and scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop.
</li></ol>
</li><li> Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all ticked. Tap <span class="button">Import</span>.
</li><li> If your account uses OAuth or you have chosen not to import your passwords, you will be prompted for your passwords.
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "286782", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 15, 2024, 5:31:01 PM", "datetime": "2024-10-15T17:31:01.310347-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP: do not localize, needs content obviously (blocked on this feature being finished)", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 15, 2024, 5:31:19 PM", "datetime": "2024-10-15T17:31:19.083335-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:32:35+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (286783,'en-US','thunderbird-android-import','Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import/revision/286783',286782,'2024-10-15T17:33:02.046090-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fix typo, needs approval from Wayne and others',1,'2024-10-15T17:33:22.247816-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','= How To Import from Thunderbird Desktop = 
== Export Thunderbird Desktop settings ==
# Click {button ≡} > {menu Tools} > {menu Export for Mobile}. 
# Tick the Email accounts you wish to export to Thunderbird for Android.
# If you have accounts that are not using OAuth and you wish to import your passwords, tick ''''''Include all accounts passwords'''''' under ''''''Security'''''' to export your passwords in the QR code.
# Click {button Export}. A QR code will be displayed. Follow the steps below on your Android phone or tablet to scan the QR code.
== Import the Desktop settings into Thunderbird for Android ==
# Open Thunderbird for Android on your phone or tablet.
## If you see a screen with two buttons: {button Start} and {button Import settings}, tap {button Import settings} > {button Scan QR code} and scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop.
## Otherwise, tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the [[Image:gear icon]] at the bottom left > tap {button Import settings} > {button Scan QR code} and scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop.
# Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all ticked. Tap {button Import}.
# If your account uses OAuth or you have chosen not to import your passwords, you will be prompted for your passwords.','<h1 id="w_how-to-import-from-thunderbird-desktop">How To Import from Thunderbird Desktop</h1>
<h2 id="w_export-thunderbird-desktop-settings">Export Thunderbird Desktop settings</h2>
<ol><li> Click <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Export for Mobile</span>. 
</li><li> Tick the Email accounts you wish to export to Thunderbird for Android.
</li><li> If you have accounts that are not using OAuth and you wish to import your passwords, tick <strong>Include all accounts passwords</strong> under <strong>Security</strong> to export your passwords in the QR code.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Export</span>. A QR code will be displayed. Follow the steps below on your Android phone or tablet to scan the QR code.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_import-the-desktop-settings-into-thunderbird-for-android">Import the Desktop settings into Thunderbird for Android</h2>
