<h1 id="w_introducing-in-app-scheduled-notifications-a-powerful-new-tool-for-thunderbird">Introducing In-App Scheduled Notifications: A Powerful New Tool for Thunderbird</h1>
<p>Thunderbird users, get ready for a game-changing feature in the next major release! As part of Thunderbird Beta 134, we''re introducing In-App Scheduled Notifications, a dynamic and intelligent notification system designed to keep you informed about essential updates—without overwhelming you. Whether it’s an important security notice, a donation appeal, or a quick survey, you''ll stay in the loop without interrupting your workflow.
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-are-in-app-scheduled-notifications">What are In-App Scheduled Notifications?</h1>
<p>Thunderbird will now be equipped with a brand-new notification system that allows us to push important, time-sensitive information directly to your Thunderbird desktop client. Unlike traditional notifications, which appear whenever they want, these notifications are scheduled in a smart and structured way. They are tailored to you, based on your Thunderbird version, operating system, and locale.
</p>
<h1 id="w_how-will-this-benefit-you">How Will This Benefit You?</h1>
<h2 id="w_stay-secure-with-timely-updates">Stay Secure with Timely Updates</h2>
<p>One of the most significant benefits of this system is that you’ll get real-time security notices when they matter most. No need to sift through emails or websites for updates—Thunderbird will inform you about critical security issues right inside your application. This ensures that your system stays safe without lifting a finger!
</p>
<h2 id="w_tailored-surveys-to-improve-your-experience">Tailored Surveys to Improve Your Experience</h2>
<p>Ever had feedback about Thunderbird? With in-app notifications, you may be invited to take short surveys that help improve your experience. These surveys will be carefully targeted, meaning you’ll only see ones that are relevant to you, based on your version of Thunderbird or locale.
</p>
<h1 id="w_how-does-it-work">How Does It Work?</h1>
<p>The notification system is designed to be both smart and respectful. Here’s how it works behind the scenes:
</p>
<ul><li> Custom-Tailored Notifications: The notifications you see are tailored to your profile. The system considers your locale, Thunderbird version, and operating system, ensuring that the messages you receive are relevant.
</li><li> Controlled Frequency: You won’t be bombarded with notifications. Thunderbird contacts our server occasionally (not too often!) and retrieves notifications relevant to you. The frequency and scheduling are carefully managed to ensure the system isn’t intrusive.
</li><li> Dynamic Display Timing: To avoid overwhelming all users at once, the system can stagger the release of notifications. So, while one user might see a notification today, another user might see it tomorrow—making it a seamless experience for everyone.
</li><li> Locally Cached for Efficiency: Once retrieved, Thunderbird will cache the notification data locally, meaning you won’t miss any critical alerts even when offline.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_security-first">Security First</h1>
<p>We take your privacy seriously. These notifications are designed to respect your personal data, pulling only what''s necessary for targeting specific content, like locale and operating system. No geographical location or sensitive information is shared. Plus, Thunderbird always opens any provided URLs in your default browser to ensure you''re secure.
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-kind-of-notifications-will-you-see">What Kind of Notifications Will You See?</h1>
<p>With this system, expect to see notifications that are truly helpful and relevant:
</p>
<ul><li> Donation Appeals: Stay informed about how you can help fund Thunderbird’s future.
</li><li> Security Notices: Get alerted to any security updates or patches you need to apply.
</li><li> Surveys: Occasionally, we’ll ask for your input on new features or Thunderbird improvements.
</li></ul>
<p>What won’t you see? Things like blog posts, office hours, or non-client-specific events are out of scope. We’re focusing on notifications that benefit you directly as a Thunderbird user.
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-makes-this-feature-special">What Makes This Feature Special?</h1>
<ul><li> Smart Targeting: Based on your Thunderbird version, locale, and operating system, the notifications will be personalized for you.
</li><li> Dynamic Notification Styling: Notifications can be customized with different levels of severity and urgency, ensuring you’re only interrupted when absolutely necessary.
</li><li> Dismiss and Move On: Don’t want to see a notification again? Dismiss it, and Thunderbird won’t bug you with the same message repeatedly.
</li><li> A Better Way to Stay Informed: Gone are the days of missing crucial updates. Whether it’s a security notice or a fundraising appeal, you’ll have the information you need when you need it.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_coming-soon-to-thunderbird">Coming Soon to Thunderbird</h1>
<p>We’re excited to bring this feature to all of our users with Thunderbird beta 134. This enhancement is just one of the many ways we’re working to improve your experience with Thunderbird.
</p><p>Stay tuned, and get ready to enjoy the benefits of in-app scheduled notifications. Your inbox is about to get smarter, safer, and even more powerful!
</p>
<h1 id="w_want-to-learn-more">Want to Learn More?</h1>
<p>For more information on Thunderbird''s upcoming features, head over to our official release notes or check out our <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-notifications">GitHub repository</a> if you are more technical.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "289488", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 26, 2024, 9:21:47 AM", "datetime": "2024-11-26T09:21:47.579749-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP: do not localize, needs heavy revision obviously (blocked on this feature being finished);", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 26, 2024, 9:22:18 AM", "datetime": "2024-11-26T09:22:18.792031-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:30:12+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (289532,'en-US','openpgp-thunderbird-android-howto','OpenPGP in Thunderbird for Android - How To','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/openpgp-thunderbird-android-howto','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/openpgp-thunderbird-android-howto/revision/289532',288882,'2024-11-26T23:50:00.815889-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP, do not localize please https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/11#issuecomment-2503034044',1,'2024-11-26T23:50:42.030961-08:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','Thunderbird for Android does not have built-in encryption capabilities. Instead, it uses an external Android application called [https://www.openkeychain.org/ OpenKeychain].

__TOC__

== Install OpenKeychain and select it as a Crypto Provider ==
You will need to install OpenKeychain if you have not already and enable it in Thunderbird for Android.
# Install OpenKeychain from wherever you get your Android apps e.g. [https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=org.sufficientlysecure.keychain Google Play]  or [https://f-droid.org/packages/org.sufficientlysecure.keychain/ F-Droid].
# Open Thunderbird for Android. Tap the application menu {button ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]} 
# Tap the email account for encryption under ''''''Accounts'''''' e.g. tap {menu jane@example.com} to set up encrypted email for ''''''jane@example.com'''''' > tap {menu End-end encryption}.
# Slide ''''''Enable OpenPGP support'''''' to the right. You will see that  {menu Configure end-to-end key} is now enabled.

== Select an encryption key or create a new key ==
# Tap {menu Configure end-to-end key}.
# A screen from the OpenKeychain app will appear:
#;[[Image:tb-android8-pgp-openkeychain-popup]]
# Tap {button This is a new address} to create a new key or Tap {button I already have a key} if you already have created or imported a key.
See the [https://www.openkeychain.org/ OpenKeychain] website for more information on creating and managing keys.
== Sharing your key with others==
Before you send someone an end-to-end encrypted email, you need their public key. They also need your public key.

Some ways to exchange public keys include:
* meeting in-person (the OpenKeychain application has a convenient interface for mutual key exchange).
* downloading from the recipient''s personal website.
* relying on the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_of_trust Web of Trust] whereby you trust somebody else''s word that a public key is valid.
* downloading the key from a [https://www.rossde.com/PGP/pgp_keyserv.html KeyServer] (but note the warning on that page about needing to verify the authenticity of keys).
* using ''''Autocrypt'''', which includes your key in the header of every email that you send. This is not supported by all mail clients.

=== Share your key using Autocrypt ===
K-9 Mail supports the Autocrypt protocol, but it needs to be enabled in the End-to-end encryption settings page:
# Tap the application menu {button ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]} 
# Tap the email account for encryption under ''''''Accounts'''''' e.g. tap {menu jane@example.com} to set up encrypted email for ''''''jane@example.com'''''' > tap {menu End-end encryption}.
# Tap {menu Autocrypt mutual mode} and tick the box in the popup > tap {button OK}:
#;[[Image:tb-android8-autocrypt-mutual-mode-popup]]

== How to send a signed and encrypted email ==
If OpenKeychain knows the PGP keys of the receipients, then you will be able to send an email that is signed and encrypted. 
* When composing e-mail after OpenKeychain has been set up, a new padlock icon appears in the top right of the composition screen:
*;[[Image:tb-android8-padlock-unlocked.png]]
(If the icon does not appear, it means that OpenKeychain does not know the PGP keys of any of the recipients).
* Tap the padlock icon to enable encryption. Once you tap the padlock icon, it turns green:
*;[[Image:tb-android8-green-padlock-locked]]
== How to send a signed and unencrypted email ==
Thunderbird for Android normally sends mails that are both encrypted and signed. You can also sign the message, which proves it was sent by you, but without encrypting it. This is sometimes useful ; e.g. in public mailing lists.
[[UI:details_start]] 
=== First, disable "Hide unencrypted signatures" for the account ===
# Tap the application menu {button ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]} 
# Tap the email account for encryption under ''''''Accounts'''''' e.g. tap {menu jane@example.com} for ''''''jane@example.com'''''' > tap {menu End-end encryption}.
# Slide {menu Hide unecrypted signatures} to the left to disable signature hiding for this account.
#;[[Image:tb-android8-hide-unencrypted-signature]]
=== Second, enable signed and unencrypted mode when composing email ===
When composing email, tap the vertical ellipsis icon {button ⋮} > tap {menu Enable PGP Sign-Only} option.
;[[Image:620px-tb-android8-enable-pgp-sign-only]]
The lock will add another icon to confirm you are only signing the message:
;[[Image:tb8-signed-not-encrypted-icon]]
Tapping the icon lets you change back to normal end-to-end encryption mode.
[[UI:details_end]]','<p>Thunderbird for Android does not have built-in encryption capabilities. Instead, it uses an external Android application called <a href="https://www.openkeychain.org/">OpenKeychain</a>.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_install-openkeychain-and-select-it-as-a-crypto-provider"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Install OpenKeychain and select it as a Crypto Provider</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_select-an-encryption-key-or-create-a-new-key"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Select an encryption key or create a new key</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_sharing-your-key-with-others"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Sharing your key with others</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_share-your-key-using-autocrypt"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Share your key using Autocrypt</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_how-to-send-a-signed-and-encrypted-email"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">How to send a signed and encrypted email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_how-to-send-a-signed-and-unencrypted-email"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">How to send a signed and unencrypted email</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_first-disable-hide-unencrypted-signatures-for-the-account"><span class="tocnumber">5.1</span> <span class="toctext">First, disable "Hide unencrypted signatures" for the account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_second-enable-signed-and-unencrypted-mode-when-composing-email"><span class="tocnumber">5.2</span> <span class="toctext">Second, enable signed and unencrypted mode when composing email</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h2 id="w_install-openkeychain-and-select-it-as-a-crypto-provider">Install OpenKeychain and select it as a Crypto Provider</h2>
<p>You will need to install OpenKeychain if you have not already and enable it in Thunderbird for Android.
</p>
<ol><li> Install OpenKeychain from wherever you get your Android apps e.g. <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=org.sufficientlysecure.keychain">Google Play</a>  or <a href="https://f-droid.org/packages/org.sufficientlysecure.keychain/">F-Droid</a>.
</li><li> Open Thunderbird for Android. Tap the application menu <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> 
</li><li> Tap the email account for encryption under <strong>Accounts</strong> e.g. tap <span class="menu"><a href="mailto:jane@example.com">jane@example.com</a></span> to set up encrypted email for <strong><a href="mailto:jane@example.com">jane@example.com</a></strong> &gt; tap <span class="menu">End-end encryption</span>.
</li><li> Slide <strong>Enable OpenPGP support</strong> to the right. You will see that  <span class="menu">Configure end-to-end key</span> is now enabled.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_select-an-encryption-key-or-create-a-new-key">Select an encryption key or create a new key</h2>
<ol><li> Tap <span class="menu">Configure end-to-end key</span>.
</li><li> A screen from the OpenKeychain app will appear:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb-android8-pgp-openkeychain-popup" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-19-22-52-07-be2e54.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">This is a new address</span> to create a new key or Tap <span class="button">I already have a key</span> if you already have created or imported a key.
</li></ol>
<p>See the <a href="https://www.openkeychain.org/">OpenKeychain</a> website for more information on creating and managing keys.
</p>
<h2 id="w_sharing-your-key-with-others">Sharing your key with others</h2>
<p>Before you send someone an end-to-end encrypted email, you need their public key. They also need your public key.
</p><p>Some ways to exchange public keys include:
</p>
<ul><li> meeting in-person (the OpenKeychain application has a convenient interface for mutual key exchange).
</li><li> downloading from the recipient''s personal website.
</li><li> relying on the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_of_trust">Web of Trust</a> whereby you trust somebody else''s word that a public key is valid.
</li><li> downloading the key from a <a href="https://www.rossde.com/PGP/pgp_keyserv.html">KeyServer</a> (but note the warning on that page about needing to verify the authenticity of keys).
</li><li> using <em>Autocrypt</em>, which includes your key in the header of every email that you send. This is not supported by all mail clients.
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_share-your-key-using-autocrypt">Share your key using Autocrypt</h3>
<p>K-9 Mail supports the Autocrypt protocol, but it needs to be enabled in the End-to-end encryption settings page:
</p>
<ol><li> Tap the application menu <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> 
</li><li> Tap the email account for encryption under <strong>Accounts</strong> e.g. tap <span class="menu"><a href="mailto:jane@example.com">jane@example.com</a></span> to set up encrypted email for <strong><a href="mailto:jane@example.com">jane@example.com</a></strong> &gt; tap <span class="menu">End-end encryption</span>.
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Autocrypt mutual mode</span> and tick the box in the popup &gt; tap <span class="button">OK</span>:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb-android8-autocrypt-mutual-mode-popup" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-19-23-19-54-fa1947.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_how-to-send-a-signed-and-encrypted-email">How to send a signed and encrypted email</h2>
<p>If OpenKeychain knows the PGP keys of the receipients, then you will be able to send an email that is signed and encrypted. 
</p>
<ul><li> When composing e-mail after OpenKeychain has been set up, a new padlock icon appears in the top right of the composition screen:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb-android8-padlock-unlocked.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-19-23-40-48-f6a1fb.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ul>
<p>(If the icon does not appear, it means that OpenKeychain does not know the PGP keys of any of the recipients).
</p>
<ul><li> Tap the padlock icon to enable encryption. Once you tap the padlock icon, it turns green:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb-android8-green-padlock-locked" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-19-23-46-48-71f95b.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_how-to-send-a-signed-and-unencrypted-email">How to send a signed and unencrypted email</h2>
<p>Thunderbird for Android normally sends mails that are both encrypted and signed. You can also sign the message, which proves it was sent by you, but without encrypting it. This is sometimes useful&nbsp;; e.g. in public mailing lists.
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details"> 
<p></p>
