-->','<p>In order for this feature to work, you have to enable and configure notifications both in Thunderbird and in your operating system.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_1-turn-on-native-notifications-in-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">1. Turn on native notifications in Thunderbird</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_2-configure-your-operating-systems-native-notifications"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">2. Configure your operating system''s native notifications</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_windows"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Windows</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_turn-on-thunderbird-email-notifications"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Turn on Thunderbird email notifications</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_example-windows-11-notification"><span class="tocnumber">2.3</span> <span class="toctext">Example: Windows 11 notification</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_macos"><span class="tocnumber">2.4</span> <span class="toctext">macOS</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_1-turn-on-native-notifications-in-thunderbird">1. Turn on native notifications in Thunderbird</h1>
<ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> &gt; search for <strong>When new messages arrive</strong>. <span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">Ensure <strong>Use the system notification</strong> is ticked "✓" </span>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Customize...</span>
</li><li> Ensure the fields and actions you would like to display in your new email notifications are ticked "✓" (<strong>Archive</strong> is only available in 141 and newer releases).
<dl><dt><img alt="borderless customize new mail alert" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-07-21-23-13-12-298a52.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
<div class="warning">Some operating systems have limits on how many action buttons are displayed in a notification. Windows 11 for example will only display 4 action buttons even if you have selected 5 or more actions.</div>
<h1 id="w_2-configure-your-operating-systems-native-notifications">2. Configure your operating system''s native notifications</h1>
<p>Please follow your operating system''s documentation to enable and configure notifications native to your operating system.
</p>
<h2 id="w_windows">Windows</h2>
<h2 id="w_turn-on-thunderbird-email-notifications">Turn on Thunderbird email notifications</h2>
<p>(Refer to Microsoft''s Knowledge Base Article for more details:  <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/notifications-and-do-not-disturb-in-windows-feeca47f-0baf-5680-16f0-8801db1a8466">Notifications and Do Not Disturb in Windows</a>.)
</p>
<ol><li> Enable notifications globally: In the Settings app  on your Windows device, open System &gt; <span class="menu">Notifications</span> and then toggle the switch next to <span class="menu">Notifications</span> to <span class="button">On</span>.
</li><li> Under <span class="menu">Notifications from apps and other senders</span>, scroll down to <span class="menu">Mozilla Thunderbird</span> and then toggle the switch to <span class="button">On</span>.
</li><li> Click on the chevron <span class="button">&gt;</span> to configure more options.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_example-windows-11-notification">Example: Windows 11 notification</h2>
<p>Here''s how notifications look in Windows 11. 
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="windows11-tb142-new-email-os-notification" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-00-51-19-907483.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_macos" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">macOS</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p>(Refer to Apple''s Knowledge Base Article for more details:  <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-uz/guide/mac-help/mh40583/15.0/mac/15.0">Notifications settings on Mac</a>.)
</p><ol><li> Choose Apple menu  &gt; <span class="menu">System Settings...</span> and then click <span class="menu">Notifications</span> in the sidebar.
</li><li> Scroll down to <span class="menu">Thunderbird</span> and then click on the chevron <span class="button">&gt;</span> 
</li><li> Next to <span class="menu">Allow notifications</span> and then toggle the switch to the right to turn on Thunderbird notifications.
</li><li> Optional: configure the rest of the notification options to suit your requirements.
</li></ol><p></p></div>


</section>

<p></p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "302716", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 19, 2025, 1:11:59 AM", "datetime": "2025-08-19T01:11:59.495534-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "added macOS section WIP, do not localize or approve please :-) For discussion please leave comments in https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/89", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:26:58+00:00');
