<p></p><p>Android''s settings and automatic screen dimming can make it difficult to read the screen due to low contrast. Here''s how to reduce screen dimming and make Thunderbird more readable.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_general-tips-and-tricks"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">General tips and tricks</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-settings"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird settings</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_use-thunderbirds-dark-theme"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Use Thunderbird''s Dark theme</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_third-party-documentation"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Third Party documentation</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_google-android-settings"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Google Android settings</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_google-pixel-settings"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Google Pixel settings</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_samsung-phones-and-tablets"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">Samsung phones and tablets</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_third-party-links-disclaimer"><span class="tocnumber">3.4</span> <span class="toctext">Third Party links disclaimer</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div> 
<h1 id="w_general-tips-and-tricks">General tips and tricks</h1>
<ul><li> A dirty proximity sensor (located near the front-facing camera) can cause your screen to dim. To prevent this, keep the area around the front-facing camera clean.
</li><li> Different manufacturers often implement different variants of Android and have slightly differently named Android settings. Also, setting names change in newer versions of Android. It is therefore impossible to give a comprehensive guide to the relevant settings for all Android devices for all Android versions.
</li></ul>
<p>Here are some specific tips that will allow you to find the relevant settings for screen dimming on your device: Look for Android settings under sections with words like: 
</p>
<ul><li> <strong>Eye comfort shield</strong>: Turn off.
</li><li> <strong>Power saving</strong>: Turn off or increase timeout before it starts.
</li><li> <strong>Battery saver</strong>: Turn off or increase timeout to prevent dimming.
</li><li> <strong>High contrast text</strong> or <strong>High contrast mode</strong>: See if turning these settings on makes your screen more readable.
</li><li> <strong>Accessibility</strong> and <strong>Display</strong>: Look for settings related to screen dimming, screen timeouts and lock settings and turn off all automatic screen dimming, extra screen dimming (often found in <span class="menu">Android Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Accessibility</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Extra dim</span>) and increase timeouts for screen locking.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-settings">Thunderbird settings</h1>
<h2 id="w_use-thunderbirds-dark-theme">Use Thunderbird''s Dark theme</h2>
<ul><li> The Dark theme helps reduce eye strain by decreasing overall screen brightness and contrast, which can make it easier to view your screen in dim environments and reduce eye strain.
</li></ul>
<ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the gear icon:<span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> at the bottom left.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">General settings</span> &gt; tap <span class="menu">Display</span> &gt; tap <span class="button">Dark</span> under <strong>Theme</strong>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_third-party-documentation">Third Party documentation</h1>
<h2 id="w_google-android-settings">Google Android settings</h2>
<ul><li> On some Android devices, using Android''s Dark theme might be helpful (in addtion to using Thunderbird''s dark theme). See Google''s documentation: <a href="https://support.google.com/accessibility/android/answer/6151800">Turn on Dark theme &amp; color inversion</a>.
</li><li> How to turn off <strong>Extra dim</strong> can be found in Google''s article: <a href="https://support.google.com/accessibility/android/answer/11183305#colorcorrection&amp;zippy=%2Cuse-color-correction%2Cdim-your-screen">Change text &amp; display settings</a>.
</li><li> For more information on the features for low-vision assistance, visit the <a href="https://support.google.com/accessibility/android">Android Accessibility Help Centre</a>.
</li></ul>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_google-pixel-settings" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Google Pixel settings</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true">Pixel phones have their own variant of Android. Please refer to the official documentation: <a href="https://support.google.com/pixelphone/answer/6111557">Manage screen &amp; display settings on a Pixel phone</a>
</p></section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_samsung-phones-and-tablets" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Samsung phones and tablets</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>Samsung has specific documentation for all of their Android devices (both non Galaxy and Galaxy):
</p><ul><li> <a href="https://www.samsung.com/us/support/answer/ANS10003431/">Stop the screen brightness from dimming on your Galaxy phone or tablet</a>.
</li><li> Follow the instructions in Samsung''s guide: <a href="https://www.samsung.com/us/support/troubleshoot/TSG10001443/">Unable to turn on Eye comfort shield on Samsung phone or tablet</a> in reverse. In other words, <strong>disable</strong> the Eye comfort shield instead of enabling it: turn on Color inversion if needed, turn on the Color filter and enable Color adjustment or Color correction.
</li></ul><p></p></div>


</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_third-party-links-disclaimer" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-expanded="false">Third Party links disclaimer</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-hidden="true"><div class="note">Please note that following the external links to Samsung and Google''s websites in the previous sections will direct you to an external website that is not operated by Mozilla. These links can  break at any time without notice.</div><p></p></div>

</section>
<p></p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "309805", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 20, 2025, 9:51:35 PM", "datetime": "2025-10-20T21:51:35.681733-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "incorporating more of laurel''s feedback WIP, do not approve or localize :-) please. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/20", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 20, 2025, 9:52:28 PM", "datetime": "2025-10-20T21:52:28.021738-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:25:16+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (311119,'en-US','configure-push-email-thunderbird-android','Configure push email for Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/configure-push-email-thunderbird-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/configure-push-email-thunderbird-android/revision/311119',298985,'2025-10-23T12:00:11.410612-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','have to turn on notifications as well as push for Push email discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/25',1,'2025-10-23T12:00:27.417286-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,1,'2026-02-20T13:29:58.402806-08:00','rtanglao','approved','By default, push email is off for all folders. Thunderbird for Android allows you to configure a limited number of folders (IMAP only; POP is not supported.) for push email so that you can see incoming email messages immediately.

__TOC__

=Turn on push email for a folder=
{warning}
WARNING:
* If you kill the Thunderbird for Android app, for example by swiping up on the recent task window, push email will stop working. Thunderbird needs to be running, and it needs network access for push email to work.
* The maximum number of push folders for an account is set in the app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]} > ''''''your email account'''''' > {menu Fetching mail} > {menu Advanced} > {menu Max folders to check with push}. Thunderbird does not warn you when this limit is exceeded. Exceeding this limit will result in push email not working on some of the folders you configured for push.
{/warning}
# Tap the app menu {menu ≡} at the top left. If your email accounts are hidden, tap {button Show accounts} at the bottom of the screen.
# Tap account, for example your Gmail account > tap {button Manage folders}.
# Tap your folder of choice. For example: tap ''''''Inbox'''''' to enable push email for your Inbox.
#;[[Image:enable-push-tb-android-8]]
# Turn on push email for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the right for ''''''Enable Push''''''.
# Turn on notifications for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the right for ''''''Notifications''''''.
# Turn off sync for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the left for ''''''Enable sync''''''.
{note}The ''''''Enable sync'''''' setting is for periodic syncs and should be disabled once push is enabled.{/note}
Once push email is enabled for at least one folder, you should see a bell icon in the Android system bar. The bell icon indicates that push email is enabled.
;[[Image:bell-icon-android-system-bar-tb8-pushmail-notification]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Alarms & reminders permission for Android 13 and above==
{note}This section is not applicable to you if your Android version is older than Android 13.{/note}
# On Android 13 and above, a notification will let you know you need to grant the ''''Alarms & reminders'''' permission. Proceed to the permissions screen by tapping the {button Missing permission to schedule alarms} notification.
#;[[Image:missing-permission-tb8-android-pushmail]]
# Add this permission by tapping until the slider is on the right for ''''''Allow setting alarms and reminders''''''. If you do not grant this permission, then push email will not work.
#;[[Image:allow-setting-alarms-reminders-tb8-android-pushmail]]
[[UI:details_end]]

=The push email service notification=
* Once the permission is granted (or right after enabling push on older Android versions), Thunderbird for Android will display a push email service notification entitled ''''''Waiting for new emails'''''':
*;[[Image:notification-waiting-for-new-emails]]
* Tapping on the {button Waiting for new emails} notification will allow you to configure the notification itself ({button Configure notification}) or to disable push email altogether ({button Disable Push}):
*;[[Image:configure-notification-or-disable-push]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Hide, change or disable the push email service notification using “Configure notification”==
You can change the display of this notification or hide it by tapping {button Configure notification}.
;[[Image:notification-settings-tb8-android-pushmail]]
*''''''Hide the push email service notification:'''''' Slide ''''''Show notifications'''''' to the left. Hiding the push email service notification does not turn off push email.
*''''''Change the push email service notification:'''''' Minimizing the notification will hide the bell icon from the Android status bar, but still keep the notification in the notification shade. You will still be able to see the status of your push email service (for example, ''''''Waiting for new emails'''''' or ''''''Sleeping until network is available'''''').
*''''''Disable Push email from the push email service notification using “Disable Push”:''''''Tap {button Disable Push} to disable push email altogether.
[[UI:details_end]]

=Turn off push email for a folder=
# Tap the app menu {menu ≡} at the top left. If your email accounts are hidden, tap {button Show accounts} at the bottom of the screen.
# Tap account, for example, your Gmail account > tap {button Manage folders}.
# Tap your folder of choice. For example, tap ''''''Inbox'''''' to disable push email for your Inbox.
# Turn off push email for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the left for ''''''Enable Push''''''.
# Turn on sync for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the right for ''''''Enable sync''''''.

=New Email notifications are completely separate from the push email service notification=
The notifications for new email messages are configured separately from the push email service notification. New email notifications are configured as follows:
* Tap the app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]} > {menu Notifications} > {menu Messages} under ''''Notification categories''''.

=Learn more=
* Wikipedia: [https://wikipedia.org/wiki/Push_email Push email]
* Google: [https://support.google.com/android/answer/9079661 Control notifications on Android]','<p>By default, push email is off for all folders. Thunderbird for Android allows you to configure a limited number of folders (IMAP only; POP is not supported.) for push email so that you can see incoming email messages immediately.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_turn-on-push-email-for-a-folder"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Turn on push email for a folder</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_alarms-reminders-permission-for-android-13-and-above"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Alarms &amp; reminders permission for Android 13 and above</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_the-push-email-service-notification"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">The push email service notification</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_hide-change-or-disable-the-push-email-service-notification-using-configure-notification"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Hide, change or disable the push email service notification using “Configure notification”</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_turn-off-push-email-for-a-folder"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Turn off push email for a folder</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_new-email-notifications-are-completely-separate-from-the-push-email-service-notification"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">New Email notifications are completely separate from the push email service notification</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_learn-more"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Learn more</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_turn-on-push-email-for-a-folder">Turn on push email for a folder</h1>
<div class="warning">
<p>WARNING:
</p>
<ul><li> If you kill the Thunderbird for Android app, for example by swiping up on the recent task window, push email will stop working. Thunderbird needs to be running, and it needs network access for push email to work.
</li><li> The maximum number of push folders for an account is set in the app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> &gt; <strong>your email account</strong> &gt; <span class="menu">Fetching mail</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Advanced</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Max folders to check with push</span>. Thunderbird does not warn you when this limit is exceeded. Exceeding this limit will result in push email not working on some of the folders you configured for push.
</li></ul>
</div>
<ol><li> Tap the app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> at the top left. If your email accounts are hidden, tap <span class="button">Show accounts</span> at the bottom of the screen.
</li><li> Tap account, for example your Gmail account &gt; tap <span class="button">Manage folders</span>.
</li><li> Tap your folder of choice. For example: tap <strong>Inbox</strong> to enable push email for your Inbox.
<dl><dt><img alt="enable-push-tb-android-8" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-11-21-10-00-62ddea.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Turn on push email for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the right for <strong>Enable Push</strong>.
</li><li> Turn on notifications for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the right for <strong>Notifications</strong>.
</li><li> Turn off sync for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the left for <strong>Enable sync</strong>.
</li></ol>
<div class="note">The <strong>Enable sync</strong> setting is for periodic syncs and should be disabled once push is enabled.</div>
<p>Once push email is enabled for at least one folder, you should see a bell icon in the Android system bar. The bell icon indicates that push email is enabled.
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="bell-icon-android-system-bar-tb8-pushmail-notification" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-11-21-15-19-df30c1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_alarms-reminders-permission-for-android-13-and-above" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Alarms &amp; reminders permission for Android 13 and above</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><div class="note">This section is not applicable to you if your Android version is older than Android 13.</div><ol><li> On Android 13 and above, a notification will let you know you need to grant the <em>Alarms &amp; reminders</em> permission. Proceed to the permissions screen by tapping the <span class="button">Missing permission to schedule alarms</span> notification.
<dl><dt><img alt="missing-permission-tb8-android-pushmail" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-11-21-21-43-416308.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Add this permission by tapping until the slider is on the right for <strong>Allow setting alarms and reminders</strong>. If you do not grant this permission, then push email will not work.
<dl><dt><img alt="allow-setting-alarms-reminders-tb8-android-pushmail" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-11-21-29-13-66fbe3.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol><p></p></div>


</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_the-push-email-service-notification">The push email service notification</h1>
<ul><li> Once the permission is granted (or right after enabling push on older Android versions), Thunderbird for Android will display a push email service notification entitled <strong>Waiting for new emails</strong>:
<dl><dt><img alt="notification-waiting-for-new-emails" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-15-14-39-10-32ff52.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Tapping on the <span class="button">Waiting for new emails</span> notification will allow you to configure the notification itself (<span class="button">Configure notification</span>) or to disable push email altogether (<span class="button">Disable Push</span>):
<dl><dt><img alt="configure-notification-or-disable-push" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-15-13-50-14-2997ea.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ul>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_hide-change-or-disable-the-push-email-service-notification-using-configure-notification" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Hide, change or disable the push email service notification using “Configure notification”</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>You can change the display of this notification or hide it by tapping <span class="button">Configure notification</span>.
</p><dl><dt><img alt="notification-settings-tb8-android-pushmail" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-11-11-21-34-51-59f0a4.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl><ul><li><strong>Hide the push email service notification:</strong> Slide <strong>Show notifications</strong> to the left. Hiding the push email service notification does not turn off push email.
</li><li><strong>Change the push email service notification:</strong> Minimizing the notification will hide the bell icon from the Android status bar, but still keep the notification in the notification shade. You will still be able to see the status of your push email service (for example, <strong>Waiting for new emails</strong> or <strong>Sleeping until network is available</strong>).
</li><li><strong>Disable Push email from the push email service notification using “Disable Push”:</strong>Tap <span class="button">Disable Push</span> to disable push email altogether.
</li></ul><p></p></div>



</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_turn-off-push-email-for-a-folder">Turn off push email for a folder</h1>
<ol><li> Tap the app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> at the top left. If your email accounts are hidden, tap <span class="button">Show accounts</span> at the bottom of the screen.
</li><li> Tap account, for example, your Gmail account &gt; tap <span class="button">Manage folders</span>.
</li><li> Tap your folder of choice. For example, tap <strong>Inbox</strong> to disable push email for your Inbox.
</li><li> Turn off push email for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the left for <strong>Enable Push</strong>.
</li><li> Turn on sync for this folder by tapping until the slider is on the right for <strong>Enable sync</strong>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_new-email-notifications-are-completely-separate-from-the-push-email-service-notification">New Email notifications are completely separate from the push email service notification</h1>
<p>The notifications for new email messages are configured separately from the push email service notification. New email notifications are configured as follows:
</p>
<ul><li> Tap the app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> &gt; <span class="menu">Notifications</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Messages</span> under <em>Notification categories</em>.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_learn-more">Learn more</h1>
<ul><li> Wikipedia: <a href="https://wikipedia.org/wiki/Push_email">Push email</a>
</li><li> Google: <a href="https://support.google.com/android/answer/9079661">Control notifications on Android</a>
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "311119", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 23, 2025, 12:00:11 PM", "datetime": "2025-10-23T12:00:11.410612-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "have to turn on notifications as well as push for Push email discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/25", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 23, 2025, 12:00:27 PM", "datetime": "2025-10-23T12:00:27.417286-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 20, 2026, 1:29:58 PM", "datetime": "2026-02-20T13:29:58.402806-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:25:13+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (311469,'en-US','stop-screen-brightness-dimming-thunderbird-android','Stop screen brightness from dimming in Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/stop-screen-brightness-dimming-thunderbird-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/stop-screen-brightness-dimming-thunderbird-android/revision/311469',309805,'2025-10-24T13:36:15.138736-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','incorporate more of Wayne & Laurel''s feedback WIP, do not approve or localize :-) please. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/20',1,'2025-10-24T13:38:08.244927-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','[[T:TBAndroidMore]]

Android''s settings and automatic screen dimming can make it difficult to read the screen due to low contrast. Here''s how to reduce screen dimming and make Thunderbird more readable.

__TOC__ 
=General tips and tricks=
* A dirty proximity sensor (located near the front-facing camera) can cause your screen to dim. To prevent this, keep the area around the front-facing camera clean.
* Different manufacturers often implement different variants of Android and have slightly differently named Android settings. Also, setting names change in newer versions of Android. It is therefore impossible to give a comprehensive guide to the relevant settings for all Android devices for all Android versions.

Here are some specific tips that will allow you to find the relevant settings for screen dimming on your device: Look for Android settings under sections with words like: 
* ''''''Eye comfort shield'''''': Turn off.
* ''''''Power saving'''''': Turn off or increase timeout before it starts.
* ''''''Battery saver'''''': Turn off or increase timeout to prevent dimming.
* ''''''High contrast text'''''' or ''''''High contrast mode'''''': See if changing these settings makes your screen more readable.
* ''''''Accessibility'''''' and ''''''Display'''''': Look for settings related to screen dimming, screen timeouts and lock settings - turn off all automatic screen dimming, extra screen dimming (often found in {menu Android Settings} > {menu Accessibility} > {menu Extra dim}) and increase timeouts for screen locking.
=Thunderbird settings=
==Use the Thunderbird theme that''s best for you==
* Use the theme that is best for you in terms of visual comfort and perceived contrast. For some, that is the Light theme, for others, the Dark theme is better.
# Tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]} at the bottom left.
# Tap {button General settings} > tap {menu Display}. Tap whatever works best: {button Dark} or {button Light} or {button Use system default} under ''''''Theme''''''.
=Third Party documentation=
== Google Android settings==
* On some Android devices, using Android''s Dark or Light theme might be helpful (in addtion to using the Thunderbird theme that suits you). See Google''s documentation: [https://support.google.com/accessibility/android/answer/6151800 Turn on Dark theme & color inversion].
* How to turn off ''''''Extra dim'''''' can be found in Google''s article: [https://support.google.com/accessibility/android/answer/11183305#colorcorrection&zippy=%2Cuse-color-correction%2Cdim-your-screen Change text & display settings].
* For more information on the features for low-vision assistance, visit the [https://support.google.com/accessibility/android Android Accessibility Help Centre].
[[UI:details_start]]
==Google Pixel settings==
Pixel phones have their own variant of Android. Please refer to the official documentation: [https://support.google.com/pixelphone/answer/6111557 Manage screen & display settings on a Pixel phone]
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Samsung phones and tablets==
Samsung has specific documentation for all of their Android devices (both non Galaxy and Galaxy):
* [https://www.samsung.com/us/support/answer/ANS10003431/ Stop the screen brightness from dimming on your Galaxy phone or tablet].
* Follow the instructions in Samsung''s guide: [https://www.samsung.com/us/support/troubleshoot/TSG10001443/ Unable to turn on Eye comfort shield on Samsung phone or tablet] in reverse. In other words, ''''''disable'''''' the Eye comfort shield instead of enabling it: turn on Color inversion if needed, turn on the Color filter and enable Color adjustment or Color correction.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Third Party links disclaimer==
{note}Please note that following the external links to Samsung and Google''s websites in the previous sections will direct you to an external website that is not operated by Mozilla. These links can  break at any time without notice.{/note}
[[UI:details_end]]','<p></p><div class="warning">More for Thunderbird for Android: <strong>o</strong> Vote on &amp; suggest improvements at <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/t5/ideas/idb-p/ideas/label-name/thunderbird%20android">Connect</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/participate/">Participate/Contribute</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird-android/form">Get Support</a></div>
<p></p><p>Android''s settings and automatic screen dimming can make it difficult to read the screen due to low contrast. Here''s how to reduce screen dimming and make Thunderbird more readable.
</p>
