<p></p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "314170", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 19, 2025, 8:46:03 PM", "datetime": "2025-11-19T20:46:03.222180-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "SMTP issues for enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/30", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 19, 2025, 8:48:03 PM", "datetime": "2025-11-19T20:48:03.599279-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:24:21+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (315283,'en-US','thunderbird-and-yahoo','OAuth2 Authentication for Yahoo, AOL and ATT','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-yahoo','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-yahoo/revision/315283',206227,'2025-12-05T13:02:19.325227-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','change 147 to 148, discussion please: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/117',1,'2025-12-05T13:03:04.792017-08:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{warning}Starting with version 148, Thunderbird is switching to a more secure authorization protocol PKCE for Yahoo, AT&T and AOL accounts, so you may have seen an alert "Upcoming Account Change". ''''''You don''t need to change anything. But when Thunderbird first fetches your email, as you have in the past, simply provide your email account and password, and then confirm that Thunderbird may access data on the provider''s servers.'''''' This is a normal and safe process for accessing existing emails, with no lost data. A major benefit is Thunderbird can now access Yahoo/AOL calendars and address books, which wasn’t possible before due to API restrictions. If you need more information see OAuth2 Authorization below.{/warning}

Mail providers that use Yahoo services such as AT&T and AOL have the same requirements as Yahoo, and so the authentication portion of this article applies to users of these mail providers.

__TOC__

= OAuth2 Authorization =

OAuth2 is the default for accessing Yahoo provided services (and providers like AOL and AT&T). The provider (not Thunderbird) presents you first with their dialog to confirm your account name and password, followed by their dialog to authorize which data types Thunderbird will be permitted to use. 

An access token sent from your provider (a string of characters, not your password) is then stored in Thunderbird''s password store to allow future access to the server. Tokens eventually expire, at which time you must again provide your account information with password. If you do not know your password, then you must use your provider''s account pages in a web browser to create a new password, often called a "password reset".

This authentication setting is automatically configured in Thunderbird Account Settings on first access for both sending and receiving email, and for accessing calendars and contacts in the respective settings of Thunderbird. OAuth2 also requires javascript and cookies, which are enabled by default in Thunderbird. 

For more information see ... <!--  information we are going to steal from https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202 -->

= Mail Settings =
== POP ==
Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN3217.html POP access settings and instructions for Yahoo Mail].

== IMAP ==
Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4075.html IMAP server settings for Yahoo Mail].

== My sign-in attempt was prevented ==
If you get the ''''''Username or password invalid'''''' warning and the ''''''Sign-in attempt prevented'''''' email when you try to connect your Yahoo Mail with Thunderbird, Yahoo has blocked Thunderbird from connecting because it''s a "less secure" non-Yahoo app. In Thunderbird below 68.5 you could enable an option to ''''''allow apps that use less secure sign-in'''''' in your Yahoo Account. See [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/sln27791.html this Yahoo support page] for instructions.<br>
Update: please read section ''Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice'' below.

In Thunderbird version 68.5 and up you can change the POP server to pop.mail.yahoo.com (port 995). 

== Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice ==
If you currently have a POP or IMAP mail account in Thunderbird and receive an email from Yahoo that says: <br>
<i>"We’ve noticed that you’re using non-Yahoo applications (such as third-party email,calendar, or contact applications) that may use a less secure sign-in method. To protect you and your data, Yahoo will no longer support the current sign-in functionality in your application starting on 20 October 2020. " </i>

''''''Check Thunderbird has cookies enabled.'''''' <br>
In versions prior to 78*:
#Select {menu ''Menu app icon'' > ''Options/Preferences'' > ''Options/Preferences''}
#In ''Options/Preferences'' left Pane select ''''''Privacy''''''
#Select checkbox ''Accept cookies from sites''

In versions 78* or later:
#Select {menu ''Menu app icon'' > ''Options/Preferences''}
#In ''Options/Preferences'' left Pane select ''''''Privacy & Security''''''
#Select checkbox ''Accept cookies from sites''

''''''Update the ''Authentication Method'' for the Pop or Imap mail account.''''''
#Select {menu ''Menu app icon'' > ''Account Settings''}
#Select mail account name in left Pane
#Bottom right: click on {button ''Edit SMTP server''}
#Set outgoing server (SMTP) ''Authentication Method'' to ''Oauth2''
#Click on {button ''OK''}
#Select ''Server Settings'' for mail account
#Set incoming server ''Authentication Method'' to ''Oauth2''
#Click on {button ''OK''}

Exit Thunderbird <br>
Wait a few moments for background process to complete <br>
Start Thunderbird

Then there will be a prompt from your Yahoo ISP partner to login. This is done with a OAuth 2.0 key exchange in Thunderbird which will authenticate the Thunderbird client in future logins.

= Import Yahoo!''s Contact List to Thunderbird =
# Open your Yahoo! Mail account.
# On the right side of the screen (left if you are using the "classic mail"), select the ''''''Contacts'''''' icon. Your contact list will open.
# At the top of the list, click the ''''''Actions'''''' menu.
# In the menu, select ''''''Export...''''''.
# Select ''''''Netscape/Thunderbird'''''' and click ''''''Export Now''''''.
# Save the {filepath yahoo_contacts.ldif} file to your desktop or some other place where you can find it later.
# Open Thunderbird.
# Under {menu Tools}, select {menu Import} and then {menu Address Books}.
# Select ''''''Text file (LDIF, .tab, .csv, .txt)'''''', then click {button Next}.
# Select the {filepath yahoo_contacts.ldif} file that you previously saved.
# Your Yahoo! contacts will be imported as a separate ''''''yahoo_contacts'''''' address book.

= Calendar =
To configure the Thunderbird calendar (Lightning) with your Yahoo! Calendar, follow the instructions at the page [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4704.html Sync Yahoo Calendar with Mozilla Lightning] on the Yahoo! support site.','<div class="warning">Starting with version 148, Thunderbird is switching to a more secure authorization protocol PKCE for Yahoo, AT&amp;T and AOL accounts, so you may have seen an alert "Upcoming Account Change". <strong>You don''t need to change anything. But when Thunderbird first fetches your email, as you have in the past, simply provide your email account and password, and then confirm that Thunderbird may access data on the provider''s servers.</strong> This is a normal and safe process for accessing existing emails, with no lost data. A major benefit is Thunderbird can now access Yahoo/AOL calendars and address books, which wasn’t possible before due to API restrictions. If you need more information see OAuth2 Authorization below.</div>
<p>Mail providers that use Yahoo services such as AT&amp;T and AOL have the same requirements as Yahoo, and so the authentication portion of this article applies to users of these mail providers.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_oauth2-authorization"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">OAuth2 Authorization</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_mail-settings"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Mail Settings</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_pop"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">POP</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_imap"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">IMAP</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_my-sign-in-attempt-was-prevented"><span class="tocnumber">2.3</span> <span class="toctext">My sign-in attempt was prevented</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_important-changes-to-authentication-method-notice"><span class="tocnumber">2.4</span> <span class="toctext">Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_import-yahoos-contact-list-to-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Import Yahoo!''s Contact List to Thunderbird</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_calendar"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Calendar</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_oauth2-authorization">OAuth2 Authorization</h1>
<p>OAuth2 is the default for accessing Yahoo provided services (and providers like AOL and AT&amp;T). The provider (not Thunderbird) presents you first with their dialog to confirm your account name and password, followed by their dialog to authorize which data types Thunderbird will be permitted to use. 
</p><p>An access token sent from your provider (a string of characters, not your password) is then stored in Thunderbird''s password store to allow future access to the server. Tokens eventually expire, at which time you must again provide your account information with password. If you do not know your password, then you must use your provider''s account pages in a web browser to create a new password, often called a "password reset".
</p><p>This authentication setting is automatically configured in Thunderbird Account Settings on first access for both sending and receiving email, and for accessing calendars and contacts in the respective settings of Thunderbird. OAuth2 also requires javascript and cookies, which are enabled by default in Thunderbird. 
</p><p>For more information see ... 
</p>
<h1 id="w_mail-settings">Mail Settings</h1>
<h2 id="w_pop">POP</h2>
<p>Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN3217.html">POP access settings and instructions for Yahoo Mail</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_imap">IMAP</h2>
<p>Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4075.html">IMAP server settings for Yahoo Mail</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_my-sign-in-attempt-was-prevented">My sign-in attempt was prevented</h2>
<p>If you get the <strong>Username or password invalid</strong> warning and the <strong>Sign-in attempt prevented</strong> email when you try to connect your Yahoo Mail with Thunderbird, Yahoo has blocked Thunderbird from connecting because it''s a "less secure" non-Yahoo app. In Thunderbird below 68.5 you could enable an option to <strong>allow apps that use less secure sign-in</strong> in your Yahoo Account. See <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/sln27791.html">this Yahoo support page</a> for instructions.<br>
Update: please read section ''Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice'' below.
</p><p>In Thunderbird version 68.5 and up you can change the POP server to <a href="http://pop.mail.yahoo.com">pop.mail.yahoo.com</a> (port 995). 
</p>
<h2 id="w_important-changes-to-authentication-method-notice">Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice</h2>
<p>If you currently have a POP or IMAP mail account in Thunderbird and receive an email from Yahoo that says: <br>
<i>"We’ve noticed that you’re using non-Yahoo applications (such as third-party email,calendar, or contact applications) that may use a less secure sign-in method. To protect you and your data, Yahoo will no longer support the current sign-in functionality in your application starting on 20 October 2020. " </i>
</p><p><strong>Check Thunderbird has cookies enabled.</strong> <br>
In versions prior to 78*:
</p>
<ol><li>Select <span class="menu">''Menu app icon'' &gt; ''Options/Preferences'' &gt; ''Options/Preferences''</span>
</li><li>In ''Options/Preferences'' left Pane select <strong>Privacy</strong>
</li><li>Select checkbox ''Accept cookies from sites''
</li></ol>
<p>In versions 78* or later:
</p>
<ol><li>Select <span class="menu">''Menu app icon'' &gt; ''Options/Preferences''</span>
</li><li>In ''Options/Preferences'' left Pane select <strong>Privacy &amp; Security</strong>
</li><li>Select checkbox ''Accept cookies from sites''
</li></ol>
<p><strong>Update the ''Authentication Method'' for the Pop or Imap mail account.</strong>
</p>
<ol><li>Select <span class="menu">''Menu app icon'' &gt; ''Account Settings''</span>
</li><li>Select mail account name in left Pane
</li><li>Bottom right: click on <span class="button">''Edit SMTP server''</span>
</li><li>Set outgoing server (SMTP) ''Authentication Method'' to ''Oauth2''
</li><li>Click on <span class="button">''OK''</span>
</li><li>Select ''Server Settings'' for mail account
</li><li>Set incoming server ''Authentication Method'' to ''Oauth2''
</li><li>Click on <span class="button">''OK''</span>
</li></ol>
<p>Exit Thunderbird <br>
Wait a few moments for background process to complete <br>
Start Thunderbird
</p><p>Then there will be a prompt from your Yahoo ISP partner to login. This is done with a OAuth 2.0 key exchange in Thunderbird which will authenticate the Thunderbird client in future logins.
</p>
<h1 id="w_import-yahoos-contact-list-to-thunderbird">Import Yahoo!''s Contact List to Thunderbird</h1>
<ol><li> Open your Yahoo! Mail account.
</li><li> On the right side of the screen (left if you are using the "classic mail"), select the <strong>Contacts</strong> icon. Your contact list will open.
</li><li> At the top of the list, click the <strong>Actions</strong> menu.
</li><li> In the menu, select <strong>Export...</strong>.
</li><li> Select <strong>Netscape/Thunderbird</strong> and click <strong>Export Now</strong>.
</li><li> Save the <span class="filepath">yahoo_contacts.ldif</span> file to your desktop or some other place where you can find it later.
</li><li> Open Thunderbird.
</li><li> Under <span class="menu">Tools</span>, select <span class="menu">Import</span> and then <span class="menu">Address Books</span>.
</li><li> Select <strong>Text file (LDIF, .tab, .csv, .txt)</strong>, then click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> Select the <span class="filepath">yahoo_contacts.ldif</span> file that you previously saved.
</li><li> Your Yahoo! contacts will be imported as a separate <strong>yahoo_contacts</strong> address book.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_calendar">Calendar</h1>
<p>To configure the Thunderbird calendar (Lightning) with your Yahoo! Calendar, follow the instructions at the page <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4704.html">Sync Yahoo Calendar with Mozilla Lightning</a> on the Yahoo! support site.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "315283", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Dec 5, 2025, 1:02:19 PM", "datetime": "2025-12-05T13:02:19.325227-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "change 147 to 148, discussion please: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/117", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Dec 5, 2025, 1:03:04 PM", "datetime": "2025-12-05T13:03:04.792017-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:24:19+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (315386,'en-US','thunderbird-and-yahoo','OAuth2 Authentication for Yahoo, AOL and ATT','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-yahoo','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-yahoo/revision/315386',315283,'2025-12-08T07:52:20.780146-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fixed sumo kb formatting, removed old material, discussion please: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/117',1,'2025-12-08T07:52:37.445575-08:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{warning}Starting with version 148, Thunderbird is switching to a more secure authorization protocol PKCE for Yahoo, AT&T and AOL accounts, so you may have seen an alert "Upcoming Account Change". ''''''You don''t need to change anything. But when Thunderbird first fetches your email, as you have in the past, simply provide your email account and password, and then confirm that Thunderbird may access data on the provider''s servers.'''''' This is a normal and safe process for accessing existing emails, with no lost data. A major benefit is Thunderbird can now access Yahoo/AOL calendars and address books, which wasn’t possible before due to API restrictions. If you need more information see OAuth2 Authorization below.{/warning}

Mail providers that use Yahoo services such as AT&T and AOL have the same requirements as Yahoo, and so the authentication portion of this article applies to users of these mail providers.

__TOC__

= OAuth2 Authorization =

OAuth2 is the default for accessing Yahoo provided services (and providers like AOL and AT&T). The provider (not Thunderbird) presents you first with their dialog to confirm your account name and password, followed by their dialog to authorize which data types Thunderbird will be permitted to use. 

An access token sent from your provider (a string of characters, not your password) is then stored in Thunderbird''s password store to allow future access to the server. Tokens eventually expire, at which time you must again provide your account information with password. If you do not know your password, then you must use your provider''s account pages in a web browser to create a new password, often called a "password reset".

This authentication setting is automatically configured in Thunderbird Account Settings on first access for both sending and receiving email, and for accessing calendars and contacts in the respective settings of Thunderbird. OAuth2 also requires javascript and cookies, which are enabled by default in Thunderbird. 

For more information see ... <!--  information we are going to steal from https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202 -->

= Mail Settings =
== POP ==
Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN3217.html POP access settings and instructions for Yahoo Mail].

== IMAP ==
Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4075.html IMAP server settings for Yahoo Mail].

== My sign-in attempt was prevented ==
If you get the ''''''Username or password invalid'''''' warning and the ''''''Sign-in attempt prevented'''''' email when you try to connect your Yahoo Mail with Thunderbird, Yahoo has blocked Thunderbird from connecting because it''s a "less secure" non-Yahoo app. In Thunderbird below 68.5 you could enable an option to ''''''allow apps that use less secure sign-in'''''' in your Yahoo Account. See [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/sln27791.html this Yahoo support page] for instructions.<br>
Update: please read section ''Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice'' below.

In Thunderbird version 68.5 and up you can change the POP server to pop.mail.yahoo.com (port 995). 

== Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice ==
If you currently have a POP or IMAP mail account in Thunderbird and receive an email from Yahoo that says: <br>
<i>"We’ve noticed that you’re using non-Yahoo applications (such as third-party email,calendar, or contact applications) that may use a less secure sign-in method. To protect you and your data, Yahoo will no longer support the current sign-in functionality in your application starting on 20 October 2020. " </i>

''''''Check Thunderbird has cookies enabled.'''''' <br>

#Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Privacy & Security}. 
#Tick checkbox {button Accept cookies from sites}.

''''''Update the ''Authentication Method'' for the POP3 or IMAP mail account.''''''
#Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings}
#Click on the mail account name in the left sidebar for example ''''nemo@thunderbird.net''''.
#Bottom right: click on {button Edit outgoing server...}.
#Set ''''''Authentication Method'''''' to {menu OAuth2}.
#Click  {button OK}.
#Click on {menu Server Settings} in the left sidebar for your  mail account.
#Set ''''''Authentication Method'''''' to {menu OAuth2}.
#Click on {button OK}.

Exit Thunderbird .<br>
Wait a few moments for background process to complete. <br>
Start Thunderbird.

Then there will be a prompt from your Yahoo ISP partner to login. This is done with a OAuth 2.0 key exchange in Thunderbird which will authenticate the Thunderbird client in future logins.

= Import Yahoo!''s Contact List to Thunderbird =
# Open your Yahoo! Mail account.
# On the right side of the screen (left if you are using the "classic mail"), select the ''''''Contacts'''''' icon. Your contact list will open.
# At the top of the list, click the ''''''Actions'''''' menu.
# In the menu, select ''''''Export...''''''.
# Select ''''''Netscape/Thunderbird'''''' and click ''''''Export Now''''''.
# Save the {filepath yahoo_contacts.ldif} file to your desktop or some other place where you can find it later.
# Open Thunderbird.
# Under {menu Tools}, select {menu Import} and then {menu Address Books}.
# Select ''''''Text file (LDIF, .tab, .csv, .txt)'''''', then click {button Next}.
# Select the {filepath yahoo_contacts.ldif} file that you previously saved.
# Your Yahoo! contacts will be imported as a separate ''''''yahoo_contacts'''''' address book.

= Calendar =
To configure the Thunderbird calendar (Lightning) with your Yahoo! Calendar, follow the instructions at the page [https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4704.html Sync Yahoo Calendar with Mozilla Lightning] on the Yahoo! support site.','<div class="warning">Starting with version 148, Thunderbird is switching to a more secure authorization protocol PKCE for Yahoo, AT&amp;T and AOL accounts, so you may have seen an alert "Upcoming Account Change". <strong>You don''t need to change anything. But when Thunderbird first fetches your email, as you have in the past, simply provide your email account and password, and then confirm that Thunderbird may access data on the provider''s servers.</strong> This is a normal and safe process for accessing existing emails, with no lost data. A major benefit is Thunderbird can now access Yahoo/AOL calendars and address books, which wasn’t possible before due to API restrictions. If you need more information see OAuth2 Authorization below.</div>
<p>Mail providers that use Yahoo services such as AT&amp;T and AOL have the same requirements as Yahoo, and so the authentication portion of this article applies to users of these mail providers.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_oauth2-authorization"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">OAuth2 Authorization</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_mail-settings"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Mail Settings</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_pop"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">POP</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_imap"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">IMAP</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_my-sign-in-attempt-was-prevented"><span class="tocnumber">2.3</span> <span class="toctext">My sign-in attempt was prevented</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_important-changes-to-authentication-method-notice"><span class="tocnumber">2.4</span> <span class="toctext">Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_import-yahoos-contact-list-to-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Import Yahoo!''s Contact List to Thunderbird</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_calendar"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Calendar</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_oauth2-authorization">OAuth2 Authorization</h1>
<p>OAuth2 is the default for accessing Yahoo provided services (and providers like AOL and AT&amp;T). The provider (not Thunderbird) presents you first with their dialog to confirm your account name and password, followed by their dialog to authorize which data types Thunderbird will be permitted to use. 
</p><p>An access token sent from your provider (a string of characters, not your password) is then stored in Thunderbird''s password store to allow future access to the server. Tokens eventually expire, at which time you must again provide your account information with password. If you do not know your password, then you must use your provider''s account pages in a web browser to create a new password, often called a "password reset".
</p><p>This authentication setting is automatically configured in Thunderbird Account Settings on first access for both sending and receiving email, and for accessing calendars and contacts in the respective settings of Thunderbird. OAuth2 also requires javascript and cookies, which are enabled by default in Thunderbird. 
</p><p>For more information see ... 
</p>
<h1 id="w_mail-settings">Mail Settings</h1>
<h2 id="w_pop">POP</h2>
<p>Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN3217.html">POP access settings and instructions for Yahoo Mail</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_imap">IMAP</h2>
<p>Refer to Yahoo!''s help page for <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4075.html">IMAP server settings for Yahoo Mail</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_my-sign-in-attempt-was-prevented">My sign-in attempt was prevented</h2>
<p>If you get the <strong>Username or password invalid</strong> warning and the <strong>Sign-in attempt prevented</strong> email when you try to connect your Yahoo Mail with Thunderbird, Yahoo has blocked Thunderbird from connecting because it''s a "less secure" non-Yahoo app. In Thunderbird below 68.5 you could enable an option to <strong>allow apps that use less secure sign-in</strong> in your Yahoo Account. See <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/sln27791.html">this Yahoo support page</a> for instructions.<br>
Update: please read section ''Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice'' below.
</p><p>In Thunderbird version 68.5 and up you can change the POP server to <a href="http://pop.mail.yahoo.com">pop.mail.yahoo.com</a> (port 995). 
</p>
<h2 id="w_important-changes-to-authentication-method-notice">Important Changes to Authentication Method Notice</h2>
<p>If you currently have a POP or IMAP mail account in Thunderbird and receive an email from Yahoo that says: <br>
<i>"We’ve noticed that you’re using non-Yahoo applications (such as third-party email,calendar, or contact applications) that may use a less secure sign-in method. To protect you and your data, Yahoo will no longer support the current sign-in functionality in your application starting on 20 October 2020. " </i>
</p><p><strong>Check Thunderbird has cookies enabled.</strong> <br>
</p>
<ol><li>Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span>. 
</li><li>Tick checkbox <span class="button">Accept cookies from sites</span>.
</li></ol>
<p><strong>Update the ''Authentication Method'' for the POP3 or IMAP mail account.</strong>
</p>
<ol><li>Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span>
</li><li>Click on the mail account name in the left sidebar for example <em><a href="mailto:nemo@thunderbird.net">nemo@thunderbird.net</a></em>.
</li><li>Bottom right: click on <span class="button">Edit outgoing server...</span>.
</li><li>Set <strong>Authentication Method</strong> to <span class="menu">OAuth2</span>.
</li><li>Click  <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li><li>Click on <span class="menu">Server Settings</span> in the left sidebar for your  mail account.
</li><li>Set <strong>Authentication Method</strong> to <span class="menu">OAuth2</span>.
</li><li>Click on <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Exit Thunderbird .<br>
Wait a few moments for background process to complete. <br>
Start Thunderbird.
</p><p>Then there will be a prompt from your Yahoo ISP partner to login. This is done with a OAuth 2.0 key exchange in Thunderbird which will authenticate the Thunderbird client in future logins.
</p>
<h1 id="w_import-yahoos-contact-list-to-thunderbird">Import Yahoo!''s Contact List to Thunderbird</h1>
<ol><li> Open your Yahoo! Mail account.
</li><li> On the right side of the screen (left if you are using the "classic mail"), select the <strong>Contacts</strong> icon. Your contact list will open.
</li><li> At the top of the list, click the <strong>Actions</strong> menu.
</li><li> In the menu, select <strong>Export...</strong>.
</li><li> Select <strong>Netscape/Thunderbird</strong> and click <strong>Export Now</strong>.
</li><li> Save the <span class="filepath">yahoo_contacts.ldif</span> file to your desktop or some other place where you can find it later.
</li><li> Open Thunderbird.
</li><li> Under <span class="menu">Tools</span>, select <span class="menu">Import</span> and then <span class="menu">Address Books</span>.
</li><li> Select <strong>Text file (LDIF, .tab, .csv, .txt)</strong>, then click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> Select the <span class="filepath">yahoo_contacts.ldif</span> file that you previously saved.
</li><li> Your Yahoo! contacts will be imported as a separate <strong>yahoo_contacts</strong> address book.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_calendar">Calendar</h1>
<p>To configure the Thunderbird calendar (Lightning) with your Yahoo! Calendar, follow the instructions at the page <a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4704.html">Sync Yahoo Calendar with Mozilla Lightning</a> on the Yahoo! support site.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "315386", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Dec 8, 2025, 7:52:20 AM", "datetime": "2025-12-08T07:52:20.780146-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fixed sumo kb formatting, removed old material, discussion please: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/117", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Dec 8, 2025, 7:52:37 AM", "datetime": "2025-12-08T07:52:37.445575-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:24:17+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (315926,'en-US','new-thunderbird-desktop','New in Thunderbird Desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop/revision/315926',313667,'2025-12-12T10:56:15.334516-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP do not approve or localize :-) please Discussion:https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/115#issuecomment-3647749575',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','[[T:TBMore]]

This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in [[Thunderbird Release|Thunderbird Release for Desktop]]. For full details, please refer to [[Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues]].

__TOC__
=Thunderbird 146=
* Drag and Drop for attachments on macOS is working again.
* You can now add your own OpenPGP Keyserver.

[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 145=
==DNS over HTTPS==
* Thunderbird now supports DNS over HTTPS.
==Account Hub supports manual configuration for EWS, IMAP and POP3 accounts==
* In the ''''''Account Hub'''''', once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking {menu MANUAL CONFIGURATION}. 
*;[[Image:tb145-account-hub-manual-configuration]]
* ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' in the ''''''Account Hub'''''' supports manual configuration of {menu EWS}, {menu IMAP} and {menu POP3} accounts.
*;[[Image:tb145-ews-imap-pop-account-hub-manual-configuration]]
* Manual configuration of EWS accounts is now supported as per the screenshots in the above two bullet points. For more details, please see the [[Thunderbird and Exchange#w_manual-ews-configuration|Manual EWS configuration section of our Thunderbird and Exchange article]].
=Thunderbird 144=
==Exchange enhancements and bug fixes==
* Another bug fix release.
* Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions. We are planning a promotional splash in one of the next few releases. Getting closer!
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 143=
==Exchange Support enhancements and bug fixes==
* Bug fixes.
* Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 142=
==Switch to the old Account Setup flow to fix issues with Hotmail and outlook.com accounts==
If you see: ''''''Exchange auth error'''''' or ''''''Authentication failed. Please check the username and password'''''' when adding a ''''''Hotmail'''''' or ''''''outlook.com'''''' account, here''s how to switch to the old ''''''Account Setup'''''' flow to fix the problem:
* Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings} > {menu General} and untick ''''''Create accounts in the new Account Hub'''''':
*;[[Image:tb142-disable-account-hub|width=350]]
* Please try adding the email account again. If you still have issues, please [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form file a support request on SUMO].
This will be fixed in a future Thunderbird release.
==Reset manual folder sorting order==
# Right click ({key Control}+click on macOS) on the account row, e.g. ''''''nemo@thunderbird.net'''''' in the ''''''Folder Pane'''''' (more details: [[Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)]] and then:
# Click the menu item {menu Reset Folder Order} to clear any previously defined manual sorting. ''''''NOTE'''''': This feature doesn’t restore the original location of a folder that was moved inside another parent folder which will be fixed in a future release. You can follow along in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979880 bug 1979880].
#;[[Image:tb142-reset-folder-order|width=350]]
==Add a visual signature to a PDF==
Just like Firefox, you can now insert your signature into a PDF by by typing, drawing or adding an image. Please refer to the Firefox article for full details: [[View and edit PDF files using Firefox’s built-in viewer#w_add-a-signature-to-a-pdf]].
==Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release==
See the 141 section for more details.
==More stable Exchange support==
This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 141=
==Archive action added to new email notifications ==
* You can now {button Archive} email from your operating system''s new email native notifications (in addition to {button Mark as Read}, {button Delete}, {button Mark as Starred} and {button Mark as Spam}. Please see [[New email notifications in Thunderbird]] for further information.
==Better Exchange support==
Now, anyone can try the Exchange support in Thunderbird. For most, we recommend waiting for a future release which will be more stable and have additional features like on premise OAuth authentication(password authentication for on premise is supported in this release).
This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
Please refer to our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
== 31 day warning for PGP Key expiry ==
If your PGP key expires in the next 31 days, you will receive a warning when you are composing an email. For extra security, you can choose to let it expire or you can extend your key''s certificate.

More info on PGP encrypted email can be found in [[Introduction to End-to-end encryption in Thunderbird]] and [[OpenPGP in Thunderbird - HOWTO and FAQ]].
== Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release ==
This is primarily a problem in the English language locales. It was discovered a few days after the release of 141. You can follow the progress of the fix in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979511 bug 1979511].
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 140 / ESR 140 Eclipse=
==Dark Message Mode==
Thunderbird now automatically displays your messages in dark mode when enabled in your operating system settings. If there are display issues with a message in dark mode, you can disable dark mode for that message by sliding the toggle to the right to disable dark message mode.
;[[Image:tb140-dark-mode-toggle]]
==New Appearance Panel in Settings==
The new Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu Appearance}, allows you to toggle your message list layout between Card and Table view, adjust your Cards view, and set your default folder sorting order and threading options. For more details, please refer to [[Appearance Panel - Layout, Message List, Card View, Table View, Sorting and Threading settings]].
==Native OS Notifications==
Thunderbird now uses your operating system''s built-in notifications, whether you’re on Windows, Linux or Mac. You can now quickly delete, archive, or use customizable actions directly from your notifications.
==Redesigned Account hub==
The newly redesigned Account hub makes it easier to add an account. Connect all of your emails, address books and calendars in a few easy steps. The Account hub is currently not enabled for your first account (you will see it when you add a second account). It is also not enabled by default for your address book. You can change the defaults in your Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu General} > search for ''''''Account hub'''''' (not enabled in ESR, we recommend the latest monthly release for the best experience).
==Manual Folder Sorting==
Drag and drop your folders to get the order you want.
==Experimental Exchange Support for advanced users==
Preliminary Microsoft Exchange support is available for you to try. Our current, initial implementation of Exchange is limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
We recommend you try this in a test [[Use multiple profiles in Thunderbird|Thunderbird profile]] in [[Thunderbird Beta]] (please ensure you back up your profile before starting). Please refer to our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
==Export for Mobile==
Generate a QR code to quickly transfer your account settings and credentials to your Thunderbird for Android app. Please refer to [[Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android]].
==Horizontal Scroll for Table View==
Table view now supports horizontal scrolling. This allows you to view wide messages – for example, data in a table with many columns.
[[UI:details_end]]
=Older Releases=
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 139==
* You can now manually sort folders by dragging and dropping them in the folder pane.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 138==
* Dark mode and high contrast mode improvements.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 137==
* Fixed Two-factor authentication via text or email for Office 365 using Oauth2.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 136==
* New Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu Appearance}.
[[UI:details_end]]','<p></p><div class="warning">More for Thunderbird: <strong>o</strong> Vote on &amp; suggest improvements at <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/t5/ideas/idb-p/ideas/label-name/thunderbird">Connect</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/participate/">Participate/Contribute</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">Get Support</a></div>
<p></p><p>This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-release">Thunderbird Release for Desktop</a>. For full details, please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-release-notes-whats-new-and-known-issu">Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues</a>.
</p>
