INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (326801,'en-US','channel-choices-thunderbird-snap-and-flatpak','Channel Choices for Thunderbird Snap and Flatpak','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/channel-choices-thunderbird-snap-and-flatpak','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/channel-choices-thunderbird-snap-and-flatpak/revision/326801',326712,'2026-04-23T08:53:11.190207-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','nits :-), discussion at: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/126pls do not approve, needs 1 or more of Wayne and Heather''s approval',1,'2026-04-23T09:10:06.889556-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','This article describes Thunderbird channels available for Snap and Flatpak, changes that make it easier to choose your preferred channel, and provides instructions on how to install a channel build or move to a channel that is different from what you currently have installed.

If you received an announcement in Thunderbird pointing to this article, the purpose is to provide you with information about what is changing and how the changes may affect you.

=Thunderbird channels=
<!-- based on https://support.mozilla.org/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux#w_thunderbird-channels -->
Thunderbird offers four channels: ''''''Release'''''' (also known as Monthly), ''''''ESR'''''' (annual major releases), ''''''Beta'''''', and ''''''Daily''''''. [[How to select a Thunderbird release channel]] has a full comparison of all four channels. [[Thunderbird ESR]], [[Thunderbird Release]], [[Thunderbird Beta]], [[Thunderbird Daily]] describe each channel in more detail. If you are using Flatpak or Snap, please continue reading this article. Otherwise, please refer to the article [[Install Thunderbird on Linux]].

=Flatpak Beta changes in 2026=
As of March 30, 2026, the application ID for the Beta channel at Flathub has changed from ''''org.mozilla.''''''T''''''hunderbird'''' to a new ''''org.mozilla.thunderbird'''' (all lower case). And you may have seen a notification in Thunderbird that “We''re switching the flatpak beta application ID from org.mozilla.''''''T''''''hunderbird to org.mozilla.thunderbird”.

As a result:
* https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.Thunderbird is changing to https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.thunderbird.
* If you have been using Thunderbird Beta prior to March 30, 2026:
** When you update, accounts and add-ons should not be affected.
** Run <code>flatpak update org.mozilla.Thunderbird</code> as normal. Flatpak will ask whether you want to change to the new application ID, because Thunderbird release management has provided flatpak with the rebase information needed to move you to the new application ID.
*** If you respond ''''''Yes'''''', no further action is needed. The application ID will have been updated and your future updates should be run with all lower case<br> <code>flatpak update org.mozilla.thunderbird</code>.
*** If you respond ''''''No'''''', then you will remain on Thunderbird 150.0b1 because the old ''''org.mozilla.Thunderbird'''' app ID will no longer receive updates. You will be prompted to update your Thunderbird app ID by future flatpak updates because this is a security risk to you.
* If you are installing Thunderbird Beta as a new user, there is no special action. Just be aware that you want to use the new application ID of ''''''org.mozilla.thunderbird''''''. For installation instructions please refer to [[Install Thunderbird on Linux]] and [https://discourse.flathub.org/t/how-to-use-flathub-beta/2111 Flathub instructions].

=Snap channel changes in 2026=
To meet user requests and better align with our other distribution methods, the default for snap will be changing to Thunderbird release channel. This transition will be gradual in steps over time, with snap ''''''latest/stable'''''' becoming Thunderbird release channel (it is currently Thunderbird ESR), and a new snap channel ''''''esr/stable'''''' becoming avalable for ESR for users who cannot or do not want to be using Thunderbird release channel. 

We are announcing and documenting this early so that users can transition when they are ready, with the last possible step happening in with Thunderbird version 153 in summer of 2026. The  documentation below provides information on how to decide what is best for you, and how to make any necessary changes.

As of April 2026 the available snap channels are:
* ''''''latest/stable'''''' (default install) - tracks the latest ''''''ESR'''''' release of Thunderbird
* ''''''monthly/stable'''''' - tracks the latest monthly release of Thunderbird
* ''''''esr/stable'''''' - a ''''''new channel'''''' which tracks the latest ESR release of Thunderbird

In late July 2026, when Thunderbird release and Thunderbird ESR both become version 153, the available snap channels will become:
* ''''''latest/stable ''''''(default install) - ''''''changes'''''' to track the latest monthly release of Thunderbird
* ''''''esr/stable'''''' - a new channel which tracks the latest ESR release of Thunderbird
* ''''''monthly/stable'''''' - will go away

What does this mean for you?

If you are currently using Thunderbird ESR, we advise that you start today to determine whether to stay with ESR channel or switch to release channel. We believe the latest monthly release is the best version for general users, because the monthly releases get more frequent fixes than ESRs and thereby deliver a more robust version of Thunderbird. See [[How to select a Thunderbird release channel]] for broad descriptions of the available Thunderbird channels. And if you have extensions installed at Thunderbird App menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add-ons and Themes} > {menu Extensions}, please install   https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/addon-compatibility-check/ to see whether any of your extensions are experiments based. If there are any and you are thinking to change to the release channel, you must decide whether you can operate without those extensions. If you cannot, then we recommend you stay with the ESR channel.

If you are currently using monthly, no changes are needed now. But in July, there is an opportunity for you switch to ESR, when for four weeks Thunderbird ESR and Thunderbird release will both be version 153. You may want to do this so that you can take advantage of experiments based add-ons that break when you are using the release channel.

<!-- Not sure where to use this ... Thank you for being willing to transition to the monthly releases! -->

==If you are on latest/stable, Thunderbird ESR, and want to change to monthly channel==

''''''Action needed from you:'''''' None

==If you are on latest/stable and need to stay with ESR==
To continue using ESR, you must change your Thunderbird snap install to only get updates from the ESR channel.

''''''Action needed from you:'''''' In a terminal, issue the command:<br />
<code>sudo snap refresh thunderbird --channel=esr/stable</code>

==If you are on esr/stable and want to stay with ESR==
You are already where you need to be.

''''''Action needed from you:'''''' None

==If you are on monthly/stable==
The monthly/stable channel will be going away because the monthly release will be in the latest/stable channel. So you will need to switch the channel that your Thunderbird snap is tracking.

''''''Action needed from you:'''''' In a terminal, issue the command::<br />
<code>sudo snap refresh thunderbird --channel=latest/stable</code>','<p>This article describes Thunderbird channels available for Snap and Flatpak, changes that make it easier to choose your preferred channel, and provides instructions on how to install a channel build or move to a channel that is different from what you currently have installed.
</p><p>If you received an announcement in Thunderbird pointing to this article, the purpose is to provide you with information about what is changing and how the changes may affect you.
</p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-channels">Thunderbird channels</h1>
<p>
Thunderbird offers four channels: <strong>Release</strong> (also known as Monthly), <strong>ESR</strong> (annual major releases), <strong>Beta</strong>, and <strong>Daily</strong>. <a href="/en-US/kb/choosing-thunderbird-release-channel">How to select a Thunderbird release channel</a> has a full comparison of all four channels. <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-esr">Thunderbird ESR</a>, <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-release">Thunderbird Release</a>, <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-beta">Thunderbird Beta</a>, <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-daily">Thunderbird Daily</a> describe each channel in more detail. If you are using Flatpak or Snap, please continue reading this article. Otherwise, please refer to the article <a href="/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux">Install Thunderbird on Linux</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_flatpak-beta-changes-in-2026">Flatpak Beta changes in 2026</h1>
<p>As of March 30, 2026, the application ID for the Beta channel at Flathub has changed from <em>org.mozilla.<strong>T</strong>hunderbird</em> to a new <em>org.mozilla.thunderbird</em> (all lower case). And you may have seen a notification in Thunderbird that “We''re switching the flatpak beta application ID from org.mozilla.<strong>T</strong>hunderbird to org.mozilla.thunderbird”.
</p><p>As a result:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.Thunderbird">https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.Thunderbird</a> is changing to <a href="https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.thunderbird">https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.thunderbird</a>.
</li><li> If you have been using Thunderbird Beta prior to March 30, 2026:
<ul><li> When you update, accounts and add-ons should not be affected.
</li><li> Run <code>flatpak update org.mozilla.Thunderbird</code> as normal. Flatpak will ask whether you want to change to the new application ID, because Thunderbird release management has provided flatpak with the rebase information needed to move you to the new application ID.
<ul><li> If you respond <strong>Yes</strong>, no further action is needed. The application ID will have been updated and your future updates should be run with all lower case<br> <code>flatpak update org.mozilla.thunderbird</code>.
</li><li> If you respond <strong>No</strong>, then you will remain on Thunderbird 150.0b1 because the old <em>org.mozilla.Thunderbird</em> app ID will no longer receive updates. You will be prompted to update your Thunderbird app ID by future flatpak updates because this is a security risk to you.
</li></ul>
</li></ul>
</li><li> If you are installing Thunderbird Beta as a new user, there is no special action. Just be aware that you want to use the new application ID of <strong>org.mozilla.thunderbird</strong>. For installation instructions please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux">Install Thunderbird on Linux</a> and <a href="https://discourse.flathub.org/t/how-to-use-flathub-beta/2111">Flathub instructions</a>.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_snap-channel-changes-in-2026">Snap channel changes in 2026</h1>
<p>To meet user requests and better align with our other distribution methods, the default for snap will be changing to Thunderbird release channel. This transition will be gradual in steps over time, with snap <strong>latest/stable</strong> becoming Thunderbird release channel (it is currently Thunderbird ESR), and a new snap channel <strong>esr/stable</strong> becoming avalable for ESR for users who cannot or do not want to be using Thunderbird release channel. 
</p><p>We are announcing and documenting this early so that users can transition when they are ready, with the last possible step happening in with Thunderbird version 153 in summer of 2026. The  documentation below provides information on how to decide what is best for you, and how to make any necessary changes.
</p><p>As of April 2026 the available snap channels are:
</p>
<ul><li> <strong>latest/stable</strong> (default install) - tracks the latest <strong>ESR</strong> release of Thunderbird
</li><li> <strong>monthly/stable</strong> - tracks the latest monthly release of Thunderbird
</li><li> <strong>esr/stable</strong> - a <strong>new channel</strong> which tracks the latest ESR release of Thunderbird
</li></ul>
<p>In late July 2026, when Thunderbird release and Thunderbird ESR both become version 153, the available snap channels will become:
</p>
<ul><li> <strong>latest/stable </strong>(default install) - <strong>changes</strong> to track the latest monthly release of Thunderbird
</li><li> <strong>esr/stable</strong> - a new channel which tracks the latest ESR release of Thunderbird
</li><li> <strong>monthly/stable</strong> - will go away
</li></ul>
<p>What does this mean for you?
</p><p>If you are currently using Thunderbird ESR, we advise that you start today to determine whether to stay with ESR channel or switch to release channel. We believe the latest monthly release is the best version for general users, because the monthly releases get more frequent fixes than ESRs and thereby deliver a more robust version of Thunderbird. See <a href="/en-US/kb/choosing-thunderbird-release-channel">How to select a Thunderbird release channel</a> for broad descriptions of the available Thunderbird channels. And if you have extensions installed at Thunderbird App menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add-ons and Themes</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Extensions</span>, please install   <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/addon-compatibility-check/">https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/addon-compatibility-check/</a> to see whether any of your extensions are experiments based. If there are any and you are thinking to change to the release channel, you must decide whether you can operate without those extensions. If you cannot, then we recommend you stay with the ESR channel.
</p><p>If you are currently using monthly, no changes are needed now. But in July, there is an opportunity for you switch to ESR, when for four weeks Thunderbird ESR and Thunderbird release will both be version 153. You may want to do this so that you can take advantage of experiments based add-ons that break when you are using the release channel.
</p><p>
</p>
<h2 id="w_if-you-are-on-lateststable-thunderbird-esr-and-want-to-change-to-monthly-channel">If you are on latest/stable, Thunderbird ESR, and want to change to monthly channel</h2>
<p><strong>Action needed from you:</strong> None
</p>
<h2 id="w_if-you-are-on-lateststable-and-need-to-stay-with-esr">If you are on latest/stable and need to stay with ESR</h2>
<p>To continue using ESR, you must change your Thunderbird snap install to only get updates from the ESR channel.
</p><p><strong>Action needed from you:</strong> In a terminal, issue the command:<br>
<code>sudo snap refresh thunderbird --channel=esr/stable</code>
</p>
<h2 id="w_if-you-are-on-esrstable-and-want-to-stay-with-esr">If you are on esr/stable and want to stay with ESR</h2>
<p>You are already where you need to be.
</p><p><strong>Action needed from you:</strong> None
</p>
<h2 id="w_if-you-are-on-monthlystable">If you are on monthly/stable</h2>
<p>The monthly/stable channel will be going away because the monthly release will be in the latest/stable channel. So you will need to switch the channel that your Thunderbird snap is tracking.
</p><p><strong>Action needed from you:</strong> In a terminal, issue the command::<br>
<code>sudo snap refresh thunderbird --channel=latest/stable</code>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "326801", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Apr 23, 2026, 8:53:11 AM", "datetime": "2026-04-23T08:53:11.190207-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "nits :-), discussion at: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/126pls do not approve, needs 1 or more of Wayne and Heather''s approval", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Apr 23, 2026, 9:10:06 AM", "datetime": "2026-04-23T09:10:06.889556-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:22:28+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (326866,'en-US','new-thunderbird-android','New in Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android/revision/326866',325042,'2026-04-23T20:12:37.929332-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','update for TB 18 for Android',1,'2026-04-23T20:12:54.774114-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','[[T:TBAndroidMore]]
This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in Thunderbird for Android. For full details, please refer to [[Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues]].

__TOC__
==Thunderbird 18==
* PNG custom avatars are now supported (as well as JPEGss and stylized letter monograms as per the Thunderbird 17 section.
* There is a new setting to display times in  ISO 8601 format ''''(YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm)'''' in the message list.
*# Tap {button ≡} > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]}.
*# Tap {button General settings} > tap {menu Display} > tap {menu Date/Time Format} > tap {button ISO}.
=Older Releases=
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 17==
* Custom avatars for email accounts are now available. JPEGs only for now. PNGs will be supported in a future release. Upload yours as follows: 
*# Tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]} at the bottom left.
*# Tap your email account that you want to customize e.g. ''''nemo@thunderbird.net'''' > tap {menu General settings} > tap {menu Image} to upload your custom JPEG. You can also use a stylized letter monogram by tapping {menu Monogram} or pick one of the built-in icons by tapping {menu Icon}.
==Thunderbird 16==
* The SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed in [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10312 #10312].
* The following issue was fixed in TB 16.1 which was released one week after Thunderbird 16.0. <del>Known Issue: Deleted/read messages in your unified inbox may not update until you manually refresh. We hope to have a fix soon. you can can follow along on issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10243 #10243]</del>
==Thunderbird 15==
* There are further SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com and the team is testing a fix. You can follow along on issue [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10312 #10312]. It looks like this will fixed in the next release, TB 16.
==Thunderbird 14==
* SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed.
==Thunderbird 13==
* The notification in status bar re-appearing multiple times a day issue (github issue [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/8049 #8049]) has been fixed.
* Special folders (Trash, Sent, Drafts, Archives, etc) not appearing after updating from TB11 to TB12 (github issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9771 #9771]) was fixed.
* The bug with outgoing email not working for certain educational and enterprise accounts is scheduled to be fixed in the next release, Thunderbird 14 for Android (github issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9160 #9160]).
==Thunderbird 12==
* If you use a password manager, Android will now use it to automatically fill in your password when you add a new account.
==Thunderbird 11==
* Mail folders are easier to navigate.
* Algorithmic darkening makes reading emails easier in dark mode.
* Thunderbird 11.1 : Selected/read/unread message states are now easier to distinguish visually.
* You can still see font settings if you search for ''''''font'''''' in Settings. This will be removed in a future Thunderbird release. Please use the Android operating system font size settings instead.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 10==
* You can now empty your Spam folder by tapping {menu Empty Spam} in the Spam folder''s menu {menu ⋮} (also known as the ''''''kebab'''''' menu because the 3 vertical dots resemble a kebab).
* On Android 10 or older, you now have an option to use the system theme.
*# Tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]} at the bottom left.
*# Tap {button General settings} > tap {menu Display} > tap {button Use system default} under ''''''Theme''''''
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 9==
* Initial Android 15 support.
* Thunderbird attempts to use your email provider''s auto configuration database. If it fails it, it uses Mozilla''s database.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 8==
* Refer to [[New in Thunderbird for Android version 8]] please.
[[UI:details_end]]','<p></p><div class="warning">More for Thunderbird for Android: <strong>o</strong> Vote on &amp; suggest improvements at <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/t5/ideas/idb-p/ideas/label-name/thunderbird%20android">Connect</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/participate/">Participate/Contribute</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird-android/form">Get Support</a></div>
