* Prior to Thunderbird 153, the OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead. We will refer to versions 152 and lower as ''''''older versions'''''' in this article.{/note}
== Automatic email account setup ==
# ''''''Start'''''': When you first run Thunderbird after installing it, the Account Hub will open into the email setup flow. After the first run, you can start the email setup flow by clicking the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} ({menu New Account} in older versions) > {menu Email}.
# ''''''Name and Email address'''''': Fill in your ''''''Full name'''''' and ''''''Email address'''''' and click {button Continue}. For example, <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>:
#;[[Image:TB153 Account Hub enter email address]] 
# ''''''Configuration lookup'''''': Thunderbird will look in its configuration database for email settings based on your email address - most email providers are in our mail provider database. If they aren’t, you can manually configure your settings. Here are the settings that Thunderbird automatically finds for [[Thunderbird Desktop and Thundermail|Thundermail]] (other providers that use OAuth like Gmail and Microsoft email are very similar to Thundermail):
#;[[Image:tb153 account hub thundermail settings]] 
# ''''''Confirm settings'''''': The default settings are suitable for most folks, so click {button Continue} to finish the setup. For email providers that use OAuth (like ''''''Thundermail''''''), a branded tab will be provided by the provider. This OAuth dialog will pop up in the system browser (for older versions, this opens a tab in Thunderbird). In our example below, Thundermail uses OAuth, so the tab is branded with Thundermail colours and graphics:
#;[[Image:tb153 opening the system browser for OAuth]] 
# ''''''Enter the password in your email provider’s branded system browser tab:'''''' In this step, you will enter your password and then click {button SIGN IN}. Some providers may require you to do multi-factor authentication during this step. When done, you will receive a notification that your account has been connected to Thunderbird and that you can close the system browser tab:
#;[[Image:tb153 oauth web browser securely connected]] 
# ''''''Add other services like Address Books and Calendars:'''''' Back in Thunderbird, if the email provider supports other services, you are prompted to add them. For most people, clicking {button Continue} to add the default other services is sensible:
#;[[Image:tb153 additional services like calendar and address book]] 
# ''''''Done!'''''' At this stage, your email account and other connected services are set up. You can click {button Finish} to end or click {button Add another email} to add another email account:
#;[[Image:tb153 account hub done!]] 
# ''''''Need help?'''''' If your email provider uses OAuth, please follow our [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth Troubleshooting Checklist]] first. If you are still stuck, please [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form ask a support question] in our community support forum.','<h1 id="w_account-hub-for-thunderbird-desktop">Account Hub for Thunderbird Desktop</h1>
<p>The Account Hub is our new streamlined, unified, and modern experience for adding email accounts, address books, and calendars.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTES</strong>
<ul><li> Prior to Thunderbird 150, the legacy Account Setup was the default. For versions older than 150, please refer to these articles: <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration">Automatic Account Configuration</a> and <a href="/en-US/kb/manual-account-configuration">Manual Account Configuration</a>.
</li><li>For simplicity, we will use the term <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth"><strong>OAuth</strong></a> instead of <strong>OAuth 2</strong> or <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
</li><li> Prior to Thunderbird 153, the OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead. We will refer to versions 152 and lower as <strong>older versions</strong> in this article.</li></ul></div>

<h2 id="w_automatic-email-account-setup">Automatic email account setup</h2>
<ol><li> <strong>Start</strong>: When you first run Thunderbird after installing it, the Account Hub will open into the email setup flow. After the first run, you can start the email setup flow by clicking the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> (<span class="menu">New Account</span> in older versions) &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> <strong>Name and Email address</strong>: Fill in your <strong>Full name</strong> and <strong>Email address</strong> and click <span class="button">Continue</span>. For example, <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>:
<dl><dt><img alt="TB153 Account Hub enter email address" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-33-21-80fd75.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Configuration lookup</strong>: Thunderbird will look in its configuration database for email settings based on your email address - most email providers are in our mail provider database. If they aren’t, you can manually configure your settings. Here are the settings that Thunderbird automatically finds for <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=Thunderbird+Desktop+and+Thundermail" title="Page does not exist.">Thundermail</a> (other providers that use OAuth like Gmail and Microsoft email are very similar to Thundermail):
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 account hub thundermail settings" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-36-47-226006.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Confirm settings</strong>: The default settings are suitable for most folks, so click <span class="button">Continue</span> to finish the setup. For email providers that use OAuth (like <strong>Thundermail</strong>), a branded tab will be provided by the provider. This OAuth dialog will pop up in the system browser (for older versions, this opens a tab in Thunderbird). In our example below, Thundermail uses OAuth, so the tab is branded with Thundermail colours and graphics:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 opening the system browser for OAuth" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-43-30-1cb019.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Enter the password in your email provider’s branded system browser tab:</strong> In this step, you will enter your password and then click <span class="button">SIGN IN</span>. Some providers may require you to do multi-factor authentication during this step. When done, you will receive a notification that your account has been connected to Thunderbird and that you can close the system browser tab:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 oauth web browser securely connected" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-47-06-840125.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Add other services like Address Books and Calendars:</strong> Back in Thunderbird, if the email provider supports other services, you are prompted to add them. For most people, clicking <span class="button">Continue</span> to add the default other services is sensible:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 additional services like calendar and address book" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-51-20-e6913b.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Done!</strong> At this stage, your email account and other connected services are set up. You can click <span class="button">Finish</span> to end or click <span class="button">Add another email</span> to add another email account:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 account hub done!" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-55-10-940bc7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Need help?</strong> If your email provider uses OAuth, please follow our <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth Troubleshooting Checklist</a> first. If you are still stuck, please <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask a support question</a> in our community support forum.
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "333958", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 18, 2026, 12:57:36 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-18T00:57:36.281228-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "do not translate discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/212", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 18, 2026, 12:58:08 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-18T00:58:08.365085-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 3, 2026, 7:27:40 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-03T19:27:40.183758-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:34+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (334125,'en-US','thunderbird-and-gmail','Thunderbird and Gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail/revision/334125',311097,'2026-06-18T14:56:55.662242-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP, please don''',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','{note}''''''NOTES'''''': 
* For simplicity, we will use the term [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth ''''''OAuth''''''] instead of ''''''OAuth 2'''''' or ''''''OAuth 2.0''''''. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
* This article is written for Thunderbird ESR 153 / monthly 153 and newer versions of Thunderbird. Thunderbird 152 and older will be referred to as “older versions”.{/note}

Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google’s [https://www.google.com/gmail/about/ Gmail] service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.

__TOC__

= Configuring your Gmail account =

To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail:

# Click Thunderbird app {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > (menu New Account} in older versions) > {menu Email}.
# ''''''The Account hub “Add your email address'''''' dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information (your name and Gmail email address) and click {button Continue}, Thunderbird will open your system web browser (older versions will open a tab in Thunderbird) with an OAuth dialogue supplied and branded by Google. This Google dialogue will:
## Ask for your Gmail address again as well as your password.
## Depending on how you or your organization have configured your Gmail, you may be prompted to do multi-factor authentication using an authenticator app or other means.
## Finally, a dialogue with a list of Google Services is presented for you to allow Thunderbird to access. This list includes email and perhaps other services like Calendar and Contacts as well. Click {button Continue} to finish the process and return to Thunderbird from your system web browser.
## Thunderbird will begin downloading your existing messages.</li></ol>

Having issues? Please follow our [[https://docs.google.com/document/d/1y5rdgrE582Pu63rrrZv-VJm3iaYpyk8SxqBQcn9EzLc/edit?pli=1&tab=t.0 OAuth Troubleshoting Checklist]] before [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form asking a support question] on our forums.

# Review [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892#zippy=%2Cthunderbird Google’s recommended settings for Thunderbird] so that your local actions (such as labelling and deleting) correspond to those that Gmail takes and won’t cause any conflicts.</p></li></ol>

For more detailed information, please refer to these guides:
* [[Automatic Account Configuration]
* [https://support.google.com/mail/topic/7280141 Gmail’s support articles on email clients]

=Gmail’s peculiarities=
== Archiving messages==

Gmail does not use a standard IMAP archive folder, unlike other mail providers that use an ''''Archive'''' folder (as described in the [[Archived Messages]] article). When you archive an email in Gmail, it simply removes the “Inbox” label while the email itself remains completely safe and searchable within your “All Mail” folder.

To achieve parity with the way Gmail handles archiving messages, set your ''''''Message Archives'''''' like so:



There is also a set of special subfolders for the ''''top level Gmail'''' folder:

* ''''''All Mail'''''': Contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including Sent and Archived. Any messages that you see in the Inbox will also appear in the top level''''/All Mail'''' folder.
* ''''''Drafts'''''': Contains your drafts.

* ''''''Sent'''''': Contains your sent items.
* ''''''Spam'''''': Contains messages that were marked as Spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* ''''''Starred'''''': Contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* ''''''Trash'''''': Contains deleted messages.
* ''''''Important'''''': Contains messages that Gmail has flagged as “important” (see [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/186531 Priority Inbox overview] for more details about this Gmail feature).

===Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)===

You can configure which folder (and its messages) you want to display and download in Thunderbird by changing folder subscriptions. By default, all folders are displayed and downloaded as described above. To change folder subscriptions, right-click (control+click on macOS) on your Gmail address (nemo@thunderbird.net for this example) and select {menu Subscribe…}:

Uncheck any folders that you don’t want to download and display in Thunderbird.

Please note: For performance and space reasons, it is recommended to unsubscribe from Gmail’s ''''''All Mail'''''' folder since you will have multiple copies of the message (one for each label in the folder for that label and one in ''''''All Mail''''''). If you do unsubscribe from All Mail, we recommend you use Gmail filters or Thunderbird filters to keep the emails you want and delete the rest.. Otherwise, you will lose local offline access to messages that aren’t actively filtered into your Inbox, Sent, the folders for each label, or custom local folders.

===Synchronizing folders (labels)===

If you want to see all of your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid the full content being downloaded to your system, you can tweak the synchronization settings to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don’t synchronize a folder, only the message header will be displayed in Thunderbird, but the actual message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message.

To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders (Click the Thunderbird menu ☰ &gt; Account Settings…). See [https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/imap-synchronization#w_synchronization IMAP Synchronization] for more information.

===Thunderbird’s message lists differ from the web interface===

If some messages are appearing in your Inbox that shouldn’t be, or aren’t appearing but should be, you may need to select the ''''Don’t override filters'''' option under Inbox &gt; Filtered mail in Gmail’s settings. The same can be achieved by turning off General &gt; Smart features and personalisation in Gmail’s settings.

You can also change whether Gmail’s interface should group messages by conversation with the ''''''Conversation View'''''' option in Gmail’s General settings page, which can match your [https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/message-threading-thunderbird Thunderbird preference].

See this support article on [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6562 how to change Gmail settings].

=Useful Add-ons=

* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/google-4-tbsync/ Google-4-TbSync: Google contact and contact group sync]
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/cardbook/ CardBook: Contact Manager, supports CardDAV4, including Google contact labels / groups]
* If you want to sync your calendars between Thunderbird and Google, you should read [https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar]
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gtrash/ GTrash] makes true deletion more convenient when using Google’s recommended deletion settings

=See also=
* [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892 Gmail’s support article on recommended settings].
* [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=402793 This ticket, which tracks issues using Gmail with Thunderbird (Bug 402793)].','<div class="note"><strong>NOTES</strong>: 
<ul><li> For simplicity, we will use the term <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth"><strong>OAuth</strong></a> instead of <strong>OAuth 2</strong> or <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
</li><li> This article is written for Thunderbird ESR 153 / monthly 153 and newer versions of Thunderbird. Thunderbird 152 and older will be referred to as “older versions”.</li></ul></div>

<p>Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google’s <a href="https://www.google.com/gmail/about/">Gmail</a> service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-your-gmail-account"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring your Gmail account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_gmails-peculiarities"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Gmail’s peculiarities</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_archiving-messages"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Archiving messages</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">2.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_synchronizing-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">2.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Synchronizing folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbirds-message-lists-differ-from-the-web-interface"><span class="tocnumber">2.1.3</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird’s message lists differ from the web interface</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_useful-add-ons"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Useful Add-ons</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_see-also"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">See also</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configuring-your-gmail-account">Configuring your Gmail account</h1>
<p>To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; (menu New Account} in older versions) &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> <strong>The Account hub “Add your email address</strong> dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information (your name and Gmail email address) and click <span class="button">Continue</span>, Thunderbird will open your system web browser (older versions will open a tab in Thunderbird) with an OAuth dialogue supplied and branded by Google. This Google dialogue will:
<ol><li> Ask for your Gmail address again as well as your password.
</li><li> Depending on how you or your organization have configured your Gmail, you may be prompted to do multi-factor authentication using an authenticator app or other means.
</li><li> Finally, a dialogue with a list of Google Services is presented for you to allow Thunderbird to access. This list includes email and perhaps other services like Calendar and Contacts as well. Click <span class="button">Continue</span> to finish the process and return to Thunderbird from your system web browser.
</li><li> Thunderbird will begin downloading your existing messages.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;
</li></ol>
</li></ol>
<p>Having issues? Please follow our [<a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1y5rdgrE582Pu63rrrZv-VJm3iaYpyk8SxqBQcn9EzLc/edit?pli=1&amp;tab=t.0">OAuth Troubleshoting Checklist</a>] before <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">asking a support question</a> on our forums.
</p>
<ol><li> Review <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892#zippy=%2Cthunderbird">Google’s recommended settings for Thunderbird</a> so that your local actions (such as labelling and deleting) correspond to those that Gmail takes and won’t cause any conflicts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;
</li></ol>
<p>For more detailed information, please refer to these guides:
</p>
<ul><li> [[Automatic Account Configuration]
</li><li> <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/topic/7280141">Gmail’s support articles on email clients</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_gmails-peculiarities">Gmail’s peculiarities</h1>
<h2 id="w_archiving-messages">Archiving messages</h2>
<p>Gmail does not use a standard IMAP archive folder, unlike other mail providers that use an <em>Archive</em> folder (as described in the <a href="/en-US/kb/archived-messages">Archived Messages</a> article). When you archive an email in Gmail, it simply removes the “Inbox” label while the email itself remains completely safe and searchable within your “All Mail” folder.
</p><p>To achieve parity with the way Gmail handles archiving messages, set your <strong>Message Archives</strong> like so:
</p><p><br>
</p><p>There is also a set of special subfolders for the <em>top level Gmail</em> folder:
</p>
<ul><li> <strong>All Mail</strong>: Contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including Sent and Archived. Any messages that you see in the Inbox will also appear in the top level<em>/All Mail</em> folder.
</li><li> <strong>Drafts</strong>: Contains your drafts.
</li></ul>
<ul><li> <strong>Sent</strong>: Contains your sent items.
</li><li> <strong>Spam</strong>: Contains messages that were marked as Spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> <strong>Starred</strong>: Contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> <strong>Trash</strong>: Contains deleted messages.
</li><li> <strong>Important</strong>: Contains messages that Gmail has flagged as “important” (see <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/186531">Priority Inbox overview</a> for more details about this Gmail feature).
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</h3>
<p>You can configure which folder (and its messages) you want to display and download in Thunderbird by changing folder subscriptions. By default, all folders are displayed and downloaded as described above. To change folder subscriptions, right-click (control+click on macOS) on your Gmail address (<a href="mailto:nemo@thunderbird.net">nemo@thunderbird.net</a> for this example) and select <span class="menu">Subscribe…</span>:
</p><p>Uncheck any folders that you don’t want to download and display in Thunderbird.
</p><p>Please note: For performance and space reasons, it is recommended to unsubscribe from Gmail’s <strong>All Mail</strong> folder since you will have multiple copies of the message (one for each label in the folder for that label and one in <strong>All Mail</strong>). If you do unsubscribe from All Mail, we recommend you use Gmail filters or Thunderbird filters to keep the emails you want and delete the rest.. Otherwise, you will lose local offline access to messages that aren’t actively filtered into your Inbox, Sent, the folders for each label, or custom local folders.
</p>
<h3 id="w_synchronizing-folders-labels">Synchronizing folders (labels)</h3>
<p>If you want to see all of your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid the full content being downloaded to your system, you can tweak the synchronization settings to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don’t synchronize a folder, only the message header will be displayed in Thunderbird, but the actual message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message.
</p><p>To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders (Click the Thunderbird menu ☰ &gt; Account Settings…). See <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/imap-synchronization#w_synchronization">IMAP Synchronization</a> for more information.
</p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbirds-message-lists-differ-from-the-web-interface">Thunderbird’s message lists differ from the web interface</h3>
<p>If some messages are appearing in your Inbox that shouldn’t be, or aren’t appearing but should be, you may need to select the <em>Don’t override filters</em> option under Inbox &gt; Filtered mail in Gmail’s settings. The same can be achieved by turning off General &gt; Smart features and personalisation in Gmail’s settings.
</p><p>You can also change whether Gmail’s interface should group messages by conversation with the <strong>Conversation View</strong> option in Gmail’s General settings page, which can match your <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/message-threading-thunderbird">Thunderbird preference</a>.
</p><p>See this support article on <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6562">how to change Gmail settings</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_useful-add-ons">Useful Add-ons</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/google-4-tbsync/">Google-4-TbSync: Google contact and contact group sync</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/cardbook/">CardBook: Contact Manager, supports CardDAV4, including Google contact labels / groups</a>
</li><li> If you want to sync your calendars between Thunderbird and Google, you should read <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar">Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gtrash/">GTrash</a> makes true deletion more convenient when using Google’s recommended deletion settings
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_see-also">See also</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892">Gmail’s support article on recommended settings</a>.
</li><li> <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=402793">This ticket, which tracks issues using Gmail with Thunderbird (Bug 402793)</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "334125", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 18, 2026, 2:56:55 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-18T14:56:55.662242-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP, please don''", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:31+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (334188,'en-US','thunderbird-and-gmail','Thunderbird and Gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail/revision/334188',311097,'2026-06-18T21:04:44.423310-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','do not translate, wip, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/206',1,'2026-06-18T21:06:09.830483-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{note}''''''NOTES'''''': 
* For simplicity, we will use the term [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth ''''''OAuth''''''] instead of ''''''OAuth 2'''''' or ''''''OAuth 2.0''''''. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
* This article is written for Thunderbird ESR 153 / monthly 153 and newer versions of Thunderbird. Thunderbird 152 and older will be referred to as “older versions”.{/note}

Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google’s [https://www.google.com/gmail/about/ Gmail] service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
__TOC__
=Configuring your Gmail account=
To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail:
# Click Thunderbird app {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu New Account} in older versions) > {menu Email}.
# The ''''''Account Hub Add your email address'''''' dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information (your name and Gmail email address), and click {button Continue}, Thunderbird will open your system web browser (older versions will open a tab in Thunderbird) with an OAuth dialogue supplied and branded by Google. This Google dialogue will:
## Ask for your Gmail address again as well as your password.
## Depending on how you or your organization have configured your Gmail, you may be prompted to do multi-factor authentication using an authenticator app or other means.
## Finally, a dialogue with a list of Google Services is presented for you to allow Thunderbird to access. This list includes email and perhaps other services like Calendar and Contacts as well. Click {button Continue} to finish the process and return to Thunderbird from your system web browser.
## Thunderbird will begin downloading your existing messages.
#;Having issues? Please follow our [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth Troubleshoting Checklist]] before [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form asking a support question] on our forums.
# Review [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892#zippy=%2Cthunderbird Google’s recommended settings for Thunderbird] so that your local actions (such as labelling and deleting) correspond to those that Gmail takes and won’t cause any conflicts.

For more detailed information, please refer to these guides:
* [[Automatic Account Configuration]]
* [https://support.google.com/mail/topic/7280141 Gmail’s support articles on email clients]
=Gmail’s peculiarities=
==Archiving messages==
Gmail does not use a standard IMAP archive folder, unlike other mail providers that use an ''''''Archive'''''' folder (as described in the [[Archived Messages]] article). When you archive an email in Gmail, it simply removes the ''''''Inbox''''''label while the email itself remains completely safe and searchable within your ''''''All Mail'''''' folder.

To achieve parity with the way Gmail handles archiving messages, tick {button Keep messages in:} and then select ''''''Other''''''under ''''''Message Archives'''''' like so:
;[[Image:tb153-message-archives]] 
==Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages==
===Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders===
Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. Because of this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in the Gmail web interface, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.

If you copy a message to another folder, Gmail will still store only one message but apply both folder names as labels to it. Thunderbird, however, will have multiple copies of the message. 

In Thunderbird, if you delete or move any copy of a message into the Trash, all labels are removed and it will no longer appear in any other folder.

In the example shown below, the folders that exist at the same level as the top level (such as FOSS) correspond to Gmail labels:
;[[Image:tb153-folder-pane-with-gmail-foss-label]] 

There is also a set of special subfolders for the ''''''top level Gmail'''''' folder:
* ''''''All Mail'''''': Contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including Sent and Archived. Any messages that you see in the Inbox will also appear in the top level''''/All Mail'''' folder.
* ''''''Drafts'''''': Contains your drafts.
* ''''''Sent'''''': Contains your sent items.
* ''''''Spam'''''': Contains messages that were marked as Spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* ''''''Starred'''''': Contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* ''''''Trash'''''': Contains deleted messages.
* ''''''Important'''''': Contains messages that Gmail has flagged as “important” (see [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/186531 Priority Inbox overview] for more details about this Gmail feature).

===Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)===
You can configure which folder (and its messages) you want to display and download in Thunderbird by changing folder subscriptions. By default, all folders are displayed and downloaded as described above. To change folder subscriptions, right-click (control+click on macOS) on your Gmail address (<code>nemo@thunderbird.net</code> for this example) and select {menu Subscribe…}:
;[[Image:tb153-subscribe-folders]] 
Uncheck any folders that you don’t want to download and display in Thunderbird.

{note}''''''Please note'''''': For performance and space reasons, it is recommended to unsubscribe from Gmail’s ''''''All Mail'''''' folder since you will have multiple copies of the message (one for each label in the folder for that label and one in ''''''All Mail''''''). If you do unsubscribe from ''''''All Mail'''''', we recommend you use Gmail filters or Thunderbird filters to keep the emails you want and delete the rest.. Otherwise, you will lose local offline access to messages that aren’t actively filtered into your ''''''Inbox'''''', ''''''Sent'''''', the folders for each label, or custom local folders.{/note}
===Synchronizing folders (labels)===
If you want to see all of your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird, but prefer to avoid the full content being downloaded to your system, you can tweak the synchronization settings to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don’t synchronize a folder, only the message header will be displayed in Thunderbird, but the actual message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message.

To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders (click the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings…}). See [[IMAP Synchronization]] for more information.

===Thunderbird’s message lists differ from the web interface===
If some messages are appearing in your Inbox that shouldn’t be, or aren’t appearing but should be, you may need to select the ''''Don’t override filters'''' option under ''''''Inbox'''''' > ''''''Filtered mail'''''' in Gmail’s settings. The same can be achieved by turning off ''''''Smart features'''''' and ''''''personalisation'''''' in Gmail’s ''''''General''''''settings.

You can also change whether Gmail’s interface should group messages by conversation with the ''''''Conversation View'''''' option in Gmail’s ''''''General'''''' settings page, which can match your [[Message threading in Thunderbird - keep messages grouped by conversation|Thunderbird conversation grouping preference]].

See this Gmail support article on [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6562 how to change Gmail settings].

=Useful Add-ons=
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/google-4-tbsync/ Google-4-TbSync: Google contact and contact group sync]
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/cardbook/ CardBook: Contact Manager, supports CardDAV4, including Google contact labels / groups]
* If you want to sync your calendars between Thunderbird and Google, please read [[Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar]].
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gtrash/ GTrash] makes true deletion more convenient when using Google’s recommended deletion settings

=See also=
* [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892 Gmail’s support article on recommended settings].
* [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=402793 This ticket, which tracks issues using Gmail with Thunderbird (Bug 402793)].','<div class="note"><strong>NOTES</strong>: 
<ul><li> For simplicity, we will use the term <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth"><strong>OAuth</strong></a> instead of <strong>OAuth 2</strong> or <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
</li><li> This article is written for Thunderbird ESR 153 / monthly 153 and newer versions of Thunderbird. Thunderbird 152 and older will be referred to as “older versions”.</li></ul></div>

<p>Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google’s <a href="https://www.google.com/gmail/about/">Gmail</a> service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-your-gmail-account"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring your Gmail account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_gmails-peculiarities"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Gmail’s peculiarities</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_archiving-messages"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Archiving messages</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_synchronizing-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.3</span> <span class="toctext">Synchronizing folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbirds-message-lists-differ-from-the-web-interface"><span class="tocnumber">2.2.4</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird’s message lists differ from the web interface</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_useful-add-ons"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Useful Add-ons</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_see-also"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">See also</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configuring-your-gmail-account">Configuring your Gmail account</h1>
<p>To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">New Account</span> in older versions) &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> The <strong>Account Hub Add your email address</strong> dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information (your name and Gmail email address), and click <span class="button">Continue</span>, Thunderbird will open your system web browser (older versions will open a tab in Thunderbird) with an OAuth dialogue supplied and branded by Google. This Google dialogue will:
<ol><li> Ask for your Gmail address again as well as your password.
</li><li> Depending on how you or your organization have configured your Gmail, you may be prompted to do multi-factor authentication using an authenticator app or other means.
</li><li> Finally, a dialogue with a list of Google Services is presented for you to allow Thunderbird to access. This list includes email and perhaps other services like Calendar and Contacts as well. Click <span class="button">Continue</span> to finish the process and return to Thunderbird from your system web browser.
</li><li> Thunderbird will begin downloading your existing messages.
</li></ol>
<dl><dt>Having issues? Please follow our <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth Troubleshoting Checklist</a> before <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">asking a support question</a> on our forums.
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Review <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892#zippy=%2Cthunderbird">Google’s recommended settings for Thunderbird</a> so that your local actions (such as labelling and deleting) correspond to those that Gmail takes and won’t cause any conflicts.
</li></ol>
<p>For more detailed information, please refer to these guides:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration">Automatic Account Configuration</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/topic/7280141">Gmail’s support articles on email clients</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_gmails-peculiarities">Gmail’s peculiarities</h1>
<h2 id="w_archiving-messages">Archiving messages</h2>
<p>Gmail does not use a standard IMAP archive folder, unlike other mail providers that use an <strong>Archive</strong> folder (as described in the <a href="/en-US/kb/archived-messages">Archived Messages</a> article). When you archive an email in Gmail, it simply removes the <strong>Inbox</strong>label while the email itself remains completely safe and searchable within your <strong>All Mail</strong> folder.
</p><p>To achieve parity with the way Gmail handles archiving messages, tick <span class="button">Keep messages in:</span> and then select <strong>Other</strong>under <strong>Message Archives</strong> like so:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153-message-archives" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-20-29-24-2180de.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
<h2 id="w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</h2>
<h3 id="w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</h3>
<p>Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. Because of this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in the Gmail web interface, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.
</p><p>If you copy a message to another folder, Gmail will still store only one message but apply both folder names as labels to it. Thunderbird, however, will have multiple copies of the message. 
</p><p>In Thunderbird, if you delete or move any copy of a message into the Trash, all labels are removed and it will no longer appear in any other folder.
</p><p>In the example shown below, the folders that exist at the same level as the top level (such as FOSS) correspond to Gmail labels:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153-folder-pane-with-gmail-foss-label" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-20-56-17-bf9d55.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
<p>There is also a set of special subfolders for the <strong>top level Gmail</strong> folder:
</p>
<ul><li> <strong>All Mail</strong>: Contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including Sent and Archived. Any messages that you see in the Inbox will also appear in the top level<em>/All Mail</em> folder.
</li><li> <strong>Drafts</strong>: Contains your drafts.
</li><li> <strong>Sent</strong>: Contains your sent items.
</li><li> <strong>Spam</strong>: Contains messages that were marked as Spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> <strong>Starred</strong>: Contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> <strong>Trash</strong>: Contains deleted messages.
</li><li> <strong>Important</strong>: Contains messages that Gmail has flagged as “important” (see <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/186531">Priority Inbox overview</a> for more details about this Gmail feature).
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</h3>
<p>You can configure which folder (and its messages) you want to display and download in Thunderbird by changing folder subscriptions. By default, all folders are displayed and downloaded as described above. To change folder subscriptions, right-click (control+click on macOS) on your Gmail address (<code>nemo@thunderbird.net</code> for this example) and select <span class="menu">Subscribe…</span>:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153-subscribe-folders" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-21-00-40-dbc3b7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
<p>Uncheck any folders that you don’t want to download and display in Thunderbird.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Please note</strong>: For performance and space reasons, it is recommended to unsubscribe from Gmail’s <strong>All Mail</strong> folder since you will have multiple copies of the message (one for each label in the folder for that label and one in <strong>All Mail</strong>). If you do unsubscribe from <strong>All Mail</strong>, we recommend you use Gmail filters or Thunderbird filters to keep the emails you want and delete the rest.. Otherwise, you will lose local offline access to messages that aren’t actively filtered into your <strong>Inbox</strong>, <strong>Sent</strong>, the folders for each label, or custom local folders.</div>
<h3 id="w_synchronizing-folders-labels">Synchronizing folders (labels)</h3>
<p>If you want to see all of your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird, but prefer to avoid the full content being downloaded to your system, you can tweak the synchronization settings to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don’t synchronize a folder, only the message header will be displayed in Thunderbird, but the actual message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message.
</p><p>To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders (click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings…</span>). See <a href="/en-US/kb/imap-synchronization">IMAP Synchronization</a> for more information.
</p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbirds-message-lists-differ-from-the-web-interface">Thunderbird’s message lists differ from the web interface</h3>
<p>If some messages are appearing in your Inbox that shouldn’t be, or aren’t appearing but should be, you may need to select the <em>Don’t override filters</em> option under <strong>Inbox</strong> &gt; <strong>Filtered mail</strong> in Gmail’s settings. The same can be achieved by turning off <strong>Smart features</strong> and <strong>personalisation</strong> in Gmail’s <strong>General</strong>settings.
</p><p>You can also change whether Gmail’s interface should group messages by conversation with the <strong>Conversation View</strong> option in Gmail’s <strong>General</strong> settings page, which can match your <a href="/en-US/kb/message-threading-thunderbird">Thunderbird conversation grouping preference</a>.
</p><p>See this Gmail support article on <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6562">how to change Gmail settings</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_useful-add-ons">Useful Add-ons</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/google-4-tbsync/">Google-4-TbSync: Google contact and contact group sync</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/cardbook/">CardBook: Contact Manager, supports CardDAV4, including Google contact labels / groups</a>
</li><li> If you want to sync your calendars between Thunderbird and Google, please read <a href="/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar">Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar</a>.
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gtrash/">GTrash</a> makes true deletion more convenient when using Google’s recommended deletion settings
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_see-also">See also</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892">Gmail’s support article on recommended settings</a>.
</li><li> <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=402793">This ticket, which tracks issues using Gmail with Thunderbird (Bug 402793)</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "334188", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 18, 2026, 9:04:44 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-18T21:04:44.423310-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "do not translate, wip, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/206", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 18, 2026, 9:06:09 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-18T21:06:09.830483-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:29+00:00');
