<h1 id="w_overview-of-custom-oauth">Overview of custom OAuth</h1>
<p>Thunderbird provides several ways for organizations or individuals to use custom OAuth2 configurations, whether you need to configure a single account, deploy organization-specific settings across many users, or integrate with an identity provider that Thunderbird does not support by default.
</p><p>Administrators can deploy OAuth2 settings centrally using an add-on, while advanced users can configure OAuth2 settings for individual accounts. This article explains the available options and helps you choose the approach that best fits your organization.
</p><p>Custom OAuth configurations for IMAP and Exchange email accounts can be created with a Thunderbird add-on or by manual configuration without code changes starting in Thunderbird 155. It is possible to do this for any OAuth provider that supports Exchange or IMAP. POP3 support is in progress (currently scheduled for 156 - see <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2061466">bug 2061466</a>).
</p><p>If you need help configuring OAuth2 for a specific provider, please contact the provider''s support team to get the configuration details (Thunderbird support cannot provide provider-specific configuration details). Use the information provided by the provider to configure Thunderbird manually or via an add-on.
</p><p>Custom OAuth is new and there may be issues. If you need help, please do not hesitate to <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird">ask for support in the Thunderbird support forum</a>. For Enterprise deployments, please ask in the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">Thunderbird Enterprise group</a>.
</p><p>For all the details, please read one or more of the following:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users">Custom OAuth for end users</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-administrators">Custom OAuth for administrators</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_future-standards-like-pacc-and-dcr-will-eliminate-most-oauth-custom-configurations">Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations</h1>
<p>Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required, including:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/">PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/">DCR (Dynamic Client Registration)</a>
</li></ul>
<p>Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configurations automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350447", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:07:45 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:07:45.128625-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "\"split into four; the three child articles are submitted separately, please approve those first\"  wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:24:49 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:24:49.440635-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:41+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (350450,'en-US','custom-oauth-end-users','Custom OAuth for end users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users/revision/350450',NULL,'2026-09-02T23:22:25.520359-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218',1,'2026-09-02T23:22:49.027777-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__

End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are two examples:

= Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange =

When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:

# Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email}.
# Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
# Click ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom left. (''''''NOTE'''''': Do not click {button Continue}, because if you do, your system’s web browser will open, which is not what you want. If you do this, please start over again at step 1.)
# In the middle, click  {button Microsoft} > {button Set up account}.
# Type your service URL in the ''''''Enter the service URL'''''' field and then click on {button Find settings}. Here are some common service URLs:
#* [https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx]
#* for Government High GCC, ask your administrator. It may be, for EWS: [https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx] and for Graph: https://graph.microsoft.us/
#* For China, ask your administrator.
# Select ''''''Authentication Method''''''  {menu OAuth2}.
# Uncheck ''''''Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings''''''.
# Enter your custom ''''''Tenant ID'''''' and ''''''AppID'''''' in the relevant fields and then click {button Connect}.

= Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration? =

The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.

If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.

This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.

See [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]] for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.

See [[Custom OAuth for Thunderbird]] for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p>End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are two examples:
</p>
<h1 id="w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</h1>
<p>When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:
</p>
<ol><li> Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
</li><li> Click <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom left. (<strong>NOTE</strong>: Do not click <span class="button">Continue</span>, because if you do, your system’s web browser will open, which is not what you want. If you do this, please start over again at step 1.)
</li><li> In the middle, click  <span class="button">Microsoft</span> &gt; <span class="button">Set up account</span>.
</li><li> Type your service URL in the <strong>Enter the service URL</strong> field and then click on <span class="button">Find settings</span>. Here are some common service URLs:
<ul><li> <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> for Government High GCC, ask your administrator. It may be, for EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a> and for Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.us/">https://graph.microsoft.us/</a>
</li><li> For China, ask your administrator.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Select <strong>Authentication Method</strong>  <span class="menu">OAuth2</span>.
</li><li> Uncheck <strong>Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings</strong>.
</li><li> Enter your custom <strong>Tenant ID</strong> and <strong>AppID</strong> in the relevant fields and then click <span class="button">Connect</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</h1>
<p>The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.
</p><p>If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.
</p><p>This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a> for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth">Custom OAuth for Thunderbird</a> for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350450", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:22:25 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:22:25.520359-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:22:49 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:22:49.027777-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:38+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (350451,'en-US','custom-oauth-administrators','Custom OAuth for administrators','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-administrators','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-administrators/revision/350451',NULL,'2026-09-02T23:29:24.800407-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip! do not translate please! discussion at: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/1218',1,'2026-09-02T23:30:00.559837-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__
= OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations) =

Who is this for?

* Organizations
* Universities
* IT administrators
* Third-party provider developers

Thunderbird provides a WebExtension API that allows add-ons to register OAuth2 providers. Please see: [https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/oauthProvider.html oauthProvider API].

An add-on can provide OAuth2 configuration including:

* Client ID
* Authorization endpoint
* Token endpoint
* Scopes
* PKCE settings
* Browser flow settings

This allows organizations to package their OAuth2 configuration once and deploy it to many users. The API now supports two deployment models:

# Registering OAuth2 providers for new mail services that Thunderbird doesn''t already know about.
# Overriding Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 configuration for selected email domains or entire hostnames. This allows an organization to replace Thunderbird''s built-in configuration only where required while continuing to use the built-in provider for everyone else.

For example, an organization using Microsoft 365 with its own Azure application registration can transparently replace Thunderbird''s built-in Microsoft OAuth2 configuration for users in its own email domain while all other Microsoft 365 users continue using Thunderbird''s built-in configuration.

Thunderbird also displays when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension, including the name of the extension, so users can easily identify where their configuration is coming from.

== Administrator workflow ==

Organizations that wish to deploy a common OAuth2 configuration to many users can package it as a Thunderbird WebExtension:

=== 1. Create the extension manifest ===

Create a {filepath manifest.json} file containing the OAuth provider registration and any required OAuth2 settings (client ID, authorization endpoint, token endpoint, scopes, PKCE settings, browser flow settings, hostname registrations, email domain overrides, etc.).
[[UI:details_start]]
=== Example manifest.json: ===
<code>
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Example Microsoft 365 OAuth Provider",
  "version": "1.0",
  "applications": {
    "gecko": {
      "id": "oauth-provider@example.org",
      "strict_min_version": "155.0"
    }
  },
  "oauth_provider": {
    "issuer": "this.must.be.unique",
    "clientId": "your-client-id-here-0000-000000000000",
    "clientSecret": "",
    "issuerIdentifier": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/v2.0",
    "authorizationEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/authorize",
    "tokenEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/token",
    "redirectionEndpoint": "https://localhost",
    "usePKCE": true,
    "useExternalBrowser": true,
    "hostnames": [
      "office365.com",
      "outlook.com"
    ],
    "emailDomains": [
      "example.com"
    ],
    "scopes": "https://outlook.office.com/IMAP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/POP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/SMTP.Send https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All offline_access"
  }
}
</code>
[[UI:details_end]]
=== 2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file ===

* Zip the manifest.json file.
* Rename the file extension from ".zip" to ".xpi.

=== 3. Deploy the extension ===

Deploy the extension to users using the organization''s preferred software deployment mechanism. Possible deployment methods include:

* Enterprise Policy ({filepath policies.json})
* Windows Group Policy (ADMX templates)
* Microsoft Intune
* macOS configuration profiles
* Linux enterprise package deployment
* Manual installation, if appropriate

Thunderbird''s Enterprise Policies already support automatically installing extensions from either local files or URLs, as well as force-installing extensions so users cannot remove them. The newer ''''''ExtensionSettings'''''' policy also allows administrators to automatically install or force-install a specific extension by ID using an install_url.
<!--
 TODO: Link to the Thunderbird Enterprise Policy documentation for extension deployment.
-->
=== 4. Install / enable the extension ===

Depending on the deployment method, the extension may be:

* installed automatically when Thunderbird starts
* force-installed by enterprise policy
* installed manually by the user via the Add-ons Manager

''''''Once installed, no additional account configuration is normally required.''''''

=== User experience ===

Once the extension is installed, Thunderbird automatically uses the registered OAuth2 provider whenever an account matches the configured hostname or email/domain rules.

Thunderbird displays an indicator when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension. The indicator includes the name of the extension being used so users can easily identify which extension, if any, is supplying their configuration. This allows users and administrators to see which extension is customizing their OAuth configuration.
<!--
TODO: Add screenshot of the indicator
-->

See [[Custom OAuth for Thunderbird]] for other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_oauth2-provider-add-ons-recommended-for-administrators-and-organizations"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations)</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_administrator-workflow"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Administrator workflow</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_1-create-the-extension-manifest"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">1. Create the extension manifest</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_example-manifest-json"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Example manifest.json:</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_2-package-the-extension-into-a-standard-thunderbird-extension-xpi-file"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.3</span> <span class="toctext">2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_3-deploy-the-extension"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.4</span> <span class="toctext">3. Deploy the extension</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_4-install-enable-the-extension"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.5</span> <span class="toctext">4. Install / enable the extension</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_user-experience"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.6</span> <span class="toctext">User experience</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_oauth2-provider-add-ons-recommended-for-administrators-and-organizations">OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations)</h1>
<p>Who is this for?
</p>
<ul><li> Organizations
</li><li> Universities
</li><li> IT administrators
</li><li> Third-party provider developers
</li></ul>
<p>Thunderbird provides a WebExtension API that allows add-ons to register OAuth2 providers. Please see: <a href="https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/oauthProvider.html">oauthProvider API</a>.
</p><p>An add-on can provide OAuth2 configuration including:
</p>
<ul><li> Client ID
</li><li> Authorization endpoint
</li><li> Token endpoint
</li><li> Scopes
</li><li> PKCE settings
</li><li> Browser flow settings
</li></ul>
<p>This allows organizations to package their OAuth2 configuration once and deploy it to many users. The API now supports two deployment models:
</p>
<ol><li> Registering OAuth2 providers for new mail services that Thunderbird doesn''t already know about.
</li><li> Overriding Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 configuration for selected email domains or entire hostnames. This allows an organization to replace Thunderbird''s built-in configuration only where required while continuing to use the built-in provider for everyone else.
</li></ol>
<p>For example, an organization using Microsoft 365 with its own Azure application registration can transparently replace Thunderbird''s built-in Microsoft OAuth2 configuration for users in its own email domain while all other Microsoft 365 users continue using Thunderbird''s built-in configuration.
</p><p>Thunderbird also displays when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension, including the name of the extension, so users can easily identify where their configuration is coming from.
</p>
<h2 id="w_administrator-workflow">Administrator workflow</h2>
<p>Organizations that wish to deploy a common OAuth2 configuration to many users can package it as a Thunderbird WebExtension:
</p>
<h3 id="w_1-create-the-extension-manifest">1. Create the extension manifest</h3>
<p>Create a <span class="filepath">manifest.json</span> file containing the OAuth provider registration and any required OAuth2 settings (client ID, authorization endpoint, token endpoint, scopes, PKCE settings, browser flow settings, hostname registrations, email domain overrides, etc.).
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_example-manifest-json" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Example manifest.json:</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p><code>
{
</code></p><pre><code> "manifest_version": 2,
 "name": "Example Microsoft 365 OAuth Provider",
 "version": "1.0",
 "applications": {
   "gecko": {
     "id": "oauth-provider@example.org",
     "strict_min_version": "155.0"
   }
 },
 "oauth_provider": {
   "issuer": "this.must.be.unique",
   "clientId": "your-client-id-here-0000-000000000000",
   "clientSecret": "",
   "issuerIdentifier": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/v2.0",
   "authorizationEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/authorize",
   "tokenEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/token",
   "redirectionEndpoint": "https://localhost",
   "usePKCE": true,
   "useExternalBrowser": true,
   "hostnames": [
     "office365.com",
     "outlook.com"
   ],
   "emailDomains": [
     "example.com"
   ],
   "scopes": "https://outlook.office.com/IMAP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/POP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/SMTP.Send https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All offline_access"
 }
</code></pre><code>
</code><p><code>}
</code>
</p></div>
</section>
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_2-package-the-extension-into-a-standard-thunderbird-extension-xpi-file">2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file</h3>
<ul><li> Zip the manifest.json file.
</li><li> Rename the file extension from ".zip" to ".xpi.
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_3-deploy-the-extension">3. Deploy the extension</h3>
<p>Deploy the extension to users using the organization''s preferred software deployment mechanism. Possible deployment methods include:
</p>
<ul><li> Enterprise Policy (<span class="filepath">policies.json</span>)
</li><li> Windows Group Policy (ADMX templates)
</li><li> Microsoft Intune
</li><li> macOS configuration profiles
</li><li> Linux enterprise package deployment
</li><li> Manual installation, if appropriate
</li></ul>
<p>Thunderbird''s Enterprise Policies already support automatically installing extensions from either local files or URLs, as well as force-installing extensions so users cannot remove them. The newer <strong>ExtensionSettings</strong> policy also allows administrators to automatically install or force-install a specific extension by ID using an install_url.

</p>
<h3 id="w_4-install-enable-the-extension">4. Install / enable the extension</h3>
<p>Depending on the deployment method, the extension may be:
</p>
<ul><li> installed automatically when Thunderbird starts
</li><li> force-installed by enterprise policy
</li><li> installed manually by the user via the Add-ons Manager
</li></ul>
<p><strong>Once installed, no additional account configuration is normally required.</strong>
</p>
<h3 id="w_user-experience">User experience</h3>
<p>Once the extension is installed, Thunderbird automatically uses the registered OAuth2 provider whenever an account matches the configured hostname or email/domain rules.
</p><p>Thunderbird displays an indicator when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension. The indicator includes the name of the extension being used so users can easily identify which extension, if any, is supplying their configuration. This allows users and administrators to see which extension is customizing their OAuth configuration.

</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth">Custom OAuth for Thunderbird</a> for other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350451", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:29:24 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:29:24.800407-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip! do not translate please! discussion at: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/1218", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:30:00 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:30:00.559837-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:36+00:00');
