</li></ul>
<p>Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configurations automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "351488", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 7, 2026, 10:38:19 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-07T22:38:19.256071-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "POP3 was added in 156, IMAP and Exchange were added in 155 <-- add comment to preserve history; discussion in 218", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 7, 2026, 10:38:36 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-07T22:38:36.118296-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Oct 27, 2026, 12:44:53 PM", "datetime": "2026-10-27T12:44:53.704193-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:15+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (351489,'en-US','custom-oauth-end-users','Custom OAuth for end users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users/revision/351489',350457,'2026-09-07T22:55:30.585084-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','don''t click continue, list only EWS and Graph Service URLs, scroll down in step 8, as per toby''s comment in https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218#issuecomment-5579685387',1,'2026-09-07T22:55:44.875594-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,1,'2026-10-27T06:15:03.503740-07:00','wsmwk','approved','__TOC__

End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are some examples:

= Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange =

When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:

# Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email}.
# Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
# Click ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom left. (''''''NOTE'''''': Do not click {button Continue} which will trigger our autoconfig process to detect settings.)
# In the middle, click  {button Microsoft} > {button Set up account}.
# Type your service URL in the ''''''Enter the service URL'''''' field and then click on {button Find settings}. Here are some common service URLs:
#* Office 365 using EWS - https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx
#* Office 365 using Graph - https://graph.microsoft.com/
#* Ask your administrator for the correct service URL if you are unsure or using another service (for example Government High GCC or 21Vianet China, etc.).
# Select ''''''Authentication Method''''''  {menu OAuth2}.
# Uncheck ''''''Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings''''''.
# Scroll down and then enter your custom ''''''Tenant ID'''''' and ''''''AppID'''''' in the relevant fields and then click {button Connect}.

= Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration? =

The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.

If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.

This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.

See [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]] for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.

See [[Custom OAuth for Thunderbird]] for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p>End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are some examples:
</p>
<h1 id="w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</h1>
<p>When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:
</p>
<ol><li> Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
</li><li> Click <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom left. (<strong>NOTE</strong>: Do not click <span class="button">Continue</span> which will trigger our autoconfig process to detect settings.)
</li><li> In the middle, click  <span class="button">Microsoft</span> &gt; <span class="button">Set up account</span>.
</li><li> Type your service URL in the <strong>Enter the service URL</strong> field and then click on <span class="button">Find settings</span>. Here are some common service URLs:
<ul><li> Office 365 using EWS - <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> Office 365 using Graph - <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/">https://graph.microsoft.com/</a>
</li><li> Ask your administrator for the correct service URL if you are unsure or using another service (for example Government High GCC or 21Vianet China, etc.).
</li></ul>
</li><li> Select <strong>Authentication Method</strong>  <span class="menu">OAuth2</span>.
</li><li> Uncheck <strong>Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings</strong>.
</li><li> Scroll down and then enter your custom <strong>Tenant ID</strong> and <strong>AppID</strong> in the relevant fields and then click <span class="button">Connect</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</h1>
<p>The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.
</p><p>If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.
</p><p>This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a> for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth">Custom OAuth for Thunderbird</a> for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "351489", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 7, 2026, 10:55:30 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-07T22:55:30.585084-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "don''t click continue, list only EWS and Graph Service URLs, scroll down in step 8, as per toby''s comment in https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218#issuecomment-5579685387", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 7, 2026, 10:55:44 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-07T22:55:44.875594-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Oct 27, 2026, 6:15:03 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-27T06:15:03.503740-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:12+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (351490,'en-US','custom-oauth-advanced-users','Custom OAuth for advanced users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users/revision/351490',350455,'2026-09-07T23:06:25.772163-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','add POP3 for Custom OAuth for advanced users as per : https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218#issuecomment-5579685387oby''s comment',1,'2026-09-07T23:06:42.242285-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,1,'2026-10-27T12:31:20.452574-07:00','wsmwk','approved','__TOC__
<!--
TODO: Add POP3 when 156 is releaased
-->
= Per-account OAuth2 manual customization (recommended for advanced users) =

Who is this for?

* Advanced users
* Testing
* Individual account configuration

If only a single account needs custom OAuth2 settings, Thunderbird also supports configuring OAuth2 directly for an individual account.

Unlike the add-on approach, these settings affect only that account and are not intended for organization-wide deployment.

This is currently available for Microsoft Exchange and IMAP accounts, with support for POP3 currently under development.
[[UI:details_start]]
== Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization ==

=== Exchange setup instructions ===
# Add a new email account: Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email} and enter a Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) and then click ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom left of the Account hub.
# Click {button Microsoft Exchange or Microsoft 365} and then click {button Set up account}.
# In the ''''''Exchange settings'''''' dialog, type one of the following URLs into the ''''''Enter the service URL'''''' field:
#* For EWS: https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx
#* For Graph: https://graph.microsoft.com/
# Click {button Find settings}. For EWS, ''''''Exchange Web Services'''''' will automatically be selected. For Graph, ''''''Microsoft Graph'''''' will be selected.
# Your Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) you used in step 1 should be automatically entered into the ''''''Username'''''' field (if not, then correct it).
# For ''''''Authentication Method'''''', select {menu OAuth2}.
# Click ''''''Advanced configuration'''''' above the {button Connect} button.
# In ''''''Server Settings'''''', click the {button Advanced…} button above and to the right of ''''''Message Storage''''''.
# Check the ''''''Override Office365 Settings'''''' box.
# Enter the application ID your organization has set up in the ''''''Application ID'''''' field. Sometimes it will be called a ''''''Client ID''''''.
# Enter your organization''s Microsoft 365 Tenant ID in the ''''''Tenant ID'''''' field.
# Optionally enter the ''''''Redirect URI''''''. Default value is: ''''''http://localhost''''''.
# Optionally enter the ''''''Endpoint Host''''''. Default value is: ''''''https://login.microsoftonline.com''''''
# Optionally enter the ''''''OAuth scopes''''''. Default values are:
#* For EWS: ''''''https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All offline_access''''''
#* For Graph: ''''''https://graph.microsoft.com/User.Read https://graph.microsoft.com/MailboxFolder.ReadWrite https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.ReadWrite https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.Send https://graph.microsoft.com/offline_access''''''
# Click the {button OK} button to close the ''''''Advanced Account Settings'''''' dialog.
# Accept the ''''''Reauthorize Access'''''' dialog and trigger an email update. Messages should download and the account should operate as normal.

=== US Government Compute Cloud (GCC) ===

GCC does not include the common Thunderbird application ID, so all GCC users must have an organization that registers a Thunderbird application ID for their tenant IDs. When following the instructions above, use the Application ID and Tenant ID provided to you by your administrator and the following values for the other fields:

* Exchange URL:
** For EWS: https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx
** For Graph: https://graph.microsoft.us/
* Redirect URI: http://localhost
* Endpoint Host: https://login.microsoftonline.us

[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
== Per-account OAuth2 IMAP and POP3 customization ==
<!-- IMAP added in 155, POP3 added in 156 -->
As of 156, Thunderbird supports  per-account OAuth2 manual configuration for IMAP and POP3 accounts. Here’s how to customize OAuth for  your IMAP (or POP3) account:

# Create your IMAP (or POP3) account as normal on the first run  or Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email}.
# Enter your full name and any valid non-Exchange email address and then click {button Continue}.
# Click ''''''EDIT CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom right (''''''NOTE'''''': Do not click {button Continue} because it  will trigger our autoconfig process to detect settings).
# Click ''''''Advanced configuration'''''' on the bottom right.
# In the ''''''Confirm Advanced Configuration'''''' dialog that pops up, click {button OK}.
# Click {button Advanced…} in ''''''Server Settings'''''' (just above ''''''Message Storage'''''').
# In the ''''''Advanced Account Settings'''''' dialog that pops up, check ✓ ''''''Use custom OAuth2'''''' settings.
# Customize using the value for your custom OAuth provided by your mail provider or mail administrator and then {button OK} to save the settings.(''''''NOTE'''''': If ''''''Use system web browser'''''' is checked, you must use  ''''''http'''''' not ''''''https'''''' on ''''''localhost'''''' in the ''''''Redirect URI:'''''' field.)
# Select ''''''Authentication Method'''''' {menu OAuth2} (if not previously selected).
[[UI:details_end]]
See [[Custom OAuth for Thunderbird]] for other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_per-account-oauth2-manual-customization-recommended-for-advanced-users"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Per-account OAuth2 manual customization (recommended for advanced users)</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_per-account-oauth2-microsoft-exchange-customization"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_exchange-setup-instructions"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Exchange setup instructions</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_us-government-compute-cloud-gcc"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">US Government Compute Cloud (GCC)</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_per-account-oauth2-imap-and-pop3-customization"><span class="tocnumber">1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Per-account OAuth2 IMAP and POP3 customization</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_per-account-oauth2-manual-customization-recommended-for-advanced-users">Per-account OAuth2 manual customization (recommended for advanced users)</h1>
<p>Who is this for?
</p>
<ul><li> Advanced users
</li><li> Testing
</li><li> Individual account configuration
</li></ul>
<p>If only a single account needs custom OAuth2 settings, Thunderbird also supports configuring OAuth2 directly for an individual account.
</p><p>Unlike the add-on approach, these settings affect only that account and are not intended for organization-wide deployment.
</p><p>This is currently available for Microsoft Exchange and IMAP accounts, with support for POP3 currently under development.
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_per-account-oauth2-microsoft-exchange-customization" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><h3 id="w_exchange-setup-instructions">Exchange setup instructions</h3><ol><li> Add a new email account: Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span> and enter a Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) and then click <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom left of the Account hub.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Microsoft Exchange or Microsoft 365</span> and then click <span class="button">Set up account</span>.
</li><li> In the <strong>Exchange settings</strong> dialog, type one of the following URLs into the <strong>Enter the service URL</strong> field:
<ul><li> For EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> For Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/">https://graph.microsoft.com/</a>
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Find settings</span>. For EWS, <strong>Exchange Web Services</strong> will automatically be selected. For Graph, <strong>Microsoft Graph</strong> will be selected.
</li><li> Your Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) you used in step 1 should be automatically entered into the <strong>Username</strong> field (if not, then correct it).
</li><li> For <strong>Authentication Method</strong>, select <span class="menu">OAuth2</span>.
</li><li> Click <strong>Advanced configuration</strong> above the <span class="button">Connect</span> button.
</li><li> In <strong>Server Settings</strong>, click the <span class="button">Advanced…</span> button above and to the right of <strong>Message Storage</strong>.
</li><li> Check the <strong>Override Office365 Settings</strong> box.
</li><li> Enter the application ID your organization has set up in the <strong>Application ID</strong> field. Sometimes it will be called a <strong>Client ID</strong>.
</li><li> Enter your organization''s Microsoft 365 Tenant ID in the <strong>Tenant ID</strong> field.
</li><li> Optionally enter the <strong>Redirect URI</strong>. Default value is: <strong><a href="http://localhost">http://localhost</a></strong>.
</li><li> Optionally enter the <strong>Endpoint Host</strong>. Default value is: <strong><a href="https://login.microsoftonline.com">https://login.microsoftonline.com</a></strong>
</li><li> Optionally enter the <strong>OAuth scopes</strong>. Default values are:
<ul><li> For EWS: <strong><a href="https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All">https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All</a> offline_access</strong>
</li><li> For Graph: <strong><a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/User.Read">https://graph.microsoft.com/User.Read</a> <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/MailboxFolder.ReadWrite">https://graph.microsoft.com/MailboxFolder.ReadWrite</a> <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.ReadWrite">https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.ReadWrite</a> <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.Send">https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.Send</a> <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/offline_access">https://graph.microsoft.com/offline_access</a></strong>
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">OK</span> button to close the <strong>Advanced Account Settings</strong> dialog.
</li><li> Accept the <strong>Reauthorize Access</strong> dialog and trigger an email update. Messages should download and the account should operate as normal.
</li></ol><h3 id="w_us-government-compute-cloud-gcc">US Government Compute Cloud (GCC)</h3><p>GCC does not include the common Thunderbird application ID, so all GCC users must have an organization that registers a Thunderbird application ID for their tenant IDs. When following the instructions above, use the Application ID and Tenant ID provided to you by your administrator and the following values for the other fields:
</p><ul><li> Exchange URL:
<ul><li> For EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> For Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.us/">https://graph.microsoft.us/</a>
</li></ul>
</li><li> Redirect URI: <a href="http://localhost">http://localhost</a>
</li><li> Endpoint Host: <a href="https://login.microsoftonline.us">https://login.microsoftonline.us</a>
</li></ul><p></p></div>





</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_per-account-oauth2-imap-and-pop3-customization" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Per-account OAuth2 IMAP and POP3 customization</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>
As of 156, Thunderbird supports  per-account OAuth2 manual configuration for IMAP and POP3 accounts. Here’s how to customize OAuth for  your IMAP (or POP3) account:
</p><ol><li> Create your IMAP (or POP3) account as normal on the first run  or Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> Enter your full name and any valid non-Exchange email address and then click <span class="button">Continue</span>.
</li><li> Click <strong>EDIT CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom right (<strong>NOTE</strong>: Do not click <span class="button">Continue</span> because it  will trigger our autoconfig process to detect settings).
</li><li> Click <strong>Advanced configuration</strong> on the bottom right.
</li><li> In the <strong>Confirm Advanced Configuration</strong> dialog that pops up, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Advanced…</span> in <strong>Server Settings</strong> (just above <strong>Message Storage</strong>).
</li><li> In the <strong>Advanced Account Settings</strong> dialog that pops up, check ✓ <strong>Use custom OAuth2</strong> settings.
</li><li> Customize using the value for your custom OAuth provided by your mail provider or mail administrator and then <span class="button">OK</span> to save the settings.(<strong>NOTE</strong>: If <strong>Use system web browser</strong> is checked, you must use  <strong>http</strong> not <strong>https</strong> on <strong>localhost</strong> in the <strong>Redirect URI:</strong> field.)
</li><li> Select <strong>Authentication Method</strong> <span class="menu">OAuth2</span> (if not previously selected).
</li></ol><p></p></div>


</section>
See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth">Custom OAuth for Thunderbird</a> for other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
