INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (367441,'en-US','filelink-large-attachments','Filelink for Large Attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments/revision/367441',359087,'2026-10-31T10:57:18.379769-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fix https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/248',1,'2026-10-31T10:57:51.302304-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,1,'2026-10-31T10:58:17.103291-07:00','rtanglao','approved','[[Template:TBproEarlyBirdInviteOnly]]
{note}''''''NOTE'''''':If you have a Thundermail invite, you can use ''''''Send'''''' as a Filelink provider by signing into Thundermail (Thunderbird app menu  {menu ≡} > {menu Sign in to Thundermail}). Please refer to the following for more details: [[Getting Started with Thunderbird Pro]] and [https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235 our Thunderbird Send Articles].{/note}

With Thunderbird''s FileLink fqeature, you can upload large attachments to the cloud and send a link instead of attaching them directly to your email message. This feature is provided by [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons].

__TOC__

= Thunderbird''s Filelink feature =
Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.

Thunderbird’s Filelink feature eliminates this problem by providing support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service, and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link to that online storage. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.

Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to [[How to Use Attachments|regular attachments]]. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message.

The Thunderbird community has made available a number of [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons] for various providers ([[#w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported|more details below]]).

= Adding Filelink =
To use this feature, you need to install one of the [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons] first, as described below. For some online storage service providers, you may have to first create a new account or configure an existing account with them.
* Click Thunderbird app menu  {menu ≡} > {menu Settings} > {menu Composition} > scroll down to the ''''''Attachments''''''  section and then click on ''''''Find more providers...''''''.
* Choose a Filelink Provider add-on, and click {button ＋ Add to Thunderbird}.
* Confirm add-on permissions from the popup panel by clicking the {button Add} button.
* In the ''''''Attachments'''''' section of the ''''Settings'''' (see above), click on the button {button Add [Your Filelink provider]}.
* Add information as requested by the addon. For example, {button Add WebDAV} if you added the ''''''WebDAV'''''' provider.
* Thunderbird will suggest using Filelink for attachments larger than 5 MB (you can change the threshold in the same section), or you can right-click on a regular attachment to convert it.
* 5MB or larger is the default threshold for FileLink to automatically offer to share an attachment with one of its providers. Change the size by clicking the up and down arrows or un-check to turn off the automatic offer
*;[[Image:tb157-macos-up-down-arrows-filelink]]
= Using Filelink =
When you attach a file to a message, and the file size exceeds the size you have specified above, Thunderbird will automatically offer to use Filelink:
;[[Image:tb157-macos-filelink-offer]]
* Click the {button Link} button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.
* If you prefer to attach the file as a [[How to Use Attachments|regular attachment]], choose {button Ignore}.
After choosing {button Link}, your file will be uploaded in the background and the displayed message will update to:
;[[Image:tb157-macos-filelink-your-file-is-being-linked]]
{note} Regardless of the file size and the Filelink notification, you can always force a file to be attached using Filelink:
* Choose the down arrow of the {button Attach | v} button on the composition toolbar to access the {menu Filelink} submenu.
* Right-click on a regular attachment and from the context menu, choose {menu Convert to...} > {menu [Your FileLink provider]}. You can choose from a list of your enabled providers.{/note}
When the linking is complete – that is, your attachment has been uploaded to the service provider – Thunderbird will automatically add a section in the message with a link to the online copy of your file  as shown below (Filelink provider specific information greyed out in the example below):
;[[Image:tb157-try2-filelink-linked-anonymous]]
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The link section will appear in plain text, if ''''''Compose messages in HTML format'''''' is turned off in Account Settings.{/note}
When your recipients receive the message, they will see the same information and clicking the link in the message will take them to a page where they can download the attachment.

= Filelink FAQ =
Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Thunderbird servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.

== Does Thunderbird know which provider the user has picked? ==
No. Thunderbird is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.

== What storage services are currently supported? ==
* Some popular file storage providers are supported through [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons]. The ''''''Send'''''' Filelink provider that is part of Thundermail is part of Thunderbird 153 and newer. No need to install it.
* You can also use Filelink with your preferred WebDAV server with the [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/filelink-provider-for-webdav/ Filelink provider for WebDAV] add-on.
<!-- [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/dl-for-thunderbird DL for Thunderbird] may be the best if you prefer using your own server instead of relying on third-party providers.-->

== Can the storage service view my attachments? ==
Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment. Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. Service providers will generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service. 

Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, without using Thunderbird’s OpenPGP or SMIME features, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol. We recommend using Thunderbird''s OpenPGP or SMIME features for maximum security of your communications.

== How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site? ==
Depending on the terms and conditions of your third-party storage service provider and your service agreement with them, the file may remain available on the provider’s site for a limited period, a certain number of downloads, or until you explicitly delete it. You will need to log in to the provider’s website to view and delete the files in your personal storage space.

== Can you support service provider X or protocol Y? ==
Please check if the Thunderbird community has provided a [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-on] for the service provider you are looking for.
[https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/cloudFile.html Developer documentation for the Filelink API] is available.

== I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work? ==
Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We are just offering you an option to upload large attachments somewhere else if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file defaults to 5 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird’s Settings:
# Click Thunderbird app menu  {menu ≡} > {menu Settings} > {menu Composition} and scroll down to the ''''''Attachments'''''' section.
# ''''''[✓] Offer to share for files larger than [5] MB''''''
Removing the checkmark will prevent the automatical Filelink notification when you attach big files, but the Filelink feature will still be available from the {button 📎 Attach | v} button dropdown as well as other attachment menus and context menus.','<p></p><div class="warning">NOTE: Thundermail (formerly known as Thunderbird Pro) is currently in its Early Bird release and available  by invitation only. To learn more and join the waitlist, visit <a href="https://tb.pro/">our website</a>. We’ll be opening Thundermail to the public once the Early Bird release concludes.</div>
<p></p>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTE</strong>:If you have a Thundermail invite, you can use <strong>Send</strong> as a Filelink provider by signing into Thundermail (Thunderbird app menu  <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Sign in to Thundermail</span>). Please refer to the following for more details: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-pro">Getting Started with Thunderbird Pro</a> and <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235">our Thunderbird Send Articles</a>.</div>
<p>With Thunderbird''s FileLink fqeature, you can upload large attachments to the cloud and send a link instead of attaching them directly to your email message. This feature is provided by <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a>.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbirds-filelink-feature"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird''s Filelink feature</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_adding-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Adding Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_using-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Using Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_filelink-faq"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Filelink FAQ</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_does-thunderbird-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked"><span class="tocnumber">4.1</span> <span class="toctext">Does Thunderbird know which provider the user has picked?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported"><span class="tocnumber">4.2</span> <span class="toctext">What storage services are currently supported?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments"><span class="tocnumber">4.3</span> <span class="toctext">Can the storage service view my attachments?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site"><span class="tocnumber">4.4</span> <span class="toctext">How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y"><span class="tocnumber">4.5</span> <span class="toctext">Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work"><span class="tocnumber">4.6</span> <span class="toctext">I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_thunderbirds-filelink-feature">Thunderbird''s Filelink feature</h1>
<p>Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.
</p><p>Thunderbird’s Filelink feature eliminates this problem by providing support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service, and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link to that online storage. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.
</p><p>Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to <a href="/en-US/kb/how-use-attachments">regular attachments</a>. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message.
</p><p>The Thunderbird community has made available a number of <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a> for various providers (<a href="#w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported">more details below</a>).
</p>
<h1 id="w_adding-filelink">Adding Filelink</h1>
<p>To use this feature, you need to install one of the <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a> first, as described below. For some online storage service providers, you may have to first create a new account or configure an existing account with them.
</p>
<ul><li> Click Thunderbird app menu  <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Composition</span> &gt; scroll down to the <strong>Attachments</strong>  section and then click on <strong>Find more providers...</strong>.
</li><li> Choose a Filelink Provider add-on, and click <span class="button">＋ Add to Thunderbird</span>.
</li><li> Confirm add-on permissions from the popup panel by clicking the <span class="button">Add</span> button.
</li><li> In the <strong>Attachments</strong> section of the <em>Settings</em> (see above), click on the button <span class="button">Add [Your Filelink provider]</span>.
</li><li> Add information as requested by the addon. For example, <span class="button">Add WebDAV</span> if you added the <strong>WebDAV</strong> provider.
</li><li> Thunderbird will suggest using Filelink for attachments larger than 5 MB (you can change the threshold in the same section), or you can right-click on a regular attachment to convert it.
</li><li> 5MB or larger is the default threshold for FileLink to automatically offer to share an attachment with one of its providers. Change the size by clicking the up and down arrows or un-check to turn off the automatic offer
<dl><dt><img alt="tb157-macos-up-down-arrows-filelink" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-10-05-00-01-07-2e8862.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_using-filelink">Using Filelink</h1>
<p>When you attach a file to a message, and the file size exceeds the size you have specified above, Thunderbird will automatically offer to use Filelink:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb157-macos-filelink-offer" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-10-05-00-26-02-834647.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<ul><li> Click the <span class="button">Link</span> button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.
</li><li> If you prefer to attach the file as a <a href="/en-US/kb/how-use-attachments">regular attachment</a>, choose <span class="button">Ignore</span>.
</li></ul>
<p>After choosing <span class="button">Link</span>, your file will be uploaded in the background and the displayed message will update to:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb157-macos-filelink-your-file-is-being-linked" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-10-05-00-29-00-53b72e.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<div class="note"> Regardless of the file size and the Filelink notification, you can always force a file to be attached using Filelink:
<ul><li> Choose the down arrow of the <span class="button">Attach | v</span> button on the composition toolbar to access the <span class="menu">Filelink</span> submenu.
</li><li> Right-click on a regular attachment and from the context menu, choose <span class="menu">Convert to...</span> &gt; <span class="menu">[Your FileLink provider]</span>. You can choose from a list of your enabled providers.</li></ul></div>

<p>When the linking is complete – that is, your attachment has been uploaded to the service provider – Thunderbird will automatically add a section in the message with a link to the online copy of your file  as shown below (Filelink provider specific information greyed out in the example below):
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb157-try2-filelink-linked-anonymous" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-10-05-00-44-07-ca21de.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The link section will appear in plain text, if <strong>Compose messages in HTML format</strong> is turned off in Account Settings.</div>
<p>When your recipients receive the message, they will see the same information and clicking the link in the message will take them to a page where they can download the attachment.
</p>
<h1 id="w_filelink-faq">Filelink FAQ</h1>
<p>Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Thunderbird servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.
</p>
<h2 id="w_does-thunderbird-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked">Does Thunderbird know which provider the user has picked?</h2>
<p>No. Thunderbird is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.
</p>
<h2 id="w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported">What storage services are currently supported?</h2>
<ul><li> Some popular file storage providers are supported through <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a>. The <strong>Send</strong> Filelink provider that is part of Thundermail is part of Thunderbird 153 and newer. No need to install it.
</li><li> You can also use Filelink with your preferred WebDAV server with the <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/filelink-provider-for-webdav/">Filelink provider for WebDAV</a> add-on.
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>
<h2 id="w_can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments">Can the storage service view my attachments?</h2>
<p>Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment. Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. Service providers will generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service. 
</p><p>Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, without using Thunderbird’s OpenPGP or SMIME features, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol. We recommend using Thunderbird''s OpenPGP or SMIME features for maximum security of your communications.
</p>
<h2 id="w_how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site">How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</h2>
<p>Depending on the terms and conditions of your third-party storage service provider and your service agreement with them, the file may remain available on the provider’s site for a limited period, a certain number of downloads, or until you explicitly delete it. You will need to log in to the provider’s website to view and delete the files in your personal storage space.
</p>
<h2 id="w_can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y">Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</h2>
<p>Please check if the Thunderbird community has provided a <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-on</a> for the service provider you are looking for.
<a href="https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/cloudFile.html">Developer documentation for the Filelink API</a> is available.
</p>
<h2 id="w_i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work">I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</h2>
<p>Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We are just offering you an option to upload large attachments somewhere else if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file defaults to 5 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird’s Settings:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu  <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Composition</span> and scroll down to the <strong>Attachments</strong> section.
</li><li> <strong>[✓] Offer to share for files larger than [5] MB</strong>
</li></ol>
<p>Removing the checkmark will prevent the automatical Filelink notification when you attach big files, but the Filelink feature will still be available from the <span class="button">📎 Attach | v</span> button dropdown as well as other attachment menus and context menus.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "367441", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 31, 2026, 10:57:18 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-31T10:57:18.379769-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fix https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/248", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 31, 2026, 10:57:51 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-31T10:57:51.302304-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Oct 31, 2026, 10:58:17 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-31T10:58:17.103291-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:14:25+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (367784,'en-US','new-thunderbird-desktop','New in Thunderbird Desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop/revision/367784',356683,'2026-11-02T20:00:53.438345-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','discussion https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/243',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','[[T:TBMore]]
This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in [[Thunderbird Release|Thunderbird Release for Desktop]]. For full details, please refer to [[Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues]].

__TOC__
=Thunderbird 159=
* More improvements to Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings}  and Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} strings. Most people will not notice since the changes are subtle and do not affect how you use Thunderbird. More technical and veteran Thunderbird users will notice some changesto frequently usd strings. For example, to access the Thunderbird [[Config Editor]] in 159, you click on {button Advanced settings} instead of {button Config Editor…}.
* More string changes are coming in 160.
* Changes to the Settings and Account Settings user interface itself are currently planned for a post 160 release.
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 158=
==Settings and Account Settings strings==
* Initial improvements to Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings}  and Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} strings.
* Thundermail add-on improvements.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thundebird 157=
==Microsoft migration to Graph and Thundermail improvements==
* If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email, and you’re currently using EWS to connect to your accounts, please read [[Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026]].
* Thundermail add-on improvements.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 156=
==Custom OAuth manual configuration for POP3==
* You can now configure custom OAuth for POP3 (as well as IMAP, Exchange and Graph).
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 155=
==Custom OAuth manual configuration for Exchange and Graph==
* POP3 manual configuration will be added in 156.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 154=
==Microsoft Graph==
* Enabled for Microsoft 365.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 153 / ESR 153 “Meadow”=
* ESR 153 “Meadow” includes all the improvements since 140, plus these version 153 highlights…
==Account Name custom account color indicator for unified folders==
* Unified folders display your custom account color indicator with an account name tooltip.
*# Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > Click your email account, for example <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
*# Click the {button button} underneath ''''''Color:'''''' and select a color for that account, for example pink.
*#; [[Image:tb153-change-account-color]]
*# You will now see an indicator with that color (in our example: pink) for that account in the message list in a unified folder. There will also be a tooltip with the account name (in our example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>).
*#; [[Image:tb-153-account-color-indicator-tooltip]]
==OAuth dialog opens in system web browser==
* [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth]] login for email accounts (for example: Gmail, Thundermail, etc.) opens in the system default web browser. Thunderbird 152 and older versions opened the OAuth login in a Thunderbird tab.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 152=
==Improved Thundermail configuration==
* One-click account setup for [[Thunderbird for Desktop and Thundermail|Thundermail]] accounts.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 151=
==Thundermail configuration==
* [[Thunderbird for Desktop and Thundermail|Thundermail]] configuration is now built-in to Thunderbird.
==EWS OAuth improvements==
* You can now override OAuth provider details for EWS accounts.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 150=
==Account hub is now the default==
* Account hub is opened on the first run of Thunderbird. It is now the default across mail, calendar and address book.

==Alphabetical sorting in Recent Destinations==
* ''''''Recent Destinations'''''' in General Settings now supports alphabetical sorting. See: Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings} > {menu General} > Scroll down to ''''''Sort order:'''''' under ''''''Folder Management Options'''''' and choose {menu Alphabetical} or {menu Most recent}.

==Custom Accent color==
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings} > {menu Appearance}.
# Next to ''''''Define the color used for primary buttons and highlighted elements:'''''', select one of the following buttons: {button Follow operating system}, {button Thunderbird blue}, {button Purple}, {button Orange}, {button Pink}, {button Ink} or {button Teal}.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 149=
==Exchange Improvements==
* Starred Exchange messages now persist across devices.

==Account Hub on for Address Books==
* Address books are now created in Account Hub.

==Dark Mode link color fixed==
* Dark mode link colors are once more readable.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 148=
==NTLM support==
* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NTLM NTLM authentication] is now supported.

==Yahoo, AOL, ATT OAuth2 authentication update==
* Thunderbird switched to a more secure authorization protocol called ''''''PKCE'''''' for Yahoo, AT&T and AOL accounts. This switch adds support for Yahoo/AOL calendars and address books. For full details, see [[Thunderbird and Yahoo]].

==Dark Mode link color issue==
* ''''''Known issue:'''''' Dark mode link colors might be hard to read. ''''''Workaround:'''''' Switch to a different theme. A fix has been developed for a future release; you can follow along in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2018015 bug 2018015].
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 147=
==Reset Folder Order==
* {menu Reset Folder Order} has been fixed (see the [[#w_thunderbird-142|142 section]]). It now restores the original location of a folder that was moved inside another parent folder.

==Localization of folder names==
* All outstanding issues with localization of folder names have been fixed. If you notice any other issues, please [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird file a Thunderbird desktop support request].
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 146=
==Drag and Drop on macOS==
* Drag and Drop for attachments on macOS is working again.

==OpenPGP Keyserver==
* You can now add your own OpenPGP Keyserver:
** Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings} > {menu Privacy & Security} > Under ''''''OpenPGP Keyservers'''''' {menu Add…} Screenshot:
[[Image:tb146-add-open-pgp-keyserver]]
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 145=
==DNS over HTTPS==
* Thunderbird now supports DNS over HTTPS.

==Account Hub supports manual configuration for EWS, IMAP and POP3 accounts==
* In the ''''''Account Hub'''''', once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking {menu MANUAL CONFIGURATION}.
*;[[Image:tb145-account-hub-manual-configuration]]
* ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' in the ''''''Account Hub'''''' supports manual configuration of {menu EWS}, {menu IMAP} and {menu POP3} accounts.
*;[[Image:tb145-ews-imap-pop-account-hub-manual-configuration]]
* Manual configuration of EWS accounts is now supported as per the screenshots in the above two bullet points. For more details, see the [[Thunderbird and Exchange#w_manual-ews-configuration|Manual EWS configuration section of our Thunderbird and Exchange article]].
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 144=
==Exchange enhancements and bug fixes==
* Another bug fix release.
* Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions. We are planning a promotional splash in one of the next few releases. Getting closer!
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 143=
==Exchange Support enhancements and bug fixes==
* Bug fixes.
* Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 142=
==Switch to the old Account Setup flow to fix issues with Hotmail and outlook&#x2E;com accounts==
If you see: ''''''Exchange auth error'''''' or ''''''Authentication failed. Please check the username and password'''''' when adding a ''''''Hotmail'''''' or ''''''outlook.com'''''' account, here''s how to switch to the old ''''''Account Setup'''''' flow to fix the problem:
* Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings} > {menu General} and untick ''''''Create accounts in the new Account Hub'''''':
*;[[Image:tb142-disable-account-hub|width=350]]
* Please try adding the email account again. If you still have issues, please [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form file a support request on SUMO].
This will be fixed in a future Thunderbird release.

==Reset manual folder sorting order==
# Right-click ({key Control} + click on macOS) on the account row, for example ''''''nemo@thunderbird.net'''''' in the ''''Folder Pane'''' (more details: [[Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)]]) and then:
# Click the menu item {menu Reset Folder Order} to clear any previously defined manual sorting. ''''''Note:'''''' This feature doesn’t restore the original location of a folder that was moved into another parent folder; this will be fixed in a future release. You can follow along in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979880 bug 1979880].
#;[[Image:tb142-reset-folder-order|width=350]]

==Add a visual signature to a PDF==
Just like Firefox, you can now insert your signature into a PDF by typing, drawing or adding an image. Please refer to the Firefox article for full details: [[View and edit PDF files using Firefox’s built-in viewer#w_add-a-signature-to-a-pdf]].

==Folders erroneously renamed to ‘Junk’ will be renamed back to ‘Spam’ and ‘Bulk’ in a post 142 release==
See the [[#w_thunderbird-141|141 section]] for more details.

==More stable Exchange support==
This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.

Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 141=
==Archive action added to new email notifications==
* You can now {button Archive} email from your operating system''s new email native notifications (in addition to {button Mark as Read}, {button Delete}, {button Mark as Starred} and {button Mark as Spam}). Please see [[New email notifications in Thunderbird]] for further information.

==Better Exchange support==
Now, anyone can try the Exchange support in Thunderbird. For most, we recommend waiting for a future release, which will be more stable and include additional features, such as on-premises OAuth authentication (password authentication for on-premises is supported in this release).

This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.

Please refer to our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.

== 31-day warning for PGP Key expiry ==
If your PGP key expires in the next 31 days, you will receive a warning when you are composing an email. For extra security, you can choose to let it expire or you can extend your key''s certificate.

More info on PGP encrypted email can be found in [[Introduction to End-to-end encryption in Thunderbird]] and [[OpenPGP in Thunderbird - HOWTO and FAQ]].

== Folders erroneously renamed to ‘Junk’ will be renamed back to ‘Spam’ and ‘Bulk’ in a post 142 release ==
This is primarily a problem in the English language locales. It was discovered a few days after the release of 141. You can follow the progress of the fix in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979511 bug 1979511].
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 140 / ESR 140 Eclipse=
==Dark Message Mode==
Thunderbird now automatically displays your messages in dark mode when enabled in your operating system settings. If there are display issues with a message in dark mode, you can disable dark mode for that message by sliding the toggle to the right to disable dark message mode.
;[[Image:tb140-dark-mode-toggle]]

==New Appearance Panel in Settings==
The new Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu Appearance}, allows you to toggle your message list layout between Card and Table view, adjust your Cards view, and set your default folder sorting order and threading options. For more details, refer to [[Appearance Panel - Layout, Message List, Card View, Table View, Sorting and Threading settings]].

==Native OS Notifications==
Thunderbird now uses your operating system''s built-in notifications, whether you’re on Windows, Linux or macOS. You can now quickly delete, archive or use customizable actions directly from your notifications.

==Redesigned Account hub==
The newly redesigned Account hub makes it easier to add an account. Connect all of your emails, address books and calendars in a few easy steps. The Account hub is currently not enabled for your first account (you will see it when you add a second account). It is also not enabled by default for your address book. You can change the defaults in your Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu General} > search for ''''''Account hub'''''' (not enabled in ESR; we recommend the latest monthly release for the best experience).

==Manual Folder Sorting==
Drag and drop your folders to get the order you want.

==Experimental Exchange Support for advanced users==
Preliminary Microsoft Exchange support is available for you to try. Our current, initial implementation of Exchange is limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.

We recommend you try this in a test [[Use multiple profiles in Thunderbird|Thunderbird profile]] in [[Thunderbird Beta]] (please ensure you back up your profile before starting). Please refer to our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.

==Export for Mobile==
Generate a QR code to quickly transfer your account settings and credentials to your Thunderbird for Android app. Please refer to [[Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android]].

==Horizontal Scroll for Table View==
Table view now supports horizontal scrolling. This allows you to view wide messages – for example, data in a table with many columns.
[[UI:details_end]]
=Older Releases=
