<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-145">Thunderbird 145</h1>
<h2 id="w_dns-over-https" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-23" aria-expanded="false">DNS over HTTPS</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-23" aria-hidden="true"><li> Thunderbird now supports DNS over HTTPS.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_account-hub-supports-manual-configuration-for-ews-imap-and-pop3-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-24" aria-expanded="false">Account Hub supports manual configuration for EWS, IMAP and POP3 accounts</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-24" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> In the <strong>Account Hub</strong>, once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking <span class="menu">MANUAL CONFIGURATION</span>.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb145-account-hub-manual-configuration" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-12-23-06-35-21b46c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> in the <strong>Account Hub</strong> supports manual configuration of <span class="menu">EWS</span>, <span class="menu">IMAP</span> and <span class="menu">POP3</span> accounts.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb145-ews-imap-pop-account-hub-manual-configuration" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-12-23-55-15-82277a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Manual configuration of EWS accounts is now supported as per the screenshots in the above two bullet points. For more details, see the <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange#w_manual-ews-configuration">Manual EWS configuration section of our Thunderbird and Exchange article</a>.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-144">Thunderbird 144</h1>
<h2 id="w_exchange-enhancements-and-bug-fixes" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-25" aria-expanded="false">Exchange enhancements and bug fixes</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-25" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Another bug fix release.
</li><li> Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions. We are planning a promotional splash in one of the next few releases. Getting closer!
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-143">Thunderbird 143</h1>
<h2 id="w_exchange-support-enhancements-and-bug-fixes" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-26" aria-expanded="false">Exchange Support enhancements and bug fixes</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-26" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Bug fixes.
</li><li> Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-142">Thunderbird 142</h1>
<h2 id="w_switch-to-the-old-account-setup-flow-to-fix-issues-with-hotmail-and-outlookx2ecom-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-27" aria-expanded="false">Switch to the old Account Setup flow to fix issues with Hotmail and <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> accounts</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-27" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you see: <strong>Exchange auth error</strong> or <strong>Authentication failed. Please check the username and password</strong> when adding a <strong>Hotmail</strong> or <strong><a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a></strong> account, here''s how to switch to the old <strong>Account Setup</strong> flow to fix the problem:
</p><ul><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> and untick <strong>Create accounts in the new Account Hub</strong>:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb142-disable-account-hub" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-27-10-54-39-2636ed.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="350">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Please try adding the email account again. If you still have issues, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">file a support request on SUMO</a>.
</li></ul><p>This will be fixed in a future Thunderbird release.
</p></div>



<h2 id="w_reset-manual-folder-sorting-order" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-28" aria-expanded="false">Reset manual folder sorting order</button></h2>
<ol class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-28" aria-hidden="true"><li> Right-click (<span class="key">Control</span> + click on macOS) on the account row, for example <strong><a href="mailto:nemo@thunderbird.net">nemo@thunderbird.net</a></strong> in the <em>Folder Pane</em> (more details: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova">Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)</a>) and then:
</li><li> Click the menu item <span class="menu">Reset Folder Order</span> to clear any previously defined manual sorting. <strong>Note:</strong> This feature doesn’t restore the original location of a folder that was moved into another parent folder; this will be fixed in a future release. You can follow along in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979880">bug 1979880</a>.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb142-reset-folder-order" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-00-23-24-8d62b7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="350">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_add-a-visual-signature-to-a-pdf" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-29" aria-expanded="false">Add a visual signature to a PDF</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-29" aria-hidden="true">Just like Firefox, you can now insert your signature into a PDF by typing, drawing or adding an image. Please refer to the Firefox article for full details: <a href="/en-US/kb/view-pdf-files-firefox-or-choose-another-viewer#w_add-a-signature-to-a-pdf">View and edit PDF files using Firefox’s built-in viewer</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-30" aria-expanded="false">Folders erroneously renamed to ‘Junk’ will be renamed back to ‘Spam’ and ‘Bulk’ in a post 142 release</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-30" aria-hidden="true">See the <a href="#w_thunderbird-141">141 section</a> for more details.
</p>
<h2 id="w_more-stable-exchange-support" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-31" aria-expanded="false">More stable Exchange support</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-31" aria-hidden="true"><p>This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
</p><p>Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p></div>
</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-141">Thunderbird 141</h1>
<h2 id="w_archive-action-added-to-new-email-notifications" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-32" aria-expanded="false">Archive action added to new email notifications</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-32" aria-hidden="true"><li> You can now <span class="button">Archive</span> email from your operating system''s new email native notifications (in addition to <span class="button">Mark as Read</span>, <span class="button">Delete</span>, <span class="button">Mark as Starred</span> and <span class="button">Mark as Spam</span>). Please see <a href="/en-US/kb/new-email-notifications-thunderbird">New email notifications in Thunderbird</a> for further information.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_better-exchange-support" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-33" aria-expanded="false">Better Exchange support</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-33" aria-hidden="true"><p>Now, anyone can try the Exchange support in Thunderbird. For most, we recommend waiting for a future release, which will be more stable and include additional features, such as on-premises OAuth authentication (password authentication for on-premises is supported in this release).
</p><p>This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
</p><p>Please refer to our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p></div>

<h2 id="w_31-day-warning-for-pgp-key-expiry" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-34" aria-expanded="false">31-day warning for PGP Key expiry</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-34" aria-hidden="true"><p>If your PGP key expires in the next 31 days, you will receive a warning when you are composing an email. For extra security, you can choose to let it expire or you can extend your key''s certificate.
</p><p>More info on PGP encrypted email can be found in <a href="/en-US/kb/introduction-to-e2e-encryption">Introduction to End-to-end encryption in Thunderbird</a> and <a href="/en-US/kb/openpgp-thunderbird-howto-and-faq">OpenPGP in Thunderbird - HOWTO and FAQ</a>.
</p></div>

<h2 id="w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release_2" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-35" aria-expanded="false">Folders erroneously renamed to ‘Junk’ will be renamed back to ‘Spam’ and ‘Bulk’ in a post 142 release</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-35" aria-hidden="true">This is primarily a problem in the English language locales. It was discovered a few days after the release of 141. You can follow the progress of the fix in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979511">bug 1979511</a>.
</p></section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-140-esr-140-eclipse">Thunderbird 140 / ESR 140 Eclipse</h1>
<h2 id="w_dark-message-mode" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-36" aria-expanded="false">Dark Message Mode</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-36" aria-hidden="true"><p>Thunderbird now automatically displays your messages in dark mode when enabled in your operating system settings. If there are display issues with a message in dark mode, you can disable dark mode for that message by sliding the toggle to the right to disable dark message mode.
</p><dl><dt><img alt="tb140-dark-mode-toggle" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-06-29-22-50-16-bf91b7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl></div>


<h2 id="w_new-appearance-panel-in-settings" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-37" aria-expanded="false">New Appearance Panel in Settings</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-37" aria-hidden="true">The new Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Appearance</span>, allows you to toggle your message list layout between Card and Table view, adjust your Cards view, and set your default folder sorting order and threading options. For more details, refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/appearance-panel-layout-message-list-card-view-tab">Appearance Panel - Layout, Message List, Card View, Table View, Sorting and Threading settings</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_native-os-notifications" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-38" aria-expanded="false">Native OS Notifications</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-38" aria-hidden="true">Thunderbird now uses your operating system''s built-in notifications, whether you’re on Windows, Linux or macOS. You can now quickly delete, archive or use customizable actions directly from your notifications.
</p>
<h2 id="w_redesigned-account-hub" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-39" aria-expanded="false">Redesigned Account hub</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-39" aria-hidden="true">The newly redesigned Account hub makes it easier to add an account. Connect all of your emails, address books and calendars in a few easy steps. The Account hub is currently not enabled for your first account (you will see it when you add a second account). It is also not enabled by default for your address book. You can change the defaults in your Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> &gt; search for <strong>Account hub</strong> (not enabled in ESR; we recommend the latest monthly release for the best experience).
</p>
<h2 id="w_manual-folder-sorting" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-40" aria-expanded="false">Manual Folder Sorting</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-40" aria-hidden="true">Drag and drop your folders to get the order you want.
</p>
<h2 id="w_experimental-exchange-support-for-advanced-users" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-41" aria-expanded="false">Experimental Exchange Support for advanced users</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-41" aria-hidden="true"><p>Preliminary Microsoft Exchange support is available for you to try. Our current, initial implementation of Exchange is limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
</p><p>We recommend you try this in a test <a href="/en-US/kb/using-multiple-profiles">Thunderbird profile</a> in <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-beta">Thunderbird Beta</a> (please ensure you back up your profile before starting). Please refer to our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p></div>

<h2 id="w_export-for-mobile" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-42" aria-expanded="false">Export for Mobile</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-42" aria-hidden="true">Generate a QR code to quickly transfer your account settings and credentials to your Thunderbird for Android app. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import">Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_horizontal-scroll-for-table-view" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-43" aria-expanded="false">Horizontal Scroll for Table View</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-43" aria-hidden="true">Table view now supports horizontal scrolling. This allows you to view wide messages – for example, data in a table with many columns.
</p></section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_older-releases">Older Releases</h1>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-139" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-44" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 139</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-44" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> You can now manually sort folders by dragging and dropping them in the folder pane.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-138" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-45" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 138</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-45" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Dark mode and high contrast mode improvements.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-137" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-46" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 137</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-46" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Fixed Two-factor authentication via text or email for Office 365 using OAuth2.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-136" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-47" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 136</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-47" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> New Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Appearance</span>.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<p></p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "367787", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 2, 2026, 8:11:40 PM", "datetime": "2026-11-02T20:11:40.036194-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "don not translate, needs Wayne''s approval, fix another typo: discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/243", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 2, 2026, 8:11:55 PM", "datetime": "2026-11-02T20:11:55.795394-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Nov 3, 2026, 6:23:07 AM", "datetime": "2026-11-03T06:23:07.078591-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:14:14+00:00');
INSERT INTO revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at) VALUES (367788,'en-US','config-editor','Config Editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor/revision/367788',284537,'2026-11-02T20:29:03.371078-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip do not translate please, needs wayne''s approval. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/251',1,'2026-11-02T20:29:44.804022-08:00','rtanglao',1,1,1,'2026-11-02T22:39:38.389438-08:00','wsmwk','approved','{warning} The Config Editor (about:config) is a powerful tool, intended to be used by software developers. Setting values to something other than default values is not supported, because Thunderbird developers may change or deprecate the preference in the future. Changes made to preferences can break Thunderbird at any time and make troubleshooting more difficult.{/warning}

Thunderbird''s Config Editor may be used to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings, which are not accessible through the normal user interface. Modifications made are unsupported and used are at your own risk.

To access the Config Editor:
* Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > {menu Settings} > {button General}
* Scroll down to the bottom and click {for not tb159}{button Config Editor…}{/for}{for tb159}{button Advanced settings}{/for}

[[Image:Warning on Config editor window]]

Clicking on the {button Show All} button in the display will show all hidden preferences in a tab called ''''Advanced Preferences''''.

[[Image:Image of typical config editor tab]]

In the Config Editor, you can type a string in the field showing ''''''Search preference name'''''' to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold. There is a revert arrow on the end of the entry to change the setting to the default value {button [[Image:tb115-undo-16x16]]}{/note}
To edit a boolean preference ({pref true}/{pref false} value), click on the ''''Toggle'''' [[Image:Config Editor Toggle]] button. For text preferences, double-clicking on the preference will enable in-place editing. Once the edit is complete, save your changes by clicking the checkmark [[Image:Config Editor Save Checkmark]]

In rare cases, you may need to add a new preference. You should not normally need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by Thunderbird documentation or one of its add-ons.

To add a new preference:
# Type or paste the preference name into the search box.
# Select the appropriate preference type (number, string or [https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Boolean boolean]) from the offered options.
# Click on the ''''Add'''' {button [[Image:Config Editor Add]]} to add the preference.
To delete a previously added preference, click the trash icon following the entry {button [[Image:Copnfig Editor Trash Icon]]}

Changes to preferences are stored immediately. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to its default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file. Only non-default preferences are stored in the preferences file.','<div class="warning"> The Config Editor (about:config) is a powerful tool, intended to be used by software developers. Setting values to something other than default values is not supported, because Thunderbird developers may change or deprecate the preference in the future. Changes made to preferences can break Thunderbird at any time and make troubleshooting more difficult.</div>
<p>Thunderbird''s Config Editor may be used to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings, which are not accessible through the normal user interface. Modifications made are unsupported and used are at your own risk.
</p><p>To access the Config Editor:
</p>
<ul><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="button">General</span>
</li><li> Scroll down to the bottom and click <span class="for" data-for="not tb159" style="display: none;"><span class="button">Config Editor…</span></span><span class="for" data-for="tb159"><span class="button">Advanced settings</span></span>
</li></ul>
<p><img alt="Warning on Config editor window" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-12-01-9fa64c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>Clicking on the <span class="button">Show All</span> button in the display will show all hidden preferences in a tab called <em>Advanced Preferences</em>.
</p><p><img alt="Image of typical config editor tab" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-17-44-66b6d9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>In the Config Editor, you can type a string in the field showing <strong>Search preference name</strong> to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold. There is a revert arrow on the end of the entry to change the setting to the default value <span class="button"><img alt="tb115-undo-16x16" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2023-10-23-10-00-44-2dce8f.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span></div>
<p>To edit a boolean preference (<span class="pref">true</span>/<span class="pref">false</span> value), click on the <em>Toggle</em> <img alt="Config Editor Toggle" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-25-33-564c26.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> button. For text preferences, double-clicking on the preference will enable in-place editing. Once the edit is complete, save your changes by clicking the checkmark&nbsp;<img alt="Config Editor Save Checkmark" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-29-50-a19d13.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>In rare cases, you may need to add a new preference. You should not normally need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by Thunderbird documentation or one of its add-ons.
</p><p>To add a new preference:
</p>
<ol><li> Type or paste the preference name into the search box.
</li><li> Select the appropriate preference type (number, string or <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Boolean">boolean</a>) from the offered options.
</li><li> Click on the <em>Add</em> <span class="button"><img alt="Config Editor Add" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-38-25-7cf634.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> to add the preference.
</li></ol>
<p>To delete a previously added preference, click the trash icon following the entry <span class="button"><img alt="Copnfig Editor Trash Icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-39-45-5e5ea2.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</p><p>Changes to preferences are stored immediately. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to its default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file. Only non-default preferences are stored in the preferences file.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "367788", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 2, 2026, 8:29:03 PM", "datetime": "2026-11-02T20:29:03.371078-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip do not translate please, needs wayne''s approval. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/251", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 2, 2026, 8:29:44 PM", "datetime": "2026-11-02T20:29:44.804022-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Nov 2, 2026, 10:39:38 PM", "datetime": "2026-11-02T22:39:38.389438-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:14:11+00:00');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54335,'en-US','thunderbird-and-gmail','Thunderbird and Gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail/revision/54335',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s [http://gmail.google.com Gmail] service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.

__TOC__

= Configuring your Gmail account =

To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail, first enable IMAP on your Gmail account. 
See [http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?answer=77695 Gmail''s instructions] for help. (IMAP is enabled on new Gmail accounts by default.)

Next, select {menu File | New | Mail Account}. The {button Mail Account Setup} dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information, Thunderbird will automatically configure your Gmail connection. See [[Automatic Account Configuration]] for more information.

To manually configure a Gmail account, see [[Configure an Account]] for instructions (which includes Gmail-specific examples). Also, refer to  [http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?hl=en&answer=180189 Gmail''s Thunderbird version 3] instructions.

See also [http://mail.google.com/support/bin/topic.py?topic=12806 Gmail''s IMAP tips].

= Archiving messages =

In order to be consistent with Gmail''s behavior, Thunderbird archives your message in the "All Mail" folder, and not in an "Archive" folder as it does for other accounts (as described in the [[Archived Messages]] article).

[[Image:Gmail Archive settings]] 

Note that the {button Archive options...} button is disabled for Gmail accounts, as it is not necessary to define the archive granularity with Gmail.

= Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages =
== Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders ==
Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. In this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in Gmail, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.

In the example shown below, the folders at the same level as the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder ("Personal", "Receipts", "Travel" and "Work" and "g+") correspond to Gmail labels.<br>

[[Image:default gmail folders]] <br>

There is also a set of special sub-folders for the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder:
* All Mail: contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including sent and archived messages. Any messages that you see in the inbox will also appear in the ''''[Gmail]/All Mail'''' folder.
* Drafts: contains draft messages.
* Sent Mail: contains sent messages.
* Spam: contains messages that were marked as spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Starred: contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Trash: contains deleted messages.
* Important: contains messages that Gmail has flagged as "important". (See [http://support.google.com/mail/bin/answer.py?hl=en&answer=186531&topic=1669050&ctx=topic Priority Inbox overview] for more details about this Gmail feature.)

Note that a message can have multiple labels (for instance, "Personal", "Travel", "All Mail" and "Starred"). In this case, a copy of this message will be downloaded and displayed in all the corresponding Thunderbird''s folders.

== Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels) ==

You can configure which Gmail folders you want to display in Thunderbird. By default all Gmail folders are displayed, as described above. To configure folder display, right-click any folder and select {menu Subscribe...}. <br>

[[Image:subscribe gmail folders]] 

Uncheck any folders that you don''t want to display in Thunderbird. Messages will still be downloaded and available through the "All Mail" folder. They will also be included in search results. However, the Gmail label (Thunderbird folder) will not be displayed. 


== Synchronizing folders (labels) ==

If you want to see all your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid all the messages from being downloaded to your system, you may want to tweak the synchronization settings. This allows you to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don''t synchronize a folder, the message header will still be displayed in Thunderbird, but the message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message header.

To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders. ({for win,mac}{menu Tools | Account Settings...}{/for}{for linux}{menu Edit | Account Settings...}{/for}) See [[IMAP Synchronization#w_synchronization]] for more information.


= Useful Add-ons =
* If you want to sync your contacts between Thunderbird and Gmail, you should try either the [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/google-contacts/ Google Contacts] or [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/gcontactsync gContactSync] add-ons.
* If you want to sync your calendar between Lightning and Google Calendar, you should read this support article: [[Using Lightning with Google Calendar]].
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/flat-folder-tree Flat Folder Tree]  is useful to show all the sub-folders of the [Gmail] Imap''s folder at the same level than your others folders.
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gmailbuttons Gmail Buttons] makes is easier to move messages to the Trash and Spam folders. Beta version also shows all Gmail labels for each message.

= Gmail and BCC addresses =

When you are using a mail client (such as Thunderbird, Outlook, Entourage, etc)  with a Gmail account and your account is configured to synchronize mail using the IMAP protocol, you cannot see BCC message recipients when you look at the message in the Sent Mail folder. (See [[Configure an Account]] for a description of the IMAP and POP protocols.)

For example, say that you sent a message with the following recipients:

[[Image:75cc304ad20b8d48904ad675f7dafcef-1295389047-245-1.jpg]]

If you subsequently viewed the message in the "Sent Mail" folder, only the "To" and "CC" addresses would be shown as message recipients. This is because Gmail strips the BCC recipient(s) from messages. The message is sent to the BCC recipient, but the BCC recipient is not displayed in the saved sent message. (The BCC recipient is also not displayed on Gmail''s web-based interface if the message originated in Thunderbird.)

To work around this problem, use the Gmail web interface to send messages when you need to include a BCC recipient. Alternatively, use an SMTP server other than Gmail''s. For example, your Internet Service Provider (ISP) probably provides access to an SMTP server, which you could use to send messages. (See [[Configure an Account]] for help.)

For more information, see [http://groups.google.com/group/Gmail-Help-POP-and-IMAP-en/browse_thread/thread/5cd20b57ab6cde83/894d0edd75c1b25f?lnk=gst&q=imap+bcc+list&pli=1 Gmail''s support article] on this topic.','<p>Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s <a href="http://gmail.google.com">Gmail</a> service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-your-gmail-account"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring your Gmail account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_archiving-messages"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Archiving messages</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_synchronizing-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">Synchronizing folders (labels)</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_useful-add-ons"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Useful Add-ons</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_gmail-and-bcc-addresses"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Gmail and BCC addresses</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configuring-your-gmail-account">Configuring your Gmail account</h1>
<p>To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail, first enable IMAP on your Gmail account. 
See <a href="http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?answer=77695">Gmail''s instructions</a> for help. (IMAP is enabled on new Gmail accounts by default.)
</p><p>Next, select <span class="menu">File | New | Mail Account</span>. The <span class="button">Mail Account Setup</span> dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information, Thunderbird will automatically configure your Gmail connection. See <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration">Automatic Account Configuration</a> for more information.
</p><p>To manually configure a Gmail account, see <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=Configure+an+Account" title="Page does not exist.">Configure an Account</a> for instructions (which includes Gmail-specific examples). Also, refer to  <a href="http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?hl=en&amp;answer=180189">Gmail''s Thunderbird version 3</a> instructions.
</p><p>See also <a href="http://mail.google.com/support/bin/topic.py?topic=12806">Gmail''s IMAP tips</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_archiving-messages">Archiving messages</h1>
<p>In order to be consistent with Gmail''s behavior, Thunderbird archives your message in the "All Mail" folder, and not in an "Archive" folder as it does for other accounts (as described in the <a href="/en-US/kb/archived-messages">Archived Messages</a> article).
</p><p><img alt="Gmail Archive settings" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-17-04-13-09-41649e.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>Note that the <span class="button">Archive options...</span> button is disabled for Gmail accounts, as it is not necessary to define the archive granularity with Gmail.
</p>
<h1 id="w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</h1>
<h2 id="w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</h2>
<p>Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. In this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in Gmail, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.
</p><p>In the example shown below, the folders at the same level as the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder ("Personal", "Receipts", "Travel" and "Work" and "g+") correspond to Gmail labels.<br>
</p><p><img alt="default gmail folders" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-22-16-07-48-8304a9.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> <br>
</p><p>There is also a set of special sub-folders for the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder:
</p>
<ul><li> All Mail: contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including sent and archived messages. Any messages that you see in the inbox will also appear in the <em>[Gmail]/All Mail</em> folder.
</li><li> Drafts: contains draft messages.
</li><li> Sent Mail: contains sent messages.
</li><li> Spam: contains messages that were marked as spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> Starred: contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> Trash: contains deleted messages.
</li><li> Important: contains messages that Gmail has flagged as "important". (See <a href="http://support.google.com/mail/bin/answer.py?hl=en&amp;answer=186531&amp;topic=1669050&amp;ctx=topic">Priority Inbox overview</a> for more details about this Gmail feature.)
</li></ul>
<p>Note that a message can have multiple labels (for instance, "Personal", "Travel", "All Mail" and "Starred"). In this case, a copy of this message will be downloaded and displayed in all the corresponding Thunderbird''s folders.
</p>
<h2 id="w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</h2>
<p>You can configure which Gmail folders you want to display in Thunderbird. By default all Gmail folders are displayed, as described above. To configure folder display, right-click any folder and select <span class="menu">Subscribe...</span>. <br>
</p><p><img alt="subscribe gmail folders" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-22-16-20-21-1f5531.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>Uncheck any folders that you don''t want to display in Thunderbird. Messages will still be downloaded and available through the "All Mail" folder. They will also be included in search results. However, the Gmail label (Thunderbird folder) will not be displayed. 
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h2 id="w_synchronizing-folders-labels">Synchronizing folders (labels)</h2>
<p>If you want to see all your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid all the messages from being downloaded to your system, you may want to tweak the synchronization settings. This allows you to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don''t synchronize a folder, the message header will still be displayed in Thunderbird, but the message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message header.
</p><p>To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders. (<span class="for" data-for="win,mac"><span class="menu">Tools | Account Settings...</span></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><span class="menu">Edit | Account Settings...</span></span>) See <a href="/en-US/kb/imap-synchronization#w_synchronization">IMAP Synchronization</a> for more information.
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_useful-add-ons">Useful Add-ons</h1>
<ul><li> If you want to sync your contacts between Thunderbird and Gmail, you should try either the <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/google-contacts/">Google Contacts</a> or <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/gcontactsync">gContactSync</a> add-ons.
</li><li> If you want to sync your calendar between Lightning and Google Calendar, you should read this support article: <a href="/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar">Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar</a>.
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/flat-folder-tree">Flat Folder Tree</a>  is useful to show all the sub-folders of the [Gmail] Imap''s folder at the same level than your others folders.
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gmailbuttons">Gmail Buttons</a> makes is easier to move messages to the Trash and Spam folders. Beta version also shows all Gmail labels for each message.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_gmail-and-bcc-addresses">Gmail and BCC addresses</h1>
<p>When you are using a mail client (such as Thunderbird, Outlook, Entourage, etc)  with a Gmail account and your account is configured to synchronize mail using the IMAP protocol, you cannot see BCC message recipients when you look at the message in the Sent Mail folder. (See <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=Configure+an+Account" title="Page does not exist.">Configure an Account</a> for a description of the IMAP and POP protocols.)
</p><p>For example, say that you sent a message with the following recipients:
</p><p><img alt="75cc304ad20b8d48904ad675f7dafcef-1295389047-245-1.jpg" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-05-42-b419eb.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>If you subsequently viewed the message in the "Sent Mail" folder, only the "To" and "CC" addresses would be shown as message recipients. This is because Gmail strips the BCC recipient(s) from messages. The message is sent to the BCC recipient, but the BCC recipient is not displayed in the saved sent message. (The BCC recipient is also not displayed on Gmail''s web-based interface if the message originated in Thunderbird.)
</p><p>To work around this problem, use the Gmail web interface to send messages when you need to include a BCC recipient. Alternatively, use an SMTP server other than Gmail''s. For example, your Internet Service Provider (ISP) probably provides access to an SMTP server, which you could use to send messages. (See <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=Configure+an+Account" title="Page does not exist.">Configure an Account</a> for help.)
</p><p>For more information, see <a href="http://groups.google.com/group/Gmail-Help-POP-and-IMAP-en/browse_thread/thread/5cd20b57ab6cde83/894d0edd75c1b25f?lnk=gst&amp;q=imap+bcc+list&amp;pli=1">Gmail''s support article</a> on this topic.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54335", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:18:01+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54342,'en-US','thunderbird-and-hotmail','Thunderbird and Microsoft email (Office365, Hotmail, outlook.com, etc)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-hotmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-hotmail/revision/54342',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','Thunderbird can be used as a client for all of Microsoft''s email services (Hotmail, Outlook<!-- -->.com and Windows Live Mail, henceforth referred to as "Hotmail"). Thunderbird will download messages from the Hotmail server and store them on your local system. Similarly, Thunderbird can be used to compose messages which are sent through Hotmail''s mail servers. You can still use Hotmail''s web-based application - Thunderbird will query the server and synchronize messages.

__TOC__

==Configuring servers==
Hotmail accounts can be configured in Thunderbird''s [[Automatic Account Configuration]]. This article provides instructions on how to manually [[Manual Account Configuration|configure an account]] or modify an existing account.

To manually configure or modify Hotmail account settings:

# {for win,linux}At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the {/for}{for mac}On the menu bar, click on the {menu Thunderbird} menu, choose {/for}{for linux} {menu Edit}{/for}{for win,mac}{menu Tools}{/for}{for win,linux} menu{/for} and select {menu Account Settings…}. The Account Settings dialog window will be displayed.
# Select your Hotmail account from the list on the left. Make sure your name and account are correctly specified in the {menu Default Identity} section.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailaccounts]]<br><br>
# In the left panel, below your Hotmail account name, click on {menu Server Settings}. The server settings will be displayed in the right panel.

===Configuring IMAP and POP accounts===
{note}''''''Note'''''': For an explanation of IMAP and POP, [https://support.mozillamessaging.com/kb/glossary-terms-including-types-accounts#w_mail-accounts click here].{/note}

====IMAP mail account settings====
*Server Name: imap-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
*Port: 993
*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')
*Connection security: SSL/TLS
*Authentication method: Normal password
*Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailimap]]

====POP mail account settings====
*Server Name: pop-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
*Port: 995
*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')
*Connection security: SSL/TLS
*Authentication method: Normal password
*Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailpop]]

===Configuring the outgoing server===
# At the bottom of the list on the left, select {menu Outgoing Server (SMTP)}. Configured outgoing servers will be displayed in the right panel. The Hotmail server will probably be called ''''''Microsoft Live Hotmail'''''' or something similar.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailsmtp]]<br><br>
# Select the server and click the {button Edit} button to alter the settings. Configure them as follows:
#*Server Name: smtp-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
#*Port: 587
#*Connection security: STARTTLS
#*Authentication method: Normal password
#*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailsmtp1]]','<p>Thunderbird can be used as a client for all of Microsoft''s email services (Hotmail, Outlook.com and Windows Live Mail, henceforth referred to as "Hotmail"). Thunderbird will download messages from the Hotmail server and store them on your local system. Similarly, Thunderbird can be used to compose messages which are sent through Hotmail''s mail servers. You can still use Hotmail''s web-based application - Thunderbird will query the server and synchronize messages.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-servers"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring servers</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configuring-imap-and-pop-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring IMAP and POP accounts</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_imap-mail-account-settings"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">IMAP mail account settings</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_pop-mail-account-settings"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">POP mail account settings</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configuring-the-outgoing-server"><span class="tocnumber">1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring the outgoing server</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h2 id="w_configuring-servers">Configuring servers</h2>
<p>Hotmail accounts can be configured in Thunderbird''s <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration">Automatic Account Configuration</a>. This article provides instructions on how to manually <a href="/en-US/kb/manual-account-configuration">configure an account</a> or modify an existing account.
</p><p>To manually configure or modify Hotmail account settings:
</p>
<ol><li> <span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the </span><span class="for" data-for="mac">On the menu bar, click on the <span class="menu">Thunderbird</span> menu, choose </span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"> <span class="menu">Edit</span></span><span class="for" data-for="win,mac"><span class="menu">Tools</span></span><span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;"> menu</span> and select <span class="menu">Account Settings…</span>. The Account Settings dialog window will be displayed.
</li><li> Select your Hotmail account from the list on the left. Make sure your name and account are correctly specified in the <span class="menu">Default Identity</span> section.<br><br><img alt="sethotmailaccounts" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-09-14-02-33-06-57dcf8.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br><br>
</li><li> In the left panel, below your Hotmail account name, click on <span class="menu">Server Settings</span>. The server settings will be displayed in the right panel.
</li></ol>
<h3 id="w_configuring-imap-and-pop-accounts">Configuring IMAP and POP accounts</h3>
<div class="note"><strong>Note</strong>: For an explanation of IMAP and POP, <a href="https://support.mozillamessaging.com/kb/glossary-terms-including-types-accounts#w_mail-accounts">click here</a>.</div>
<h4 id="w_imap-mail-account-settings">IMAP mail account settings</h4>
<ul><li>Server Name: imap-mail.outlook.com
</li><li>Port: 993
</li><li>User Name: your username (for example <em><a href="mailto:j.kowal@outlook.com">j.kowal@outlook.com</a></em>)
</li><li>Connection security: SSL/TLS
</li><li>Authentication method: Normal password
</li><li>Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br><img alt="sethotmailimap" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-09-14-02-37-04-ed4e42.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ul>
<h4 id="w_pop-mail-account-settings">POP mail account settings</h4>
<ul><li>Server Name: pop-mail.outlook.com
</li><li>Port: 995
</li><li>User Name: your username (for example <em><a href="mailto:j.kowal@outlook.com">j.kowal@outlook.com</a></em>)
</li><li>Connection security: SSL/TLS
</li><li>Authentication method: Normal password
</li><li>Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br><img alt="sethotmailpop" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-09-14-02-37-45-24ee8a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ul>
<h3 id="w_configuring-the-outgoing-server">Configuring the outgoing server</h3>
<ol><li> At the bottom of the list on the left, select <span class="menu">Outgoing Server (SMTP)</span>. Configured outgoing servers will be displayed in the right panel. The Hotmail server will probably be called <strong>Microsoft Live Hotmail</strong> or something similar.<br><br><img alt="sethotmailsmtp" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-09-14-02-44-07-f24e88.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br><br>
</li><li> Select the server and click the <span class="button">Edit</span> button to alter the settings. Configure them as follows:
<ul><li>Server Name: smtp-mail.outlook.com
</li><li>Port: 587
</li><li>Connection security: STARTTLS
</li><li>Authentication method: Normal password
</li><li>User Name: your username (for example <em><a href="mailto:j.kowal@outlook.com">j.kowal@outlook.com</a></em>)<br><br><img alt="sethotmailsmtp1" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2013-09-14-02-41-42-7e14b8.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ul>
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54342", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:18:25+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54356,'en-US','using-identities','Using Identities','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-identities','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-identities/revision/54356',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','When you configure an account, Thunderbird creates a default "identity" for the account that contains information like your name, your email address, the location where your messages should be stored, signature, etc. You can manually create additional identities for your account (via {menu Tools | Account Settings},  {button Manage Identities}). When you create (or reply) to a message, you can specify any of the identities you have configured by selecting it from the ''''''From'''''' drop-down list.

If you are using Google''s Gmail, there are some additional configuration steps described on [[Configure Origin Address for Multiple Gmail Accounts]].

= About identities =

As an example of how identities are useful, consider the scenario where you have a "default" address for a domain (such as "feedback@example.com") that is published on your website. People might send messages to this address on a variety of topics: customer inquiries, vendor inquiries, feedback on the site''s technical issues, etc. 

When you configure multiple identities, you can specify the identity that is used when you reply to a message. For example, you could configure an identity for "sales@example.com", with a name and email address different than "feedback@example.com". Then, when you reply to "feedback@example.com", you could specify the "sales@example.com" identity.

Other uses for identities:

* using an email forwarding service that redirects messages sent to an old email account to a new email account
* switching message configuration for specific messages (such as encryption or SMTP server)

In this article, identities refer to separate configurations for a single account. However, if you have multiple email accounts, there is a default profile configured for each account that is used in the same manner as identities configured for a single account.

= Creating identities =

To create an identity, click {menu Tools | Account Settings}. Select the desired account from the list on the left, then click the {button Manage Identities} button in the bottom right. This displays a list of the identities previously created for the account. To create a new identity, click {button Add}.

Similar to configuring the default identity when you create a new account, the identity dialog enables you to specify various aspects:

*''''''Settings'''''': name, address, signature, etc
*''''''Copies & Folders'''''': the locations where Thunderbird should store messages
*''''''Composition & Addressing'''''': text format, quote management, etc
*''''''Security'''''': digital signatures and encryption

= Using identities =

When you create a new message, Thunderbird uses the default identity for the selected account as the message''s ''''''From'''''' value. Click the drop-down list in the ''''''From'''''' field to select a different identity. The drop-down list will display both identities for the selected account and identities for other accounts.

When you reply to a message or forward a message, Thunderbird scans the recipients of the message and, if available, chooses the identity the original author used when sending the message to you. (Sometimes this information is not available, such as with certain mailing lists.) This identity will then be the default identity in the compose window. You may select a different identity as described above.','<p>When you configure an account, Thunderbird creates a default "identity" for the account that contains information like your name, your email address, the location where your messages should be stored, signature, etc. You can manually create additional identities for your account (via <span class="menu">Tools | Account Settings</span>,  <span class="button">Manage Identities</span>). When you create (or reply) to a message, you can specify any of the identities you have configured by selecting it from the <strong>From</strong> drop-down list.
</p><p>If you are using Google''s Gmail, there are some additional configuration steps described on <a href="/en-US/kb/configure-origin-address-for-multiple-gmail-accounts">Configure Origin Address for Multiple Gmail Accounts</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_about-identities">About identities</h1>
<p>As an example of how identities are useful, consider the scenario where you have a "default" address for a domain (such as "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>") that is published on your website. People might send messages to this address on a variety of topics: customer inquiries, vendor inquiries, feedback on the site''s technical issues, etc. 
</p><p>When you configure multiple identities, you can specify the identity that is used when you reply to a message. For example, you could configure an identity for "<a href="mailto:sales@example.com">sales@example.com</a>", with a name and email address different than "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>". Then, when you reply to "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>", you could specify the "<a href="mailto:sales@example.com">sales@example.com</a>" identity.
</p><p>Other uses for identities:
</p>
<ul><li> using an email forwarding service that redirects messages sent to an old email account to a new email account
</li><li> switching message configuration for specific messages (such as encryption or SMTP server)
</li></ul>
<p>In this article, identities refer to separate configurations for a single account. However, if you have multiple email accounts, there is a default profile configured for each account that is used in the same manner as identities configured for a single account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_creating-identities">Creating identities</h1>
<p>To create an identity, click <span class="menu">Tools | Account Settings</span>. Select the desired account from the list on the left, then click the <span class="button">Manage Identities</span> button in the bottom right. This displays a list of the identities previously created for the account. To create a new identity, click <span class="button">Add</span>.
</p><p>Similar to configuring the default identity when you create a new account, the identity dialog enables you to specify various aspects:
</p>
<ul><li><strong>Settings</strong>: name, address, signature, etc
</li><li><strong>Copies &amp; Folders</strong>: the locations where Thunderbird should store messages
</li><li><strong>Composition &amp; Addressing</strong>: text format, quote management, etc
</li><li><strong>Security</strong>: digital signatures and encryption
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_using-identities">Using identities</h1>
<p>When you create a new message, Thunderbird uses the default identity for the selected account as the message''s <strong>From</strong> value. Click the drop-down list in the <strong>From</strong> field to select a different identity. The drop-down list will display both identities for the selected account and identities for other accounts.
</p><p>When you reply to a message or forward a message, Thunderbird scans the recipients of the message and, if available, chooses the identity the original author used when sending the message to you. (Sometimes this information is not available, such as with certain mailing lists.) This identity will then be the default identity in the compose window. You may select a different identity as described above.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54356", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:19:39+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54366,'en-US','installing-thunderbird-linux','Install Thunderbird on Linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux/revision/54366',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','This article will show you how to install Thunderbird on the Ubuntu distribution of Linux. Other distributions of Linux are similar but are not covered specifically in this article.

Many Linux distributions include Thunderbird by default, and most have a package management system that enables you to easily install Thunderbird. Generally, you should install from the package management system, because it will:

*Ensure that you have all the required libraries
*Install Thunderbird in a way that works best with your distribution
*Create shortcuts to launch Thunderbird
*Make Thunderbird available to all users of your computer
*Make removing Thunderbird work the same as removing any other application 
*Help remind you about Thunderbird updates

Package management also has some disadvantages:

*It may not give you the latest version of Thunderbird
*It may give you a version without Thunderbird branding 

Therefore we recommend installing Thunderbird from the Ubuntu package system which as of Ubuntu 10.04 is the Ubuntu Software Center. See "Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center" below.


__TOC__


= Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center =
== Install the latest official release ==
On Ubuntu systems (10.04 or newer), the easiest way to install is via the Ubuntu Software Center. Note that you will need super user rights. Go to {button Applications} -> {button Ubuntu Software Center} and search for {pref Thunderbird }:

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287599481-461-1.png|]]

Click on {button install} and let the system install the Thunderbird application. 

Once the installation has finished, you will be able to start Thunderbird from {button Applications} -> {button Internet}-> {button Mozilla Thunderbird Mail/News}. Pressing the right button on the item will allow you to copy it to different places, like the panel or your desktop:

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287596800-874-1.png|]]


= Installing via command-line =

 <code>sudo apt-get install thunderbird</code>
This is the command you need to install the latest thunderbird.

= Downloading and installing from the Mozilla Messaging website =

If you choose not to install via your system''s package management system, you can download and install from an installer file. 

#Download the installation archive from Mozilla web site
#Unpack the Thunderbird installation archive
#Create a Menu Item for Thunderbird

== Downloading the Thunderbird archive ==

Open a web browser, go to [http://www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/ www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/] and click the download button to download the en-US version of Thunderbird. For a different language version, select the preferred language in the drop-down box in the lower right corner of the page. On the subsequent page select the type of the installation you want. In the  download window, select {button Save File and} click {button OK}.

== Unpacking the Thunderbird archive ==

When the download has completed, open the Downloads window, right click on the file you have downloaded and select {button Open}.

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325035-314-1.jpg|]]

Click {button Extract}.

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325154-79-1.jpg|]]

Select the {filepath /home/username/} folder and click {button Extract}.
      
When the extraction has ended, click {button Quit} to finish the process. 
      
== Adding Thunderbird to the panel ==

To make it easy to start Thunderbird, you can place a startup shortcut on your desktop or into a panel for fast access. On non-Ubuntu distributions of Linux, you should be able to customize it a similar way as described below for Ubuntu users. See [http://www.ubuntugeek.com/howto-add-entries-in-gnome-menu.html How to add entries in GNOME menu] to learn more. 

= Using Terminal to download and install Thunderbird =

You can perform the whole download and installation procedure in Terminal if you want. The first two steps described above (downloading and unpacking Thunderbird archive) can be done with the following command:

<code>wget ''
http://releases.mozilla.org/pub/mozilla.org/thunderbird/releases/3.1.4/linux-i686/en-US/thunderbird-3.1.4.tar.bz2 -O- | ''
sudo tar xj -C /opt && sudo ln -s /opt/thunderbird/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>

Note that this will also create a link in {filepath /usr/bin/} so that you can launch Thunderbird from the terminal without specifying its folder. Modify the following parts of that command to suit your environment:
*change {pref en-US} to your locale
*change thunderbird-{pref 3.1.4}.tar.bz2 to the version requested.

Enter your password to confirm the action.

To update Thunderbird, you have to launch it as an administrator:
 <code>sudo thunderbird</code>

To uninstall Thunderbird, enter:
 <code>sudo rm -rf /opt/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>

= apt-get Command Line Thunderbird Installation =
Ubuntu Software Center is a graphical front end to the command line "apt-get" system that makes it much easier for non technical users to install Thunderbird. Since apt-get requires command line knowledge and is more technical, it won''t be covered here. 

If you are more technical and wish to use apt-get to install Thunderbird, please consult the [http://wiki.debian.org/Apt apt-get documentation] on the Debian website (Ubuntu Linux is based on Debian Linux).

=See Also=

*[http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch009_ubuntu/ Installing Thunderbird on Ubuntu chapter]  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
*[http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/other-linux-distributions/ Installing Thunderbird on Other Linux Distributions chapter]  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
*[http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch011_removing-thunderbird/ Uninstalling chapter]   in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual','<p>This article will show you how to install Thunderbird on the Ubuntu distribution of Linux. Other distributions of Linux are similar but are not covered specifically in this article.
</p><p>Many Linux distributions include Thunderbird by default, and most have a package management system that enables you to easily install Thunderbird. Generally, you should install from the package management system, because it will:
</p>
<ul><li>Ensure that you have all the required libraries
</li><li>Install Thunderbird in a way that works best with your distribution
</li><li>Create shortcuts to launch Thunderbird
</li><li>Make Thunderbird available to all users of your computer
</li><li>Make removing Thunderbird work the same as removing any other application 
</li><li>Help remind you about Thunderbird updates
</li></ul>
<p>Package management also has some disadvantages:
</p>
<ul><li>It may not give you the latest version of Thunderbird
</li><li>It may give you a version without Thunderbird branding 
</li></ul>
<p>Therefore we recommend installing Thunderbird from the Ubuntu package system which as of Ubuntu 10.04 is the Ubuntu Software Center. See "Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center" below.
</p><p><br>
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_installing-from-the-ubuntu-software-center"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_install-the-latest-official-release"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Install the latest official release</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_installing-via-command-line"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Installing via command-line</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_downloading-and-installing-from-the-mozilla-messaging-website"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Downloading and installing from the Mozilla Messaging website</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_downloading-the-thunderbird-archive"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Downloading the Thunderbird archive</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_unpacking-the-thunderbird-archive"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Unpacking the Thunderbird archive</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_adding-thunderbird-to-the-panel"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">Adding Thunderbird to the panel</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_using-terminal-to-download-and-install-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Using Terminal to download and install Thunderbird</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_apt-get-command-line-thunderbird-installation"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">apt-get Command Line Thunderbird Installation</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_see-also"><span class="tocnumber">6</span> <span class="toctext">See Also</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_installing-from-the-ubuntu-software-center">Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center</h1>
<h2 id="w_install-the-latest-official-release">Install the latest official release</h2>
<p>On Ubuntu systems (10.04 or newer), the easiest way to install is via the Ubuntu Software Center. Note that you will need super user rights. Go to <span class="button">Applications</span> -&gt; <span class="button">Ubuntu Software Center</span> and search for <span class="pref">Thunderbird </span>:
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287599481-461-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-44-8be127.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p>Click on <span class="button">install</span> and let the system install the Thunderbird application. 
</p><p>Once the installation has finished, you will be able to start Thunderbird from <span class="button">Applications</span> -&gt; <span class="button">Internet</span>-&gt; <span class="button">Mozilla Thunderbird Mail/News</span>. Pressing the right button on the item will allow you to copy it to different places, like the panel or your desktop:
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287596800-874-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-03-96f059.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_installing-via-command-line">Installing via command-line</h1>
<pre><code>sudo apt-get install thunderbird</code>
</pre>
<p>This is the command you need to install the latest thunderbird.
</p>
<h1 id="w_downloading-and-installing-from-the-mozilla-messaging-website">Downloading and installing from the Mozilla Messaging website</h1>
<p>If you choose not to install via your system''s package management system, you can download and install from an installer file. 
</p>
<ol><li>Download the installation archive from Mozilla web site
</li><li>Unpack the Thunderbird installation archive
</li><li>Create a Menu Item for Thunderbird
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_downloading-the-thunderbird-archive">Downloading the Thunderbird archive</h2>
<p>Open a web browser, go to <a href="http://www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/">www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/</a> and click the download button to download the en-US version of Thunderbird. For a different language version, select the preferred language in the drop-down box in the lower right corner of the page. On the subsequent page select the type of the installation you want. In the  download window, select <span class="button">Save File and</span> click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_unpacking-the-thunderbird-archive">Unpacking the Thunderbird archive</h2>
<p>When the download has completed, open the Downloads window, right click on the file you have downloaded and select <span class="button">Open</span>.
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325035-314-1.jpg" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-05-41-dca2df.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p>Click <span class="button">Extract</span>.
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325154-79-1.jpg" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-05-41-7488c6.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p>Select the <span class="filepath">/home/username/</span> folder and click <span class="button">Extract</span>.
</p><p>When the extraction has ended, click <span class="button">Quit</span> to finish the process. 
</p>
<h2 id="w_adding-thunderbird-to-the-panel">Adding Thunderbird to the panel</h2>
<p>To make it easy to start Thunderbird, you can place a startup shortcut on your desktop or into a panel for fast access. On non-Ubuntu distributions of Linux, you should be able to customize it a similar way as described below for Ubuntu users. See <a href="http://www.ubuntugeek.com/howto-add-entries-in-gnome-menu.html">How to add entries in GNOME menu</a> to learn more. 
</p>
<h1 id="w_using-terminal-to-download-and-install-thunderbird">Using Terminal to download and install Thunderbird</h1>
<p>You can perform the whole download and installation procedure in Terminal if you want. The first two steps described above (downloading and unpacking Thunderbird archive) can be done with the following command:
</p><p><code>wget ''
http://releases.mozilla.org/pub/mozilla.org/thunderbird/releases/3.1.4/linux-i686/en-US/thunderbird-3.1.4.tar.bz2 -O- | ''
sudo tar xj -C /opt &amp;&amp; sudo ln -s /opt/thunderbird/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>
</p><p>Note that this will also create a link in <span class="filepath">/usr/bin/</span> so that you can launch Thunderbird from the terminal without specifying its folder. Modify the following parts of that command to suit your environment:
</p>
<ul><li>change <span class="pref">en-US</span> to your locale
</li><li>change thunderbird-<span class="pref">3.1.4</span>.tar.bz2 to the version requested.
</li></ul>
<p>Enter your password to confirm the action.
</p><p>To update Thunderbird, you have to launch it as an administrator:
</p><pre><code>sudo thunderbird</code>
</pre>
<p>To uninstall Thunderbird, enter:
</p><pre><code>sudo rm -rf /opt/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>
</pre>
<h1 id="w_apt-get-command-line-thunderbird-installation">apt-get Command Line Thunderbird Installation</h1>
<p>Ubuntu Software Center is a graphical front end to the command line "apt-get" system that makes it much easier for non technical users to install Thunderbird. Since apt-get requires command line knowledge and is more technical, it won''t be covered here. 
</p><p>If you are more technical and wish to use apt-get to install Thunderbird, please consult the <a href="http://wiki.debian.org/Apt">apt-get documentation</a> on the Debian website (Ubuntu Linux is based on Debian Linux).
</p>
<h1 id="w_see-also">See Also</h1>
<ul><li><a href="http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch009_ubuntu/">Installing Thunderbird on Ubuntu chapter</a>  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
</li><li><a href="http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/other-linux-distributions/">Installing Thunderbird on Other Linux Distributions chapter</a>  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
</li><li><a href="http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch011_removing-thunderbird/">Uninstalling chapter</a>   in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54366", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:11:24+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54375,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/54375',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','Thunderbird can save usernames and passwords that you use to access your mail accounts. If you share a computer with anyone, it is recommended that you use a master password.

After you have defined a master password, you will be prompted to enter it whenever Thunderbird needs to access your stored passwords.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The master password only needs to be entered once per Thunderbird session.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__


= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use master passwords to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the {menu Tools} menu, and select {menu Options...}.
# Click the {menu Security} icon.
# Click the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Check mark ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/> <br/>[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]<br/> <br/>
# The Change Master Password dialog appears. As you enter your master password, the Password quality meter indicates the difficulty of guessing the password. To create a quality password, your master password should include the following:
#* At least one capital letter
#* One or more digits
#* At least one non-alphanumeric character, such as the following: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/> <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.
# The master password is defined.

= Changing or removing the master password =
You can change or remove your master password at any time:
# At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the {menu Tools} menu, and select {menu Options...}.
# Click the {menu Security} icon.
# Click the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
#* If you''ve forgotten your master password, see [[Forgot my master password]].
# Enter the new master password twice.
#* To remove the master password, leave the new master password fields empty.
# To accept the changed master password, click {button OK}.
# The master password is changed.


= Forgotten Password =

If you have lost or forgotten your master password or you want to disable the feature, you can reset the master password. Resetting the master password will remove all stored password information. Upon resetting, you will lose all the stored information in the Password Manager, as this is a built-in security feature to prevent people from simply resetting your Master Password to gain access to your passwords.

# Choose {menu Tools -> Error Console}
# Paste the expression <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
# Press the Evaluate button.
# Accept when prompted with the confirmation dialog box.

<!-- MZ credit -->
<br/> <br/>
''''''''''Based on information from  [http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password Master password (mozillaZine KB)]''''''''''','<p>Thunderbird can save usernames and passwords that you use to access your mail accounts. If you share a computer with anyone, it is recommended that you use a master password.
</p><p>After you have defined a master password, you will be prompted to enter it whenever Thunderbird needs to access your stored passwords.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The master password only needs to be entered once per Thunderbird session.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing or removing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_forgotten-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Forgotten Password</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use master passwords to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the <span class="menu">Tools</span> menu, and select <span class="menu">Options...</span>.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Security</span> icon.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Check mark <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br> <br><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> <br>
</li><li> The Change Master Password dialog appears. As you enter your master password, the Password quality meter indicates the difficulty of guessing the password. To create a quality password, your master password should include the following:
<ul><li> At least one capital letter
</li><li> One or more digits
</li><li> At least one non-alphanumeric character, such as the following: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li><li> The master password is defined.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password">Changing or removing the master password</h1>
<p>You can change or remove your master password at any time:
</p>
<ol><li> At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the <span class="menu">Tools</span> menu, and select <span class="menu">Options...</span>.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Security</span> icon.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
<ul><li> If you''ve forgotten your master password, see <a href="/en-US/kb/reset-your-primary-password-if-youve-forgotten-it">Reset your Primary Password if you''ve forgotten it</a>.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
<ul><li> To remove the master password, leave the new master password fields empty.
</li></ul>
</li><li> To accept the changed master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li><li> The master password is changed.
</li></ol>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_forgotten-password">Forgotten Password</h1>
<p>If you have lost or forgotten your master password or you want to disable the feature, you can reset the master password. Resetting the master password will remove all stored password information. Upon resetting, you will lose all the stored information in the Password Manager, as this is a built-in security feature to prevent people from simply resetting your Master Password to gain access to your passwords.
</p>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>
</li><li> Paste the expression <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
</li><li> Press the Evaluate button.
</li><li> Accept when prompted with the confirmation dialog box.
</li></ol>
<p>
<br> <br>
<em><strong>Based on information from  <a href="http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password">Master password (mozillaZine KB)</a></strong></em>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54375", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:14:21+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54438,'en-US','templateoptionspreferences','Template:optionspreferences TB','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/templateoptionspreferences','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/templateoptionspreferences/revision/54438',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','{for win}At the top of the Thunderbird window, click the {menu Tools} menu and select {menu Options...}{/for}{for mac}In the menu bar, click the {menu Thunderbird} menu and select {menu Preferences...}{/for}{for linux}At the top of the Thunderbird window, click the {menu Edit} menu and select {menu Preferences}{/for}','<p><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">At the top of the Thunderbird window, click the <span class="menu">Tools</span> menu and select <span class="menu">Options...</span></span><span class="for" data-for="mac">In the menu bar, click the <span class="menu">Thunderbird</span> menu and select <span class="menu">Preferences...</span></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">At the top of the Thunderbird window, click the <span class="menu">Edit</span> menu and select <span class="menu">Preferences</span></span>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54438", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:15:28+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54448,'en-US','config-editor','Config Editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor/revision/54448',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.

To access the config editor, go to {menu {for mac}Thunderbird | Preferences{/for}{for win}Tools | Options{/for}{for linux}Edit | Preferences{/for}}, select the {menu Advanced} panel, select the {menu General} tab, and click {button Config Editor...}.

[[Image:Config Editor Warning]] 
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful. 

{warning}Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config  editor; therefore please proceed with caution.{/warning}

Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click {button I''ll be careful, I promise!} to proceed.

[[Image:Config Editor]] 
In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Filter" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.

Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.

To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select {menu Modify} (for true/false preferences, select {menu Toggle}). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select {menu Reset}.

In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the {menu New} menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, {menu Reset} it as described above.

Modifications to preferences happen immediately, i.e., you don''t have to click a {button Save} button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect.Note a restart requires all windows to be closed including the config editor window and {for win}Options{/for}{for mac,linux}Preferences{/for} windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!

It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.','<p>You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.
</p><p>To access the config editor, go to <span class="menu"><span class="for" data-for="mac">Thunderbird | Preferences</span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Tools | Options</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">Edit | Preferences</span></span>, select the <span class="menu">Advanced</span> panel, select the <span class="menu">General</span> tab, and click <span class="button">Config Editor...</span>.
</p><p><img alt="Config Editor Warning" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-16-17-917d30.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful. 
</p>
<div class="warning">Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config  editor; therefore please proceed with caution.</div>
<p>Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click <span class="button">I''ll be careful, I promise!</span> to proceed.
</p><p><img alt="Config Editor" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-17-04-220cb6.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Filter" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.
</p><p>Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.
</p><p>To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Modify</span> (for true/false preferences, select <span class="menu">Toggle</span>). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Reset</span>.
</p><p>In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the <span class="menu">New</span> menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, <span class="menu">Reset</span> it as described above.
</p><p>Modifications to preferences happen immediately, i.e., you don''t have to click a <span class="button">Save</span> button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect.Note a restart requires all windows to be closed including the config editor window and <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Options</span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux">Preferences</span> windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!
</p><p>It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54448", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:06:27+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (54492,'en-US','filelink-large-attachments','Filelink for Large Attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments/revision/54492',NULL,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','Thunderbird Migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/','Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44',1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration',1,0,1,'2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00','tb_migration','approved','Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.

Starting in Thunderbird 13, Thunderbird Filelink eliminates this problem by adding support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.

Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to [[how to use attachments|conventional attachments]]. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message. 

The instructions below show how to configure and use Filelink with the [https://one.ubuntu.com/ Ubuntu One] online storage service. Other providers (including [https://www.yousendit.com/ YouSendIt] and [https://www.box.com/ Box]) are also supported. Simply substitute the service you are using as required.

__TOC__

= Configuring Filelink =

To use Filelink, you must first create a new account (or configure an existing account) with an online storage service provider. 

[[Template:optionspreferences]] Click on the {button Attachments} button, then click {button Outgoing}. Select the desired service provider from the drop-down list. Click the {button Add} button. Enter the username for  your online storage account, or click {button Need an account?}.

[[Image:ubuntu-account]]

If you are creating a new account, a browser window will open that displays the service provider''s registration page. Create an account as instructed. The service provider will send you a validation email message. Click the validation link in the message to activate your account. Enter your username (your email address) in the dialog shown above and enter your password when prompted.

In the main configuration dialog, check {button Offer to share for files larger than...} and specify a file size (in megabytes) if you want Thunderbird to prompt you to use Filelink when a message attachment exceeds the specified size.

= Using Filelink =

When you attach a file to a message, if the file size exceeds the amount specified above, Thunderbird will offer to let you use Filelink:

[[Image:filelink-prompt-ubuntu-one]]

To force a file to be attached using Filelink (rather than relying on Thunderbird to determine if the attached file exceeds the specified size), you can click the down arrow next to the {button Attach} button on the message toolbar to access the Filelink menu option.

Click the {button Link} button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.  (Alternatively click {button Ignore} to attach the file as a [[how to use attachments|conventional attachment]].)

[[Image:filelink attachment-ubuntu-one]]

When the recipient receives the message, they will be informed that a file is linked to the message:

[[Image:recipient filelink-ubuntu-one]]

When the recipient clicks on the link in the message, some services like YouSendIt display an intermediary web page with an option to download the file. 

[[Image:filelink link]]

Other services (like Ubuntu One) allow you to download the file immediately without an intermediate web page.


= Filelink FAQ =

Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Mozilla servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.

== Q: Does Mozilla know which provider the user has picked? ==

A: No. Mozilla is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.

== Q: What storage services are currently supported? ==

A: As of Thunderbird 16, we have reached agreement with [https://www.box.com/ Box], [https://one.ubuntu.com/ Ubuntu One] and [https://www.yousendit.com/ YouSendIt] to integrate  their services directly into Thunderbird. You can install support for more providers with add-ons, like:
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dropbox-for-filelink/ Dropbox]
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/fileswapcom-filelink FileSwap.com] 
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dl-for-thunderbird/ DL for Thunderbird] may be the best if you prefer using your own server instead of relying on 3rd-party providers.
* Last but not least, you can use Filelink with a WebDAV server with the [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/webdav-for-filelink WebDAV for Filelink] add-on.

== Q: Can the storage service view my attachments? ==

A: Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment.  Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. (Service providers generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service.) Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol.

== Q: How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site? ==

A: The file will remain available on the storage service provider''s site until you specifically delete it. Log in to the provider''s website to view and delete the files in your storage space.

== Q: Can you support service provider X or protocol Y? ==

A: We plan to support SpiderOak. We also have an "[http://blog.mozilla.org/thunderbird/ Up-for-grabs]" project available if anybody would like to add  support for more services or protocols. Developer documentation for the Filelink feature can be found here: [https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Thunderbird/Filelink_Providers]

== Q: I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work? ==

A: Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We''ve just given you the ability to choose to upload large attachments somewhere else, if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file  defaults to 1 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird''s Preferences. ([[Template:optionspreferences]]) The offer to upload can also be disabled entirely.','<p>Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.
</p><p>Starting in Thunderbird 13, Thunderbird Filelink eliminates this problem by adding support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.
</p><p>Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=how+to+use+attachments" title="Page does not exist.">conventional attachments</a>. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message. 
</p><p>The instructions below show how to configure and use Filelink with the <a href="https://one.ubuntu.com/">Ubuntu One</a> online storage service. Other providers (including <a href="https://www.yousendit.com/">YouSendIt</a> and <a href="https://www.box.com/">Box</a>) are also supported. Simply substitute the service you are using as required.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_using-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Using Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_filelink-faq"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Filelink FAQ</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-does-mozilla-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Q: Does Mozilla know which provider the user has picked?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-what-storage-services-are-currently-supported"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Q: What storage services are currently supported?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">Q: Can the storage service view my attachments?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site"><span class="tocnumber">3.4</span> <span class="toctext">Q: How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y"><span class="tocnumber">3.5</span> <span class="toctext">Q: Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work"><span class="tocnumber">3.6</span> <span class="toctext">Q: I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configuring-filelink">Configuring Filelink</h1>
<p>To use Filelink, you must first create a new account (or configure an existing account) with an online storage service provider. 
</p><p><span class="for" data-for="mac">In the Menu bar at the top of the screen, click <span class="menu">Firefox</span> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span> (or <span class="menu">Preferences</span>, in some cases).</span><span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">Click the menu button <img alt="Fx89menuButton" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-15-11-18-38-e5b736.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span>.</span>
 Click on the <span class="button">Attachments</span> button, then click <span class="button">Outgoing</span>. Select the desired service provider from the drop-down list. Click the <span class="button">Add</span> button. Enter the username for  your online storage account, or click <span class="button">Need an account?</span>.
</p><p><img alt="ubuntu-account" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-13-53-53-6cf0db.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>If you are creating a new account, a browser window will open that displays the service provider''s registration page. Create an account as instructed. The service provider will send you a validation email message. Click the validation link in the message to activate your account. Enter your username (your email address) in the dialog shown above and enter your password when prompted.
</p><p>In the main configuration dialog, check <span class="button">Offer to share for files larger than...</span> and specify a file size (in megabytes) if you want Thunderbird to prompt you to use Filelink when a message attachment exceeds the specified size.
</p>
<h1 id="w_using-filelink">Using Filelink</h1>
<p>When you attach a file to a message, if the file size exceeds the amount specified above, Thunderbird will offer to let you use Filelink:
</p><p><img alt="filelink-prompt-ubuntu-one" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-14-02-35-1c9f4c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>To force a file to be attached using Filelink (rather than relying on Thunderbird to determine if the attached file exceeds the specified size), you can click the down arrow next to the <span class="button">Attach</span> button on the message toolbar to access the Filelink menu option.
</p><p>Click the <span class="button">Link</span> button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.  (Alternatively click <span class="button">Ignore</span> to attach the file as a <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=how+to+use+attachments" title="Page does not exist.">conventional attachment</a>.)
</p><p><img alt="filelink attachment-ubuntu-one" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-14-14-01-1dc88f.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>When the recipient receives the message, they will be informed that a file is linked to the message:
</p><p><img alt="recipient filelink-ubuntu-one" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-14-23-26-d21d6f.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>When the recipient clicks on the link in the message, some services like YouSendIt display an intermediary web page with an option to download the file. 
</p><p><img alt="filelink link" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-05-30-14-10-16-364781.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>Other services (like Ubuntu One) allow you to download the file immediately without an intermediate web page.
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_filelink-faq">Filelink FAQ</h1>
<p>Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Mozilla servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-does-mozilla-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked">Q: Does Mozilla know which provider the user has picked?</h2>
<p>A: No. Mozilla is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-what-storage-services-are-currently-supported">Q: What storage services are currently supported?</h2>
<p>A: As of Thunderbird 16, we have reached agreement with <a href="https://www.box.com/">Box</a>, <a href="https://one.ubuntu.com/">Ubuntu One</a> and <a href="https://www.yousendit.com/">YouSendIt</a> to integrate  their services directly into Thunderbird. You can install support for more providers with add-ons, like:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dropbox-for-filelink/">Dropbox</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/fileswapcom-filelink">FileSwap.com</a> 
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dl-for-thunderbird/">DL for Thunderbird</a> may be the best if you prefer using your own server instead of relying on 3rd-party providers.
</li><li> Last but not least, you can use Filelink with a WebDAV server with the <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/webdav-for-filelink">WebDAV for Filelink</a> add-on.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_q-can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments">Q: Can the storage service view my attachments?</h2>
<p>A: Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment.  Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. (Service providers generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service.) Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site">Q: How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</h2>
<p>A: The file will remain available on the storage service provider''s site until you specifically delete it. Log in to the provider''s website to view and delete the files in your storage space.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y">Q: Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</h2>
<p>A: We plan to support SpiderOak. We also have an "<a href="http://blog.mozilla.org/thunderbird/">Up-for-grabs</a>" project available if anybody would like to add  support for more services or protocols. Developer documentation for the Filelink feature can be found here: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Thunderbird/Filelink_Providers">https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Thun.../Filelink_Providers</a>
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work">Q: I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</h2>
<p>A: Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We''ve just given you the ability to choose to upload large attachments somewhere else, if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file  defaults to 1 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird''s Preferences. (<span class="for" data-for="mac">In the Menu bar at the top of the screen, click <span class="menu">Firefox</span> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span> (or <span class="menu">Preferences</span>, in some cases).</span><span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">Click the menu button <img alt="Fx89menuButton" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-15-11-18-38-e5b736.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span>.</span>
) The offer to upload can also be disabled entirely.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "54492", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Thunderbird Migration", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/tb_migration/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Imported from SUMOMO 2014-02-14 09:44:44", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 13, 2014, 4:00:00 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-13T16:00:00-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "tb_migration", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:08:43+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (56466,'en-US','thunderbird-and-gmail','Thunderbird and Gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail/revision/56466',54335,'2014-02-24T02:12:25-08:00','Swarnava Sengupta','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/','added share template',1,'2014-02-24T02:12:43-08:00','Swarnava',1,0,1,'2014-02-24T02:12:43-08:00','Swarnava','approved','Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s [http://gmail.google.com Gmail] service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.

__TOC__

= Configuring your Gmail account =

To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail, first enable IMAP on your Gmail account. 
See [http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?answer=77695 Gmail''s instructions] for help. (IMAP is enabled on new Gmail accounts by default.)

Next, select {menu File | New | Mail Account}. The {button Mail Account Setup} dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information, Thunderbird will automatically configure your Gmail connection. See [[Automatic Account Configuration]] for more information.

To manually configure a Gmail account, see [[Configure an Account]] for instructions (which includes Gmail-specific examples). Also, refer to  [http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?hl=en&answer=180189 Gmail''s Thunderbird version 3] instructions.

See also [http://mail.google.com/support/bin/topic.py?topic=12806 Gmail''s IMAP tips].

= Archiving messages =

In order to be consistent with Gmail''s behavior, Thunderbird archives your message in the "All Mail" folder, and not in an "Archive" folder as it does for other accounts (as described in the [[Archived Messages]] article).

[[Image:Gmail Archive settings]] 

Note that the {button Archive options...} button is disabled for Gmail accounts, as it is not necessary to define the archive granularity with Gmail.

= Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages =
== Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders ==
Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. In this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in Gmail, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.

In the example shown below, the folders at the same level as the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder ("Personal", "Receipts", "Travel" and "Work" and "g+") correspond to Gmail labels.<br>

[[Image:default gmail folders]] <br>

There is also a set of special sub-folders for the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder:
* All Mail: contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including sent and archived messages. Any messages that you see in the inbox will also appear in the ''''[Gmail]/All Mail'''' folder.
* Drafts: contains draft messages.
* Sent Mail: contains sent messages.
* Spam: contains messages that were marked as spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Starred: contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Trash: contains deleted messages.
* Important: contains messages that Gmail has flagged as "important". (See [http://support.google.com/mail/bin/answer.py?hl=en&answer=186531&topic=1669050&ctx=topic Priority Inbox overview] for more details about this Gmail feature.)

Note that a message can have multiple labels (for instance, "Personal", "Travel", "All Mail" and "Starred"). In this case, a copy of this message will be downloaded and displayed in all the corresponding Thunderbird''s folders.

== Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels) ==

You can configure which Gmail folders you want to display in Thunderbird. By default all Gmail folders are displayed, as described above. To configure folder display, right-click any folder and select {menu Subscribe...}. <br>

[[Image:subscribe gmail folders]] 

Uncheck any folders that you don''t want to display in Thunderbird. Messages will still be downloaded and available through the "All Mail" folder. They will also be included in search results. However, the Gmail label (Thunderbird folder) will not be displayed. 


== Synchronizing folders (labels) ==

If you want to see all your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid all the messages from being downloaded to your system, you may want to tweak the synchronization settings. This allows you to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don''t synchronize a folder, the message header will still be displayed in Thunderbird, but the message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message header.

To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders. ({for win,mac}{menu Tools | Account Settings...}{/for}{for linux}{menu Edit | Account Settings...}{/for}) See [[IMAP Synchronization#w_synchronization]] for more information.


= Useful Add-ons =
* If you want to sync your contacts between Thunderbird and Gmail, you should try either the [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/google-contacts/ Google Contacts] or [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/gcontactsync gContactSync] add-ons.
* If you want to sync your calendar between Lightning and Google Calendar, you should read this support article: [[Using Lightning with Google Calendar]].
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/flat-folder-tree Flat Folder Tree]  is useful to show all the sub-folders of the [Gmail] Imap''s folder at the same level than your others folders.
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gmailbuttons Gmail Buttons] makes is easier to move messages to the Trash and Spam folders. Beta version also shows all Gmail labels for each message.

= Gmail and BCC addresses =

When you are using a mail client (such as Thunderbird, Outlook, Entourage, etc)  with a Gmail account and your account is configured to synchronize mail using the IMAP protocol, you cannot see BCC message recipients when you look at the message in the Sent Mail folder. (See [[Configure an Account]] for a description of the IMAP and POP protocols.)

For example, say that you sent a message with the following recipients:

[[Image:75cc304ad20b8d48904ad675f7dafcef-1295389047-245-1.jpg]]

If you subsequently viewed the message in the "Sent Mail" folder, only the "To" and "CC" addresses would be shown as message recipients. This is because Gmail strips the BCC recipient(s) from messages. The message is sent to the BCC recipient, but the BCC recipient is not displayed in the saved sent message. (The BCC recipient is also not displayed on Gmail''s web-based interface if the message originated in Thunderbird.)

To work around this problem, use the Gmail web interface to send messages when you need to include a BCC recipient. Alternatively, use an SMTP server other than Gmail''s. For example, your Internet Service Provider (ISP) probably provides access to an SMTP server, which you could use to send messages. (See [[Configure an Account]] for help.)

For more information, see [http://groups.google.com/group/Gmail-Help-POP-and-IMAP-en/browse_thread/thread/5cd20b57ab6cde83/894d0edd75c1b25f?lnk=gst&q=imap+bcc+list&pli=1 Gmail''s support article] on this topic.

[[T:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/MoPZt7]]','<p>Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s <a href="http://gmail.google.com">Gmail</a> service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-your-gmail-account"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring your Gmail account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_archiving-messages"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Archiving messages</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_synchronizing-folders-labels"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">Synchronizing folders (labels)</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_useful-add-ons"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Useful Add-ons</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_gmail-and-bcc-addresses"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Gmail and BCC addresses</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configuring-your-gmail-account">Configuring your Gmail account</h1>
<p>To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail, first enable IMAP on your Gmail account. 
See <a href="http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?answer=77695">Gmail''s instructions</a> for help. (IMAP is enabled on new Gmail accounts by default.)
</p><p>Next, select <span class="menu">File | New | Mail Account</span>. The <span class="button">Mail Account Setup</span> dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information, Thunderbird will automatically configure your Gmail connection. See <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration">Automatic Account Configuration</a> for more information.
</p><p>To manually configure a Gmail account, see <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=Configure+an+Account" title="Page does not exist.">Configure an Account</a> for instructions (which includes Gmail-specific examples). Also, refer to  <a href="http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?hl=en&amp;answer=180189">Gmail''s Thunderbird version 3</a> instructions.
</p><p>See also <a href="http://mail.google.com/support/bin/topic.py?topic=12806">Gmail''s IMAP tips</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_archiving-messages">Archiving messages</h1>
<p>In order to be consistent with Gmail''s behavior, Thunderbird archives your message in the "All Mail" folder, and not in an "Archive" folder as it does for other accounts (as described in the <a href="/en-US/kb/archived-messages">Archived Messages</a> article).
</p><p><img alt="Gmail Archive settings" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-17-04-13-09-41649e.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>Note that the <span class="button">Archive options...</span> button is disabled for Gmail accounts, as it is not necessary to define the archive granularity with Gmail.
</p>
<h1 id="w_subscribing-to-folders-and-synchronizing-messages">Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages</h1>
<h2 id="w_understanding-gmail-labels-and-thunderbird-folders">Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders</h2>
<p>Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. In this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in Gmail, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.
</p><p>In the example shown below, the folders at the same level as the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder ("Personal", "Receipts", "Travel" and "Work" and "g+") correspond to Gmail labels.<br>
</p><p><img alt="default gmail folders" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-22-16-07-48-8304a9.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> <br>
</p><p>There is also a set of special sub-folders for the "<i>[Gmail]</i>" folder:
</p>
<ul><li> All Mail: contains all of the messages in your Gmail account, including sent and archived messages. Any messages that you see in the inbox will also appear in the <em>[Gmail]/All Mail</em> folder.
</li><li> Drafts: contains draft messages.
</li><li> Sent Mail: contains sent messages.
</li><li> Spam: contains messages that were marked as spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> Starred: contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
</li><li> Trash: contains deleted messages.
</li><li> Important: contains messages that Gmail has flagged as "important". (See <a href="http://support.google.com/mail/bin/answer.py?hl=en&amp;answer=186531&amp;topic=1669050&amp;ctx=topic">Priority Inbox overview</a> for more details about this Gmail feature.)
</li></ul>
<p>Note that a message can have multiple labels (for instance, "Personal", "Travel", "All Mail" and "Starred"). In this case, a copy of this message will be downloaded and displayed in all the corresponding Thunderbird''s folders.
</p>
<h2 id="w_subscribing-to-or-unsubscribing-from-folders-labels">Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels)</h2>
<p>You can configure which Gmail folders you want to display in Thunderbird. By default all Gmail folders are displayed, as described above. To configure folder display, right-click any folder and select <span class="menu">Subscribe...</span>. <br>
</p><p><img alt="subscribe gmail folders" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-03-22-16-20-21-1f5531.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>Uncheck any folders that you don''t want to display in Thunderbird. Messages will still be downloaded and available through the "All Mail" folder. They will also be included in search results. However, the Gmail label (Thunderbird folder) will not be displayed. 
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h2 id="w_synchronizing-folders-labels">Synchronizing folders (labels)</h2>
<p>If you want to see all your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid all the messages from being downloaded to your system, you may want to tweak the synchronization settings. This allows you to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don''t synchronize a folder, the message header will still be displayed in Thunderbird, but the message content will only be downloaded when you click on the message header.
</p><p>To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders. (<span class="for" data-for="win,mac"><span class="menu">Tools | Account Settings...</span></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><span class="menu">Edit | Account Settings...</span></span>) See <a href="/en-US/kb/imap-synchronization#w_synchronization">IMAP Synchronization</a> for more information.
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_useful-add-ons">Useful Add-ons</h1>
<ul><li> If you want to sync your contacts between Thunderbird and Gmail, you should try either the <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/google-contacts/">Google Contacts</a> or <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/gcontactsync">gContactSync</a> add-ons.
</li><li> If you want to sync your calendar between Lightning and Google Calendar, you should read this support article: <a href="/en-US/kb/using-lightning-google-calendar">Using Thunderbird with Google Calendar</a>.
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/flat-folder-tree">Flat Folder Tree</a>  is useful to show all the sub-folders of the [Gmail] Imap''s folder at the same level than your others folders.
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gmailbuttons">Gmail Buttons</a> makes is easier to move messages to the Trash and Spam folders. Beta version also shows all Gmail labels for each message.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_gmail-and-bcc-addresses">Gmail and BCC addresses</h1>
<p>When you are using a mail client (such as Thunderbird, Outlook, Entourage, etc)  with a Gmail account and your account is configured to synchronize mail using the IMAP protocol, you cannot see BCC message recipients when you look at the message in the Sent Mail folder. (See <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=Configure+an+Account" title="Page does not exist.">Configure an Account</a> for a description of the IMAP and POP protocols.)
</p><p>For example, say that you sent a message with the following recipients:
</p><p><img alt="75cc304ad20b8d48904ad675f7dafcef-1295389047-245-1.jpg" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-05-42-b419eb.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>If you subsequently viewed the message in the "Sent Mail" folder, only the "To" and "CC" addresses would be shown as message recipients. This is because Gmail strips the BCC recipient(s) from messages. The message is sent to the BCC recipient, but the BCC recipient is not displayed in the saved sent message. (The BCC recipient is also not displayed on Gmail''s web-based interface if the message originated in Thunderbird.)
</p><p>To work around this problem, use the Gmail web interface to send messages when you need to include a BCC recipient. Alternatively, use an SMTP server other than Gmail''s. For example, your Internet Service Provider (ISP) probably provides access to an SMTP server, which you could use to send messages. (See <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=Configure+an+Account" title="Page does not exist.">Configure an Account</a> for help.)
</p><p>For more information, see <a href="http://groups.google.com/group/Gmail-Help-POP-and-IMAP-en/browse_thread/thread/5cd20b57ab6cde83/894d0edd75c1b25f?lnk=gst&amp;q=imap+bcc+list&amp;pli=1">Gmail''s support article</a> on this topic.
</p><p>&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "56466", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:12:25 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:12:25-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Swarnava Sengupta", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "added share template", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:12:43 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:12:43-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:12:43 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:12:43-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:17:58+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (56475,'en-US','config-editor','Config Editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor/revision/56475',54448,'2014-02-24T02:31:38-08:00','Swarnava Sengupta','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/','added share template',1,'2014-02-24T02:33:03-08:00','Swarnava',1,0,1,'2014-02-24T02:33:03-08:00','Swarnava','approved','You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.

To access the config editor, go to {menu {for mac}Thunderbird | Preferences{/for}{for win}Tools | Options{/for}{for linux}Edit | Preferences{/for}}, select the {menu Advanced} panel, select the {menu General} tab, and click {button Config Editor...}.

[[Image:Config Editor Warning]] 
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful. 

{warning}Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config  editor; therefore please proceed with caution.{/warning}

Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click {button I''ll be careful, I promise!} to proceed.

[[Image:Config Editor]] 
In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Filter" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.

Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.

To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select {menu Modify} (for true/false preferences, select {menu Toggle}). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select {menu Reset}.

In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the {menu New} menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, {menu Reset} it as described above.

Modifications to preferences happen immediately, i.e., you don''t have to click a {button Save} button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect.Note a restart requires all windows to be closed including the config editor window and {for win}Options{/for}{for mac,linux}Preferences{/for} windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!

It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.

[[T:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/1gx91XC]]','<p>You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.
</p><p>To access the config editor, go to <span class="menu"><span class="for" data-for="mac">Thunderbird | Preferences</span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Tools | Options</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">Edit | Preferences</span></span>, select the <span class="menu">Advanced</span> panel, select the <span class="menu">General</span> tab, and click <span class="button">Config Editor...</span>.
</p><p><img alt="Config Editor Warning" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-16-17-917d30.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful. 
</p>
<div class="warning">Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config  editor; therefore please proceed with caution.</div>
<p>Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click <span class="button">I''ll be careful, I promise!</span> to proceed.
</p><p><img alt="Config Editor" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-17-04-220cb6.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Filter" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.
</p><p>Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.
</p><p>To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Modify</span> (for true/false preferences, select <span class="menu">Toggle</span>). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Reset</span>.
</p><p>In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the <span class="menu">New</span> menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, <span class="menu">Reset</span> it as described above.
</p><p>Modifications to preferences happen immediately, i.e., you don''t have to click a <span class="button">Save</span> button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect.Note a restart requires all windows to be closed including the config editor window and <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Options</span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux">Preferences</span> windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!
</p><p>It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.
</p><p>&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "56475", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:31:38 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:31:38-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Swarnava Sengupta", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "added share template", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:33:03 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:33:03-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:33:03 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:33:03-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:06:24+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (56478,'en-US','installing-thunderbird-linux','Install Thunderbird on Linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux/revision/56478',54366,'2014-02-24T02:52:30-08:00','Swarnava Sengupta','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/','added share template',1,'2014-02-24T02:52:50-08:00','Swarnava',1,0,1,'2014-02-24T02:52:50-08:00','Swarnava','approved','This article will show you how to install Thunderbird on the Ubuntu distribution of Linux. Other distributions of Linux are similar but are not covered specifically in this article.

Many Linux distributions include Thunderbird by default, and most have a package management system that enables you to easily install Thunderbird. Generally, you should install from the package management system, because it will:

*Ensure that you have all the required libraries
*Install Thunderbird in a way that works best with your distribution
*Create shortcuts to launch Thunderbird
*Make Thunderbird available to all users of your computer
*Make removing Thunderbird work the same as removing any other application 
*Help remind you about Thunderbird updates

Package management also has some disadvantages:

*It may not give you the latest version of Thunderbird
*It may give you a version without Thunderbird branding 

Therefore we recommend installing Thunderbird from the Ubuntu package system which as of Ubuntu 10.04 is the Ubuntu Software Center. See "Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center" below.


__TOC__


= Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center =
== Install the latest official release ==
On Ubuntu systems (10.04 or newer), the easiest way to install is via the Ubuntu Software Center. Note that you will need super user rights. Go to {button Applications} -> {button Ubuntu Software Center} and search for {pref Thunderbird }:

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287599481-461-1.png|]]

Click on {button install} and let the system install the Thunderbird application. 

Once the installation has finished, you will be able to start Thunderbird from {button Applications} -> {button Internet}-> {button Mozilla Thunderbird Mail/News}. Pressing the right button on the item will allow you to copy it to different places, like the panel or your desktop:

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287596800-874-1.png|]]


= Installing via command-line =

 <code>sudo apt-get install thunderbird</code>
This is the command you need to install the latest thunderbird.

= Downloading and installing from the Mozilla Messaging website =

If you choose not to install via your system''s package management system, you can download and install from an installer file. 

#Download the installation archive from Mozilla web site
#Unpack the Thunderbird installation archive
#Create a Menu Item for Thunderbird

== Downloading the Thunderbird archive ==

Open a web browser, go to [http://www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/ www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/] and click the download button to download the en-US version of Thunderbird. For a different language version, select the preferred language in the drop-down box in the lower right corner of the page. On the subsequent page select the type of the installation you want. In the  download window, select {button Save File and} click {button OK}.

== Unpacking the Thunderbird archive ==

When the download has completed, open the Downloads window, right click on the file you have downloaded and select {button Open}.

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325035-314-1.jpg|]]

Click {button Extract}.

[[Image:d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325154-79-1.jpg|]]

Select the {filepath /home/username/} folder and click {button Extract}.
      
When the extraction has ended, click {button Quit} to finish the process. 
      
== Adding Thunderbird to the panel ==

To make it easy to start Thunderbird, you can place a startup shortcut on your desktop or into a panel for fast access. On non-Ubuntu distributions of Linux, you should be able to customize it a similar way as described below for Ubuntu users. See [http://www.ubuntugeek.com/howto-add-entries-in-gnome-menu.html How to add entries in GNOME menu] to learn more. 

= Using Terminal to download and install Thunderbird =

You can perform the whole download and installation procedure in Terminal if you want. The first two steps described above (downloading and unpacking Thunderbird archive) can be done with the following command:

<code>wget ''
http://releases.mozilla.org/pub/mozilla.org/thunderbird/releases/3.1.4/linux-i686/en-US/thunderbird-3.1.4.tar.bz2 -O- | ''
sudo tar xj -C /opt && sudo ln -s /opt/thunderbird/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>

Note that this will also create a link in {filepath /usr/bin/} so that you can launch Thunderbird from the terminal without specifying its folder. Modify the following parts of that command to suit your environment:
*change {pref en-US} to your locale
*change thunderbird-{pref 3.1.4}.tar.bz2 to the version requested.

Enter your password to confirm the action.

To update Thunderbird, you have to launch it as an administrator:
 <code>sudo thunderbird</code>

To uninstall Thunderbird, enter:
 <code>sudo rm -rf /opt/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>

= apt-get Command Line Thunderbird Installation =
Ubuntu Software Center is a graphical front end to the command line "apt-get" system that makes it much easier for non technical users to install Thunderbird. Since apt-get requires command line knowledge and is more technical, it won''t be covered here. 

If you are more technical and wish to use apt-get to install Thunderbird, please consult the [http://wiki.debian.org/Apt apt-get documentation] on the Debian website (Ubuntu Linux is based on Debian Linux).

=See Also=

*[http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch009_ubuntu/ Installing Thunderbird on Ubuntu chapter]  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
*[http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/other-linux-distributions/ Installing Thunderbird on Other Linux Distributions chapter]  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
*[http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch011_removing-thunderbird/ Uninstalling chapter]   in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual

[[T:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/OuiabW]]','<p>This article will show you how to install Thunderbird on the Ubuntu distribution of Linux. Other distributions of Linux are similar but are not covered specifically in this article.
</p><p>Many Linux distributions include Thunderbird by default, and most have a package management system that enables you to easily install Thunderbird. Generally, you should install from the package management system, because it will:
</p>
<ul><li>Ensure that you have all the required libraries
</li><li>Install Thunderbird in a way that works best with your distribution
</li><li>Create shortcuts to launch Thunderbird
</li><li>Make Thunderbird available to all users of your computer
</li><li>Make removing Thunderbird work the same as removing any other application 
</li><li>Help remind you about Thunderbird updates
</li></ul>
<p>Package management also has some disadvantages:
</p>
<ul><li>It may not give you the latest version of Thunderbird
</li><li>It may give you a version without Thunderbird branding 
</li></ul>
<p>Therefore we recommend installing Thunderbird from the Ubuntu package system which as of Ubuntu 10.04 is the Ubuntu Software Center. See "Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center" below.
</p><p><br>
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_installing-from-the-ubuntu-software-center"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_install-the-latest-official-release"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Install the latest official release</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_installing-via-command-line"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Installing via command-line</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_downloading-and-installing-from-the-mozilla-messaging-website"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Downloading and installing from the Mozilla Messaging website</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_downloading-the-thunderbird-archive"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Downloading the Thunderbird archive</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_unpacking-the-thunderbird-archive"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Unpacking the Thunderbird archive</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_adding-thunderbird-to-the-panel"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">Adding Thunderbird to the panel</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_using-terminal-to-download-and-install-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Using Terminal to download and install Thunderbird</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_apt-get-command-line-thunderbird-installation"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">apt-get Command Line Thunderbird Installation</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_see-also"><span class="tocnumber">6</span> <span class="toctext">See Also</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_installing-from-the-ubuntu-software-center">Installing from the Ubuntu Software Center</h1>
<h2 id="w_install-the-latest-official-release">Install the latest official release</h2>
<p>On Ubuntu systems (10.04 or newer), the easiest way to install is via the Ubuntu Software Center. Note that you will need super user rights. Go to <span class="button">Applications</span> -&gt; <span class="button">Ubuntu Software Center</span> and search for <span class="pref">Thunderbird </span>:
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287599481-461-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-44-8be127.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p>Click on <span class="button">install</span> and let the system install the Thunderbird application. 
</p><p>Once the installation has finished, you will be able to start Thunderbird from <span class="button">Applications</span> -&gt; <span class="button">Internet</span>-&gt; <span class="button">Mozilla Thunderbird Mail/News</span>. Pressing the right button on the item will allow you to copy it to different places, like the panel or your desktop:
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1287596800-874-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-03-96f059.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_installing-via-command-line">Installing via command-line</h1>
<pre><code>sudo apt-get install thunderbird</code>
</pre>
<p>This is the command you need to install the latest thunderbird.
</p>
<h1 id="w_downloading-and-installing-from-the-mozilla-messaging-website">Downloading and installing from the Mozilla Messaging website</h1>
<p>If you choose not to install via your system''s package management system, you can download and install from an installer file. 
</p>
<ol><li>Download the installation archive from Mozilla web site
</li><li>Unpack the Thunderbird installation archive
</li><li>Create a Menu Item for Thunderbird
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_downloading-the-thunderbird-archive">Downloading the Thunderbird archive</h2>
<p>Open a web browser, go to <a href="http://www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/">www.mozillamessaging.com/thunderbird/</a> and click the download button to download the en-US version of Thunderbird. For a different language version, select the preferred language in the drop-down box in the lower right corner of the page. On the subsequent page select the type of the installation you want. In the  download window, select <span class="button">Save File and</span> click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_unpacking-the-thunderbird-archive">Unpacking the Thunderbird archive</h2>
<p>When the download has completed, open the Downloads window, right click on the file you have downloaded and select <span class="button">Open</span>.
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325035-314-1.jpg" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-05-41-dca2df.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p>Click <span class="button">Extract</span>.
</p><p><img alt="d5e22db3ac060a8e1952640b08027039-1280325154-79-1.jpg" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-05-41-7488c6.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif">
</p><p>Select the <span class="filepath">/home/username/</span> folder and click <span class="button">Extract</span>.
</p><p>When the extraction has ended, click <span class="button">Quit</span> to finish the process. 
</p>
<h2 id="w_adding-thunderbird-to-the-panel">Adding Thunderbird to the panel</h2>
<p>To make it easy to start Thunderbird, you can place a startup shortcut on your desktop or into a panel for fast access. On non-Ubuntu distributions of Linux, you should be able to customize it a similar way as described below for Ubuntu users. See <a href="http://www.ubuntugeek.com/howto-add-entries-in-gnome-menu.html">How to add entries in GNOME menu</a> to learn more. 
</p>
<h1 id="w_using-terminal-to-download-and-install-thunderbird">Using Terminal to download and install Thunderbird</h1>
<p>You can perform the whole download and installation procedure in Terminal if you want. The first two steps described above (downloading and unpacking Thunderbird archive) can be done with the following command:
</p><p><code>wget ''
http://releases.mozilla.org/pub/mozilla.org/thunderbird/releases/3.1.4/linux-i686/en-US/thunderbird-3.1.4.tar.bz2 -O- | ''
sudo tar xj -C /opt &amp;&amp; sudo ln -s /opt/thunderbird/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>
</p><p>Note that this will also create a link in <span class="filepath">/usr/bin/</span> so that you can launch Thunderbird from the terminal without specifying its folder. Modify the following parts of that command to suit your environment:
</p>
<ul><li>change <span class="pref">en-US</span> to your locale
</li><li>change thunderbird-<span class="pref">3.1.4</span>.tar.bz2 to the version requested.
</li></ul>
<p>Enter your password to confirm the action.
</p><p>To update Thunderbird, you have to launch it as an administrator:
</p><pre><code>sudo thunderbird</code>
</pre>
<p>To uninstall Thunderbird, enter:
</p><pre><code>sudo rm -rf /opt/thunderbird /usr/bin/thunderbird</code>
</pre>
<h1 id="w_apt-get-command-line-thunderbird-installation">apt-get Command Line Thunderbird Installation</h1>
<p>Ubuntu Software Center is a graphical front end to the command line "apt-get" system that makes it much easier for non technical users to install Thunderbird. Since apt-get requires command line knowledge and is more technical, it won''t be covered here. 
</p><p>If you are more technical and wish to use apt-get to install Thunderbird, please consult the <a href="http://wiki.debian.org/Apt">apt-get documentation</a> on the Debian website (Ubuntu Linux is based on Debian Linux).
</p>
<h1 id="w_see-also">See Also</h1>
<ul><li><a href="http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch009_ubuntu/">Installing Thunderbird on Ubuntu chapter</a>  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
</li><li><a href="http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/other-linux-distributions/">Installing Thunderbird on Other Linux Distributions chapter</a>  in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
</li><li><a href="http://en.flossmanuals.net/thunderbird/ch011_removing-thunderbird/">Uninstalling chapter</a>   in the Thunderbird FLOSS manual
</li></ul>
<p>&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "56478", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:52:30 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:52:30-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Swarnava Sengupta", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "added share template", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:52:50 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:52:50-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 24, 2014, 2:52:50 AM", "datetime": "2014-02-24T02:52:50-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:11:22+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (56804,'en-US','filelink-large-attachments','Filelink for Large Attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments/revision/56804',54492,'2014-02-27T16:32:14-08:00','Swarnava Sengupta','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/','added share template',1,'2014-02-27T16:32:25-08:00','Swarnava',1,0,1,'2014-02-27T16:32:38-08:00','Swarnava','approved','Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.

Starting in Thunderbird 13, Thunderbird Filelink eliminates this problem by adding support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.

Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to [[how to use attachments|conventional attachments]]. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message. 

The instructions below show how to configure and use Filelink with the [https://one.ubuntu.com/ Ubuntu One] online storage service. Other providers (including [https://www.yousendit.com/ YouSendIt] and [https://www.box.com/ Box]) are also supported. Simply substitute the service you are using as required.

__TOC__

= Configuring Filelink =

To use Filelink, you must first create a new account (or configure an existing account) with an online storage service provider. 

[[Template:optionspreferences]] Click on the {button Attachments} button, then click {button Outgoing}. Select the desired service provider from the drop-down list. Click the {button Add} button. Enter the username for  your online storage account, or click {button Need an account?}.

[[Image:ubuntu-account]]

If you are creating a new account, a browser window will open that displays the service provider''s registration page. Create an account as instructed. The service provider will send you a validation email message. Click the validation link in the message to activate your account. Enter your username (your email address) in the dialog shown above and enter your password when prompted.

In the main configuration dialog, check {button Offer to share for files larger than...} and specify a file size (in megabytes) if you want Thunderbird to prompt you to use Filelink when a message attachment exceeds the specified size.

= Using Filelink =

When you attach a file to a message, if the file size exceeds the amount specified above, Thunderbird will offer to let you use Filelink:

[[Image:filelink-prompt-ubuntu-one]]

To force a file to be attached using Filelink (rather than relying on Thunderbird to determine if the attached file exceeds the specified size), you can click the down arrow next to the {button Attach} button on the message toolbar to access the Filelink menu option.

Click the {button Link} button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.  (Alternatively click {button Ignore} to attach the file as a [[how to use attachments|conventional attachment]].)

[[Image:filelink attachment-ubuntu-one]]

When the recipient receives the message, they will be informed that a file is linked to the message:

[[Image:recipient filelink-ubuntu-one]]

When the recipient clicks on the link in the message, some services like YouSendIt display an intermediary web page with an option to download the file. 

[[Image:filelink link]]

Other services (like Ubuntu One) allow you to download the file immediately without an intermediate web page.


= Filelink FAQ =

Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Mozilla servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.

== Q: Does Mozilla know which provider the user has picked? ==

A: No. Mozilla is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.

== Q: What storage services are currently supported? ==

A: As of Thunderbird 16, we have reached agreement with [https://www.box.com/ Box], [https://one.ubuntu.com/ Ubuntu One] and [https://www.yousendit.com/ YouSendIt] to integrate  their services directly into Thunderbird. You can install support for more providers with add-ons, like:
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dropbox-for-filelink/ Dropbox]
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/fileswapcom-filelink FileSwap.com] 
* [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dl-for-thunderbird/ DL for Thunderbird] may be the best if you prefer using your own server instead of relying on 3rd-party providers.
* Last but not least, you can use Filelink with a WebDAV server with the [https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/webdav-for-filelink WebDAV for Filelink] add-on.

== Q: Can the storage service view my attachments? ==

A: Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment.  Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. (Service providers generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service.) Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol.

== Q: How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site? ==

A: The file will remain available on the storage service provider''s site until you specifically delete it. Log in to the provider''s website to view and delete the files in your storage space.

== Q: Can you support service provider X or protocol Y? ==

A: We plan to support SpiderOak. We also have an "[http://blog.mozilla.org/thunderbird/ Up-for-grabs]" project available if anybody would like to add  support for more services or protocols. Developer documentation for the Filelink feature can be found here: [https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Thunderbird/Filelink_Providers]

== Q: I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work? ==

A: Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We''ve just given you the ability to choose to upload large attachments somewhere else, if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file  defaults to 1 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird''s Preferences. ([[Template:optionspreferences]]) The offer to upload can also be disabled entirely.

[[T:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/1fNR49i]]','<p>Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.
</p><p>Starting in Thunderbird 13, Thunderbird Filelink eliminates this problem by adding support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.
</p><p>Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=how+to+use+attachments" title="Page does not exist.">conventional attachments</a>. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message. 
</p><p>The instructions below show how to configure and use Filelink with the <a href="https://one.ubuntu.com/">Ubuntu One</a> online storage service. Other providers (including <a href="https://www.yousendit.com/">YouSendIt</a> and <a href="https://www.box.com/">Box</a>) are also supported. Simply substitute the service you are using as required.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configuring-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configuring Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_using-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Using Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_filelink-faq"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Filelink FAQ</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-does-mozilla-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Q: Does Mozilla know which provider the user has picked?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-what-storage-services-are-currently-supported"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Q: What storage services are currently supported?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">Q: Can the storage service view my attachments?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site"><span class="tocnumber">3.4</span> <span class="toctext">Q: How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y"><span class="tocnumber">3.5</span> <span class="toctext">Q: Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_q-i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work"><span class="tocnumber">3.6</span> <span class="toctext">Q: I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configuring-filelink">Configuring Filelink</h1>
<p>To use Filelink, you must first create a new account (or configure an existing account) with an online storage service provider. 
</p><p><span class="for" data-for="mac">In the Menu bar at the top of the screen, click <span class="menu">Firefox</span> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span> (or <span class="menu">Preferences</span>, in some cases).</span><span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">Click the menu button <img alt="Fx89menuButton" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-15-11-18-38-e5b736.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span>.</span>
 Click on the <span class="button">Attachments</span> button, then click <span class="button">Outgoing</span>. Select the desired service provider from the drop-down list. Click the <span class="button">Add</span> button. Enter the username for  your online storage account, or click <span class="button">Need an account?</span>.
</p><p><img alt="ubuntu-account" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-13-53-53-6cf0db.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>If you are creating a new account, a browser window will open that displays the service provider''s registration page. Create an account as instructed. The service provider will send you a validation email message. Click the validation link in the message to activate your account. Enter your username (your email address) in the dialog shown above and enter your password when prompted.
</p><p>In the main configuration dialog, check <span class="button">Offer to share for files larger than...</span> and specify a file size (in megabytes) if you want Thunderbird to prompt you to use Filelink when a message attachment exceeds the specified size.
</p>
<h1 id="w_using-filelink">Using Filelink</h1>
<p>When you attach a file to a message, if the file size exceeds the amount specified above, Thunderbird will offer to let you use Filelink:
</p><p><img alt="filelink-prompt-ubuntu-one" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-14-02-35-1c9f4c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>To force a file to be attached using Filelink (rather than relying on Thunderbird to determine if the attached file exceeds the specified size), you can click the down arrow next to the <span class="button">Attach</span> button on the message toolbar to access the Filelink menu option.
</p><p>Click the <span class="button">Link</span> button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.  (Alternatively click <span class="button">Ignore</span> to attach the file as a <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=how+to+use+attachments" title="Page does not exist.">conventional attachment</a>.)
</p><p><img alt="filelink attachment-ubuntu-one" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-14-14-01-1dc88f.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>When the recipient receives the message, they will be informed that a file is linked to the message:
</p><p><img alt="recipient filelink-ubuntu-one" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-08-24-14-23-26-d21d6f.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>When the recipient clicks on the link in the message, some services like YouSendIt display an intermediary web page with an option to download the file. 
</p><p><img alt="filelink link" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-05-30-14-10-16-364781.jpg" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p><p>Other services (like Ubuntu One) allow you to download the file immediately without an intermediate web page.
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_filelink-faq">Filelink FAQ</h1>
<p>Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Mozilla servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-does-mozilla-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked">Q: Does Mozilla know which provider the user has picked?</h2>
<p>A: No. Mozilla is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-what-storage-services-are-currently-supported">Q: What storage services are currently supported?</h2>
<p>A: As of Thunderbird 16, we have reached agreement with <a href="https://www.box.com/">Box</a>, <a href="https://one.ubuntu.com/">Ubuntu One</a> and <a href="https://www.yousendit.com/">YouSendIt</a> to integrate  their services directly into Thunderbird. You can install support for more providers with add-ons, like:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dropbox-for-filelink/">Dropbox</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/fileswapcom-filelink">FileSwap.com</a> 
</li><li> <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/dl-for-thunderbird/">DL for Thunderbird</a> may be the best if you prefer using your own server instead of relying on 3rd-party providers.
</li><li> Last but not least, you can use Filelink with a WebDAV server with the <a href="https://addons.mozilla.org/thunderbird/addon/webdav-for-filelink">WebDAV for Filelink</a> add-on.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_q-can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments">Q: Can the storage service view my attachments?</h2>
<p>A: Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment.  Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. (Service providers generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service.) Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site">Q: How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</h2>
<p>A: The file will remain available on the storage service provider''s site until you specifically delete it. Log in to the provider''s website to view and delete the files in your storage space.
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y">Q: Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</h2>
<p>A: We plan to support SpiderOak. We also have an "<a href="http://blog.mozilla.org/thunderbird/">Up-for-grabs</a>" project available if anybody would like to add  support for more services or protocols. Developer documentation for the Filelink feature can be found here: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Thunderbird/Filelink_Providers">https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Thun.../Filelink_Providers</a>
</p>
<h2 id="w_q-i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work">Q: I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</h2>
<p>A: Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We''ve just given you the ability to choose to upload large attachments somewhere else, if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file  defaults to 1 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird''s Preferences. (<span class="for" data-for="mac">In the Menu bar at the top of the screen, click <span class="menu">Firefox</span> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span> (or <span class="menu">Preferences</span>, in some cases).</span><span class="for" data-for="win,linux" style="display: none;">Click the menu button <img alt="Fx89menuButton" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-15-11-18-38-e5b736.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> and select <span class="menu">Settings</span>.</span>
) The offer to upload can also be disabled entirely.
</p><p>&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "56804", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 27, 2014, 4:32:14 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-27T16:32:14-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Swarnava Sengupta", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "added share template", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 27, 2014, 4:32:25 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-27T16:32:25-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 27, 2014, 4:32:38 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-27T16:32:38-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:08:41+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (56806,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/56806',54375,'2014-02-27T16:34:13-08:00','Swarnava Sengupta','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/','added share template',1,'2014-02-27T16:34:31-08:00','Swarnava',1,0,1,'2014-02-27T16:34:31-08:00','Swarnava','approved','Thunderbird can save usernames and passwords that you use to access your mail accounts. If you share a computer with anyone, it is recommended that you use a master password.

After you have defined a master password, you will be prompted to enter it whenever Thunderbird needs to access your stored passwords.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The master password only needs to be entered once per Thunderbird session.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__


= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use master passwords to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the {menu Tools} menu, and select {menu Options...}.
# Click the {menu Security} icon.
# Click the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Check mark ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/> <br/>[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]<br/> <br/>
# The Change Master Password dialog appears. As you enter your master password, the Password quality meter indicates the difficulty of guessing the password. To create a quality password, your master password should include the following:
#* At least one capital letter
#* One or more digits
#* At least one non-alphanumeric character, such as the following: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/> <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.
# The master password is defined.

= Changing or removing the master password =
You can change or remove your master password at any time:
# At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the {menu Tools} menu, and select {menu Options...}.
# Click the {menu Security} icon.
# Click the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
#* If you''ve forgotten your master password, see [[Forgot my master password]].
# Enter the new master password twice.
#* To remove the master password, leave the new master password fields empty.
# To accept the changed master password, click {button OK}.
# The master password is changed.


= Forgotten Password =

If you have lost or forgotten your master password or you want to disable the feature, you can reset the master password. Resetting the master password will remove all stored password information. Upon resetting, you will lose all the stored information in the Password Manager, as this is a built-in security feature to prevent people from simply resetting your Master Password to gain access to your passwords.

# Choose {menu Tools -> Error Console}
# Paste the expression <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
# Press the Evaluate button.
# Accept when prompted with the confirmation dialog box.

<!-- MZ credit -->
<br/> <br/>
''''''''''Based on information from  [http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password Master password (mozillaZine KB)]''''''''''

[[T:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/1hofAP3]]','<p>Thunderbird can save usernames and passwords that you use to access your mail accounts. If you share a computer with anyone, it is recommended that you use a master password.
</p><p>After you have defined a master password, you will be prompted to enter it whenever Thunderbird needs to access your stored passwords.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The master password only needs to be entered once per Thunderbird session.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing or removing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_forgotten-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Forgotten Password</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use master passwords to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the <span class="menu">Tools</span> menu, and select <span class="menu">Options...</span>.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Security</span> icon.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Check mark <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br> <br><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> <br>
</li><li> The Change Master Password dialog appears. As you enter your master password, the Password quality meter indicates the difficulty of guessing the password. To create a quality password, your master password should include the following:
<ul><li> At least one capital letter
</li><li> One or more digits
</li><li> At least one non-alphanumeric character, such as the following: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li><li> The master password is defined.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password">Changing or removing the master password</h1>
<p>You can change or remove your master password at any time:
</p>
<ol><li> At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the <span class="menu">Tools</span> menu, and select <span class="menu">Options...</span>.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Security</span> icon.
</li><li> Click the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
<ul><li> If you''ve forgotten your master password, see <a href="/en-US/kb/reset-your-primary-password-if-youve-forgotten-it">Reset your Primary Password if you''ve forgotten it</a>.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
<ul><li> To remove the master password, leave the new master password fields empty.
</li></ul>
</li><li> To accept the changed master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li><li> The master password is changed.
</li></ol>
<p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_forgotten-password">Forgotten Password</h1>
<p>If you have lost or forgotten your master password or you want to disable the feature, you can reset the master password. Resetting the master password will remove all stored password information. Upon resetting, you will lose all the stored information in the Password Manager, as this is a built-in security feature to prevent people from simply resetting your Master Password to gain access to your passwords.
</p>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>
</li><li> Paste the expression <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
</li><li> Press the Evaluate button.
</li><li> Accept when prompted with the confirmation dialog box.
</li></ol>
<p>
<br> <br>
<em><strong>Based on information from  <a href="http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password">Master password (mozillaZine KB)</a></strong></em>
</p><p>&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "56806", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Feb 27, 2014, 4:34:13 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-27T16:34:13-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Swarnava Sengupta", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Swarnava/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "added share template", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Feb 27, 2014, 4:34:31 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-27T16:34:31-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Feb 27, 2014, 4:34:31 PM", "datetime": "2014-02-27T16:34:31-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "Swarnava", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:14:19+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (57270,'en-US','using-identities','Using Identities','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-identities','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-identities/revision/57270',54356,'2014-03-05T01:38:57-08:00','Dyvik Chenna','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/dyvik1001/','Share this article added',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','When you configure an account, Thunderbird creates a default "identity" for the account that contains information like your name, your email address, the location where your messages should be stored, signature, etc. You can manually create additional identities for your account (via {menu Tools | Account Settings},  {button Manage Identities}). When you create (or reply) to a message, you can specify any of the identities you have configured by selecting it from the ''''''From'''''' drop-down list.

If you are using Google''s Gmail, there are some additional configuration steps described on [[Configure Origin Address for Multiple Gmail Accounts]].

= About identities =

As an example of how identities are useful, consider the scenario where you have a "default" address for a domain (such as "feedback@example.com") that is published on your website. People might send messages to this address on a variety of topics: customer inquiries, vendor inquiries, feedback on the site''s technical issues, etc. 

When you configure multiple identities, you can specify the identity that is used when you reply to a message. For example, you could configure an identity for "sales@example.com", with a name and email address different than "feedback@example.com". Then, when you reply to "feedback@example.com", you could specify the "sales@example.com" identity.

Other uses for identities:

* using an email forwarding service that redirects messages sent to an old email account to a new email account
* switching message configuration for specific messages (such as encryption or SMTP server)

In this article, identities refer to separate configurations for a single account. However, if you have multiple email accounts, there is a default profile configured for each account that is used in the same manner as identities configured for a single account.

= Creating identities =

To create an identity, click {menu Tools | Account Settings}. Select the desired account from the list on the left, then click the {button Manage Identities} button in the bottom right. This displays a list of the identities previously created for the account. To create a new identity, click {button Add}.

Similar to configuring the default identity when you create a new account, the identity dialog enables you to specify various aspects:

*''''''Settings'''''': name, address, signature, etc
*''''''Copies & Folders'''''': the locations where Thunderbird should store messages
*''''''Composition & Addressing'''''': text format, quote management, etc
*''''''Security'''''': digital signatures and encryption

= Using identities =

When you create a new message, Thunderbird uses the default identity for the selected account as the message''s ''''''From'''''' value. Click the drop-down list in the ''''''From'''''' field to select a different identity. The drop-down list will display both identities for the selected account and identities for other accounts.

When you reply to a message or forward a message, Thunderbird scans the recipients of the message and, if available, chooses the identity the original author used when sending the message to you. (Sometimes this information is not available, such as with certain mailing lists.) This identity will then be the default identity in the compose window. You may select a different identity as described above.
[[Template:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/NwuAPN]]','<p>When you configure an account, Thunderbird creates a default "identity" for the account that contains information like your name, your email address, the location where your messages should be stored, signature, etc. You can manually create additional identities for your account (via <span class="menu">Tools | Account Settings</span>,  <span class="button">Manage Identities</span>). When you create (or reply) to a message, you can specify any of the identities you have configured by selecting it from the <strong>From</strong> drop-down list.
</p><p>If you are using Google''s Gmail, there are some additional configuration steps described on <a href="/en-US/kb/configure-origin-address-for-multiple-gmail-accounts">Configure Origin Address for Multiple Gmail Accounts</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_about-identities">About identities</h1>
<p>As an example of how identities are useful, consider the scenario where you have a "default" address for a domain (such as "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>") that is published on your website. People might send messages to this address on a variety of topics: customer inquiries, vendor inquiries, feedback on the site''s technical issues, etc. 
</p><p>When you configure multiple identities, you can specify the identity that is used when you reply to a message. For example, you could configure an identity for "<a href="mailto:sales@example.com">sales@example.com</a>", with a name and email address different than "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>". Then, when you reply to "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>", you could specify the "<a href="mailto:sales@example.com">sales@example.com</a>" identity.
</p><p>Other uses for identities:
</p>
<ul><li> using an email forwarding service that redirects messages sent to an old email account to a new email account
</li><li> switching message configuration for specific messages (such as encryption or SMTP server)
</li></ul>
<p>In this article, identities refer to separate configurations for a single account. However, if you have multiple email accounts, there is a default profile configured for each account that is used in the same manner as identities configured for a single account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_creating-identities">Creating identities</h1>
<p>To create an identity, click <span class="menu">Tools | Account Settings</span>. Select the desired account from the list on the left, then click the <span class="button">Manage Identities</span> button in the bottom right. This displays a list of the identities previously created for the account. To create a new identity, click <span class="button">Add</span>.
</p><p>Similar to configuring the default identity when you create a new account, the identity dialog enables you to specify various aspects:
</p>
<ul><li><strong>Settings</strong>: name, address, signature, etc
</li><li><strong>Copies &amp; Folders</strong>: the locations where Thunderbird should store messages
</li><li><strong>Composition &amp; Addressing</strong>: text format, quote management, etc
</li><li><strong>Security</strong>: digital signatures and encryption
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_using-identities">Using identities</h1>
<p>When you create a new message, Thunderbird uses the default identity for the selected account as the message''s <strong>From</strong> value. Click the drop-down list in the <strong>From</strong> field to select a different identity. The drop-down list will display both identities for the selected account and identities for other accounts.
</p><p>When you reply to a message or forward a message, Thunderbird scans the recipients of the message and, if available, chooses the identity the original author used when sending the message to you. (Sometimes this information is not available, such as with certain mailing lists.) This identity will then be the default identity in the compose window. You may select a different identity as described above.
&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "57270", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Mar 5, 2014, 1:38:57 AM", "datetime": "2014-03-05T01:38:57-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Dyvik Chenna", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/dyvik1001/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Share this article added", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:19:37+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (57385,'en-US','thunderbird-and-hotmail','Thunderbird and Microsoft email (Office365, Hotmail, outlook.com, etc)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-hotmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-hotmail/revision/57385',54342,'2014-03-05T18:22:22-08:00','Dyvik Chenna','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/dyvik1001/','Share this article added',1,'2014-05-01T21:11:55-07:00','MattAuSupport',1,0,1,'2014-08-19T10:33:20-07:00','user917725','approved','Thunderbird can be used as a client for all of Microsoft''s email services (Hotmail, Outlook<!-- -->.com and Windows Live Mail, henceforth referred to as "Hotmail"). Thunderbird will download messages from the Hotmail server and store them on your local system. Similarly, Thunderbird can be used to compose messages which are sent through Hotmail''s mail servers. You can still use Hotmail''s web-based application - Thunderbird will query the server and synchronize messages.

__TOC__

==Configuring servers==
Hotmail accounts can be configured in Thunderbird''s [[Automatic Account Configuration]]. This article provides instructions on how to manually [[Manual Account Configuration|configure an account]] or modify an existing account.

To manually configure or modify Hotmail account settings:

# {for win,linux}At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the {/for}{for mac}On the menu bar, click on the {menu Thunderbird} menu, choose {/for}{for linux} {menu Edit}{/for}{for win,mac}{menu Tools}{/for}{for win,linux} menu{/for} and select {menu Account Settings…}. The Account Settings dialog window will be displayed.
# Select your Hotmail account from the list on the left. Make sure your name and account are correctly specified in the {menu Default Identity} section.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailaccounts]]<br><br>
# In the left panel, below your Hotmail account name, click on {menu Server Settings}. The server settings will be displayed in the right panel.

===Configuring IMAP and POP accounts===
{note}''''''Note'''''': For an explanation of IMAP and POP, [https://support.mozillamessaging.com/kb/glossary-terms-including-types-accounts#w_mail-accounts click here].{/note}

====IMAP mail account settings====
*Server Name: imap-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
*Port: 993
*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')
*Connection security: SSL/TLS
*Authentication method: Normal password
*Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailimap]]

====POP mail account settings====
*Server Name: pop-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
*Port: 995
*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')
*Connection security: SSL/TLS
*Authentication method: Normal password
*Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailpop]]

===Configuring the outgoing server===
# At the bottom of the list on the left, select {menu Outgoing Server (SMTP)}. Configured outgoing servers will be displayed in the right panel. The Hotmail server will probably be called ''''''Microsoft Live Hotmail'''''' or something similar.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailsmtp]]<br><br>
# Select the server and click the {button Edit} button to alter the settings. Configure them as follows:
#*Server Name: smtp-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
#*Port: 587
#*Connection security: STARTTLS
#*Authentication method: Normal password
#*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailsmtp1]]
[[Template:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/1hOuP3S]]','<p>Thunderbird can be used as a client for all of Microsoft''s email services (Hotmail, Outlook.com and Windows Live Mail, henceforth referred to as "Hotmail"). Thunderbird will download messages from the Hotmail server and store them on your local system. Similarly, Thunderbird can be used to compose messages which are sent through Hotmail''s mail servers. You can still use Hotmail''s web-based application - Thunderbird will query the server and synchronize messages.
</p>
