<h2 id="w_q-i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work">Q: I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</h2>
<p>A: Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We''ve just given you the ability to choose to upload large attachments somewhere else, if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file defaults to 1 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird''s Preferences:
</p>
<ol><li> The template "optionspreferences tb" does not exist or has no approved revision..
</li><li> Click the <strong>Attachment</strong> panel and choose the <strong>Outgoing</strong> tab.
</li><li> Set the file size for Filelink warning.
</li></ol>
<p>The offer to upload can also be disabled entirely by removing the selection from the <strong>Offer to share for files larger than</strong> option.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "95221", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "May 25, 2015, 9:48:24 PM", "datetime": "2015-05-25T21:48:24-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Artist", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Artist/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "minor edit (spelling only)", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "May 26, 2015, 7:05:35 AM", "datetime": "2015-05-26T07:05:35-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "May 26, 2015, 7:05:35 AM", "datetime": "2015-05-26T07:05:35-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:08:02+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (95973,'en-US','thunderbird-and-hotmail','Thunderbird and Microsoft email (Office365, Hotmail, outlook.com, etc)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-hotmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-hotmail/revision/95973',93793,'2015-06-05T09:08:03-07:00','Tonnes','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Tonnes/','shorten search summary, nits to last line',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','Thunderbird can be used as a client for all of Microsoft''s email services (Hotmail, Outlook<!-- -->.com and Windows Live Mail, henceforth referred to as "Hotmail"). Thunderbird will download messages from the Hotmail server and store them on your local system. Similarly, Thunderbird can be used to compose messages which are sent through Hotmail''s mail servers. You can still use Hotmail''s web-based application - Thunderbird will query the server and synchronize messages.

__TOC__

==Configuring servers==
Hotmail accounts can be configured in Thunderbird''s [[Automatic Account Configuration]]. This article provides instructions on how to manually [[Manual Account Configuration|configure an account]] or modify an existing account.

To manually configure or modify Hotmail account settings:

# {for win,linux}At the top of the Thunderbird window, click on the {/for}{for mac}On the menu bar, click on the {menu Thunderbird} menu, choose {/for}{for linux} {menu Edit}{/for}{for win,mac}{menu Tools}{/for}{for win,linux} menu{/for} and select {menu Account Settings…}. The Account Settings dialog window will be displayed.
# Select your Hotmail account from the list on the left. Make sure your name and account are correctly specified in the {menu Default Identity} section.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailaccounts]]<br><br>
# In the left panel, below your Hotmail account name, click on {menu Server Settings}. The server settings will be displayed in the right panel.

===Configuring IMAP and POP accounts===
{note}''''''Note'''''': For an explanation of IMAP and POP, [https://support.mozillamessaging.com/kb/glossary-terms-including-types-accounts#w_mail-accounts click here].{/note}

====IMAP mail account settings====
*Server Name: imap-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
*Port: 993
*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')
*Connection security: SSL/TLS
*Authentication method: Normal password
*Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailimap]]

====POP mail account settings====
*Server Name: pop-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
*Port: 995
*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')
*Connection security: SSL/TLS
*Authentication method: Normal password
*Configure the rest of the server settings according to your preferences.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailpop]]

===Configuring the outgoing server===
# At the bottom of the list on the left, select {menu Outgoing Server (SMTP)}. Configured outgoing servers will be displayed in the right panel. The Hotmail server will probably be called ''''''Microsoft Live Hotmail'''''' or something similar.<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailsmtp]]<br><br>
# Select the server and click the {button Edit} button to alter the settings. Configure them as follows:
#*Server Name: smtp-mail.outlook<!-- -->.com
#*Port: 587
#*Connection security: STARTTLS
#*Authentication method: Normal password
#*User Name: your username (for example ''''j.kowal@outlook.com'''')<br><br>[[Image:sethotmailsmtp1]]
*You have to  sign in to the webmail and enable POP settings under the option section of your Outlook.com email account, or you will receive a password error in Thunderbird.','<p>Thunderbird can be used as a client for all of Microsoft''s email services (Hotmail, Outlook.com and Windows Live Mail, henceforth referred to as "Hotmail"). Thunderbird will download messages from the Hotmail server and store them on your local system. Similarly, Thunderbird can be used to compose messages which are sent through Hotmail''s mail servers. You can still use Hotmail''s web-based application - Thunderbird will query the server and synchronize messages.
</p>
