It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to its default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file. It is only non-default preferences that are stored.','<p>You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.
</p><p>To access the config editor, go to <span class="menu"><span class="for" data-for="mac">Thunderbird &gt; Preferences</span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Tools &gt; Options</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">Edit &gt; Preferences</span></span>, select the <span class="menu">Advanced</span> panel, select the <span class="menu">General</span> tab, and click <span class="button">Config Editor…</span>.
</p><p><span class="for" data-for="not tb52" style="display: none;"><img alt="Config Editor Warning" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-16-17-917d30.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful.
</p>
<div class="warning">Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config editor; therefore please proceed with caution.</div>
<p>Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click <span class="button"><span class="for" data-for="not tb52" style="display: none;">I''ll be careful, I promise!</span><span class="for" data-for="tb52">I accept the risk!</span></span> to proceed.
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="Config Editor" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-17-04-220cb6.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<p>In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Search" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.
</p><p>Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.
</p><p>To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Modify</span> (for true/false preferences, select <span class="menu">Toggle</span>). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Reset</span>.
</p><p>In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the <span class="menu">New</span> menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, <span class="menu">Reset</span> it as described above.
</p><p>Modifications to preferences happen immediately, which means you don''t have to click a <span class="button">Save</span> button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. <strong>Note:</strong> A restart requires all windows to be closed, including the config editor window and <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Options</span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux">Preferences</span> windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!
</p><p>It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to its default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file. It is only non-default preferences that are stored.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "172230", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 28, 2018, 8:31:39 AM", "datetime": "2018-11-28T08:31:39-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Chris Ilias", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Chris_Ilias/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "removed ; before image", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 28, 2018, 8:32:51 AM", "datetime": "2018-11-28T08:32:51-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Chris_Ilias", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:06:03+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (172235,'en-US','config-editor','Config Editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor/revision/172235',172230,'2018-11-28T15:48:32-08:00','Tonnes','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Tonnes/','properly hide hidden image',1,'2018-11-28T22:34:40-08:00','Chris_Ilias',1,0,1,'2018-11-30T13:14:45-08:00','Chris_Ilias','approved','You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.

To access the config editor, go to {menu {for mac}Thunderbird > Preferences{/for}{for win}Tools > Options{/for}{for linux}Edit > Preferences{/for}}, select the {menu Advanced} panel, select the {menu General} tab, and click {button Config Editor…}.

{for not tb52}
;[[Image:Config Editor Warning]]
{/for}
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful.

{warning}Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config editor; therefore please proceed with caution.{/warning}

Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click {button {for not tb52}I''ll be careful, I promise!{/for}{for tb52}I accept the risk!{/for}} to proceed.

;[[Image:Config Editor]]
In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Search" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.

Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.

To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select {menu Modify} (for true/false preferences, select {menu Toggle}). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select {menu Reset}.

In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the {menu New} menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, {menu Reset} it as described above.

Modifications to preferences happen immediately, which means you don''t have to click a {button Save} button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. ''''''Note:'''''' A restart requires all windows to be closed, including the config editor window and {for win}Options{/for}{for mac,linux}Preferences{/for} windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!

It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to its default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file. It is only non-default preferences that are stored.','<p>You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.
</p><p>To access the config editor, go to <span class="menu"><span class="for" data-for="mac">Thunderbird &gt; Preferences</span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Tools &gt; Options</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">Edit &gt; Preferences</span></span>, select the <span class="menu">Advanced</span> panel, select the <span class="menu">General</span> tab, and click <span class="button">Config Editor…</span>.
</p><div class="for" data-for="not tb52" style="display: none;">
<dl><dt><img alt="Config Editor Warning" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-16-17-917d30.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</div><p>The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful.
</p>
<div class="warning">Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config editor; therefore please proceed with caution.</div>
<p>Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click <span class="button"><span class="for" data-for="not tb52" style="display: none;">I''ll be careful, I promise!</span><span class="for" data-for="tb52">I accept the risk!</span></span> to proceed.
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="Config Editor" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-17-04-220cb6.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<p>In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Search" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.
</p><p>Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.
</p><p>To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Modify</span> (for true/false preferences, select <span class="menu">Toggle</span>). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Reset</span>.
</p><p>In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the <span class="menu">New</span> menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, <span class="menu">Reset</span> it as described above.
</p><p>Modifications to preferences happen immediately, which means you don''t have to click a <span class="button">Save</span> button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. <strong>Note:</strong> A restart requires all windows to be closed, including the config editor window and <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Options</span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux">Preferences</span> windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!
</p><p>It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to its default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file. It is only non-default preferences that are stored.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "172235", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 28, 2018, 3:48:32 PM", "datetime": "2018-11-28T15:48:32-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Tonnes", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Tonnes/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "properly hide hidden image", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 28, 2018, 10:34:40 PM", "datetime": "2018-11-28T22:34:40-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Chris_Ilias", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Nov 30, 2018, 1:14:45 PM", "datetime": "2018-11-30T13:14:45-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "Chris_Ilias", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:06:00+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (184502,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/184502',130379,'2019-07-11T21:25:44-07:00','Wayne Mery','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/','clarify that master password does not protect messages',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.

{note}''''''Want to prevent others from seeing your <u>messages</u>?'''''' Master password does not protect or prevent viewing of messages already downloaded to your computer. So if you share a computer and want to project your messages, create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account for each person, and make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password. Because Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information in a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|profile]] separately for each {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account, each account will not be able to see the other accounts'' messages.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__

= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Put a check mark next to ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/><br/>{for mac}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for linux}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/>{/for}
# The ''''Change Master Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>{for mac}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for linux}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]{/for} <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.

Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Changing or removing the master password =

# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new master password twice.
#* ''''''To remove the master password'''''', leave both of the new master password fields empty.
# Click {button OK}.

= Forgotten password =
If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.{/warning}

# Choose {menu Tools -> Error Console}.
# Paste the following text: <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
# Press the {button Evaluate} button.
# Accept when prompted with the confirmation dialog box.

<!-- MZ credit -->
<br/> <br/>
''''''''''Based on information from  [http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password Master password (mozillaZine KB)]''''''''''','<p>Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Want to prevent others from seeing your <u>messages</u>?</strong> Master password does not protect or prevent viewing of messages already downloaded to your computer. So if you share a computer and want to project your messages, create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Because Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information in a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">profile</a> separately for each <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account, each account will not be able to see the other accounts'' messages.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing or removing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_forgotten-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Forgotten password</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Put a check mark next to <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br><br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span>
</li><li> The <em>Change Master Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password">Changing or removing the master password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
<ul><li> <strong>To remove the master password</strong>, leave both of the new master password fields empty.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_forgotten-password">Forgotten password</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>.
</li><li> Paste the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
</li><li> Press the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button.
</li><li> Accept when prompted with the confirmation dialog box.
</li></ol>
<p>
<br> <br>
<em><strong>Based on information from  <a href="http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password">Master password (mozillaZine KB)</a></strong></em>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "184502", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jul 11, 2019, 9:25:44 PM", "datetime": "2019-07-11T21:25:44-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Wayne Mery", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "clarify that master password does not protect messages", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:52+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (186710,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/186710',184502,'2019-08-30T20:49:29-07:00','Toad-Hall','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Toad-Hall/','Forgotten Master Password so info matches process. Added  images as guide. Please check the code line that starts openDialog as I''m having difficulty getting the copy paste line all in one line and not split over two lines.',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.

{note}''''''Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?'''''' If you share a computer, create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account for each person, and make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__

= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Put a check mark next to ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/><br/>{for mac}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for linux}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/>{/for}
# The ''''Change Master Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>{for mac}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for linux}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]{/for} <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.

Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Changing or removing the master password =

# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new master password twice.
#* ''''''To remove the master password'''''', leave both of the new master password fields empty.
# Click {button OK}.

= Forgotten password =
If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.{/warning}

# Choose {menu Menu Icon -> Tools -> Developer Tools -> Error Console}.<br/>[[Image:Developer Tools - Error Console]] <br/>
# Click on the Bin icon to clear errors.
# Copy the following text:<br/><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code> <br/>[[Image:Error Console]] <br/> and paste into the text input area at the bottom of the window.
# Press the Enter/Return key
# When prompted, Click on ''Reset'' button in the Reset Master Password confirmation window.<br/>[[Image:Error Console reset confirm]] 


<!-- MZ credit -->
<br/> <br/>
''''''''''Based on information from  [http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password Master password (mozillaZine KB)]''''''''''','<p>Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?</strong> If you share a computer, create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing or removing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_forgotten-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Forgotten password</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Put a check mark next to <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br><br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span>
</li><li> The <em>Change Master Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password">Changing or removing the master password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
<ul><li> <strong>To remove the master password</strong>, leave both of the new master password fields empty.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_forgotten-password">Forgotten password</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Menu Icon -&gt; Tools -&gt; Developer Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>.<br><img alt="Developer Tools - Error Console" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2019-08-31-02-44-16-f472ef.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> <br>
</li><li> Click on the Bin icon to clear errors.
</li><li> Copy the following text:<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code> <br><img alt="Error Console" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2019-08-31-03-11-36-243f79.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> <br> and paste into the text input area at the bottom of the window.
</li><li> Press the Enter/Return key
</li><li> When prompted, Click on ''Reset'' button in the Reset Master Password confirmation window.<br><img alt="Error Console reset confirm" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2019-08-31-03-38-09-9a22fd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</li></ol>
<p><br>

<br> <br>
<em><strong>Based on information from  <a href="http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password">Master password (mozillaZine KB)</a></strong></em>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "186710", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 30, 2019, 8:49:29 PM", "datetime": "2019-08-30T20:49:29-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Toad-Hall", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Toad-Hall/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Forgotten Master Password so info matches process. Added  images as guide. Please check the code line that starts openDialog as I''m having difficulty getting the copy paste line all in one line and not split over two lines.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:50+00:00','');
