</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changing-or-removing-the-master-password">Changing or removing the master password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
<ul><li> <strong>To remove the master password</strong>, leave both of the new master password fields empty.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_forgotten-password">Forgotten password</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Developer Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>.
</li><li> Paste the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
</li><li> Press the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button.
</li><li> If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.
</li></ol>
<p>
<br> <br>
<em><strong>Based on information from  <a href="http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password">Master password (mozillaZine KB)</a></strong></em>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "189887", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 31, 2019, 4:11:21 AM", "datetime": "2019-10-31T04:11:21-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Toad-Hall", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Toad-Hall/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "updating ''forgotten password'' section as it was outdated with incorrect instructions.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:47+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (193857,'en-US','installing-thunderbird-linux','Install Thunderbird on Linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux/revision/193857',165331,'2020-01-20T07:58:05-08:00','Sancus','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Sancus/','update links to thunderbird site',1,'2021-03-20T09:41:06-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','This article will show you how to install Thunderbird on Linux. For other operating systems, see [[Installing Thunderbird on Windows]] and [[Installing Thunderbird on Mac]].

Many Linux distributions include Thunderbird by default, and most have a package management system that enables you to easily install Thunderbird. Generally, you should install from the package management system, because it will:
*Ensure that you have all the required libraries
*Install Thunderbird in a way that works best with your distribution
*Create shortcuts to launch Thunderbird
*Make Thunderbird available to all users of your computer
*Make removing Thunderbird work the same as removing any other application 

Package management also has some disadvantages:

*It may not give you the latest version of Thunderbird
*It may give you a version without Thunderbird branding 

= Installing from a package manager =
To install Thunderbird using the package manager, please refer to the documentation of the Linux distribution you''re using.

= Installing outside of a package manager =
Complete instructions for installing Thunderbird outside of package management may be available at your distribution''s support website. For example:
* [https://help.ubuntu.com/community/ThunderbirdNewVersion Installing Thunderbird on Ubuntu]

{note}
* ''''''Before you install Thunderbird'''''', make sure that your computer has the '''''' [https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/system-requirements/ required libraries]'''''' installed. Missing libraries will cause Thunderbird to be inoperable.
* The installation file provided by Mozilla in ''''''.tar.bz2'''''' format does not contain sources but pre-compiled binary files, therefore you can simply unpack and run them. There is no need to compile the program from source.
* The following instructions will install Thunderbird into your home directory, and ''''''only the current user'''''' will be able to ''''''run it''''''.{/note}

# Download Thunderbird from [https://www.thunderbird.net the Thunderbird download page] to your home directory. 
# Open a ''''''Terminal'''''' and go to your home directory: <code>cd ~</code>
# Extract the contents of the downloaded file: <code>tar xjf thunderbird-*.tar.bz2</code>
# Close Thunderbird if it''s open.
# To start Thunderbird, run the {filepath thunderbird} script in the {filepath thunderbird} folder: <code>~/thunderbird/thunderbird</code>

Thunderbird should now start. You can then create a launcher on your desktop to run this command.

== libstdc++5 error ==
As noted above, you need to install the [https://www.thunderbird.net/system-requirements/ required libraries] for Thunderbird to work. Many distributions don''t include libstdc++5 by default.

== "Thunderbird not installed" message or wrong version of Thunderbird starts ==
If Thunderbird is installed following the instructions given above, it must be started (in a ''''''Terminal'''''' or in a launcher on the Desktop, for example) using the command: <code>~/thunderbird/thunderbird</code>

If you try to start Thunderbird in a ''''''Terminal'''''' with the command: <code>thunderbird</code>, it will either start the package-manager-installed version of Thunderbird or will tell you the program is not installed.','<p>This article will show you how to install Thunderbird on Linux. For other operating systems, see <a href="/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-windows">Install Thunderbird on Windows</a> and <a href="/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-on-mac">Install Thunderbird on Mac</a>.
</p><p>Many Linux distributions include Thunderbird by default, and most have a package management system that enables you to easily install Thunderbird. Generally, you should install from the package management system, because it will:
</p>
<ul><li>Ensure that you have all the required libraries
</li><li>Install Thunderbird in a way that works best with your distribution
</li><li>Create shortcuts to launch Thunderbird
</li><li>Make Thunderbird available to all users of your computer
</li><li>Make removing Thunderbird work the same as removing any other application 
</li></ul>
<p>Package management also has some disadvantages:
</p>
<ul><li>It may not give you the latest version of Thunderbird
</li><li>It may give you a version without Thunderbird branding 
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_installing-from-a-package-manager">Installing from a package manager</h1>
<p>To install Thunderbird using the package manager, please refer to the documentation of the Linux distribution you''re using.
</p>
<h1 id="w_installing-outside-of-a-package-manager">Installing outside of a package manager</h1>
<p>Complete instructions for installing Thunderbird outside of package management may be available at your distribution''s support website. For example:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://help.ubuntu.com/community/ThunderbirdNewVersion">Installing Thunderbird on Ubuntu</a>
</li></ul>
<div class="note">
<ul><li> <strong>Before you install Thunderbird</strong>, make sure that your computer has the <strong> <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/system-requirements/">required libraries</a></strong> installed. Missing libraries will cause Thunderbird to be inoperable.
</li><li> The installation file provided by Mozilla in <strong>.tar.bz2</strong> format does not contain sources but pre-compiled binary files, therefore you can simply unpack and run them. There is no need to compile the program from source.
</li><li> The following instructions will install Thunderbird into your home directory, and <strong>only the current user</strong> will be able to <strong>run it</strong>.</li></ul></div>

<ol><li> Download Thunderbird from <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net">the Thunderbird download page</a> to your home directory. 
</li><li> Open a <strong>Terminal</strong> and go to your home directory: <code>cd ~</code>
</li><li> Extract the contents of the downloaded file: <code>tar xjf thunderbird-*.tar.bz2</code>
</li><li> Close Thunderbird if it''s open.
</li><li> To start Thunderbird, run the <span class="filepath">thunderbird</span> script in the <span class="filepath">thunderbird</span> folder: <code>~/thunderbird/thunderbird</code>
</li></ol>
<p>Thunderbird should now start. You can then create a launcher on your desktop to run this command.
</p>
<h2 id="w_libstdc5-error">libstdc++5 error</h2>
<p>As noted above, you need to install the <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/system-requirements/">required libraries</a> for Thunderbird to work. Many distributions don''t include libstdc++5 by default.
</p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-not-installed-message-or-wrong-version-of-thunderbird-starts">"Thunderbird not installed" message or wrong version of Thunderbird starts</h2>
<p>If Thunderbird is installed following the instructions given above, it must be started (in a <strong>Terminal</strong> or in a launcher on the Desktop, for example) using the command: <code>~/thunderbird/thunderbird</code>
</p><p>If you try to start Thunderbird in a <strong>Terminal</strong> with the command: <code>thunderbird</code>, it will either start the package-manager-installed version of Thunderbird or will tell you the program is not installed.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "193857", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jan 20, 2020, 7:58:05 AM", "datetime": "2020-01-20T07:58:05-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Sancus", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Sancus/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "update links to thunderbird site", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Mar 20, 2021, 9:41:06 AM", "datetime": "2021-03-20T09:41:06-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:10:51+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (200755,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/200755',189887,'2020-05-23T09:21:41-07:00','Matt','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/MattAuSupport/','Updated Toads revision with the Xhtlm and the hidden prefs.',1,'2020-05-23T19:37:09-07:00','MattAuSupport',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.

{note}''''''Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?'''''' The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account{for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account.

A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using operating system account does address that.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__

= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Put a check mark next to ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/><br/>{for mac}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for linux}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/>{/for}
# The ''''Change Master Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>{for mac}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for linux}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]{/for} <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.

Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Changing the master password =

# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new master password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Removing your master password =

Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.

= Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten =
If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.{/warning}

# Choose {menu Tools -> Developer Tools -> Error Console}.
# Paste the following text: <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
# Press the {button Evaluate} button.
# If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.
# When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.

<!-- MZ credit -->
<br/> <br/>
''''''''''Originally based on information from  [http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password Master password (mozillaZine KB)]''''''''''','<p>Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?</strong> The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account<span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account.
A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=config+editor" title="Page does not exist.">config editor</a> to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using operating system account does address that.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Put a check mark next to <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br><br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span>
</li><li> The <em>Change Master Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changing-the-master-password">Changing the master password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password">Removing your master password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.
</p>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Developer Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>.
</li><li> Paste the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</li><li> Press the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button.
</li><li> If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.
</li></ol>
<p>
<br> <br>
<em><strong>Originally based on information from  <a href="http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password">Master password (mozillaZine KB)</a></strong></em>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "200755", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "May 23, 2020, 9:21:41 AM", "datetime": "2020-05-23T09:21:41-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Matt", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/MattAuSupport/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Updated Toads revision with the Xhtlm and the hidden prefs.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "May 23, 2020, 7:37:09 PM", "datetime": "2020-05-23T19:37:09-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "MattAuSupport", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:45+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (200769,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/200769',200755,'2020-05-23T22:44:36-07:00','Matt','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/MattAuSupport/','corrected a spelling error',1,'2020-05-23T22:44:56-07:00','MattAuSupport',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.

{note}''''''Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?'''''' The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.

A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__

= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Put a check mark next to ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/><br/>{for mac}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for linux}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/>{/for}
# The ''''Change Master Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>{for mac}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for linux}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]{/for} <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.

Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Changing the master password =

# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new master password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Removing your master password =

Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.

= Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten =
If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.{/warning}

# Choose {menu Tools -> Developer Tools -> Error Console}.
# Paste the following text: <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
# Press the {button Evaluate} button.
# If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.
# When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.

<!-- MZ credit -->
<br/> <br/>
''''''''''Originally based on information from  [http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password Master password (mozillaZine KB)]''''''''''','<p>Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?</strong> The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.
A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=config+editor" title="Page does not exist.">config editor</a> to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Put a check mark next to <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br><br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span>
</li><li> The <em>Change Master Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changing-the-master-password">Changing the master password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password">Removing your master password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.
</p>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Developer Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>.
</li><li> Paste the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</li><li> Press the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button.
</li><li> If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.
</li></ol>
<p>
<br> <br>
<em><strong>Originally based on information from  <a href="http://kb.mozillazine.org/Master_password">Master password (mozillaZine KB)</a></strong></em>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "200769", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "May 23, 2020, 10:44:36 PM", "datetime": "2020-05-23T22:44:36-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Matt", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/MattAuSupport/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "corrected a spelling error", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "May 23, 2020, 10:44:56 PM", "datetime": "2020-05-23T22:44:56-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "MattAuSupport", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:42+00:00','M');
