<div class="note"><strong>Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?</strong> The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.
A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=config+editor" title="Page does not exist.">config editor</a> to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Put a check mark next to <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br><br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span>
</li><li> The <em>Change Master Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changing-the-master-password">Changing the master password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password">Removing your master password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.
</p>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Developer Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>.
</li><li> Copy and paste the relevance text as shown below. The choice is determined by the version you are using.
</li></ol>
<p>If using a version prior to 78*:<br>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
</p><p>If using version 78* or later: <br>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</p><p>3. Press the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button.  If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.<br>
4. When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.
</p><p><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="TB68_Reset_Master_Password_Win" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2020-07-10-04-49-22-509589.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "204683", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 4, 2020, 2:56:06 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-04T02:56:06-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Toad-Hall", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Toad-Hall/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "If forgotten and need to reset, Version in use determines the text to copy.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 6, 2020, 10:39:03 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-06T10:39:03-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "MattAuSupport", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Aug 6, 2020, 10:39:32 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-06T10:39:32-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "MattAuSupport", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:35+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (204854,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/204854',204683,'2020-08-07T03:21:39-07:00','Angela Lazar','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/anlazar/','Add template about primary password (still master in thunderbird)',1,'2020-08-07T03:21:48-07:00','anlazar',1,0,1,'2020-08-07T03:21:48-07:00','anlazar','approved','[[Template:primarypassword]]

Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.

{note}''''''Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?'''''' The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.

A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__

= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Put a check mark next to ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/><br/>{for mac}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for linux}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/>{/for}
# The ''''Change Master Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>{for mac}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for linux}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]{/for} <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.

Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Changing the master password =

# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new master password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Removing your master password =

Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.

= Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten =
If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.{/warning}

# Choose {menu Tools -> Developer Tools -> Error Console}.
# Copy and paste the relevance text as shown below. The choice is determined by the version you are using.
If using a version prior to 78*:<br/>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>

If using version 78* or later: <br/>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>

3. Press the {button Evaluate} button.  If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.<br/>
4. When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.

{for win}[[Image:TB68_Reset_Master_Password_Win]]{/for}','<p></p><div class="note"><strong>Primary Password is replacing Master Password.</strong> Firefox is removing terminology from the browser that has been identified as derogatory or exclusionary. <a href="/en-US/kb/primary-password-replacing-master-password">Learn more</a> about why we’re making this change.</div>
<p></p><p>Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?</strong> The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.
A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=config+editor" title="Page does not exist.">config editor</a> to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.</div>
