</p><p><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="TB68_Reset_Master_Password_Win" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2020-07-10-04-49-22-509589.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "204854", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 7, 2020, 3:21:39 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-07T03:21:39-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Angela Lazar", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/anlazar/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Add template about primary password (still master in thunderbird)", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 7, 2020, 3:21:48 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-07T03:21:48-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "anlazar", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Aug 7, 2020, 3:21:48 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-07T03:21:48-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "anlazar", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:33+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (205025,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/205025',204854,'2020-08-08T07:10:00-07:00','Chris Ilias','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Chris_Ilias/','reverting previous edit - template is referring to Firefox, not Thunderbird',1,'2020-08-08T07:11:57-07:00','Chris_Ilias',1,0,1,'2020-08-10T22:25:38-07:00','Chris_Ilias','approved','Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.

{note}''''''Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?'''''' The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.

A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.{/note}
[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

__TOC__

= Defining a master password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Put a check mark next to ''''''Use a master password''''''. <br/><br/>{for mac}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for linux}[[Image:Create_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/> {/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png]]<br/>{/for}
# The ''''Change Master Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>.
#* Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
# The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br/> <br/>{for mac}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for linux}[[Image:Choose_Master_Password-Mac]]<br/>{/for}{for win}[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png]]{/for} <br/>
# To set the master password, click {button OK}.

Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Changing the master password =

# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Security} panel.
# Select the {menu Passwords} tab.
# Click {button Change Master Password}.
# Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new master password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Removing your master password =

Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.

= Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten =
If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.{/warning}

# Choose {menu Tools -> Developer Tools -> Error Console}.
# Copy and paste the relevance text as shown below. The choice is determined by the version you are using.
If using a version prior to 78*:<br/>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>

If using version 78* or later: <br/>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br/> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>

3. Press the {button Evaluate} button.  If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.<br/>
4. When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.

{for win}[[Image:TB68_Reset_Master_Password_Win]]{/for}','<p>Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?</strong> The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">MacOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.
A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=config+editor" title="Page does not exist.">config editor</a> to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.</div>
<p><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-0c0fbe.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_defining-a-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Defining a master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changing-the-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing the master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_defining-a-master-password">Defining a master password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a master password to protect stored credentials. To define a master password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Put a check mark next to <strong>Use a master password</strong>. <br><br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Create_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-39-17-24daf9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br> </span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-3.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-07-50-a9f968.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span>
</li><li> The <em>Change Master Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your master password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ).
</li><li> Also, remember to make your password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess to ensure that you, and only you, will be able to retrieve this secure information.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to confirm that you can enter it consistently.<br> <br><span class="for" data-for="mac"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;"><img alt="Choose_Master_Password-Mac" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2016-04-23-22-43-02-3197a1.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""><br></span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-1.png" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2011-03-14-10-08-05-ea94cc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> <br>
</li><li> To set the master password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your master password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changing-the-master-password">Changing the master password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Security</span> panel.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Passwords</span> tab.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Change Master Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter the current master password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new master password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password">Removing your master password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to change the password, but leave both of the new master password fields empty.
</p>
<h1 id="w_removing-your-master-password-if-the-master-password-has-been-forgotten">Removing your master password if the master password has been forgotten</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your master password you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Choose <span class="menu">Tools -&gt; Developer Tools -&gt; Error Console</span>.
</li><li> Copy and paste the relevance text as shown below. The choice is determined by the version you are using.
</li></ol>
<p>If using a version prior to 78*:<br>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
</p><p>If using version 78* or later: <br>
Copy and Paste all of the following text: <br> <code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</p><p>3. Press the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button.  If you do not see an ''Evaluate'' button, Press the ''Return/Enter'' key on keyboard.<br>
4. When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click on ''Reset'' button.
</p><p><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;"><img alt="TB68_Reset_Master_Password_Win" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2020-07-10-04-49-22-509589.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "205025", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 8, 2020, 7:10:00 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-08T07:10:00-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Chris Ilias", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Chris_Ilias/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "reverting previous edit - template is referring to Firefox, not Thunderbird", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 8, 2020, 7:11:57 AM", "datetime": "2020-08-08T07:11:57-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "Chris_Ilias", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Aug 10, 2020, 10:25:38 PM", "datetime": "2020-08-10T22:25:38-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "Chris_Ilias", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:30+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (212874,'en-US','config-editor','Config Editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor/revision/212874',172235,'2020-12-19T05:02:27-08:00','arfan','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Arfantrendy09/','yes',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.

To access the config editor, go to {menu {for mac}Thunderbird | Preferences{/for}{for win}Tools | Options{/for}{for linux}Edit | Preferences{/for}}, select the {menu Advanced} panel, select the {menu General} tab, and click {button Config Editor...}.

[[Image:Config Editor Warning]] 
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful. 

{warning}Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config  editor; therefore please proceed with caution.{/warning}

Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click {button I''ll be careful, I promise!} to proceed.

[[Image:Config Editor]] 
In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Filter" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.

Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.

To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select {menu Modify} (for true/false preferences, select {menu Toggle}). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select {menu Reset}.

In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the {menu New} menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, {menu Reset} it as described above.

Modifications to preferences happen immediately, i.e., you don''t have to click a {button Save} button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect.Note a restart requires all windows to be closed including the config editor window and {for win}Options{/for}{for mac,linux}Preferences{/for} windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!

It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.

[[T:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/1gx91XC]]','<p>You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.
</p><p>To access the config editor, go to <span class="menu"><span class="for" data-for="mac">Thunderbird | Preferences</span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Tools | Options</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">Edit | Preferences</span></span>, select the <span class="menu">Advanced</span> panel, select the <span class="menu">General</span> tab, and click <span class="button">Config Editor...</span>.
</p><p><img alt="Config Editor Warning" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-16-17-917d30.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
The first time you do this, you will get a warning telling you to be careful. 
</p>
<div class="warning">Please take this warning seriously; it is possible to make Thunderbird unusable by changing preferences in the config  editor; therefore please proceed with caution.</div>
<p>Uncheck the "Show this warning next time" box if you don''t want to see the warning again, then click <span class="button">I''ll be careful, I promise!</span> to proceed.
</p><p><img alt="Config Editor" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2012-09-30-22-17-04-220cb6.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
In the config editor, you can type a string in the "Filter" field to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.
</p><p>Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold.
</p><p>To edit a preference, double-click on it, or right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Modify</span> (for true/false preferences, select <span class="menu">Toggle</span>). To reset a preference to its default value, right-click on it and select <span class="menu">Reset</span>.
</p><p>In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons. To add a new preference, right-click anywhere within the list of preferences, select the <span class="menu">New</span> menu item, and then select the appropriate sub-menu item for the type of preference you need to create. To delete a previously added preference, <span class="menu">Reset</span> it as described above.
</p><p>Modifications to preferences happen immediately, i.e., you don''t have to click a <span class="button">Save</span> button. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect.Note a restart requires all windows to be closed including the config editor window and <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Options</span><span class="for" data-for="mac,linux">Preferences</span> windows in addition to the main Thunderbird window!
</p><p>It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.
</p><p>&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "212874", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Dec 19, 2020, 5:02:27 AM", "datetime": "2020-12-19T05:02:27-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "arfan", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Arfantrendy09/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:05:58+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (214902,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/214902',205025,'2021-02-03T22:05:00-08:00','Marcelo Ghelman','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/marcelo.ghelman/','Formatting and minor changes',1,'2021-07-07T05:43:34-07:00','kewisch',1,0,1,'2021-07-07T05:43:34-07:00','kewisch','approved','Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the master password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a master password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a master password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.

[[Image:ef5f631b4f4f00424fd9f2a09ddaeb4e-1290640828-218-2.png]]

{note}''''''Want to prevent others from seeing your messages?'''''' The best secure method if you share a computer, is to create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}MacOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.<br><br>

A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True.  This will prevent the display of email until the master password is entered.  It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file.  Using an operating system account does address that.{/note}

