<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The previously inbuilt support for the <em>WeTransfer</em> FileLink provider has been removed.</div>
<h1 id="w_adding-filelink">Adding Filelink</h1>
<p>To use this feature, you need to install one of the <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a> first, as described below. For some online storage service providers, you may have to first create a new account or configure an existing account with them.
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Preferences</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Composition</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Attachments</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Find more providers...</span>
</li><li> Choose a FileLink Provider add-on, and click <span class="button">＋ Add to Thunderbird</span>.
</li><li> Confirm add-on permissions from the popup panel with <span class="button">Add</span>.
</li><li> In the <span class="menu">Attachments</span> section of the <em>Preferences</em> (see above), click on the button <span class="button">Add [Your FileLink provider]</span>.
</li><li> Add information as requested by the addon.
</li><li> Thunderbird will suggest using FileLink for attachments larger than 5 MB (you can change the threshold in the same section), or you can right-click on a regular attachment to convert it.
</li></ul>
<div class="note">
<ul><li> If you want to be prompted to use Filelink when a message attachment exceeds a certain size, tick the following option:
</li></ul>
<p><strong>[✓] Offer to share for files larger than [ 5] MB</strong>
</p>
<ul><li> You may also change the threshold file size as required.</li></ul></div>

<h1 id="w_using-filelink">Using Filelink</h1>
<p>When you attach a file to a message, and the file size exceeds the size you have specified above, Thunderbird will automatically offer to use Filelink:
</p><p><img alt="Proton-FileLink-LargeFileNotification" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-10-14-54-16-912351.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<ul><li> Click the <span class="button">Link</span> button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.
</li><li> If you prefer to attach the file as a <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=how+to+use+attachments" title="Page does not exist.">regular attachment</a>, choose <span class="button">Ignore</span>.
</li></ul>
<p>After choosing <span class="button">Link</span>, your file will be uploaded in the background and the displayed message will update to:
</p><p><img alt="Proton-FileLink-UploadingFileNotification" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-10-14-51-18-5d2e96.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div class="note"> Regardless of the file size and the Filelink notification, you can always force a file to be attached using Filelink:
<ul><li> Choose the down arrow of the <span class="button">Attach | v</span> button on the composition toolbar to access the <span class="menu">Filelink</span> submenu.
</li><li> Right-click on a regular attachment and from the context menu, choose <span class="menu">Convert to...</span> &gt; <span class="menu">[Your FileLink provider]</span>. You can choose from a list of your enabled providers.</li></ul></div>

<p>When the linking is complete – that is, your attachment has been uploaded to the service provider – Thunderbird will automatically add a section in the message with a link to the online copy of your file  as shown below:
</p><p><img alt="TB78-filelink-linked-anonymous" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-08-29-10-09-54-3855ee.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The link section will appear in plain text, if <strong>Compose messages in HTML format</strong> is turned off in Account Settings.</div>
<p>When your recipients receive the message, they will see the same information and clicking the link in the message will take them to a page where they can download the attachment.
</p>
<hr>
<h1 id="w_filelink-faq">Filelink FAQ</h1>
<p>Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Thunderbird servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.
</p>
<h2 id="w_does-thunderbird-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked">Does Thunderbird know which provider the user has picked?</h2>
<p>No. Thunderbird is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.
</p>
<h2 id="w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported">What storage services are currently supported?</h2>
<ul><li> A number of popular file storage providers are supported through <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a>.
</li><li> You can also use Filelink with your preferred WebDAV server with the <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/filelink-provider-for-webdav/">Filelink provider for WebDAV</a> add-on.
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>
<h2 id="w_can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments">Can the storage service view my attachments?</h2>
<p>Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment. Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. Service providers will generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service. 
</p><p>Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, without using Thunderbird’s OpenPGP or SMIME features, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol. We recommend using Thunderbirds OpenPGP or SMIME features for maximum security of your communications.
</p>
<h2 id="w_how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site">How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</h2>
<p>Depending on the terms and conditions of your third-party storage service provider and your service agreement with them, the file may remain available on the provider’s site for a limited period, a certain number of downloads, or until you explicitly delete it. You will need to log in to the provider’s website to view and delete the files in your personal storage space.
</p>
<h2 id="w_can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y">Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</h2>
<p>Please check if the Thunderbird community has provided a <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-on</a> for the service provider you are looking for.
<a href="https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/78/cloudFile.html">Developer documentation for the Filelink API</a> is available.
</p>
<h2 id="w_i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work">I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</h2>
<p>Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We are just offering you an option to upload large attachments somewhere else if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file defaults to 5 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird’s Preferences:
</p>
<ol><li> Click <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Preferences</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Composition</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Attachments</span>
</li><li> <strong>[✓] Offer to share for files larger than [ 5] MB</strong>
</li></ol>
<p>Removing the checkmark will prevent the automatical Filelink notification when you attach big files, but the Filelink feature will still be available from the <span class="button">Attach | v</span> button dropdown as well as other attachment menus and context menus.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "228106", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 30, 2021, 3:46:50 AM", "datetime": "2021-08-30T03:46:50-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Michele Rodaro", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/michro/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Edited a Link/anchor not working properly", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 30, 2021, 11:14:42 AM", "datetime": "2021-08-30T11:14:42-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "firefox877", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Aug 30, 2021, 11:14:42 AM", "datetime": "2021-08-30T11:14:42-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "firefox877", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:07:14+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (229936,'en-US','config-editor','Config Editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor/revision/229936',224055,'2021-10-02T10:42:38-07:00','Matt','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/MattAuSupport/','updated the aticle for TG91.  So wholesale image and text updates.',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.

To access the config editor, go to {menu {for mac}Thunderbird | Preferences{/for}{for win}Tools | Preferences{/for}{for linux}Edit | Preferences{/for}}, search for config and click on the cofig editor button.

[[Image:Warning on Config editor window]] 

Clicking on the all button in the display will show all hidden preferences in a tab called "Advanced Preferences"

[[Image:Image of typical config editor tab]] 

In the config editor, you can type a string in the field showing "search preference name to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.

{note}''''''Note:'''''' Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold. and there is a revert arrow on the end of the entry torevert the setting to default [[Image:Config Editor RevertIcon]]  {/note}

To edit a preference in the case of a true/false preference click on the [[Image:Config Editor Toggle]]  , for text preferences, double clicking on the preference will enable inplace editing.  Once the edit is complete save your changes by clicking the checkmark. [[Image:Config Editor Save Checkmark]] 

In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t not normally need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons.
To add a new preference: 
# type or paste the preference name into the search box
# Select the appropriate preference type (number, string or [https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Boolean boolean] from the offered options.
# click on the [[Image:Config Editor Add]] to add the preference
To delete a previously added preference click the trash icon folowing the entry. [[Image:Copnfig Editor Trash Icon]] 

Modifications to preferences happen immediately. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.

[[T:ShareArticle|link=http://mzl.la/1gx91XC]]','<p>You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.
</p><p>To access the config editor, go to <span class="menu"><span class="for" data-for="mac">Thunderbird | Preferences</span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Tools | Preferences</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">Edit | Preferences</span></span>, search for config and click on the cofig editor button.
</p><p><img alt="Warning on Config editor window" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-12-01-9fa64c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>Clicking on the all button in the display will show all hidden preferences in a tab called "Advanced Preferences"
</p><p><img alt="Image of typical config editor tab" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-17-44-66b6d9.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>In the config editor, you can type a string in the field showing "search preference name to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold. and there is a revert arrow on the end of the entry torevert the setting to default <img alt="Config Editor RevertIcon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-23-59-0714d2.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">  </div>
<p>To edit a preference in the case of a true/false preference click on the <img alt="Config Editor Toggle" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-25-33-564c26.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">  , for text preferences, double clicking on the preference will enable inplace editing.  Once the edit is complete save your changes by clicking the checkmark. <img alt="Config Editor Save Checkmark" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-29-50-a19d13.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t not normally need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons.
To add a new preference: 
</p>
<ol><li> type or paste the preference name into the search box
</li><li> Select the appropriate preference type (number, string or <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Boolean">boolean</a> from the offered options.
</li><li> click on the <img alt="Config Editor Add" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-38-25-7cf634.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> to add the preference
</li></ol>
<p>To delete a previously added preference click the trash icon folowing the entry. <img alt="Copnfig Editor Trash Icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-10-02-17-39-45-5e5ea2.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</p><p>Modifications to preferences happen immediately. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.
</p><p>&nbsp;

</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "229936", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 2, 2021, 10:42:38 AM", "datetime": "2021-10-02T10:42:38-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Matt", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/MattAuSupport/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "updated the aticle for TG91.  So wholesale image and text updates.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:05:53+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (229937,'en-US','config-editor','Config Editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/config-editor/revision/229937',229936,'2021-10-02T10:47:47-07:00','Matt','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/MattAuSupport/','updated the accessing to use the appenu which is common accross platforms for the location of preferecnes.',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','You may sometimes need to view or edit Thunderbird preference settings which are not accessible through the normal user interface. You can use Thunderbird''s "config editor" to do this.

To access the config editor, go to [[Image:Fx57Menu]] and seelct preferences. Search for config and click on the cofig editor button when it is displayed.

[[Image:Warning on Config editor window]] 

Clicking on the all button in the display will show all hidden preferences in a tab called "Advanced Preferences"

[[Image:Image of typical config editor tab]] 

In the config editor, you can type a string in the field showing "search preference name to filter the list of preferences to only those that contain the specified string in their names or values.

{note}''''''Note:'''''' Preferences that have been changed from their default values are shown in bold. and there is a revert arrow on the end of the entry torevert the setting to default [[Image:Config Editor RevertIcon]]  {/note}

To edit a preference in the case of a true/false preference click on the [[Image:Config Editor Toggle]]  , for text preferences, double clicking on the preference will enable inplace editing.  Once the edit is complete save your changes by clicking the checkmark. [[Image:Config Editor Save Checkmark]] 

In rare cases you may need to add a new preference. You shouldn''t not normally need to do this unless you are instructed to do so by documentation for Thunderbird or one of its add-ons.
To add a new preference: 
# type or paste the preference name into the search box
# Select the appropriate preference type (number, string or [https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Boolean boolean] from the offered options.
# click on the [[Image:Config Editor Add]] to add the preference
To delete a previously added preference click the trash icon folowing the entry. [[Image:Copnfig Editor Trash Icon]] 

Modifications to preferences happen immediately. However, some parts of Thunderbird or many add-ons only read their preferences on start-up, so it is good practice to restart Thunderbird after editing preferences for your changes to take effect. It is also important to know that when you restore a preference to it''s default value, Thunderbird does not store that preference in the preferences file.  It is only non default preferences that are stored.

