If you have cookies disabled in Thunderbird then the OAuth process will fail, because cookies must be enabled for OAuth 2.0 authentication flow to work properly. Without cookies to store data between page updates, the authentication flow cannot successfully complete the web pages served by Google.

To avoid or correct the problem, do one of the following:
* Ensure that ''''''Accept cookies from sites'''''' is enabled at {button ≡} > {menu Preferences} > type ''''''cookies'''''' into the search field. You can disable cookies after the OAuth process is completed, and receiving and sending mail will work with cookies disabled. But when the OAuth token for Gmail access expires at some date in the future, the OAuth process will be needed again, which will require cookies to be enabled.
* Create an exception with cookies disabled at {button ≡} > {menu Preferences} > {menu Privacy & Security} > {menu Web Content} > {menu Accept cookies from sites} (with {menu Accept} unchecked), click {button Exceptions} and enter an address of ''''''https://accounts.google.com''''''. Pick {button Allow}, then {button Save Changes}.

For greater detail about cookies see the article [[Privacy panel - Remote content and cookie settings in Thunderbird]].

If you still have problems, you will want to use "Get Help" to ask a question in Thunderbird support.','<p>Thunderbird 91.8.0, released 5th April 2022, converts Gmail accounts to OAuth&nbsp;2.0 to help users comply with <a href="https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/6010255">Google''s requirement of OAuth&nbsp;2.0 for Gmail logins</a>. These changes are <strong>required</strong> by Google.
</p><p>If you have cookies disabled in Thunderbird then the OAuth process will fail, because cookies must be enabled for OAuth&nbsp;2.0 authentication flow to work properly. Without cookies to store data between page updates, the authentication flow cannot successfully complete the web pages served by Google.
</p><p>To avoid or correct the problem, do one of the following:
</p>
<ul><li> Ensure that <strong>Accept cookies from sites</strong> is enabled at <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Preferences</span> &gt; type <strong>cookies</strong> into the search field. You can disable cookies after the OAuth process is completed, and receiving and sending mail will work with cookies disabled. But when the OAuth token for Gmail access expires at some date in the future, the OAuth process will be needed again, which will require cookies to be enabled.
</li><li> Create an exception with cookies disabled at <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Preferences</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Web Content</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Accept cookies from sites</span> (with <span class="menu">Accept</span> unchecked), click <span class="button">Exceptions</span> and enter an address of <strong><a href="https://accounts.google.com">https://accounts.google.com</a></strong>. Pick <span class="button">Allow</span>, then <span class="button">Save Changes</span>.
</li></ul>
<p>For greater detail about cookies see the article <a href="/en-US/kb/privacy-panel-settings-in-thunderbird">Privacy panel - Remote content and cookie settings in Thunderbird</a>.
</p><p>If you still have problems, you will want to use "Get Help" to ask a question in Thunderbird support.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "243421", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "May 4, 2022, 5:41:21 AM", "datetime": "2022-05-04T05:41:21-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Wayne Mery", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "point to Privacy panel - Remote content and cookie settings in Thunderbird", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "May 4, 2022, 5:42:50 AM", "datetime": "2022-05-04T05:42:50-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "May 4, 2022, 5:42:50 AM", "datetime": "2022-05-04T05:42:50-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:04:27+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (243422,'en-US','automatic-conversion-google-mail-accounts-oauth20','Automatic Conversion of Google mail accounts to OAuth 2.0 Authentication','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/automatic-conversion-google-mail-accounts-oauth20','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/automatic-conversion-google-mail-accounts-oauth20/revision/243422',243421,'2022-05-04T06:03:15-07:00','Wayne Mery','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/','reverse the  order of thetwo choices. And make the steps and description more explicit',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','Thunderbird 91.8.0, released 5th April 2022, converts Gmail accounts to OAuth 2.0 to help users comply with [https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/6010255 Google''s requirement of OAuth 2.0 for Gmail logins]. These changes are ''''''required'''''' by Google.

If you have cookies disabled in Thunderbird then the OAuth process will fail, because cookies must be enabled for OAuth 2.0 authentication flow to work properly. Without cookies to store data between page updates, the authentication flow cannot successfully complete the web pages served by Google.

To avoid or correct an OAauth failure, ''''''assuming your current settings are {menu Accept cookies from sites} is {menu Accept} unchecked)'''''', do one of the following:
* Create an exception for not accepting cookies at {button ≡} > {menu Preferences} > {menu Privacy & Security} > {menu Web Content} > {menu Accept cookies from sites} (with {menu Accept} unchecked) - click {button Exceptions} and enter an address of ''''''https://accounts.google.com''''''. Pick {button Allow}, then {button Save Changes}.
* Allow ''''''all'''''' cookies by setting ''''''Accept cookies from sites'''''' to enabled at {button ≡} > {menu Preferences} > {menu Privacy & Security} > {menu Web Content} > {menu Accept cookies from sites} (with {menu Accept} checked). (After you have gone through the OAuth process, if you prefer, you can again disable cookies - because receiving and sending mail will work with cookies disabled. But disabling all cookies has the disadvantage that when the OAuth token for Gmail access expires at some date in the future the OAuth process will be needed again, which will require cookies to be enabled.)


For greater detail about cookies see the article [[Privacy panel - Remote content and cookie settings in Thunderbird]].

If you still have problems, you will want to use "Get Help" to ask a question in Thunderbird support.','<p>Thunderbird 91.8.0, released 5th April 2022, converts Gmail accounts to OAuth&nbsp;2.0 to help users comply with <a href="https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/6010255">Google''s requirement of OAuth&nbsp;2.0 for Gmail logins</a>. These changes are <strong>required</strong> by Google.
</p><p>If you have cookies disabled in Thunderbird then the OAuth process will fail, because cookies must be enabled for OAuth&nbsp;2.0 authentication flow to work properly. Without cookies to store data between page updates, the authentication flow cannot successfully complete the web pages served by Google.
</p><p>To avoid or correct an OAauth failure, <strong>assuming your current settings are <span class="menu">Accept cookies from sites</span> is <span class="menu">Accept</span> unchecked)</strong>, do one of the following:
</p>
