If you have cookies disabled in Thunderbird then the OAuth process will fail, because cookies must be allowed for OAuth 2.0 authentication flow to work properly. Without cookies to store data between page updates, the authentication flow cannot successfully complete the web pages served by Google.

To avoid or correct an OAauth failure, ''''''assuming you have non-default setings to reject cookies'''''', do one of the following and then go through the OAuth login process again:
* Create an exception, to allow cookies from Google. Do {button ≡} > {menu Preferences} > {menu Privacy & Security} > {menu Web Content} > {menu Accept cookies from sites} with {menu Accept cookies from sites} unchecked, then click {button Exceptions} and enter an address of ''''''https://accounts.google.com''''''. Pick {button Allow}, then {button Save Changes}.
