</p><p>If you have cookies disabled in Thunderbird then the OAuth process will fail, because cookies must be allowed for OAuth&nbsp;2.0 authentication flow to work properly. Without cookies to store data between page updates, the authentication flow cannot successfully complete the web pages served by Google.
</p><p>To avoid or correct an OAauth failure, <strong>assuming you have non-default setings to reject cookies</strong>, do one of the following and then go through the OAuth login process again:
</p>
<ul><li> Create an exception, to allow cookies from Google. Do <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Preferences</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Web Content</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Accept cookies from sites</span> with <span class="menu">Accept cookies from sites</span> unchecked, then click <span class="button">Exceptions</span> and enter an address of <strong><a href="https://accounts.google.com">https://accounts.google.com</a></strong>. Pick <span class="button">Allow</span>, then <span class="button">Save Changes</span>.
