# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Put a checkmark next to ''''''Use a Primary Password''''''.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password]]
# The ''''Change Primary Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>
#* Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only ''''you yourself'''' will be able to see your saved passwords.
# The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog]]
# To set your Primary Password, click {button OK}.
Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Change your Primary Password =
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Click the button {button Change Primary Password}.
# Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new Primary Password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Remove your Primary Password =
Follow the steps above to ''''Change your Primary Password'''', but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty.

= Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it =
If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.{/warning}
# Press the shortcut keys {key Ctrl+Shift+J} ({key Command+Shift+J} on macOS) to open the error console
# Copy the text given below and paste it into the Console. The choice is determined by [[Find what version of Thunderbird you are using|the version you are using]]:
#* If using a version prior to 78, copy and paste the following text:<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
#* If using version 78 or later, copy and paste the following text:<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
# Click the {button Evaluate} button. If you do not see an ''''Evaluate'''' button, press {key Return} or {key Enter} on your keyboard.
# When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the {button Reset} button.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog]]','<div class="note"><em>Primary Password</em> has replaced <em>Master Password.</em> Learn more about <a href="/en-US/kb/primary-password-replacing-master-password-thunderbird">why we’ve made this change</a>.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">What does the Primary Password protect?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_set-a-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Set a Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_change-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Change your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_remove-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Remove your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect">What does the Primary Password protect?</h1>
<div class="warning"><strong>Your primary password protects your saved passwords, but it <em>does not</em> prevent others from seeing your messages in Thunderbird.</strong></div><strong>
</strong>
<p>Thunderbird can save passwords for each account, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. You can view them and remove the ones you don''t use anymore.
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords? With the Primary Password feature, Thunderbird will require you to enter a Primary Password in order to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. When you have a Primary Password set, you''ll only need to enter it once per session.
</p><p><img alt="tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-12-29-50-749867.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Do you want to prevent others from seeing your messages?</strong> If you are sharing a computer, the most secure way is to create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, and make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password. Thunderbird stores accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.

</p>
</div>
<h1 id="w_set-a-primary-password">Set a Primary Password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Put a checkmark next to <strong>Use a Primary Password</strong>.<br><br><img alt="tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-09-05-dfd94c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> The <em>Change Primary Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( )
</li><li> Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only <em>you yourself</em> will be able to see your saved passwords.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br><br><img alt="tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-59-36-393ff3.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> To set your Primary Password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_change-your-primary-password">Change your Primary Password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Click the button <span class="button">Change Primary Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new Primary Password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_remove-your-primary-password">Remove your Primary Password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to <em>Change your Primary Password</em>, but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty.
</p>
<h1 id="w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Press the shortcut keys <span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span> (<span class="key">Command</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span> on macOS) to open the error console
</li><li> Copy the text given below and paste it into the Console. The choice is determined by <a href="/en-US/kb/find-what-version-thunderbird-you-are-using">the version you are using</a>:
<ul><li> If using a version prior to 78, copy and paste the following text:<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xul")</code>
</li><li> If using version 78 or later, copy and paste the following text:<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button. If you do not see an <em>Evaluate</em> button, press <span class="key">Return</span> or <span class="key">Enter</span> on your keyboard.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the <span class="button">Reset</span> button.<br><br><img alt="tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-15-48-30-8ed674.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "281315", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 20, 2024, 5:28:52 AM", "datetime": "2024-06-20T05:28:52.905026-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "AliceWyman", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/AliceWyman/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Setting->Set, Changing->Change, etc.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jul 2, 2024, 5:07:45 PM", "datetime": "2024-07-02T17:07:45.945759-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "AliceWyman", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:16+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (283347,'en-US','thunderbird-and-gmail','Thunderbird and Gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-gmail/revision/283347',276408,'2024-07-29T19:38:12.738193-07:00','Wayne Mery','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/','recommend unsubscribing All Mail folder. Clarify that unsubscribing causes messages to not download.',1,'2024-07-30T22:07:57.834706-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s [https://www.google.com/gmail/about/ Gmail] service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
{warning}As of May 30, 2022, ''''''Google no longer allows [https://myaccount.google.com/lesssecureapps Less secure apps] access'''''' for Gmail accounts, thus ''''''OAuth is required''''''. If you are using Google supplied “app passwords“, Thunderbird version 91.8.0 and newer will [[Automatic Conversion of Google mail accounts to OAuth 2.0 Authentication|make that conversion]] for you. Further, ''''''Gmail accounts do not work with Thunderbird 31 and older'''''' because they do not support Google''s OAuth (implemented in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=849540 bug 849540]).{/warning}

__TOC__

= Configuring your Gmail account =
{for win}
[[Video:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JXs_YE9QyUQ]]
{/for}
To configure Thunderbird to work with Gmail, first ensure IMAP is enabled on your Gmail account (the default for new Gmail accounts). See [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/7126229 Gmail''s instructions] for help.
{for not tb38}
Double check that “Access for less secure apps” is enabled. See [https://www.google.com/settings/security/lesssecureapps Google''s article on “less secure apps”] for help.
{/for}

Next, select {menu File} > {menu New} > {menu Existing Mail Account…} The ''''Mail Account Setup'''' dialog is displayed. After you provide your account information (real name, email address{for not tb38}, password{/for}), Thunderbird will automatically configure your Gmail connection. Click {button Done}{for tb38}, sign in with your Google account if prompted{/for}. Thunderbird will begin downloading your existing messages.

Lastly, review [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892#zippy=%2Cthunderbird Google''s recommended settings for Thunderbird] so that your local actions, such as labelling and deleting, correspond to those which Gmail takes and do not cause any conflicts.

For more detailed information, please refer to:
* [[Automatic Account Configuration]]
* [https://support.google.com/mail/topic/7280141 Gmail''s support articles on email clients]

= Gmail''s particularities =
== Archiving messages ==
In order to be consistent with Gmail''s behavior, Thunderbird archives your message in the ''''All Mail'''' folder, and not in an ''''Archive'''' folder as it does for other accounts (as described in the [[Archived Messages]] article).
;[[Image:Gmail Archive settings]]
{note}The {button Archive options…} button is disabled for Gmail accounts, as it is not necessary to define the archive granularity with Gmail.{/note}

== Subscribing to folders and synchronizing messages ==
=== Understanding Gmail labels and Thunderbird folders ===
Gmail uses a special implementation of IMAP. In this implementation, Gmail labels become Thunderbird folders. When you apply a label to a message in Gmail, Thunderbird creates a folder with the same name as the label and stores the message in that folder. Similarly, if you move a message to a folder in Thunderbird, Gmail will create a label with the folder name and assign it to the message.

If you copy a message to another folder, Gmail will still store only one message but apply both folder names as labels to it. Moving any copy of a message into the Trash will remove all labels from it, and it will no longer appear in any other folder.

In the example shown below, the folders at the same level as the ''''[Gmail]'''' folder (''''Personal'''', ''''Receipts'''', ''''Travel'''' and ''''Work'''') correspond to Gmail labels.
;[[Image:default gmail folders]]
There is also a set of special sub-folders for the ''''[Gmail]'''' folder:
* All Mail: contains all the messages of your Gmail account, including sent and archived messages. Any messages that you see in the inbox will also appear in the ''''[Gmail]/All Mail'''' folder. (It is recommended to unsubscribe this folder.)
* Drafts: contains draft messages.
* Sent Mail: contains sent messages.
* Spam: contains messages that were marked as spam (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Starred: contains messages that were starred (either by Gmail or Thunderbird).
* Trash: contains deleted messages.
* Important: contains messages that Gmail has flagged as “important”. (See [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/186531 Priority Inbox overview] for more details about this Gmail feature.)

=== Subscribing to or unsubscribing from folders (labels) ===
You can configure which folder (and its messages) you want to display and download in Thunderbird by changing folder subscriptions. By default, all folders are displayed and downloaded, as described above. To change folder subscription, right-click the account name and select {menu Subscribe…}
;[[Image:subscribe gmail folders]]
Uncheck any folders that you don''t want to display in Thunderbird. 

{note}For performance and space reasons it is recommended to unsubscribe the ''''All Mail'''' folder.
{/note}


=== Synchronizing folders (labels) ===
If you want to see all your Gmail labels and messages in Thunderbird but prefer to avoid all the messages being downloaded to your system, you may want to tweak the synchronization settings. This allows you to define which folder should be synchronized. If you don''t synchronize a folder, the message header will still be displayed in Thunderbird, but the message content will only be downloaded when you click the message header.

To change how messages are downloaded, set synchronization options for the account and for individual folders. ({for win,mac}{menu Tools} > {menu Account Settings…}{/for}{for linux}{menu Edit} > {menu Account Settings…}{/for}) See [[IMAP Synchronization#w_synchronization]] for more information.

=== Thunderbird''s message lists differ from the web interface ===
If some messages are appearing in your Inbox that shouldn''t be, or aren''t appearing but should be, you may need to select the ''''Don''t override filters'''' option under {menu Inbox} > {menu Filtered mail} in Gmail''s settings.
The same can be achieved by turning off {menu General} > {menu Smart features and personalisation} in Gmail''s settings.

You can also change whether Gmail''s interface should group messages by conversation with the ''''''Conversation View'''''' setting in Gmail''s {menu General} settings page, to match your [[Message threading in Thunderbird - keep messages grouped by conversation|Thunderbird preference]].

See the support article on [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6562 how to change Gmail settings].

= Updating a Gmail account to use OAuth =
Thunderbird will normally set up or update your Gmail account to OAuth automatically. If something goes wrong, you can use the hints below to check and set up manually. For more details and how to resolve various OAuth issues, see [[Automatic Conversion of Google mail accounts to OAuth 2.0 Authentication]].

The following applies to both POP and IMAP mail accounts.
*Enable cookies in Thunderbird:
*#Click {menu ≡} > {menu Settings}.
*#Select {menu Privacy & Security}.
*#Under ''''Web Content'''' section, tick ''''''Accept cookies from sites''''''.
*You may need to set an exception:
*#Click {button Exceptions…} button.
*#Add an exception for ''''''https://accounts.google<!-- -->.com''''''
*#Click {button Allow}.
*#Click {button Save Changes}.
*Change the authentication method in ''''Account Settings'''':
*#Click {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings}, which opens in a new tab.
**Change the Outgoing SMTP authentication method:
**#In the left pane, select the POP/IMAP account name and look bottom right for ''''Outgoing Server (SMTP)''''.
**#Click {button Edit Server SMTP…} button.
**#Set ''''Authentication Method'''' to ''''''OAuth2''''''.
**#Click {button OK}.
**Change the Incoming authentication method:
**#In the left pane, select {menu Server Settings} for Gmail account.
**#Set ''''Authentication Method'''' to ''''''OAuth2''''''.

Exit Thunderbird and wait a few moments for background processes to finish. Then restart Thunderbird.

Gmail will prompt you to enter your Gmail email address and password, which is the same password you use to access Gmail webmail account. Follow the instructions to allow Thunderbird to access server.

An OAuth token will get saved in Thunderbird, in the same location as passwords.

= Useful Add-ons =
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/google-4-tbsync/ Google contact and contact group sync].
* If you want to sync your calendar between Thunderbird Calendar and Google Calendar, you should read [[Using Lightning with Google Calendar]].
* [https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/gtrash/ GTrash] to make true deletion more convenient when using Google''s recommended deletion settings.

= See also =
* [https://support.google.com/mail/answer/78892 Gmail''s support article on recommended settings].
* [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=402793 The ticket which tracks issues using Gmail with Thunderbird (Bug 402793)].','<p>Thunderbird can be configured to work seamlessly with Google''s <a href="https://www.google.com/gmail/about/">Gmail</a> service. Messages are synchronized between your local version of Thunderbird and the web-based Gmail.
</p>
<div class="warning">As of May 30, 2022, <strong>Google no longer allows <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/lesssecureapps">Less secure apps</a> access</strong> for Gmail accounts, thus <strong>OAuth is required</strong>. If you are using Google supplied “app passwords“, Thunderbird version 91.8.0 and newer will <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-conversion-google-mail-accounts-oauth20">make that conversion</a> for you. Further, <strong>Gmail accounts do not work with Thunderbird 31 and older</strong> because they do not support Google''s OAuth (implemented in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=849540">bug 849540</a>).</div>
