</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Setup</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account settings dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-23-12-9340ff.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange Web Services</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange Web Services</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><p></p></div>



</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</h1>
<p>Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">send and receive emails</a> just like an IMAP or POP account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_limitations">Limitations</h1>
<p>Not yet supported:
</p>
<ul><li> Address book
</li><li> Calendar
</li><li> Filters (Phase 5)
</li><li> Folder quotas (Phase 5)
</li><li> Folder subscription (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message retention policies (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message download sync for offline use (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message tagging (Phase 5)
</li><li> NTLM authentication (Phase 5)
</li><li> OAuth authentication with on-premise instances (Phase 5)
</li><li> Shared mailboxes (Phase 5)
</li><li> Star message (Phase 5)
</li></ul>
<p>"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&amp;hide_resolved=1">this bug list</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_feedback-and-bug-reports">Feedback and Bug Reports</h1>
<p>To report a problem when using version 145 or newer, please <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&amp;component=Networking:%20Exchange&amp;short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&amp;blocked=1847846&amp;bug_type=defect">file a bug report</a>.
</p><p>To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p><p>You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta">beta group</a> or the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">enterprise group</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</h1>
<p>Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "314113", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 19, 2025, 11:29:05 AM", "datetime": "2025-11-19T11:29:05.517685-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Teo", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/teo951/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Collapsible blocks should be start with \"details_start\" and end with \"details_end\" corrected. Added image for radio button.n", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 19, 2025, 2:32:38 PM", "datetime": "2025-11-19T14:32:38.054091-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Nov 19, 2025, 2:32:38 PM", "datetime": "2025-11-19T14:32:38.054091-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:15:49+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (314170,'en-US','new-thunderbird-android','New in Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android/revision/314170',312053,'2025-11-19T20:46:03.222180-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','SMTP issues for enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/30',1,'2025-11-19T20:48:03.599279-08:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','[[T:TBAndroidMore]]
This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in Thunderbird for Android. For full details, please refer to [[Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues]].

__TOC__
=Thunderbird 14=
* SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed.
=Older Releases=
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 13==
* The notification in status bar re-appearing multiple times a day issue (github issue [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/8049 #8049]) has been fixed.
* Special folders (Trash, Sent, Drafts, Archives, etc) not appearing after updating from TB11 to TB12 (github issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9771 #9771]) was fixed.
* The bug with outgoing email not working for certain educational and enterprise accounts is scheduled to be fixed in the next release, Thunderbird 14 for Android (github issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9160 #9160]).
==Thunderbird 12==
<!--
for discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/22
see: https://matrix.to/#/!xPTYfLywxFMryjbnJl:mozilla.org/$okoHEWLX71BqfNVgYgNggAYMLJgg1-xGW4gq9BUs4Dg?via=mozilla.org&via=matrix.org&via=chat.rhaalovely.net
* You can now add your avatar in Account Settings <-- commented out until I can figure out 
how to make this work
-->
* If you use a password manager, Android will now use it to automatically fill in your password when you add a new account.
==Thunderbird 11==
* Mail folders are easier to navigate.
* Algorithmic darkening makes reading emails easier in dark mode.
* Thunderbird 11.1 : Selected/read/unread message states are now easier to distinguish visually.
* You can still see font settings if you search for ''''''font'''''' in Settings. This will be removed in a future Thunderbird release. Please use the Android operating system font size settings instead.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 10==
* You can now empty your Spam folder by tapping {menu Empty Spam} in the Spam folder''s menu {menu ⋮} (also known as the ''''''kebab'''''' menu because the 3 vertical dots resemble a kebab).
* On Android 10 or older, you now have an option to use the system theme.
*# Tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]} at the bottom left.
*# Tap {button General settings} > tap {menu Display} > tap {button Use system default} under ''''''Theme''''''
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 9==
* Initial Android 15 support.
* Thunderbird attempts to use your email provider''s auto configuration database. If it fails it, it uses Mozilla''s database.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 8==
* Refer to [[New in Thunderbird for Android version 8]] please.
[[UI:details_end]]','<p></p><div class="warning">More for Thunderbird for Android: <strong>o</strong> Vote on &amp; suggest improvements at <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/t5/ideas/idb-p/ideas/label-name/thunderbird%20android">Connect</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/participate/">Participate/Contribute</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird-android/form">Get Support</a></div>
This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in Thunderbird for Android. For full details, please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-release-notes-whats-new-and-known-issu">Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues</a>.
<p></p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-14"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 14</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_older-releases"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Older Releases</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-13"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 13</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-12"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 12</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-11"><span class="tocnumber">2.3</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 11</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-10"><span class="tocnumber">2.4</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 10</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-9"><span class="tocnumber">2.5</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 9</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-8"><span class="tocnumber">2.6</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 8</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-14">Thunderbird 14</h1>
<ul><li> SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> have been fixed.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_older-releases">Older Releases</h1>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-13" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 13</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><li> The notification in status bar re-appearing multiple times a day issue (github issue <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/8049">#8049</a>) has been fixed.
</li><li> Special folders (Trash, Sent, Drafts, Archives, etc) not appearing after updating from TB11 to TB12 (github issue: <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9771">#9771</a>) was fixed.
</li><li> The bug with outgoing email not working for certain educational and enterprise accounts is scheduled to be fixed in the next release, Thunderbird 14 for Android (github issue: <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9160">#9160</a>).
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-12" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 12</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>
</p><ul><li> If you use a password manager, Android will now use it to automatically fill in your password when you add a new account.
</li></ul></div>


<h2 id="w_thunderbird-11" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 11</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Mail folders are easier to navigate.
</li><li> Algorithmic darkening makes reading emails easier in dark mode.
</li><li> Thunderbird 11.1&nbsp;: Selected/read/unread message states are now easier to distinguish visually.
</li><li> You can still see font settings if you search for <strong>font</strong> in Settings. This will be removed in a future Thunderbird release. Please use the Android operating system font size settings instead.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-10" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 10</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> You can now empty your Spam folder by tapping <span class="menu">Empty Spam</span> in the Spam folder''s menu <span class="menu">⋮</span> (also known as the <strong>kebab</strong> menu because the 3 vertical dots resemble a kebab).
</li><li> On Android 10 or older, you now have an option to use the system theme.
<ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the gear icon:<span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> at the bottom left.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">General settings</span> &gt; tap <span class="menu">Display</span> &gt; tap <span class="button">Use system default</span> under <strong>Theme</strong>
</li></ol>
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-9" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 9</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Initial Android 15 support.
</li><li> Thunderbird attempts to use your email provider''s auto configuration database. If it fails it, it uses Mozilla''s database.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-8" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-5" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 8</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-5" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android-version-8">New in Thunderbird for Android version 8</a> please.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<p></p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "314170", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 19, 2025, 8:46:03 PM", "datetime": "2025-11-19T20:46:03.222180-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "SMTP issues for enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/android-knowledgebase-issues/issues/30", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 19, 2025, 8:48:03 PM", "datetime": "2025-11-19T20:48:03.599279-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:24:21+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (314186,'en-US','thunderbird-and-exchange','Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange/revision/314186',314113,'2025-11-20T03:06:22.978185-08:00','Mark Heijl','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/','minor edits, bringing back ‘the’ in two sentences.',1,'2025-11-20T09:36:22.917507-08:00','wsmwk',1,0,1,'2025-11-20T09:36:22.917507-08:00','wsmwk','approved','{warning}
For the best Exchange experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
Thunderbird implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services), ''''''currently limited to email''''''.

Calendar and address book using EWS will follow at a later date. Also in the future, is Exchange via ''''''Microsoft Graph'''''', so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.

Exchange is new, so we very much welcome your [[#w_feedback-and-bug-reports|feedback and bug reports]]. For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].{/warning}

__TOC__

=Setup=
When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the ''''''Exchange Web Services'''''' option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, ''''''Exchange'''''' should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first ''''''Exchange'''''' option.

==Configure your Exchange account==
Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird ''''''Account Hub''''''.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Hub'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account type selection dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-exchange-support-button|width=450]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}

====Manual EWS Configuration====
There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
# In [[New in Thunderbird Desktop|145]] and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking {menu MANUAL CONFIGURATION}. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
# Under ''''''Protocol'''''', select {menu EWS}.
# Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
[[UI:details_end]]

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profile with no accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with no accounts, you will see the older ''''Add account'''' wizard.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Setup'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account settings dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange Web Services'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange Web Services} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}
[[UI:details_end]]

=Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account=
Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to [[Send and receive messages in Thunderbird|send and receive emails]] just like an IMAP or POP account.

=Limitations=
Not yet supported:
* Address book
* Calendar
* Filters (Phase 5)
* Folder quotas (Phase 5)
* Folder subscription (Phase 5)
* Message retention policies (Phase 5)
* Message download sync for offline use (Phase 5)
* Message tagging (Phase 5)
* NTLM authentication (Phase 5)
* OAuth authentication with on-premise instances (Phase 5)
* Shared mailboxes (Phase 5)
* Star message (Phase 5)
"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&hide_resolved=1 this bug list].

=Feedback and Bug Reports=
To report a problem when using version 145 or newer, please [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&component=Networking:%20Exchange&short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&blocked=1847846&bug_type=defect file a bug report].

To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].

You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta beta group] or the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise enterprise group].

=Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers=
Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].','<div class="warning">
<p>For the best Exchange experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
Thunderbird implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services), <strong>currently limited to email</strong>.
</p><p>Calendar and address book using EWS will follow at a later date. Also in the future, is Exchange via <strong>Microsoft Graph</strong>, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.
</p>
Exchange is new, so we very much welcome your <a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports">feedback and bug reports</a>. For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_setup"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Setup</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configure-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure your Exchange account</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-4"><a href="#w_manual-ews-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Manual EWS Configuration</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_limitations"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Limitations</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Feedback and Bug Reports</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_setup">Setup</h1>
<p>When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the <strong>Exchange Web Services</strong> option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, <strong>Exchange</strong> should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first <strong>Exchange</strong> option.
</p>
<h2 id="w_configure-your-exchange-account">Configure your Exchange account</h2>
<p>Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
</p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird <strong>Account Hub</strong>.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Hub</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account type selection dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-exchange-support-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-03-20-2802fd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><h4 id="w_manual-ews-configuration">Manual EWS Configuration</h4><p>There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
</p><ol><li> In <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop">145</a> and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking <span class="menu">MANUAL CONFIGURATION</span>. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
</li><li> Under <strong>Protocol</strong>, select <span class="menu">EWS</span>.
</li><li> Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
</li></ol><p></p></div>






</section>
<p></p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with no accounts, you will see the older <em>Add account</em> wizard.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Setup</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account settings dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-23-12-9340ff.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange Web Services</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange Web Services</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><p></p></div>



</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</h1>
<p>Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">send and receive emails</a> just like an IMAP or POP account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_limitations">Limitations</h1>
<p>Not yet supported:
</p>
<ul><li> Address book
</li><li> Calendar
</li><li> Filters (Phase 5)
</li><li> Folder quotas (Phase 5)
</li><li> Folder subscription (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message retention policies (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message download sync for offline use (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message tagging (Phase 5)
</li><li> NTLM authentication (Phase 5)
</li><li> OAuth authentication with on-premise instances (Phase 5)
</li><li> Shared mailboxes (Phase 5)
</li><li> Star message (Phase 5)
</li></ul>
<p>"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&amp;hide_resolved=1">this bug list</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_feedback-and-bug-reports">Feedback and Bug Reports</h1>
<p>To report a problem when using version 145 or newer, please <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&amp;component=Networking:%20Exchange&amp;short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&amp;blocked=1847846&amp;bug_type=defect">file a bug report</a>.
</p><p>To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p><p>You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta">beta group</a> or the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">enterprise group</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</h1>
<p>Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "314186", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 20, 2025, 3:06:22 AM", "datetime": "2025-11-20T03:06:22.978185-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Mark Heijl", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "minor edits, bringing back ‘the’ in two sentences.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Nov 20, 2025, 9:36:22 AM", "datetime": "2025-11-20T09:36:22.917507-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Nov 20, 2025, 9:36:22 AM", "datetime": "2025-11-20T09:36:22.917507-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:15:46+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (314611,'en-US','installing-thunderbird-linux','Install Thunderbird on Linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-linux/revision/314611',304145,'2025-11-26T05:41:57.920813-08:00','g.tasop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/g.tasop/','I think there was a typo on the command that downloads and saves the .desktop file. It was placed in "$HOME/.local/bin/thunderbird" instead of "$HOME/.local/share/applications/" which is the usual place for desktop files.',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','This article explains how to download and install Thunderbird on Linux. {for mac,win}This article only applies to Linux. {for mac}For instructions to install Thunderbird on Mac, see [[Install Thunderbird on Mac]]. {/for}{for win}For instructions to install Thunderbird on Windows, see [[Install Thunderbird on Windows]].{/for}{/for}
{note}Note: if you have Thunderbird installed via multiple methods (''''deb'''', ''''rpm'''', ''''flatpak'''', ''''snap'''', etc. ) you may have multiple application icons that are identical. To avoid this, we recommend installing using only one installation method at a time.{/note}

__TOC__

= Install from Flatpak =
The [https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.Thunderbird Thunderbird flatpak] package and tarballs are maintained directly by the Thunderbird staff. Therefore, these tend to be more quickly updated to newer stable versions than other sources.

To install Thunderbird from Flatpak, you will need to install flatpak and setup flathub, if not already on your system. Flathub is the central repository for flatpaks and contains well maintained [https://flathub.org/setup instructions on setting up flatpak installs for various distributions]. 

Once flatpak and flathub are all set up, you can either download and install it all from the command line, or separately download the {filepath flatpakref} file and head to the command line to install it.

== How to install with the command line (easier and much cooler) ==
<code>
flatpak install flathub org.mozilla.Thunderbird
</code>

== How to install with the Graphical User Interface ==
# Visit [https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.Thunderbird Thunderbird on Flathub].
# Click {button Install} and follow the instructions. You can find more options in the dropdown menu next to that button, if necessary.

= Install from Snap =
The [https://snapcraft.io/thunderbird Thunderbird snap] package is maintained by Canonical and Thunderbird. 

To install Thunderbird from Snap:
# [https://snapcraft.io/docs/installing-snapd Install Snap] on your computer.
# Visit [https://snapcraft.io/thunderbird Snapcraft''s Store page].
# Click {button Install} and follow the instructions.

= Installing from your distribution package manager =
Linux distributions normally create packages for installing Mozilla Thunderbird easily, using tools that are included with the Linux distribution. The package may contain modifications for better integration with the operating system. Maintainers of the Linux distribution control when new updates of their Thunderbird software are made available, so there may be a delay between when your distribution provides a newest available version or update compared to when an official new version or update is available at [https://www.thunderbird.net Thunderbird.net] or in the flatpak. Your distribution may also provide Thunderbird without the Thunderbird branding.

To install Thunderbird using your distribution package manager, please refer to your Linux distribution''s documentation. This method has the benefit of ensuring that Thunderbird and all the required libraries are installed and configured optimally for your distribution. 

= Installing Thunderbird manually for a specific release channel (For advanced users) =
{note}''''''NOTE'''''': This instruction set is for users that wish to install for troubleshooting purposes or to use a specific [[#w_thunderbird-channels|release channel]], for example beta or daily. Unless you know what you are doing and are familiar with using a command line, please install Thunderbird using one of the methods previously described.{/note}

== Thunderbird channels ==
Thunderbird offers four channels: ''''''Release'''''' (also known as Monthly, available March 2025), ''''''ESR'''''' (annual major releases), ''''''Beta'''''', and ''''''Daily''''''. These are compared at [[How to select a Thunderbird release channel]], with further information and tips in the articles about [[Thunderbird Beta]], [[Thunderbird Daily]]. Please make sure your computer has the required libraries installed. Missing libraries may cause Thunderbird to be inoperable or unreliable. The requirement list can vary by channel: [https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/128.8.0esr/system-requirements/ ESR requirements], [https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/136.0/system-requirements/ Release, Beta, Daily requirements].

==Changing channels==
It is possible to change channels and keep using your existing Thunderbird data, for example to go from ESR to Release. But if you want to use a different channel and not change your existing Thunderbird data to that channel, see [[#w_running-multiple-channels|Running multiple channels]]. When changing channels you will want avoid complications and protect your profile data:
* Backup your [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile data]].
* Only move to a channel which offers a higher or equal version number. Therefore, the possible progressions are to go from ESR to Release, Release to Beta, and Beta to Daily. Once your profile data has been loaded with a newer version, you will not be able to load it with a lower version.
* You must install the newer version in the ''''''exact same'''''' installation directory name as the older version. If using the ''''''system'''''' method below, do <code>rm -rf /opt/thunderbird</code> and then install the chosen download into {filepath /opt/thunderbird/}. If using the ''''''home'''''' method below, do <code>rm -rf $HOME/thunderbird</code> and then install the chosen download into {filepath $HOME/thunderbird/}.

==Running multiple channels==
It is possible to have multiple channels of Thunderbird installed, and even run them simultaneously. Each must be installed in a ''''''different'''''' installation directory, which will force [[Dedicated profile per Thunderbird installation|multiple Thunderbird data profiles to be used]] in order to protect and keep your data separated.

== Installation location ==
When installing Thunderbird manually, you can choose to install it either in the ''''''system'''''' or ''''''home'''''' folder. Below you will find instructions for both.

=== System Thunderbird installation ===
{note}
* To install Thunderbird with this method, you ''''''must be'''''' able to login as root or execute <code>sudo</code> commands.
* This installation will have priority over the Thunderbird version installed through your package manager. To run the version installed with your package manager, you will need to execute the binary from a terminal. To do so in most distributions, open a terminal and type: <code>/usr/bin/thunderbird</code>.
{/note}
#Download your choice of installation channel from the [https://thunderbird.net/download/ download page]. The file will be in the form of {filepath thunderbird-<version>.tar.bz2}.
#Open a terminal and go to the folder where your download has been saved. For example:<br><code>cd ~/Downloads</code>
#Extract the downloaded file contents. This will create a folder named {filepath thunderbird}. After that, you can delete the archive:<br><code>tar xjf thunderbird-*.tar.bz2<br>rm thunderbird-*.tar.bz2</code>
#Move the uncompressed Thunderbird folder to {filepath /opt}:<br><code>sudo mv thunderbird /opt</code>
#Create a symlink to the Thunderbird executable:<br><code>sudo ln -s /opt/thunderbird/thunderbird /usr/local/bin/thunderbird</code>
#Download a copy of the desktop file:<br><code>wget https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mozilla/sumo-kb/main/installing-thunderbird-linux/thunderbird.desktop -P /usr/local/share/applications</code>
>
Alternatively, if <code>wget</code> is not installed on your computer, go to the URL mentioned above, right-click on the page to open the contextual menu and select {menu Save Page As}. After you downloaded the file, move it to {filepath /usr/local/share/applications}.

To confirm everything is installed correctly, open Thunderbird from the newly created desktop file, and go to {menu ≡} > {menu Help} > {menu Troubleshooting Information}. In the ''''Application Basics'''' section of the ''''Troubleshooting Information'''' page, the value of ''''Application Binary'''' should be <code>/opt/thunderbird/thunderbird-bin</code>

=== Home Thunderbird installation - local in a user''s account ===
If you don''t have access to login as root or execute <code>sudo</code> commands, or just prefer to use a local Thunderbird in your account, you can make a local installation. You can also do this to have multiple Thunderbird installations for different builds.
#Download your choice of installation channel from the [https://thunderbird.net/download/ download page]. The file will be in the form of {filepath thunderbird-<version>.tar.bz2}.
#Open a terminal and go to the folder where your download has been saved. For example:<br><code>cd ~/Downloads</code>
#Extract the downloaded file contents. This will create a folder named {filepath thunderbird}. After that, you can delete the archive:<br><code>tar xjf thunderbird-*.tar.bz2<br>rm thunderbird-*.tar.bz2</code>
#If you have downloaded a specific build, you may want to rename the uncompressed folder accordingly. For example:<br><code>mv thunderbird thunderbird-beta</code>
#You can leave the uncompressed folder in your {filepath download} folder, or move it to another place in your account. For example:<br><code>mv thunderbird $HOME/thunderbird</code>
#Thunderbird is now ready to be used. You can run it directly from the terminal:<br><code>$HOME/thunderbird/thunderbird &</code>
#Create a desktop shortcut. Note this step may be different in your Linux distribution. (Some Linux distros will allow you to right-click on the desktop and choose {menu Create launcher} from the context menu):<br><code>wget https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mozilla/sumo-kb/main/installing-thunderbird-linux/thunderbird.desktop -P $HOME/.local/share/applications/</code>
#Since the icon for the desktop shortcut will be found in {filepath $HOME/thunderbird/chrome/icons/default/} and the executable is in {filepath $HOME/thunderbird}, we need to change these lines in our desktop file that we just downloaded:<br><code>sed -i.bak -e "s|Exec=thunderbird %u|Exec=$HOME/thunderbird/thunderbird %u|" $HOME/.local/share/applications/thunderbird.desktop<br><br>sed -i.bak -e "s|Icon=/opt/thunderbird/chrome/icons/default/default128.png|Icon=$HOME/thunderbird/chrome/icons/default/default128.png|" $HOME/.local/share/applications/thunderbird.desktop</code>

Now, your Thunderbird application icon should be found among your other applications and you can launch Thunderbird as you expect.

== Set Thunderbird as the default mail application ==
This step adds the required [https://specifications.freedesktop.org/mime-apps-spec/latest-single/#associations MIME-type associations] that enable you to select Thunderbird as the default mail application in your desktop settings.

# Open {filepath ~/.config/mimeapps.list} with your text editor and scroll down to the section named <code>[Added Associations]</code>.
# Find the MIME type <code>x-scheme-handler/mailto</code> and append <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> to it.

The {filepath .desktop} file you downloaded earlier defines some extra MIME types for Thunderbird, such as <code>application/x-xpinstall</code> (for handling Thunderbird extensions); so the example below will include it. There are a number of other types commonly defined by packagers ([https://hg-edge.mozilla.org/releases/comm-release/file/tip/taskcluster/docker/tb-flatpak/org.mozilla.Thunderbird.appdata.xml.in ¹] [https://git.launchpad.net/~desktop-snappers/thunderbird/+git/snap/tree/snap/gui/thunderbird.desktop?h=stable-core24 ²] [https://gitlab.archlinux.org/archlinux/packaging/packages/thunderbird/-/blob/141.0-1/org.mozilla.Thunderbird.desktop?ref_type=tags ³]); by adding <code>text/calendar</code>, for example, the GNOME desktop will list Thunderbird as an option for ''''Calendar''''.

<code>
[Added Associations]
<br>
x-scheme-handler/mailto=thunderbird.desktop;
<br>
application/x-xpinstall=thunderbird.desktop;
</code>

So far we''ve only defined what Thunderbird ''''can'''' handle, which is enough to get it listed as an option in your desktop settings. When you select a certain app as the default handler for a task, you''re actually writing to the other section named <code>[Default Applications]</code>. This can be done with a GUI provided by your desktop; by manually editing {filepath ~/.config/mimeapps.list}, as we''ve been doing; or on the command-line. The CLI usage is described below.

<code>
xdg-mime default thunderbird.desktop x-scheme-handler/mailto
<br>
xdg-mime default thunderbird.desktop application/x-xpinstall
</code>

If you skip the type associations we''ve manually added to the section <code>[Added Associations]</code>, the default app selection will still work, but the Thunderbird option will be gone as soon as you decide to change the default handler to something else. By following these steps, Thunderbird will always be listed as a default app candidate.

== Thunderbird icon is displayed as a generic icon ==
If you are using GNOME and the Thunderbird icon is displayed as a generic icon (e.g., a gear icon), additional configuration of the <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> launcher is required. The following ensures the correct application icon is displayed for Thunderbird.

Identify the <code>wmclass</code> for Thunderbird:
# Press <code>Alt+F2</code> while Thunderbird is running to launch the run command console.
# Type <code>lg</code> and press Enter.
# In the window that appears, click on <code>Windows</code> in the upper-right corner.
# Locate Thunderbird''s <code>wmclass</code> in the displayed list.
Modify the <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> launcher:
# Open the <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> file in a text editor.
# In the <code>[Desktop Entry]</code> section, add or update the <code>StartupWMClass</code> key to include the <code>wmclass</code> value you found earlier. For example: <code>StartupWMClass=thunderbird-esr</code>

= Troubleshooting =

== Snap Profile Issues ==

If you ''''DO NOT'''' have an already existing Thunderbird profile in your home directory, then the snap will generate and use a profile located in the Thunderbird snap location: {filepath ~/snap/thunderbird/common/.thunderbird}.

If you ''''DO'''' have an already existing Thunderbird profile in your home directory, then the snap will use it.

If for some reason you feel like your normal profile (whichever profile location you''ve been using) is not being used, here are some steps you can take to try and recover:
# Remove the snap. When snapd removes a snap from your system, it automatically makes a backup of that snap and stores it in the {filepath /var/lib/snapd/snapshots/} directory. If your profile directory was inside the snap at {filepath ~/snap/thunderbird/common/.thunderbird}, then the backup can take a while (because profile directories can be quite large). Be patient while the backup is generated. (Note that this is not the only way to create a backup. You can manually intiate a backup with <code>snap save thunderbird</code>)
# Make sure there is no existing {filepath ~/.thunderbird} profile directory:<br><code>$ mv ~/.thunderbird ~/.thunderbird-backup</code>
# Re-install the Thunderbird snap:<br><code>$ sudo snap install thunderbird</code>
# Restore from your snapshot. This will apply the thunderbird profile inside that snapshot to your fresh snap install. Look in the {filepath /var/lib/snapd/snapshots/} directory to locate the most recent snapshot or the one you want to use. The leading number corresponds to the snapshot number. For example if there is a {filepath /var/lib/snapd/snapshots/14_thunderbird_128.11.0esr-1_735.zip}, the restore command would be:<br><code>$ sudo snap restore 14</code>

[https://snapcraft.io/docs/snapshots You can read more about snap snapshots here.]','<p>This article explains how to download and install Thunderbird on Linux. <span class="for" data-for="mac,win">This article only applies to Linux. <span class="for" data-for="mac">For instructions to install Thunderbird on Mac, see <a href="/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-on-mac">Install Thunderbird on Mac</a>. </span><span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">For instructions to install Thunderbird on Windows, see <a href="/en-US/kb/installing-thunderbird-windows">Install Thunderbird on Windows</a>.</span></span>
</p>
<div class="note">Note: if you have Thunderbird installed via multiple methods (<em>deb</em>, <em>rpm</em>, <em>flatpak</em>, <em>snap</em>, etc. ) you may have multiple application icons that are identical. To avoid this, we recommend installing using only one installation method at a time.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_install-from-flatpak"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Install from Flatpak</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_how-to-install-with-the-command-line-easier-and-much-cooler"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">How to install with the command line (easier and much cooler)</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_how-to-install-with-the-graphical-user-interface"><span class="tocnumber">1.2</span> <span class="toctext">How to install with the Graphical User Interface</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_install-from-snap"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Install from Snap</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_installing-from-your-distribution-package-manager"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Installing from your distribution package manager</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_installing-thunderbird-manually-for-a-specific-release-channel-for-advanced-users"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Installing Thunderbird manually for a specific release channel (For advanced users)</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-channels"><span class="tocnumber">4.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird channels</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_changing-channels"><span class="tocnumber">4.2</span> <span class="toctext">Changing channels</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_running-multiple-channels"><span class="tocnumber">4.3</span> <span class="toctext">Running multiple channels</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_installation-location"><span class="tocnumber">4.4</span> <span class="toctext">Installation location</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_system-thunderbird-installation"><span class="tocnumber">4.4.1</span> <span class="toctext">System Thunderbird installation</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_home-thunderbird-installation-local-in-a-users-account"><span class="tocnumber">4.4.2</span> <span class="toctext">Home Thunderbird installation - local in a user''s account</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_set-thunderbird-as-the-default-mail-application"><span class="tocnumber">4.5</span> <span class="toctext">Set Thunderbird as the default mail application</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-icon-is-displayed-as-a-generic-icon"><span class="tocnumber">4.6</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird icon is displayed as a generic icon</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_troubleshooting"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Troubleshooting</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_snap-profile-issues"><span class="tocnumber">5.1</span> <span class="toctext">Snap Profile Issues</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_install-from-flatpak">Install from Flatpak</h1>
<p>The <a href="https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.Thunderbird">Thunderbird flatpak</a> package and tarballs are maintained directly by the Thunderbird staff. Therefore, these tend to be more quickly updated to newer stable versions than other sources.
</p><p>To install Thunderbird from Flatpak, you will need to install flatpak and setup flathub, if not already on your system. Flathub is the central repository for flatpaks and contains well maintained <a href="https://flathub.org/setup">instructions on setting up flatpak installs for various distributions</a>. 
</p><p>Once flatpak and flathub are all set up, you can either download and install it all from the command line, or separately download the <span class="filepath">flatpakref</span> file and head to the command line to install it.
</p>
<h2 id="w_how-to-install-with-the-command-line-easier-and-much-cooler">How to install with the command line (easier and much cooler)</h2>
<p><code>
flatpak install flathub org.mozilla.Thunderbird
</code>
</p>
<h2 id="w_how-to-install-with-the-graphical-user-interface">How to install with the Graphical User Interface</h2>
<ol><li> Visit <a href="https://flathub.org/apps/org.mozilla.Thunderbird">Thunderbird on Flathub</a>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Install</span> and follow the instructions. You can find more options in the dropdown menu next to that button, if necessary.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_install-from-snap">Install from Snap</h1>
<p>The <a href="https://snapcraft.io/thunderbird">Thunderbird snap</a> package is maintained by Canonical and Thunderbird. 
</p><p>To install Thunderbird from Snap:
</p>
<ol><li> <a href="https://snapcraft.io/docs/installing-snapd">Install Snap</a> on your computer.
</li><li> Visit <a href="https://snapcraft.io/thunderbird">Snapcraft''s Store page</a>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Install</span> and follow the instructions.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_installing-from-your-distribution-package-manager">Installing from your distribution package manager</h1>
<p>Linux distributions normally create packages for installing Mozilla Thunderbird easily, using tools that are included with the Linux distribution. The package may contain modifications for better integration with the operating system. Maintainers of the Linux distribution control when new updates of their Thunderbird software are made available, so there may be a delay between when your distribution provides a newest available version or update compared to when an official new version or update is available at <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net">Thunderbird.net</a> or in the flatpak. Your distribution may also provide Thunderbird without the Thunderbird branding.
</p><p>To install Thunderbird using your distribution package manager, please refer to your Linux distribution''s documentation. This method has the benefit of ensuring that Thunderbird and all the required libraries are installed and configured optimally for your distribution. 
</p>
<h1 id="w_installing-thunderbird-manually-for-a-specific-release-channel-for-advanced-users">Installing Thunderbird manually for a specific release channel (For advanced users)</h1>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTE</strong>: This instruction set is for users that wish to install for troubleshooting purposes or to use a specific <a href="#w_thunderbird-channels">release channel</a>, for example beta or daily. Unless you know what you are doing and are familiar with using a command line, please install Thunderbird using one of the methods previously described.</div>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-channels">Thunderbird channels</h2>
<p>Thunderbird offers four channels: <strong>Release</strong> (also known as Monthly, available March 2025), <strong>ESR</strong> (annual major releases), <strong>Beta</strong>, and <strong>Daily</strong>. These are compared at <a href="/en-US/kb/choosing-thunderbird-release-channel">How to select a Thunderbird release channel</a>, with further information and tips in the articles about <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-beta">Thunderbird Beta</a>, <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-daily">Thunderbird Daily</a>. Please make sure your computer has the required libraries installed. Missing libraries may cause Thunderbird to be inoperable or unreliable. The requirement list can vary by channel: <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/128.8.0esr/system-requirements/">ESR requirements</a>, <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/136.0/system-requirements/">Release, Beta, Daily requirements</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_changing-channels">Changing channels</h2>
<p>It is possible to change channels and keep using your existing Thunderbird data, for example to go from ESR to Release. But if you want to use a different channel and not change your existing Thunderbird data to that channel, see <a href="#w_running-multiple-channels">Running multiple channels</a>. When changing channels you will want avoid complications and protect your profile data:
</p>
<ul><li> Backup your <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile data</a>.
</li><li> Only move to a channel which offers a higher or equal version number. Therefore, the possible progressions are to go from ESR to Release, Release to Beta, and Beta to Daily. Once your profile data has been loaded with a newer version, you will not be able to load it with a lower version.
</li><li> You must install the newer version in the <strong>exact same</strong> installation directory name as the older version. If using the <strong>system</strong> method below, do <code>rm -rf /opt/thunderbird</code> and then install the chosen download into <span class="filepath">/opt/thunderbird/</span>. If using the <strong>home</strong> method below, do <code>rm -rf $HOME/thunderbird</code> and then install the chosen download into <span class="filepath">$HOME/thunderbird/</span>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_running-multiple-channels">Running multiple channels</h2>
<p>It is possible to have multiple channels of Thunderbird installed, and even run them simultaneously. Each must be installed in a <strong>different</strong> installation directory, which will force <a href="/en-US/kb/dedicated-profile-thunderbird-installation">multiple Thunderbird data profiles to be used</a> in order to protect and keep your data separated.
</p>
<h2 id="w_installation-location">Installation location</h2>
<p>When installing Thunderbird manually, you can choose to install it either in the <strong>system</strong> or <strong>home</strong> folder. Below you will find instructions for both.
</p>
<h3 id="w_system-thunderbird-installation">System Thunderbird installation</h3>
<div class="note">
<ul><li> To install Thunderbird with this method, you <strong>must be</strong> able to login as root or execute <code>sudo</code> commands.
</li><li> This installation will have priority over the Thunderbird version installed through your package manager. To run the version installed with your package manager, you will need to execute the binary from a terminal. To do so in most distributions, open a terminal and type: <code>/usr/bin/thunderbird</code>.
</li></ul>
</div>
<ol><li>Download your choice of installation channel from the <a href="https://thunderbird.net/download/">download page</a>. The file will be in the form of <span class="filepath">thunderbird-&lt;version&gt;.tar.bz2</span>.
</li><li>Open a terminal and go to the folder where your download has been saved. For example:<br><code>cd ~/Downloads</code>
</li><li>Extract the downloaded file contents. This will create a folder named <span class="filepath">thunderbird</span>. After that, you can delete the archive:<br><code>tar xjf thunderbird-*.tar.bz2<br>rm thunderbird-*.tar.bz2</code>
</li><li>Move the uncompressed Thunderbird folder to <span class="filepath">/opt</span>:<br><code>sudo mv thunderbird /opt</code>
</li><li>Create a symlink to the Thunderbird executable:<br><code>sudo ln -s /opt/thunderbird/thunderbird /usr/local/bin/thunderbird</code>
</li><li>Download a copy of the desktop file:<br><code>wget https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mozilla/sumo-kb/main/installing-thunderbird-linux/thunderbird.desktop -P /usr/local/share/applications</code>
</li></ol>
<p>&gt;
Alternatively, if <code>wget</code> is not installed on your computer, go to the URL mentioned above, right-click on the page to open the contextual menu and select <span class="menu">Save Page As</span>. After you downloaded the file, move it to <span class="filepath">/usr/local/share/applications</span>.
</p><p>To confirm everything is installed correctly, open Thunderbird from the newly created desktop file, and go to <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Help</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Troubleshooting Information</span>. In the <em>Application Basics</em> section of the <em>Troubleshooting Information</em> page, the value of <em>Application Binary</em> should be <code>/opt/thunderbird/thunderbird-bin</code>
</p>
<h3 id="w_home-thunderbird-installation-local-in-a-users-account">Home Thunderbird installation - local in a user''s account</h3>
<p>If you don''t have access to login as root or execute <code>sudo</code> commands, or just prefer to use a local Thunderbird in your account, you can make a local installation. You can also do this to have multiple Thunderbird installations for different builds.
</p>
<ol><li>Download your choice of installation channel from the <a href="https://thunderbird.net/download/">download page</a>. The file will be in the form of <span class="filepath">thunderbird-&lt;version&gt;.tar.bz2</span>.
</li><li>Open a terminal and go to the folder where your download has been saved. For example:<br><code>cd ~/Downloads</code>
</li><li>Extract the downloaded file contents. This will create a folder named <span class="filepath">thunderbird</span>. After that, you can delete the archive:<br><code>tar xjf thunderbird-*.tar.bz2<br>rm thunderbird-*.tar.bz2</code>
</li><li>If you have downloaded a specific build, you may want to rename the uncompressed folder accordingly. For example:<br><code>mv thunderbird thunderbird-beta</code>
</li><li>You can leave the uncompressed folder in your <span class="filepath">download</span> folder, or move it to another place in your account. For example:<br><code>mv thunderbird $HOME/thunderbird</code>
</li><li>Thunderbird is now ready to be used. You can run it directly from the terminal:<br><code>$HOME/thunderbird/thunderbird &amp;</code>
</li><li>Create a desktop shortcut. Note this step may be different in your Linux distribution. (Some Linux distros will allow you to right-click on the desktop and choose <span class="menu">Create launcher</span> from the context menu):<br><code>wget https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mozilla/sumo-kb/main/installing-thunderbird-linux/thunderbird.desktop -P $HOME/.local/share/applications/</code>
</li><li>Since the icon for the desktop shortcut will be found in <span class="filepath">$HOME/thunderbird/chrome/icons/default/</span> and the executable is in <span class="filepath">$HOME/thunderbird</span>, we need to change these lines in our desktop file that we just downloaded:<br><code>sed -i.bak -e "s|Exec=thunderbird %u|Exec=$HOME/thunderbird/thunderbird %u|" $HOME/.local/share/applications/thunderbird.desktop<br><br>sed -i.bak -e "s|Icon=/opt/thunderbird/chrome/icons/default/default128.png|Icon=$HOME/thunderbird/chrome/icons/default/default128.png|" $HOME/.local/share/applications/thunderbird.desktop</code>
</li></ol>
<p>Now, your Thunderbird application icon should be found among your other applications and you can launch Thunderbird as you expect.
</p>
<h2 id="w_set-thunderbird-as-the-default-mail-application">Set Thunderbird as the default mail application</h2>
<p>This step adds the required <a href="https://specifications.freedesktop.org/mime-apps-spec/latest-single/#associations">MIME-type associations</a> that enable you to select Thunderbird as the default mail application in your desktop settings.
</p>
<ol><li> Open <span class="filepath">~/.config/mimeapps.list</span> with your text editor and scroll down to the section named <code>[Added Associations]</code>.
</li><li> Find the MIME type <code>x-scheme-handler/mailto</code> and append <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> to it.
</li></ol>
<p>The <span class="filepath">.desktop</span> file you downloaded earlier defines some extra MIME types for Thunderbird, such as <code>application/x-xpinstall</code> (for handling Thunderbird extensions); so the example below will include it. There are a number of other types commonly defined by packagers (<a href="https://hg-edge.mozilla.org/releases/comm-release/file/tip/taskcluster/docker/tb-flatpak/org.mozilla.Thunderbird.appdata.xml.in">¹</a> <a href="https://git.launchpad.net/~desktop-snappers/thunderbird/+git/snap/tree/snap/gui/thunderbird.desktop?h=stable-core24">²</a> <a href="https://gitlab.archlinux.org/archlinux/packaging/packages/thunderbird/-/blob/141.0-1/org.mozilla.Thunderbird.desktop?ref_type=tags">³</a>); by adding <code>text/calendar</code>, for example, the GNOME desktop will list Thunderbird as an option for <em>Calendar</em>.
</p><p><code>
[Added Associations]
<br>
x-scheme-handler/mailto=thunderbird.desktop;
<br>
application/x-xpinstall=thunderbird.desktop;
</code>
</p><p>So far we''ve only defined what Thunderbird <em>can</em> handle, which is enough to get it listed as an option in your desktop settings. When you select a certain app as the default handler for a task, you''re actually writing to the other section named <code>[Default Applications]</code>. This can be done with a GUI provided by your desktop; by manually editing <span class="filepath">~/.config/mimeapps.list</span>, as we''ve been doing; or on the command-line. The CLI usage is described below.
</p><p><code>
xdg-mime default thunderbird.desktop x-scheme-handler/mailto
<br>
xdg-mime default thunderbird.desktop application/x-xpinstall
</code>
</p><p>If you skip the type associations we''ve manually added to the section <code>[Added Associations]</code>, the default app selection will still work, but the Thunderbird option will be gone as soon as you decide to change the default handler to something else. By following these steps, Thunderbird will always be listed as a default app candidate.
</p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-icon-is-displayed-as-a-generic-icon">Thunderbird icon is displayed as a generic icon</h2>
<p>If you are using GNOME and the Thunderbird icon is displayed as a generic icon (e.g., a gear icon), additional configuration of the <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> launcher is required. The following ensures the correct application icon is displayed for Thunderbird.
</p><p>Identify the <code>wmclass</code> for Thunderbird:
</p>
<ol><li> Press <code>Alt+F2</code> while Thunderbird is running to launch the run command console.
</li><li> Type <code>lg</code> and press Enter.
</li><li> In the window that appears, click on <code>Windows</code> in the upper-right corner.
</li><li> Locate Thunderbird''s <code>wmclass</code> in the displayed list.
</li></ol>
<p>Modify the <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> launcher:
</p>
<ol><li> Open the <code>thunderbird.desktop</code> file in a text editor.
</li><li> In the <code>[Desktop Entry]</code> section, add or update the <code>StartupWMClass</code> key to include the <code>wmclass</code> value you found earlier. For example: <code>StartupWMClass=thunderbird-esr</code>
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h1>
<h2 id="w_snap-profile-issues">Snap Profile Issues</h2>
<p>If you <em>DO NOT</em> have an already existing Thunderbird profile in your home directory, then the snap will generate and use a profile located in the Thunderbird snap location: <span class="filepath">~/snap/thunderbird/common/.thunderbird</span>.
</p><p>If you <em>DO</em> have an already existing Thunderbird profile in your home directory, then the snap will use it.
</p><p>If for some reason you feel like your normal profile (whichever profile location you''ve been using) is not being used, here are some steps you can take to try and recover:
</p>
<ol><li> Remove the snap. When snapd removes a snap from your system, it automatically makes a backup of that snap and stores it in the <span class="filepath">/var/lib/snapd/snapshots/</span> directory. If your profile directory was inside the snap at <span class="filepath">~/snap/thunderbird/common/.thunderbird</span>, then the backup can take a while (because profile directories can be quite large). Be patient while the backup is generated. (Note that this is not the only way to create a backup. You can manually intiate a backup with <code>snap save thunderbird</code>)
</li><li> Make sure there is no existing <span class="filepath">~/.thunderbird</span> profile directory:<br><code>$ mv ~/.thunderbird ~/.thunderbird-backup</code>
</li><li> Re-install the Thunderbird snap:<br><code>$ sudo snap install thunderbird</code>
</li><li> Restore from your snapshot. This will apply the thunderbird profile inside that snapshot to your fresh snap install. Look in the <span class="filepath">/var/lib/snapd/snapshots/</span> directory to locate the most recent snapshot or the one you want to use. The leading number corresponds to the snapshot number. For example if there is a <span class="filepath">/var/lib/snapd/snapshots/14_thunderbird_128.11.0esr-1_735.zip</span>, the restore command would be:<br><code>$ sudo snap restore 14</code>
</li></ol>
<p><a href="https://snapcraft.io/docs/snapshots">You can read more about snap snapshots here.</a>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "314611", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 26, 2025, 5:41:57 AM", "datetime": "2025-11-26T05:41:57.920813-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "g.tasop", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/g.tasop/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "I think there was a typo on the command that downloads and saves the .desktop file. It was placed in \"$HOME/.local/bin/thunderbird\" instead of \"$HOME/.local/share/applications/\" which is the usual place for desktop files.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:09:41+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (314835,'en-US','thunderbird-and-exchange','Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange/revision/314835',314186,'2025-11-29T00:37:35.555724-08:00','Wayne Mery','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/','clarify that current availability is for desktop only',1,'2025-12-01T10:33:55.798052-08:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{warning}
For the best Exchange experience on desktops, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services), ''''''currently limited to email''''''.

Calendar and address book using EWS on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is a) Exchange via ''''''Microsoft Graph'''''' and b) Exchange support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.

Support for Exchange on desktop is new, so we very much welcome your [[#w_feedback-and-bug-reports|feedback and bug reports]]. For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].{/warning}

__TOC__

=Setup=
When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the ''''''Exchange Web Services'''''' option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, ''''''Exchange'''''' should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first ''''''Exchange'''''' option.

==Configure your Exchange account==
Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird ''''''Account Hub''''''.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Hub'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account type selection dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-exchange-support-button|width=450]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}

====Manual EWS Configuration====
There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
# In [[New in Thunderbird Desktop|145]] and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking {menu MANUAL CONFIGURATION}. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
# Under ''''''Protocol'''''', select {menu EWS}.
# Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
[[UI:details_end]]

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profile with no accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with no accounts, you will see the older ''''Add account'''' wizard.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Setup'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account settings dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange Web Services'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange Web Services} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}
[[UI:details_end]]

=Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account=
Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to [[Send and receive messages in Thunderbird|send and receive emails]] just like an IMAP or POP account.

=Limitations=
Not yet supported:
* Address book
* Calendar
* Filters (Phase 5)
* Folder quotas (Phase 5)
* Folder subscription (Phase 5)
* Message retention policies (Phase 5)
* Message download sync for offline use (Phase 5)
* Message tagging (Phase 5)
* NTLM authentication (Phase 5)
* OAuth authentication with on-premise instances (Phase 5)
* Shared mailboxes (Phase 5)
* Star message (Phase 5)
"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&hide_resolved=1 this bug list].

=Feedback and Bug Reports=
To report a problem when using version 145 or newer, please [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&component=Networking:%20Exchange&short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&blocked=1847846&bug_type=defect file a bug report].

To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].

You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta beta group] or the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise enterprise group].

=Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers=
Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].','<div class="warning">
<p>For the best Exchange experience on desktops, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services), <strong>currently limited to email</strong>.
</p><p>Calendar and address book using EWS on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is a) Exchange via <strong>Microsoft Graph</strong> and b) Exchange support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.
</p>
Support for Exchange on desktop is new, so we very much welcome your <a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports">feedback and bug reports</a>. For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_setup"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Setup</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configure-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure your Exchange account</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-4"><a href="#w_manual-ews-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Manual EWS Configuration</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_limitations"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Limitations</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Feedback and Bug Reports</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_setup">Setup</h1>
<p>When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the <strong>Exchange Web Services</strong> option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, <strong>Exchange</strong> should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first <strong>Exchange</strong> option.
</p>
<h2 id="w_configure-your-exchange-account">Configure your Exchange account</h2>
<p>Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
</p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird <strong>Account Hub</strong>.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Hub</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account type selection dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-exchange-support-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-03-20-2802fd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><h4 id="w_manual-ews-configuration">Manual EWS Configuration</h4><p>There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
</p><ol><li> In <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop">145</a> and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking <span class="menu">MANUAL CONFIGURATION</span>. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
</li><li> Under <strong>Protocol</strong>, select <span class="menu">EWS</span>.
</li><li> Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
</li></ol><p></p></div>






</section>
<p></p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with no accounts, you will see the older <em>Add account</em> wizard.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Setup</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account settings dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-23-12-9340ff.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange Web Services</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange Web Services</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><p></p></div>



</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</h1>
<p>Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">send and receive emails</a> just like an IMAP or POP account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_limitations">Limitations</h1>
<p>Not yet supported:
</p>
<ul><li> Address book
</li><li> Calendar
</li><li> Filters (Phase 5)
</li><li> Folder quotas (Phase 5)
</li><li> Folder subscription (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message retention policies (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message download sync for offline use (Phase 5)
</li><li> Message tagging (Phase 5)
</li><li> NTLM authentication (Phase 5)
</li><li> OAuth authentication with on-premise instances (Phase 5)
</li><li> Shared mailboxes (Phase 5)
</li><li> Star message (Phase 5)
</li></ul>
<p>"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&amp;hide_resolved=1">this bug list</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_feedback-and-bug-reports">Feedback and Bug Reports</h1>
<p>To report a problem when using version 145 or newer, please <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&amp;component=Networking:%20Exchange&amp;short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&amp;blocked=1847846&amp;bug_type=defect">file a bug report</a>.
</p><p>To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p><p>You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta">beta group</a> or the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">enterprise group</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</h1>
<p>Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "314835", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Nov 29, 2025, 12:37:35 AM", "datetime": "2025-11-29T00:37:35.555724-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Wayne Mery", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "clarify that current availability is for desktop only", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Dec 1, 2025, 10:33:55 AM", "datetime": "2025-12-01T10:33:55.798052-08:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:15:44+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (315926,'en-US','new-thunderbird-desktop','New in Thunderbird Desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop/revision/315926',313667,'2025-12-12T10:56:15.334516-08:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP do not approve or localize :-) please Discussion:https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/115#issuecomment-3647749575',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','[[T:TBMore]]

This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in [[Thunderbird Release|Thunderbird Release for Desktop]]. For full details, please refer to [[Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues]].

__TOC__
=Thunderbird 146=
* Drag and Drop for attachments on macOS is working again.
* You can now add your own OpenPGP Keyserver.

[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 145=
==DNS over HTTPS==
* Thunderbird now supports DNS over HTTPS.
==Account Hub supports manual configuration for EWS, IMAP and POP3 accounts==
* In the ''''''Account Hub'''''', once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking {menu MANUAL CONFIGURATION}. 
*;[[Image:tb145-account-hub-manual-configuration]]
* ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' in the ''''''Account Hub'''''' supports manual configuration of {menu EWS}, {menu IMAP} and {menu POP3} accounts.
*;[[Image:tb145-ews-imap-pop-account-hub-manual-configuration]]
* Manual configuration of EWS accounts is now supported as per the screenshots in the above two bullet points. For more details, please see the [[Thunderbird and Exchange#w_manual-ews-configuration|Manual EWS configuration section of our Thunderbird and Exchange article]].
=Thunderbird 144=
==Exchange enhancements and bug fixes==
* Another bug fix release.
* Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions. We are planning a promotional splash in one of the next few releases. Getting closer!
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 143=
==Exchange Support enhancements and bug fixes==
* Bug fixes.
* Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 142=
==Switch to the old Account Setup flow to fix issues with Hotmail and outlook.com accounts==
If you see: ''''''Exchange auth error'''''' or ''''''Authentication failed. Please check the username and password'''''' when adding a ''''''Hotmail'''''' or ''''''outlook.com'''''' account, here''s how to switch to the old ''''''Account Setup'''''' flow to fix the problem:
* Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings} > {menu General} and untick ''''''Create accounts in the new Account Hub'''''':
*;[[Image:tb142-disable-account-hub|width=350]]
* Please try adding the email account again. If you still have issues, please [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form file a support request on SUMO].
This will be fixed in a future Thunderbird release.
==Reset manual folder sorting order==
# Right click ({key Control}+click on macOS) on the account row, e.g. ''''''nemo@thunderbird.net'''''' in the ''''''Folder Pane'''''' (more details: [[Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)]] and then:
# Click the menu item {menu Reset Folder Order} to clear any previously defined manual sorting. ''''''NOTE'''''': This feature doesn’t restore the original location of a folder that was moved inside another parent folder which will be fixed in a future release. You can follow along in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979880 bug 1979880].
#;[[Image:tb142-reset-folder-order|width=350]]
==Add a visual signature to a PDF==
Just like Firefox, you can now insert your signature into a PDF by by typing, drawing or adding an image. Please refer to the Firefox article for full details: [[View and edit PDF files using Firefox’s built-in viewer#w_add-a-signature-to-a-pdf]].
==Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release==
See the 141 section for more details.
==More stable Exchange support==
This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
Please refer to [[Thunderbird and Exchange]] and our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 141=
==Archive action added to new email notifications ==
* You can now {button Archive} email from your operating system''s new email native notifications (in addition to {button Mark as Read}, {button Delete}, {button Mark as Starred} and {button Mark as Spam}. Please see [[New email notifications in Thunderbird]] for further information.
==Better Exchange support==
Now, anyone can try the Exchange support in Thunderbird. For most, we recommend waiting for a future release which will be more stable and have additional features like on premise OAuth authentication(password authentication for on premise is supported in this release).
This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
Please refer to our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
== 31 day warning for PGP Key expiry ==
If your PGP key expires in the next 31 days, you will receive a warning when you are composing an email. For extra security, you can choose to let it expire or you can extend your key''s certificate.

More info on PGP encrypted email can be found in [[Introduction to End-to-end encryption in Thunderbird]] and [[OpenPGP in Thunderbird - HOWTO and FAQ]].
== Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release ==
This is primarily a problem in the English language locales. It was discovered a few days after the release of 141. You can follow the progress of the fix in [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979511 bug 1979511].
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
=Thunderbird 140 / ESR 140 Eclipse=
==Dark Message Mode==
Thunderbird now automatically displays your messages in dark mode when enabled in your operating system settings. If there are display issues with a message in dark mode, you can disable dark mode for that message by sliding the toggle to the right to disable dark message mode.
;[[Image:tb140-dark-mode-toggle]]
==New Appearance Panel in Settings==
The new Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu Appearance}, allows you to toggle your message list layout between Card and Table view, adjust your Cards view, and set your default folder sorting order and threading options. For more details, please refer to [[Appearance Panel - Layout, Message List, Card View, Table View, Sorting and Threading settings]].
==Native OS Notifications==
Thunderbird now uses your operating system''s built-in notifications, whether you’re on Windows, Linux or Mac. You can now quickly delete, archive, or use customizable actions directly from your notifications.
==Redesigned Account hub==
The newly redesigned Account hub makes it easier to add an account. Connect all of your emails, address books and calendars in a few easy steps. The Account hub is currently not enabled for your first account (you will see it when you add a second account). It is also not enabled by default for your address book. You can change the defaults in your Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu General} > search for ''''''Account hub'''''' (not enabled in ESR, we recommend the latest monthly release for the best experience).
==Manual Folder Sorting==
Drag and drop your folders to get the order you want.
==Experimental Exchange Support for advanced users==
Preliminary Microsoft Exchange support is available for you to try. Our current, initial implementation of Exchange is limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
We recommend you try this in a test [[Use multiple profiles in Thunderbird|Thunderbird profile]] in [[Thunderbird Beta]] (please ensure you back up your profile before starting). Please refer to our [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] for full instructions.
==Export for Mobile==
Generate a QR code to quickly transfer your account settings and credentials to your Thunderbird for Android app. Please refer to [[Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android]].
==Horizontal Scroll for Table View==
Table view now supports horizontal scrolling. This allows you to view wide messages – for example, data in a table with many columns.
[[UI:details_end]]
=Older Releases=
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 139==
* You can now manually sort folders by dragging and dropping them in the folder pane.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 138==
* Dark mode and high contrast mode improvements.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 137==
* Fixed Two-factor authentication via text or email for Office 365 using Oauth2.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 136==
* New Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button Settings} > {menu Appearance}.
[[UI:details_end]]','<p></p><div class="warning">More for Thunderbird: <strong>o</strong> Vote on &amp; suggest improvements at <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/t5/ideas/idb-p/ideas/label-name/thunderbird">Connect</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/participate/">Participate/Contribute</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">Get Support</a></div>
<p></p><p>This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-release">Thunderbird Release for Desktop</a>. For full details, please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-release-notes-whats-new-and-known-issu">Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues</a>.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-146"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 146</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-145"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 145</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_dns-over-https"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">DNS over HTTPS</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_account-hub-supports-manual-configuration-for-ews-imap-and-pop3-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Account Hub supports manual configuration for EWS, IMAP and POP3 accounts</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-144"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 144</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_exchange-enhancements-and-bug-fixes"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Exchange enhancements and bug fixes</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-143"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 143</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_exchange-support-enhancements-and-bug-fixes"><span class="tocnumber">4.1</span> <span class="toctext">Exchange Support enhancements and bug fixes</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-142"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 142</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_switch-to-the-old-account-setup-flow-to-fix-issues-with-hotmail-and-outlook-com-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">5.1</span> <span class="toctext">Switch to the old Account Setup flow to fix issues with Hotmail and outlook.com accounts</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_reset-manual-folder-sorting-order"><span class="tocnumber">5.2</span> <span class="toctext">Reset manual folder sorting order</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_add-a-visual-signature-to-a-pdf"><span class="tocnumber">5.3</span> <span class="toctext">Add a visual signature to a PDF</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release"><span class="tocnumber">5.4</span> <span class="toctext">Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_more-stable-exchange-support"><span class="tocnumber">5.5</span> <span class="toctext">More stable Exchange support</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-141"><span class="tocnumber">6</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 141</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_archive-action-added-to-new-email-notifications"><span class="tocnumber">6.1</span> <span class="toctext">Archive action added to new email notifications</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_better-exchange-support"><span class="tocnumber">6.2</span> <span class="toctext">Better Exchange support</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_31-day-warning-for-pgp-key-expiry"><span class="tocnumber">6.3</span> <span class="toctext">31 day warning for PGP Key expiry</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release_2"><span class="tocnumber">6.4</span> <span class="toctext">Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-140-esr-140-eclipse"><span class="tocnumber">7</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 140 / ESR 140 Eclipse</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_dark-message-mode"><span class="tocnumber">7.1</span> <span class="toctext">Dark Message Mode</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_new-appearance-panel-in-settings"><span class="tocnumber">7.2</span> <span class="toctext">New Appearance Panel in Settings</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_native-os-notifications"><span class="tocnumber">7.3</span> <span class="toctext">Native OS Notifications</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_redesigned-account-hub"><span class="tocnumber">7.4</span> <span class="toctext">Redesigned Account hub</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_manual-folder-sorting"><span class="tocnumber">7.5</span> <span class="toctext">Manual Folder Sorting</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_experimental-exchange-support-for-advanced-users"><span class="tocnumber">7.6</span> <span class="toctext">Experimental Exchange Support for advanced users</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_export-for-mobile"><span class="tocnumber">7.7</span> <span class="toctext">Export for Mobile</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_horizontal-scroll-for-table-view"><span class="tocnumber">7.8</span> <span class="toctext">Horizontal Scroll for Table View</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_older-releases"><span class="tocnumber">8</span> <span class="toctext">Older Releases</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-139"><span class="tocnumber">8.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 139</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-138"><span class="tocnumber">8.2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 138</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-137"><span class="tocnumber">8.3</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 137</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-136"><span class="tocnumber">8.4</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 136</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-146">Thunderbird 146</h1>
<ul><li> Drag and Drop for attachments on macOS is working again.
</li><li> You can now add your own OpenPGP Keyserver.
</li></ul>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-145">Thunderbird 145</h1>
<h2 id="w_dns-over-https" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">DNS over HTTPS</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><li> Thunderbird now supports DNS over HTTPS.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_account-hub-supports-manual-configuration-for-ews-imap-and-pop3-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Account Hub supports manual configuration for EWS, IMAP and POP3 accounts</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> In the <strong>Account Hub</strong>, once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking <span class="menu">MANUAL CONFIGURATION</span>. 
<dl><dt><img alt="tb145-account-hub-manual-configuration" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-12-23-06-35-21b46c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> in the <strong>Account Hub</strong> supports manual configuration of <span class="menu">EWS</span>, <span class="menu">IMAP</span> and <span class="menu">POP3</span> accounts.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb145-ews-imap-pop-account-hub-manual-configuration" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-12-23-55-15-82277a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Manual configuration of EWS accounts is now supported as per the screenshots in the above two bullet points. For more details, please see the <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange#w_manual-ews-configuration">Manual EWS configuration section of our Thunderbird and Exchange article</a>.
</li></ul><h1 id="w_thunderbird-144">Thunderbird 144</h1></div>


<h2 id="w_exchange-enhancements-and-bug-fixes" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-expanded="false">Exchange enhancements and bug fixes</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Another bug fix release.
</li><li> Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions. We are planning a promotional splash in one of the next few releases. Getting closer!
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-143">Thunderbird 143</h1>
<h2 id="w_exchange-support-enhancements-and-bug-fixes" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-expanded="false">Exchange Support enhancements and bug fixes</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Bug fixes.
</li><li> Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-142">Thunderbird 142</h1>
<h2 id="w_switch-to-the-old-account-setup-flow-to-fix-issues-with-hotmail-and-outlook-com-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-expanded="false">Switch to the old Account Setup flow to fix issues with Hotmail and <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> accounts</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you see: <strong>Exchange auth error</strong> or <strong>Authentication failed. Please check the username and password</strong> when adding a <strong>Hotmail</strong> or <strong><a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a></strong> account, here''s how to switch to the old <strong>Account Setup</strong> flow to fix the problem:
</p><ul><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> and untick <strong>Create accounts in the new Account Hub</strong>:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb142-disable-account-hub" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-27-10-54-39-2636ed.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="350">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Please try adding the email account again. If you still have issues, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">file a support request on SUMO</a>.
</li></ul><p>This will be fixed in a future Thunderbird release.
</p></div>



<h2 id="w_reset-manual-folder-sorting-order" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-5" aria-expanded="false">Reset manual folder sorting order</button></h2>
<ol class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-5" aria-hidden="true"><li> Right click (<span class="key">Control</span>+click on macOS) on the account row, e.g. <strong><a href="mailto:nemo@thunderbird.net">nemo@thunderbird.net</a></strong> in the <strong>Folder Pane</strong> (more details: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova">Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)</a> and then:
</li><li> Click the menu item <span class="menu">Reset Folder Order</span> to clear any previously defined manual sorting. <strong>NOTE</strong>: This feature doesn’t restore the original location of a folder that was moved inside another parent folder which will be fixed in a future release. You can follow along in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979880">bug 1979880</a>.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb142-reset-folder-order" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-00-23-24-8d62b7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="350">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_add-a-visual-signature-to-a-pdf" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-6" aria-expanded="false">Add a visual signature to a PDF</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-6" aria-hidden="true">Just like Firefox, you can now insert your signature into a PDF by by typing, drawing or adding an image. Please refer to the Firefox article for full details: <a href="/en-US/kb/view-pdf-files-firefox-or-choose-another-viewer#w_add-a-signature-to-a-pdf">View and edit PDF files using Firefox’s built-in viewer</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-7" aria-expanded="false">Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-7" aria-hidden="true">See the 141 section for more details.
</p>
<h2 id="w_more-stable-exchange-support" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-8" aria-expanded="false">More stable Exchange support</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-8" aria-hidden="true">This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p></section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-141">Thunderbird 141</h1>
<h2 id="w_archive-action-added-to-new-email-notifications" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-9" aria-expanded="false">Archive action added to new email notifications</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-9" aria-hidden="true"><li> You can now <span class="button">Archive</span> email from your operating system''s new email native notifications (in addition to <span class="button">Mark as Read</span>, <span class="button">Delete</span>, <span class="button">Mark as Starred</span> and <span class="button">Mark as Spam</span>. Please see <a href="/en-US/kb/new-email-notifications-thunderbird">New email notifications in Thunderbird</a> for further information.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_better-exchange-support" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-10" aria-expanded="false">Better Exchange support</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-10" aria-hidden="true">Now, anyone can try the Exchange support in Thunderbird. For most, we recommend waiting for a future release which will be more stable and have additional features like on premise OAuth authentication(password authentication for on premise is supported in this release).
This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
Please refer to our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p>
<h2 id="w_31-day-warning-for-pgp-key-expiry" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-11" aria-expanded="false">31 day warning for PGP Key expiry</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-11" aria-hidden="true"><p>If your PGP key expires in the next 31 days, you will receive a warning when you are composing an email. For extra security, you can choose to let it expire or you can extend your key''s certificate.
</p><p>More info on PGP encrypted email can be found in <a href="/en-US/kb/introduction-to-e2e-encryption">Introduction to End-to-end encryption in Thunderbird</a> and <a href="/en-US/kb/openpgp-thunderbird-howto-and-faq">OpenPGP in Thunderbird - HOWTO and FAQ</a>.
</p></div>

<h2 id="w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release_2" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-12" aria-expanded="false">Folders erroneously renamed to ''Junk'' will be renamed back to ''Spam'' and ''Bulk'' in a post 142 release</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-12" aria-hidden="true">This is primarily a problem in the English language locales. It was discovered a few days after the release of 141. You can follow the progress of the fix in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979511">bug 1979511</a>.
</p></section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-140-esr-140-eclipse">Thunderbird 140 / ESR 140 Eclipse</h1>
<h2 id="w_dark-message-mode" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-13" aria-expanded="false">Dark Message Mode</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-13" aria-hidden="true"><p>Thunderbird now automatically displays your messages in dark mode when enabled in your operating system settings. If there are display issues with a message in dark mode, you can disable dark mode for that message by sliding the toggle to the right to disable dark message mode.
</p><dl><dt><img alt="tb140-dark-mode-toggle" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-06-29-22-50-16-bf91b7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl></div>


<h2 id="w_new-appearance-panel-in-settings" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-14" aria-expanded="false">New Appearance Panel in Settings</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-14" aria-hidden="true">The new Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Appearance</span>, allows you to toggle your message list layout between Card and Table view, adjust your Cards view, and set your default folder sorting order and threading options. For more details, please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/appearance-panel-layout-message-list-card-view-tab">Appearance Panel - Layout, Message List, Card View, Table View, Sorting and Threading settings</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_native-os-notifications" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-15" aria-expanded="false">Native OS Notifications</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-15" aria-hidden="true">Thunderbird now uses your operating system''s built-in notifications, whether you’re on Windows, Linux or Mac. You can now quickly delete, archive, or use customizable actions directly from your notifications.
</p>
<h2 id="w_redesigned-account-hub" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-16" aria-expanded="false">Redesigned Account hub</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-16" aria-hidden="true">The newly redesigned Account hub makes it easier to add an account. Connect all of your emails, address books and calendars in a few easy steps. The Account hub is currently not enabled for your first account (you will see it when you add a second account). It is also not enabled by default for your address book. You can change the defaults in your Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> &gt; search for <strong>Account hub</strong> (not enabled in ESR, we recommend the latest monthly release for the best experience).
</p>
<h2 id="w_manual-folder-sorting" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-17" aria-expanded="false">Manual Folder Sorting</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-17" aria-hidden="true">Drag and drop your folders to get the order you want.
</p>
<h2 id="w_experimental-exchange-support-for-advanced-users" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-18" aria-expanded="false">Experimental Exchange Support for advanced users</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-18" aria-hidden="true">Preliminary Microsoft Exchange support is available for you to try. Our current, initial implementation of Exchange is limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
We recommend you try this in a test <a href="/en-US/kb/using-multiple-profiles">Thunderbird profile</a> in <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-beta">Thunderbird Beta</a> (please ensure you back up your profile before starting). Please refer to our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p>
<h2 id="w_export-for-mobile" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-19" aria-expanded="false">Export for Mobile</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-19" aria-hidden="true">Generate a QR code to quickly transfer your account settings and credentials to your Thunderbird for Android app. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import">Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_horizontal-scroll-for-table-view" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-20" aria-expanded="false">Horizontal Scroll for Table View</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-20" aria-hidden="true">Table view now supports horizontal scrolling. This allows you to view wide messages – for example, data in a table with many columns.
</p></section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_older-releases">Older Releases</h1>
