</li><li>  For <strong>Username:</strong>, enter your Thundermail email address. For example <strong>nemo@thundermail.com</strong>.
</li><li> For <strong>URL/Hostname</strong>, enter <strong><a href="https://mail.thundermail.com">https://mail.thundermail.com</a></strong>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Continue</span>.
</li><li> Select your Thundermail Address Book and click <span class="button">Continue</span>.
</li></ol><p></p></div>

</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_custom-domain-catch-all-and-custom-aliases">Custom Domain catch-all and custom aliases</h1>
<p>How to setup Thunderbird Desktop for <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51253809827347-Custom-Domains-and-DNS-Configuration">custom domains</a> in Thundermail:
</p>
<h2 id="w_send-and-receive-email-with-thundermail-custom-domain-aliases-using-thunderbird-identities">Send and receive email with Thundermail custom domain aliases using Thunderbird identities</h2>
<p>For every custom domain <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases">alias</a> you create in Thundermail, you will have to create a new <a href="/en-US/kb/using-identities">identity</a> in Thunderbird in order to send email with that alias. Here''s how using our example custom domain <code>glamroc.com</code>, our example Thundermail address of <code>roc@thundermail.com</code>, and custom domain alias of <code>roc@glamroc.com</code>:
</p>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; Click on the <a href="http://thundermail.com">thundermail.com</a> account in the left sidebar, which in our example is: <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> &gt; <span class="button">Manage Identities…"</span> &gt; <span class="button">Add…</span>.
</li><li> Add your custom domain alias e.g. <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by filling in the <strong>New Identity</strong> form, and then click <span class="button">OK</span> when you are done.
</li><li> If you want this to be the default identity when composing new email, click on the new identity, and then click on <span class="button">Set Default</span> and <span class="button">Close</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now the custom domain alias <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> is the default identity when writing new email, and you can send and receive email on both <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> and <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by selecting the appropriate identity.
</p>
<h2 id="w_send-and-reply-to-email-using-a-thundermail-catch-all-alias">Send and reply to email using a Thundermail catch-all alias</h2>
<ul><li> If you have a <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias">catch-all alias configured in Thundermail for a custom domain</a>, here''s how to use it:
<ol><li> When composing or replying to an email, click on the email address next to <strong>From</strong>.
</li><li> Click on <span class="button">Customize From Address…</span>. 
</li><li> Finally, type in an email address for the custom domain you created the catch-all in.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_send-thundermail-use-your-thundermail-calendar-and-thundermail-address-book-and-have-some-fun">Send Thundermail, use your Thundermail Calendar and Thundermail Address Book, and have some fun!</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">Send and receive email</a> with Thundermail. If you have large files, you can also use <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235"><strong>Thunderbird Send</strong></a>. which is included with your Thunderbird Pro subscription.
</li><li> Enjoy your Thundermail Calendar: <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird/email-and-messaging/calendar">Create some events, invite people to invites, etc</a>.
</li><li> Use your Thundermail Address Book for <a href="/en-US/kb/addressing-email">composing email</a>, managing your contacts, etc.
</li><li> New to Thunderbird? Check out <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird">Get started with Thunderbird</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "330111", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 2, 2026, 1:49:29 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-02T13:49:29.795184-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "MadHatter", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/madhatter/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Just proofreading on this one. Left all the \"Thunderbird Pro\" verbiage the same for now, wasn''t sure if we were switching docs to just say \"Thundermail\" yet.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:18:53+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (330112,'en-US','using-identities','Using Identities','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-identities','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/using-identities/revision/330112',329959,'2026-06-02T14:04:11.214111-07:00','MadHatter','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/madhatter/','Mostly proofreading.',1,'2026-06-02T23:18:06.714129-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','When you configure an account, Thunderbird creates a default ''''''identity'''''' for the account that contains information like your name, email address, the location where your messages should be stored, signature, etc. You can manually create additional identities for your account using Thunderbird. When you create (or reply) to a message, you can specify any of the identities you have configured by selecting it from the ''''''From'''''' drop-down list.


= About identities =
As an example of how identities are useful, consider a scenario where you have a "default" address for a domain (such as "feedback@example.com") that is published on your website. People might send messages to this address on a variety of topics: Customer and vendor inquiries, feedback on the site''s technical issues, etc. 

When you configure multiple identities, you can specify the identity that is used when you reply to a message. For example, you could configure an identity for "sales@example.com", with a name and email address different than "feedback@example.com". Then, when you reply, you could specify using the "sales@example.com" identity for that reply.

Other uses for identities:

* Using an email forwarding service that redirects messages sent to an old email account to a new one
* Switching message configurations for specific messages (such as encryption or an SMTP server)

In this article, identities refer to separate configurations for a single account. However, if you have multiple email accounts, there is a default profile configured for each account that is used in the same manner as identities configured for a single account.

= Creating identities =
To create an identity:
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > click on the desired account in the left sidebar.
# Click the {button Manage Identities…} button in the bottom right. This displays a list of the identities previously created for the account. To create a new identity, click {button Add}.

Similar to configuring the default identity when you create a new account, the identity dialog enables you to specify various aspects:

*''''''Settings'''''': Name, address, signature, etc
*''''''Copies & Folders'''''': cc, bcc, and where to store messages
*''''''Composition & Addressing'''''': Text format, quote management, signature, etc

= Using identities =
When you create a new message, Thunderbird uses the default identity for the selected account as the message''s ''''''From'''''' value. Click the drop-down list in the ''''''From'''''' field to select a different identity. The drop-down list will display both identities for the selected account and identities for other accounts.

When you reply to a message or forward a message, Thunderbird scans the recipients and, if available, chooses the identity the original author used when sending the message to you (Sometimes this information is not available, such as with certain mailing lists). This identity will then be the default identity in the compose window. You may select a different identity as described above.','<p>When you configure an account, Thunderbird creates a default <strong>identity</strong> for the account that contains information like your name, email address, the location where your messages should be stored, signature, etc. You can manually create additional identities for your account using Thunderbird. When you create (or reply) to a message, you can specify any of the identities you have configured by selecting it from the <strong>From</strong> drop-down list.
</p><p><br>
</p>
<h1 id="w_about-identities">About identities</h1>
<p>As an example of how identities are useful, consider a scenario where you have a "default" address for a domain (such as "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>") that is published on your website. People might send messages to this address on a variety of topics: Customer and vendor inquiries, feedback on the site''s technical issues, etc. 
</p><p>When you configure multiple identities, you can specify the identity that is used when you reply to a message. For example, you could configure an identity for "<a href="mailto:sales@example.com">sales@example.com</a>", with a name and email address different than "<a href="mailto:feedback@example.com">feedback@example.com</a>". Then, when you reply, you could specify using the "<a href="mailto:sales@example.com">sales@example.com</a>" identity for that reply.
</p><p>Other uses for identities:
</p>
<ul><li> Using an email forwarding service that redirects messages sent to an old email account to a new one
</li><li> Switching message configurations for specific messages (such as encryption or an SMTP server)
</li></ul>
<p>In this article, identities refer to separate configurations for a single account. However, if you have multiple email accounts, there is a default profile configured for each account that is used in the same manner as identities configured for a single account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_creating-identities">Creating identities</h1>
<p>To create an identity:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; click on the desired account in the left sidebar.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Manage Identities…</span> button in the bottom right. This displays a list of the identities previously created for the account. To create a new identity, click <span class="button">Add</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Similar to configuring the default identity when you create a new account, the identity dialog enables you to specify various aspects:
</p>
<ul><li><strong>Settings</strong>: Name, address, signature, etc
</li><li><strong>Copies &amp; Folders</strong>: cc, bcc, and where to store messages
</li><li><strong>Composition &amp; Addressing</strong>: Text format, quote management, signature, etc
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_using-identities">Using identities</h1>
<p>When you create a new message, Thunderbird uses the default identity for the selected account as the message''s <strong>From</strong> value. Click the drop-down list in the <strong>From</strong> field to select a different identity. The drop-down list will display both identities for the selected account and identities for other accounts.
</p><p>When you reply to a message or forward a message, Thunderbird scans the recipients and, if available, chooses the identity the original author used when sending the message to you (Sometimes this information is not available, such as with certain mailing lists). This identity will then be the default identity in the compose window. You may select a different identity as described above.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "330112", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 2, 2026, 2:04:11 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-02T14:04:11.214111-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "MadHatter", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/madhatter/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Mostly proofreading.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 2, 2026, 11:18:06 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-02T23:18:06.714129-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:19:20+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (330134,'en-US','thundermail-and-thunderbird-android','Thundermail and Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thundermail-and-thunderbird-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thundermail-and-thunderbird-android/revision/330134',330106,'2026-06-02T16:32:09.353267-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP needs monica''s review. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/201',1,'2026-06-02T16:32:38.565213-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','[[Template:TBproEarlyBirdInviteOnly]]
Here’s how to get started with Mozilla’s Thundermail service. 
__TOC__
=Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android=
{note} ''''''NOTE'''''': Thunderbird Pro requires Thunderbird for Android version 18 or newer (also K-9). We recommend using the latest monthly release version documented in [[New in Thunderbird for Android]].{/note}
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]} > {menu Add account}.
# Enter your Thundermail email address. For example <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
# Click {button Next}.
# The next screen will prompt you to ''''''Please specify the special folders for your account'''''', and offer some suggested settings for your Thunderbird special folders. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click {button Next}. 
# On the next screen, configure your user name under ''''''Your name'''''' and optionally your signature and click {button Next}.
# The next screen has sync and notification options. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click {button Next} .

=Manual Settings=
Thunderbird should automatically configure your Thundermail email settings to match your [https://accounts.tb.pro/mail TB Pro accounts page]. If it doesn’t, please  manually change the receiving email and sending email settings according to the sections below. If manual setup fails, please create a support ticket using our form at: https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new.
[[UI:details_start]]

==Receiving email==
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]}
# Under ''''''Accounts'''''', tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
# Tap {menu Fetching mail} > {menu Incoming Server} and set the following values:
*''''''Server:'''''' <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> ''''''Security:'''''' SSL/TLS ''''''Port:'''''' 993
*''''''Authentication:'''''' OAuth 2.0
*''''''Username:'''''' your full Thundermail email address, for example <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.

==Sending email==
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]}
# Under ''''''Accounts'''''', tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
# Tap {menu Sending mail} > {menu Outgoing server} and set the following values:
* ''''''Server Name:'''''' <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> ''''''Connection'''''' ''''''Security:'''''' SSL/TLS ''''''Port:'''''' 465
* ''''''Authentication:'''''' OAuth 2.0
* ''''''Username:'''''' your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.

[[UI:details_end]]

=Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, tb.pro, and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird=
==Set up a Thunderbird identity for each each alias you want to reply from==

The steps are the same for [https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias catch-All], [https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases tb.pro, Thundermail and custom domain aliases], so we will just use the term ''''''alias'''''' to refer to all of these types of aliases in the instructions below:

You will have to setup an ''''''identity'''''' in Thunderbird for every alias you want to send email from.

For example, if your catch-all is for <code>glamroc.com</code> and you would like to reply as  <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code> in addition to the default alias for <code>glamroc.com</code>, you will have to setup identities for both <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code>. 

Here''s how:
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]}.
# Tap on your account, e.g. <code>roc@glamcroc.com</code> > {menu Sending mail}.
# Tap {menu Manage identities} > {menu ⋮} (also known as the ''''kebab'''' menu because the three vertical dots evoke a kebab > {menu New identity}.
# Enter the desired alias in the ''''''Email address'''''' field e.g. <code>alice@glamroc.com</code>. All other fields are optional, but we recommend you enter a memorable shorter name in the ''''''Your name'''''' field.
# Save the identity by tapping {button SAVE}. When replying, you can now change the ''''''From'''''' field to this identity by tapping on it.
==Send email with the identities for your aliases==
# When composing or replying, tap on the email address in the  ''''''From'''''' field.
# Tap on the relevant identity for the alias you wish to send from.
# Write and send the email as usual.
=Have some fun now that email setup is done!=
Congratulations, you have done successfully setup Thundermail with Thunderbird for Android! Next:
* Send and receive email with Thundermail.
* New to Thunderbird for Android? Then check out [[Getting started with Thunderbird for Android]].
<!--
* If you have large files, you can send them with FIXME because Send on Mobile needs some polish: add link to Thunderbird Send which is included with your Thunderbird Pro subscription.
-->','<p></p><div class="warning">NOTE: Thundermail (formerly known as Thunderbird Pro) is currently in its Early Bird release and available  by invitation only. To learn more and join the waitlist, visit <a href="https://tb.pro/">our website</a>. We’ll be opening Thundermail to the public once the Early Bird release concludes.</div>
Here’s how to get started with Mozilla’s Thundermail service. 
<p></p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configure-thundermail-in-thunderbird-for-android"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_manual-settings"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Manual Settings</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_receiving-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Receiving email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_sending-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Sending email</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configure-catch-all-thundermail-tb-pro-and-custom-domain-aliases-for-use-with-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, tb.pro, and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_set-up-a-thunderbird-identity-for-each-each-alias-you-want-to-reply-from"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Set up a Thunderbird identity for each each alias you want to reply from</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_send-email-with-the-identities-for-your-aliases"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Send email with the identities for your aliases</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_have-some-fun-now-that-email-setup-is-done"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Have some fun now that email setup is done!</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configure-thundermail-in-thunderbird-for-android">Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android</h1>
<div class="note"> <strong>NOTE</strong>: Thunderbird Pro requires Thunderbird for Android version 18 or newer (also K-9). We recommend using the latest monthly release version documented in <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android">New in Thunderbird for Android</a>.</div>
<ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add account</span>.
</li><li> Enter your Thundermail email address. For example <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> The next screen will prompt you to <strong>Please specify the special folders for your account</strong>, and offer some suggested settings for your Thunderbird special folders. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click <span class="button">Next</span>. 
</li><li> On the next screen, configure your user name under <strong>Your name</strong> and optionally your signature and click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> The next screen has sync and notification options. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click <span class="button">Next</span> .
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_manual-settings">Manual Settings</h1>
<p>Thunderbird should automatically configure your Thundermail email settings to match your <a href="https://accounts.tb.pro/mail">TB Pro accounts page</a>. If it doesn’t, please  manually change the receiving email and sending email settings according to the sections below. If manual setup fails, please create a support ticket using our form at: <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new">https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new</a>.
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_receiving-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Receiving email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</li><li> Under <strong>Accounts</strong>, tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Fetching mail</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Incoming Server</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Server:</strong> <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> <strong>Security:</strong> SSL/TLS <strong>Port:</strong> 993
</li><li><strong>Authentication:</strong> OAuth 2.0
</li><li><strong>Username:</strong> your full Thundermail email address, for example <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
</li></ul></div>


<h2 id="w_sending-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Sending email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</li><li> Under <strong>Accounts</strong>, tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Sending mail</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Outgoing server</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li> <strong>Server Name:</strong> <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> <strong>Connection</strong> <strong>Security:</strong> SSL/TLS <strong>Port:</strong> 465
</li><li> <strong>Authentication:</strong> OAuth 2.0
</li><li> <strong>Username:</strong> your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>.
</li></ul><p></p></div>


</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_configure-catch-all-thundermail-tb-pro-and-custom-domain-aliases-for-use-with-thunderbird">Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, <a href="http://tb.pro">tb.pro</a>, and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird</h1>
<h2 id="w_set-up-a-thunderbird-identity-for-each-each-alias-you-want-to-reply-from">Set up a Thunderbird identity for each each alias you want to reply from</h2>
<p>The steps are the same for <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias">catch-All</a>, <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases">tb.pro, Thundermail and custom domain aliases</a>, so we will just use the term <strong>alias</strong> to refer to all of these types of aliases in the instructions below:
</p><p>You will have to setup an <strong>identity</strong> in Thunderbird for every alias you want to send email from.
</p><p>For example, if your catch-all is for <code>glamroc.com</code> and you would like to reply as  <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code> in addition to the default alias for <code>glamroc.com</code>, you will have to setup identities for both <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code>. 
</p><p>Here''s how:
</p>
<ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>.
</li><li> Tap on your account, e.g. <code>roc@glamcroc.com</code> &gt; <span class="menu">Sending mail</span>.
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Manage identities</span> &gt; <span class="menu">⋮</span> (also known as the <em>kebab</em> menu because the three vertical dots evoke a kebab &gt; <span class="menu">New identity</span>.
</li><li> Enter the desired alias in the <strong>Email address</strong> field e.g. <code>alice@glamroc.com</code>. All other fields are optional, but we recommend you enter a memorable shorter name in the <strong>Your name</strong> field.
</li><li> Save the identity by tapping <span class="button">SAVE</span>. When replying, you can now change the <strong>From</strong> field to this identity by tapping on it.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_send-email-with-the-identities-for-your-aliases">Send email with the identities for your aliases</h2>
<ol><li> When composing or replying, tap on the email address in the  <strong>From</strong> field.
</li><li> Tap on the relevant identity for the alias you wish to send from.
</li><li> Write and send the email as usual.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_have-some-fun-now-that-email-setup-is-done">Have some fun now that email setup is done!</h1>
<p>Congratulations, you have done successfully setup Thundermail with Thunderbird for Android! Next:
</p>
<ul><li> Send and receive email with Thundermail.
</li><li> New to Thunderbird for Android? Then check out <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-android">Getting started with Thunderbird for Android</a>.
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "330134", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 2, 2026, 4:32:09 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-02T16:32:09.353267-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "WIP needs monica''s review. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/201", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 2, 2026, 4:32:38 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-02T16:32:38.565213-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:20:11+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (330147,'en-US','new-thunderbird-android','New in Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android/revision/330147',326868,'2026-06-02T19:41:32.514453-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP, please do not translate Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/204',1,'2026-06-02T19:42:30.382922-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','[[T:TBAndroidMore]]
This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in Thunderbird for Android. For full details, please refer to [[Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues]].

__TOC__
==Thunderbird 19==
* The QR Code scanner now works with Thundermail QR codes to quickly setup your Thundermail email account. Full instructions:[[Thundermail and Thunderbird for Android]].
=Older Releases=
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 18==
* PNG custom avatars are now supported (as well as JPEGs and stylized letter monograms as per the Thunderbird 17 section).
* There is a new setting to display times in ISO 8601 format ''''(YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm)'''' in the message list.
*# Tap {button ≡} > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]}.
*# Tap {button General settings} > tap {menu Display} > under ''''''Messsage lists'''''', tap {menu Date/Time Format} > tap {button ISO}.
==Thunderbird 17==
* Custom avatars for email accounts are now available. JPEGs only for now. PNGs will be supported in a future release. Upload yours as follows: 
*# Tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]} at the bottom left.
*# Tap your email account that you want to customize e.g. <code>nemo@thunderbird.net</code> > tap {menu General settings} > tap {menu Image} to upload your custom JPEG. You can also use a stylized letter monogram by tapping {menu Monogram} or pick one of the built-in icons by tapping {menu Icon}.
==Thunderbird 16==
* The SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed in [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10312 #10312].
* The following issue was fixed in TB 16.1 which was released one week after Thunderbird 16.0. <del>Known Issue: Deleted/read messages in your unified inbox may not update until you manually refresh. We hope to have a fix soon. you can can follow along on issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10243 #10243]</del>
==Thunderbird 15==
* There are further SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com and the team is testing a fix. You can follow along on issue [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10312 #10312]. It looks like this will fixed in the next release, TB 16.
==Thunderbird 14==
* SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on outlook.com have been fixed.
==Thunderbird 13==
* The notification in status bar re-appearing multiple times a day issue (github issue [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/8049 #8049]) has been fixed.
* Special folders (Trash, Sent, Drafts, Archives, etc) not appearing after updating from TB11 to TB12 (github issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9771 #9771]) was fixed.
* The bug with outgoing email not working for certain educational and enterprise accounts is scheduled to be fixed in the next release, Thunderbird 14 for Android (github issue: [https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9160 #9160]).
==Thunderbird 12==
* If you use a password manager, Android will now use it to automatically fill in your password when you add a new account.
==Thunderbird 11==
* Mail folders are easier to navigate.
* Algorithmic darkening makes reading emails easier in dark mode.
* Thunderbird 11.1 : Selected/read/unread message states are now easier to distinguish visually.
* You can still see font settings if you search for ''''''font'''''' in Settings. This will be removed in a future Thunderbird release. Please use the Android operating system font size settings instead.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 10==
* You can now empty your Spam folder by tapping {menu Empty Spam} in the Spam folder''s menu {menu ⋮} (also known as the ''''''kebab'''''' menu because the 3 vertical dots resemble a kebab).
* On Android 10 or older, you now have an option to use the system theme.
*# Tap {button ≡} at the top left > tap the gear icon:{button [[Image:gear icon]]} at the bottom left.
*# Tap {button General settings} > tap {menu Display} > tap {button Use system default} under ''''''Theme''''''
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 9==
* Initial Android 15 support.
* Thunderbird attempts to use your email provider''s auto configuration database. If it fails it, it uses Mozilla''s database.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==Thunderbird 8==
* Refer to [[New in Thunderbird for Android version 8]] please.
[[UI:details_end]]','<p></p><div class="warning">More for Thunderbird for Android: <strong>o</strong> Vote on &amp; suggest improvements at <a href="https://connect.mozilla.org/t5/ideas/idb-p/ideas/label-name/thunderbird%20android">Connect</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://www.thunderbird.net/participate/">Participate/Contribute</a> <strong>o</strong> <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird-android/form">Get Support</a></div>
This article introduces the most interesting new features, improvements, and changes visible to users which are available in Thunderbird for Android. For full details, please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-release-notes-whats-new-and-known-issu">Thunderbird release notes - What''s new and known issues</a>.
<p></p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-19"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 19</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_older-releases"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Older Releases</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-18"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 18</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-17"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 17</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-16"><span class="tocnumber">2.3</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 16</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-15"><span class="tocnumber">2.4</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 15</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-14"><span class="tocnumber">2.5</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 14</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-13"><span class="tocnumber">2.6</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 13</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-12"><span class="tocnumber">2.7</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 12</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-11"><span class="tocnumber">2.8</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 11</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-10"><span class="tocnumber">2.9</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 10</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-9"><span class="tocnumber">2.10</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 9</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thunderbird-8"><span class="tocnumber">2.11</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird 8</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-19">Thunderbird 19</h2>
<ul><li> The QR Code scanner now works with Thundermail QR codes to quickly setup your Thundermail email account. Full instructions:<a href="/en-US/kb/thundermail-and-thunderbird-android">Thundermail and Thunderbird for Android</a>.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_older-releases">Older Releases</h1>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-18" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 18</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><li> PNG custom avatars are now supported (as well as JPEGs and stylized letter monograms as per the Thunderbird 17 section).
</li><li> There is a new setting to display times in ISO 8601 format <em>(YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm)</em> in the message list.
<ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> &gt; tap the gear icon:<span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">General settings</span> &gt; tap <span class="menu">Display</span> &gt; under <strong>Messsage lists</strong>, tap <span class="menu">Date/Time Format</span> &gt; tap <span class="button">ISO</span>.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-17" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 17</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><li> Custom avatars for email accounts are now available. JPEGs only for now. PNGs will be supported in a future release. Upload yours as follows: 
<ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the gear icon:<span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> at the bottom left.
</li><li> Tap your email account that you want to customize e.g. <code>nemo@thunderbird.net</code> &gt; tap <span class="menu">General settings</span> &gt; tap <span class="menu">Image</span> to upload your custom JPEG. You can also use a stylized letter monogram by tapping <span class="menu">Monogram</span> or pick one of the built-in icons by tapping <span class="menu">Icon</span>.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-16" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 16</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-hidden="true"><li> The SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> have been fixed in <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10312">#10312</a>.
</li><li> The following issue was fixed in TB 16.1 which was released one week after Thunderbird 16.0. <del>Known Issue: Deleted/read messages in your unified inbox may not update until you manually refresh. We hope to have a fix soon. you can can follow along on issue: <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10243">#10243</a></del>
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-15" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 15</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-hidden="true"><li> There are further SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> and the team is testing a fix. You can follow along on issue <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/10312">#10312</a>. It looks like this will fixed in the next release, TB 16.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-14" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 14</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-hidden="true"><li> SMTP issues with enterprise and educational accounts on <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> have been fixed.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-13" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-5" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 13</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-5" aria-hidden="true"><li> The notification in status bar re-appearing multiple times a day issue (github issue <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/8049">#8049</a>) has been fixed.
</li><li> Special folders (Trash, Sent, Drafts, Archives, etc) not appearing after updating from TB11 to TB12 (github issue: <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9771">#9771</a>) was fixed.
</li><li> The bug with outgoing email not working for certain educational and enterprise accounts is scheduled to be fixed in the next release, Thunderbird 14 for Android (github issue: <a href="https://github.com/thunderbird/thunderbird-android/issues/9160">#9160</a>).
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-12" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-6" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 12</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-6" aria-hidden="true"><li> If you use a password manager, Android will now use it to automatically fill in your password when you add a new account.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-11" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-7" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 11</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-7" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Mail folders are easier to navigate.
</li><li> Algorithmic darkening makes reading emails easier in dark mode.
</li><li> Thunderbird 11.1&nbsp;: Selected/read/unread message states are now easier to distinguish visually.
</li><li> You can still see font settings if you search for <strong>font</strong> in Settings. This will be removed in a future Thunderbird release. Please use the Android operating system font size settings instead.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
