</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_a-screen-that-indicates-it-administrator-approval-is-required-for-the-app">A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app</h2>
<ul><li> You must ask your administrator to authorize Thunderbird – approval must be done, but only once.
</li><li> Per Microsoft documentation, administrators should visit <code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/adminconsent?client_id=9e5f94bc-e8a4-4e73-b8be-63364c29d753</code> and grant the following permissions in order to authorize Mozilla Thunderbird: <code><strong>IMAP.AccessAsUser.All</strong></code>, <code><strong>POP.AccessAsUser.All</strong></code>, <code><strong>SMTP.Send</strong></code> and <strong>offline_access</strong>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</h2>
<ul><li> Try to sign in with a new Thunderbird profile (see <a href="/en-US/kb/profile-manager-create-and-remove-thunderbird-profiles">Profile Manager - Create and remove Thunderbird profiles</a> for instructions on how to create a new profile).
</li><li> If a new Thunderbird profile works, we recommend continuing using the new profile.<strong>For experienced users who want to keep other changes made in the config editor:</strong> Open the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the <a href="/en-US/kb/config-editor">Config Editor</a> to filter for <strong>oauth2</strong>, find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for <code><strong>oauth2.issuer</strong></code> and <code><strong>auth2.scope</strong></code>.
</li><li> If that doesn’t work, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask for support</a>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_imappop3-works-but-smtp-does-not-work">IMAP/POP3 works, but SMTP does not work</h2>
<ul><li> If you have a Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled. Read Microsoft’s article <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission">Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online</a> for instructions.
</li><li> If you have a Microsoft 365/Hotmail/etc. <strong>personal</strong> account, use basic authentication (read Microsoft’s guide <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040">POP, IMAP, and SMTP settings for Outlook.com</a> for instructions).
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_calendar-does-not-work">Calendar does not work</h2>
<ul><li> Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_where-to-get-help">Where to get help</h1>
<ul><li> If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance from within your organization.
</li><li> If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft’s hosted services, Please follow our <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth Troubleshoting Checklist</a> before <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">asking a support question</a> on our forums.
</li></ul>
</div>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "335353", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 23, 2026, 12:05:58 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-23T00:05:58.736494-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "please don''t approve, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/214", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:16+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (335354,'en-US','microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202','Microsoft OAuth Authentication and Thunderbird in 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202/revision/335354',335353,'2026-06-23T00:08:13.531119-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','please don''t approve, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/214',1,'2026-06-23T00:08:33.231686-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','=Microsoft OAuth authentication and Thunderbird in 2026=
{note}''''''NOTES'''''': 
* For simplicity, we will use the term [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth ''''''OAuth''''''] instead of ''''''OAuth 2'''''' or ''''''OAuth 2.0''''''. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
* Prior to Thunderbird 153, the Microsoft OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the Microsoft OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead.

Microsoft has made some changes to authentication for their hosted email services for business and academic accounts. This article describes these changes and how to adjust to them.

{warning}As of June 2026, this information is still evolving and subject to change - please [[Update Thunderbird to the latest version|update Thunderbird to the latest release]] in order to have the best experience with Microsoft’s email services. Due to Microsoft’s ever-changing diversity of email servers and deployments, issues can emerge without warning that cause Thunderbird and other non-Microsoft email clients to suddenly stop working.{/warning}

__TOC__
=Changes to authentication=
Microsoft has instituted the following changes:
* [https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/deprecation-of-basic-authentication-exchange-online Deprecated basic authentication] (username/password), now requiring [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth]] authentication.
* In some cases, SMTP authentication has been completely disabled. For new accounts, SMTP always starts disabled. In addition, there are some restrictions on SMTP that are not currently understood.
Microsoft has also changed the way they classify certain clients, so Thunderbird’s previous OAuth setup does not properly qualify it as a desktop client. As a result, we have been forced to make configuration changes to Thunderbird, which may have side effects for users.

=Changes or problems you may encounter=
For [http://outlook.com outlook.com], [http://hotmail.com hotmail.com], Microsoft 365 (formerly known as Office 365 and often abbreviated as O365), or other Microsoft-hosted email services, you may see the following issues:

==Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email==
* Thunderbird might display an error message similar to: <code>Login to server outlook.office365.com with username youremail@hotmail.com failed.</code>
* Solution
** Ensure two-step verification is turned on for your Microsoft account (see Microsoft’s support article: [https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/account-billing/how-to-use-two-step-verification-with-your-microsoft-account-c7910146-672f-01e9-50a0-93b4585e7eb4 How to use two-step verification with your Microsoft account]).
** Enable cookies for Microsoft’s Outlook or Hotmail websites in Thunderbird, otherwise you will not be able to log in to your Outlook or Hotmail account using OAuth2 authentication:
**# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > {menu Settings} > {menu Privacy & Security}.
**# Under the ''''Web Content'''' section, tick ''''''Accept cookies from sites''''''.
**# Click {button Exceptions…} next to  ''''''Accept cookies from sites'''''' to make sure you are not blocking cookies from Microsoft sites, such as [http://outlook.com outlook.com], [http://hotmail.com hotmail.com] or [http://office365.com office365.com].
** Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for incoming mail (IMAP or POP):
**# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings}.
**# On the left side, click {menu Server Settings}} for your [http://outlook.com outlook.com] or [http://hotmail.com hotmail.com] account.
**# Select Authentication method: {menu OAuth2} (instead of {menu Normal password}).
**#;[[Image:tb153-select-oauth2]] 

* Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for sending messages (SMTP):
*# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings} and click on your Microsoft email account on the left side. For example: <code>tbtesttb@hotmail.com</code>
*# At the bottom right, click {button Edit outgoing server…}:
*#;[[Image:tb153-microsoft-email-tbtesttb-edit-outgoing-server]] 
*# Select ''''''Authentication method'''''': {menu OAuth2} (instead of ''''''Normal password''''''). The Server Name might need to be changed <code>to smtp-mail.outlook.com</code> :
*#;[[Image:tbg153-microsoft-email-smtp-server]] 
*#Click {button OK} to save your changes.

==A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app==
* You must ask your administrator to authorize Thunderbird – approval must be done, but only once.
* Per Microsoft documentation, administrators should visit <code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/adminconsent?client_id=9e5f94bc-e8a4-4e73-b8be-63364c29d753</code> and grant the following permissions in order to authorize Mozilla Thunderbird: <code>''''''IMAP.AccessAsUser.All''''''</code>, <code>''''''POP.AccessAsUser.All''''''</code>, <code>''''''SMTP.Send''''''</code> and ''''''offline_access''''''.

==An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later==
* Try to sign in with a new Thunderbird profile (see [[Profile Manager - Create and remove Thunderbird profiles]] for instructions on how to create a new profile).
* If a new Thunderbird profile works, we recommend continuing using the new profile.''''''For experienced users who want to keep other changes made in the config editor:'''''' Open the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the [[Config Editor]] to filter for ''''''oauth2'''''', find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for <code>''''''oauth2.issuer''''''</code> and <code>''''''auth2.scope''''''</code>.
* If that doesn’t work, please [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form ask for support].

==IMAP/POP3 works, but SMTP does not work==
* If you have a Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled. Read Microsoft’s article [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online] for instructions.
* If you have a Microsoft 365/Hotmail/etc. ''''''personal'''''' account, use basic authentication (read Microsoft’s guide [https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040 POP, IMAP, and SMTP settings for Outlook.com] for instructions).
==Calendar does not work==
* Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.

=Where to get help=
* If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance from within your organization.
* If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft’s hosted services, Please follow our [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth Troubleshoting Checklist]] before [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form asking a support question] on our forums.','<h1 id="w_microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-in-2026">Microsoft OAuth authentication and Thunderbird in 2026</h1>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTES</strong>: 
<ul><li> For simplicity, we will use the term <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth"><strong>OAuth</strong></a> instead of <strong>OAuth 2</strong> or <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
</li><li> Prior to Thunderbird 153, the Microsoft OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the Microsoft OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead.
</li></ul>
<p>Microsoft has made some changes to authentication for their hosted email services for business and academic accounts. This article describes these changes and how to adjust to them.
</p>
<div class="warning">As of June 2026, this information is still evolving and subject to change - please <a href="/en-US/kb/updating-thunderbird">update Thunderbird to the latest release</a> in order to have the best experience with Microsoft’s email services. Due to Microsoft’s ever-changing diversity of email servers and deployments, issues can emerge without warning that cause Thunderbird and other non-Microsoft email clients to suddenly stop working.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-in-2026"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft OAuth authentication and Thunderbird in 2026</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changes-to-authentication"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changes to authentication</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changes-or-problems-you-may-encounter"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Changes or problems you may encounter</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_your-outlook-or-hotmail-password-no-longer-works-with-thunderbird-and-you-cannot-send-or-receive-email"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_a-screen-that-indicates-it-administrator-approval-is-required-for-the-app"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_imappop3-works-but-smtp-does-not-work"><span class="tocnumber">3.4</span> <span class="toctext">IMAP/POP3 works, but SMTP does not work</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_calendar-does-not-work"><span class="tocnumber">3.5</span> <span class="toctext">Calendar does not work</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_where-to-get-help"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Where to get help</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_changes-to-authentication">Changes to authentication</h1>
<p>Microsoft has instituted the following changes:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/deprecation-of-basic-authentication-exchange-online">Deprecated basic authentication</a> (username/password), now requiring <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth</a> authentication.
</li><li> In some cases, SMTP authentication has been completely disabled. For new accounts, SMTP always starts disabled. In addition, there are some restrictions on SMTP that are not currently understood.
</li></ul>
<p>Microsoft has also changed the way they classify certain clients, so Thunderbird’s previous OAuth setup does not properly qualify it as a desktop client. As a result, we have been forced to make configuration changes to Thunderbird, which may have side effects for users.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changes-or-problems-you-may-encounter">Changes or problems you may encounter</h1>
<p>For <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a>, <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a>, Microsoft 365 (formerly known as Office 365 and often abbreviated as O365), or other Microsoft-hosted email services, you may see the following issues:
</p>
<h2 id="w_your-outlook-or-hotmail-password-no-longer-works-with-thunderbird-and-you-cannot-send-or-receive-email">Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email</h2>
<ul><li> Thunderbird might display an error message similar to: <code>Login to server outlook.office365.com with username youremail@hotmail.com failed.</code>
</li><li> Solution
<ul><li> Ensure two-step verification is turned on for your Microsoft account (see Microsoft’s support article: <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/account-billing/how-to-use-two-step-verification-with-your-microsoft-account-c7910146-672f-01e9-50a0-93b4585e7eb4">How to use two-step verification with your Microsoft account</a>).
</li><li> Enable cookies for Microsoft’s Outlook or Hotmail websites in Thunderbird, otherwise you will not be able to log in to your Outlook or Hotmail account using OAuth2 authentication:
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span>.
</li><li> Under the <em>Web Content</em> section, tick <strong>Accept cookies from sites</strong>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Exceptions…</span> next to  <strong>Accept cookies from sites</strong> to make sure you are not blocking cookies from Microsoft sites, such as <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a>, <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a> or <a href="http://office365.com">office365.com</a>.
</li></ol>
</li><li> Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for incoming mail (IMAP or POP):
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span>.
</li><li> On the left side, click <span class="menu">Server Settings</span>} for your <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> or <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a> account.
</li><li> Select Authentication method: <span class="menu">OAuth2</span> (instead of <span class="menu">Normal password</span>).
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153-select-oauth2" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-22-23-37-14-e54c2e.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
</li></ul>
<ul><li> Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for sending messages (SMTP):
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> and click on your Microsoft email account on the left side. For example: <code>tbtesttb@hotmail.com</code>
</li><li> At the bottom right, click <span class="button">Edit outgoing server…</span>:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153-microsoft-email-tbtesttb-edit-outgoing-server" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-22-23-54-55-bcf1bd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Select <strong>Authentication method</strong>: <span class="menu">OAuth2</span> (instead of <strong>Normal password</strong>). The Server Name might need to be changed <code>to smtp-mail.outlook.com</code>&nbsp;:
<dl><dt><img alt="tbg153-microsoft-email-smtp-server" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-23-00-01-02-80edb0.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li>Click <span class="button">OK</span> to save your changes.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_a-screen-that-indicates-it-administrator-approval-is-required-for-the-app">A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app</h2>
<ul><li> You must ask your administrator to authorize Thunderbird – approval must be done, but only once.
</li><li> Per Microsoft documentation, administrators should visit <code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/adminconsent?client_id=9e5f94bc-e8a4-4e73-b8be-63364c29d753</code> and grant the following permissions in order to authorize Mozilla Thunderbird: <code><strong>IMAP.AccessAsUser.All</strong></code>, <code><strong>POP.AccessAsUser.All</strong></code>, <code><strong>SMTP.Send</strong></code> and <strong>offline_access</strong>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</h2>
<ul><li> Try to sign in with a new Thunderbird profile (see <a href="/en-US/kb/profile-manager-create-and-remove-thunderbird-profiles">Profile Manager - Create and remove Thunderbird profiles</a> for instructions on how to create a new profile).
</li><li> If a new Thunderbird profile works, we recommend continuing using the new profile.<strong>For experienced users who want to keep other changes made in the config editor:</strong> Open the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the <a href="/en-US/kb/config-editor">Config Editor</a> to filter for <strong>oauth2</strong>, find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for <code><strong>oauth2.issuer</strong></code> and <code><strong>auth2.scope</strong></code>.
</li><li> If that doesn’t work, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask for support</a>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_imappop3-works-but-smtp-does-not-work">IMAP/POP3 works, but SMTP does not work</h2>
<ul><li> If you have a Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled. Read Microsoft’s article <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission">Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online</a> for instructions.
</li><li> If you have a Microsoft 365/Hotmail/etc. <strong>personal</strong> account, use basic authentication (read Microsoft’s guide <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040">POP, IMAP, and SMTP settings for Outlook.com</a> for instructions).
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_calendar-does-not-work">Calendar does not work</h2>
<ul><li> Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_where-to-get-help">Where to get help</h1>
<ul><li> If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance from within your organization.
</li><li> If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft’s hosted services, Please follow our <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth Troubleshoting Checklist</a> before <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">asking a support question</a> on our forums.
</li></ul>
</div>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "335354", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 23, 2026, 12:08:13 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-23T00:08:13.531119-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "please don''t approve, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/214", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 23, 2026, 12:08:33 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-23T00:08:33.231686-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:13+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (335355,'en-US','microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202-redirect-2','Microsoft OAuth Authentication and Thunderbird in 2024','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202-redirect-2','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202-redirect-2/revision/335355',NULL,'2026-06-23T00:09:54.528102-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','REDIRECT [[Microsoft OAuth Authentication and Thunderbird in 2026]]','<p>REDIRECT <a href="/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202">Microsoft OAuth Authentication and Thunderbird in 2026</a>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "335355", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 23, 2026, 12:09:54 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-23T00:09:54.528102-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:05+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (335356,'en-US','microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202','Microsoft OAuth Authentication and Thunderbird in 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-202/revision/335356',335354,'2026-06-23T00:12:34.345151-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fix typo;please don''t approve, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/214',1,'2026-06-23T00:12:58.743044-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,1,'2026-07-21T10:03:56.641660-07:00','rtanglao','approved','=Microsoft OAuth authentication and Thunderbird in 2026=
{note}''''''NOTES'''''': 
* For simplicity, we will use the term [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth ''''''OAuth''''''] instead of ''''''OAuth 2'''''' or ''''''OAuth 2.0''''''. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
* Prior to Thunderbird 153, the Microsoft OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the Microsoft OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead.

Microsoft has made some changes to authentication for their hosted email services for business and academic accounts. This article describes these changes and how to adjust to them.

{warning}As of June 2026, this information is still evolving and subject to change - please [[Update Thunderbird to the latest version|update Thunderbird to the latest release]] in order to have the best experience with Microsoft’s email services. Due to Microsoft’s ever-changing diversity of email servers and deployments, issues can emerge without warning that cause Thunderbird and other non-Microsoft email clients to suddenly stop working.{/warning}

__TOC__
=Changes to authentication=
Microsoft has instituted the following changes:
* [https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/deprecation-of-basic-authentication-exchange-online Deprecated basic authentication] (username/password), now requiring [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth]] authentication.
* In some cases, SMTP authentication has been completely disabled. For new accounts, SMTP always starts disabled. In addition, there are some restrictions on SMTP that are not currently understood.
Microsoft has also changed the way they classify certain clients, so Thunderbird’s previous OAuth setup does not properly qualify it as a desktop client. As a result, we have been forced to make configuration changes to Thunderbird, which may have side effects for users.

=Changes or problems you may encounter=
For [http://outlook.com outlook.com], [http://hotmail.com hotmail.com], Microsoft 365 (formerly known as Office 365 and often abbreviated as O365), or other Microsoft-hosted email services, you may see the following issues:

==Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email==
* Thunderbird might display an error message similar to: <code>Login to server outlook.office365.com with username youremail@hotmail.com failed.</code>
* Solution
** Ensure two-step verification is turned on for your Microsoft account (see Microsoft’s support article: [https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/account-billing/how-to-use-two-step-verification-with-your-microsoft-account-c7910146-672f-01e9-50a0-93b4585e7eb4 How to use two-step verification with your Microsoft account]).
** Enable cookies for Microsoft’s Outlook or Hotmail websites in Thunderbird, otherwise you will not be able to log in to your Outlook or Hotmail account using OAuth2 authentication:
**# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > {menu Settings} > {menu Privacy & Security}.
**# Under the ''''Web Content'''' section, tick ''''''Accept cookies from sites''''''.
**# Click {button Exceptions…} next to  ''''''Accept cookies from sites'''''' to make sure you are not blocking cookies from Microsoft sites, such as [http://outlook.com outlook.com], [http://hotmail.com hotmail.com] or [http://office365.com office365.com].
** Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for incoming mail (IMAP or POP):
**# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings}.
**# On the left side, click {menu Server Settings}} for your [http://outlook.com outlook.com] or [http://hotmail.com hotmail.com] account.
**# Select Authentication method: {menu OAuth2} (instead of {menu Normal password}).
**#;[[Image:tb153-select-oauth2]] 

* Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for sending messages (SMTP):
*# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > {menu Account Settings} and click on your Microsoft email account on the left side. For example: <code>tbtesttb@hotmail.com</code>
*# At the bottom right, click {button Edit outgoing server…}:
*#;[[Image:tb153-microsoft-email-tbtesttb-edit-outgoing-server]] 
*# Select ''''''Authentication method'''''': {menu OAuth2} (instead of ''''''Normal password''''''). The Server Name might need to be changed <code>to smtp-mail.outlook.com</code> :
*#;[[Image:tbg153-microsoft-email-smtp-server]] 
*#Click {button OK} to save your changes.

==A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app==
* You must ask your administrator to authorize Thunderbird – approval must be done, but only once.
* Per Microsoft documentation, administrators should visit <code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/adminconsent?client_id=9e5f94bc-e8a4-4e73-b8be-63364c29d753</code> and grant the following permissions in order to authorize Mozilla Thunderbird: <code>''''''IMAP.AccessAsUser.All''''''</code>, <code>''''''POP.AccessAsUser.All''''''</code>, <code>''''''SMTP.Send''''''</code> and ''''''offline_access''''''.

==An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later==
* Try to sign in with a new Thunderbird profile (see [[Profile Manager - Create and remove Thunderbird profiles]] for instructions on how to create a new profile).
* If a new Thunderbird profile works, we recommend continuing using the new profile.''''''For experienced users who want to keep other changes made in the config editor:'''''' Open the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the [[Config Editor]] to filter for ''''''oauth2'''''', find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for <code>''''''oauth2.issuer''''''</code> and <code>''''''auth2.scope''''''</code>.
* If that doesn’t work, please [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form ask for support].

==IMAP/POP3 works, but SMTP does not work==
* If you have a Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled. Read Microsoft’s article [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online] for instructions.
* If you have a Microsoft 365/Hotmail/etc. ''''''personal'''''' account, use basic authentication (read Microsoft’s guide [https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040 POP, IMAP, and SMTP settings for Outlook.com] for instructions).
==Calendar does not work==
* Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.

=Where to get help=
* If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance from within your organization.
* If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft’s hosted services, Please follow our [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth Troubleshooting Checklist]] before [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form asking a support question] on our forums.','<h1 id="w_microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-in-2026">Microsoft OAuth authentication and Thunderbird in 2026</h1>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTES</strong>: 
<ul><li> For simplicity, we will use the term <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth"><strong>OAuth</strong></a> instead of <strong>OAuth 2</strong> or <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
</li><li> Prior to Thunderbird 153, the Microsoft OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the Microsoft OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead.
</li></ul>
<p>Microsoft has made some changes to authentication for their hosted email services for business and academic accounts. This article describes these changes and how to adjust to them.
</p>
<div class="warning">As of June 2026, this information is still evolving and subject to change - please <a href="/en-US/kb/updating-thunderbird">update Thunderbird to the latest release</a> in order to have the best experience with Microsoft’s email services. Due to Microsoft’s ever-changing diversity of email servers and deployments, issues can emerge without warning that cause Thunderbird and other non-Microsoft email clients to suddenly stop working.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-oauth-authentication-and-thunderbird-in-2026"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft OAuth authentication and Thunderbird in 2026</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changes-to-authentication"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Changes to authentication</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_changes-or-problems-you-may-encounter"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Changes or problems you may encounter</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_your-outlook-or-hotmail-password-no-longer-works-with-thunderbird-and-you-cannot-send-or-receive-email"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_a-screen-that-indicates-it-administrator-approval-is-required-for-the-app"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later"><span class="tocnumber">3.3</span> <span class="toctext">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_imappop3-works-but-smtp-does-not-work"><span class="tocnumber">3.4</span> <span class="toctext">IMAP/POP3 works, but SMTP does not work</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_calendar-does-not-work"><span class="tocnumber">3.5</span> <span class="toctext">Calendar does not work</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_where-to-get-help"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Where to get help</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_changes-to-authentication">Changes to authentication</h1>
<p>Microsoft has instituted the following changes:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/deprecation-of-basic-authentication-exchange-online">Deprecated basic authentication</a> (username/password), now requiring <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth</a> authentication.
</li><li> In some cases, SMTP authentication has been completely disabled. For new accounts, SMTP always starts disabled. In addition, there are some restrictions on SMTP that are not currently understood.
</li></ul>
<p>Microsoft has also changed the way they classify certain clients, so Thunderbird’s previous OAuth setup does not properly qualify it as a desktop client. As a result, we have been forced to make configuration changes to Thunderbird, which may have side effects for users.
</p>
<h1 id="w_changes-or-problems-you-may-encounter">Changes or problems you may encounter</h1>
<p>For <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a>, <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a>, Microsoft 365 (formerly known as Office 365 and often abbreviated as O365), or other Microsoft-hosted email services, you may see the following issues:
</p>
<h2 id="w_your-outlook-or-hotmail-password-no-longer-works-with-thunderbird-and-you-cannot-send-or-receive-email">Your Outlook or Hotmail password no longer works with Thunderbird and you cannot send or receive email</h2>
<ul><li> Thunderbird might display an error message similar to: <code>Login to server outlook.office365.com with username youremail@hotmail.com failed.</code>
</li><li> Solution
<ul><li> Ensure two-step verification is turned on for your Microsoft account (see Microsoft’s support article: <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/account-billing/how-to-use-two-step-verification-with-your-microsoft-account-c7910146-672f-01e9-50a0-93b4585e7eb4">How to use two-step verification with your Microsoft account</a>).
</li><li> Enable cookies for Microsoft’s Outlook or Hotmail websites in Thunderbird, otherwise you will not be able to log in to your Outlook or Hotmail account using OAuth2 authentication:
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span>.
</li><li> Under the <em>Web Content</em> section, tick <strong>Accept cookies from sites</strong>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Exceptions…</span> next to  <strong>Accept cookies from sites</strong> to make sure you are not blocking cookies from Microsoft sites, such as <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a>, <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a> or <a href="http://office365.com">office365.com</a>.
</li></ol>
</li><li> Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for incoming mail (IMAP or POP):
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span>.
</li><li> On the left side, click <span class="menu">Server Settings</span>} for your <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> or <a href="http://hotmail.com">hotmail.com</a> account.
</li><li> Select Authentication method: <span class="menu">OAuth2</span> (instead of <span class="menu">Normal password</span>).
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153-select-oauth2" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-22-23-37-14-e54c2e.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
</li></ul>
<ul><li> Change Thunderbird’s authentication method for sending messages (SMTP):
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> and click on your Microsoft email account on the left side. For example: <code>tbtesttb@hotmail.com</code>
</li><li> At the bottom right, click <span class="button">Edit outgoing server…</span>:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153-microsoft-email-tbtesttb-edit-outgoing-server" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-22-23-54-55-bcf1bd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Select <strong>Authentication method</strong>: <span class="menu">OAuth2</span> (instead of <strong>Normal password</strong>). The Server Name might need to be changed <code>to smtp-mail.outlook.com</code>&nbsp;:
<dl><dt><img alt="tbg153-microsoft-email-smtp-server" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-23-00-01-02-80edb0.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li>Click <span class="button">OK</span> to save your changes.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_a-screen-that-indicates-it-administrator-approval-is-required-for-the-app">A screen that indicates IT administrator approval is required for the app</h2>
<ul><li> You must ask your administrator to authorize Thunderbird – approval must be done, but only once.
</li><li> Per Microsoft documentation, administrators should visit <code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/adminconsent?client_id=9e5f94bc-e8a4-4e73-b8be-63364c29d753</code> and grant the following permissions in order to authorize Mozilla Thunderbird: <code><strong>IMAP.AccessAsUser.All</strong></code>, <code><strong>POP.AccessAsUser.All</strong></code>, <code><strong>SMTP.Send</strong></code> and <strong>offline_access</strong>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_an-account-worked-on-thunderbird-102-6-1-but-does-not-work-on-102-7-1-or-later">An account worked on Thunderbird 102.6.1, but does not work on 102.7.1 or later</h2>
<ul><li> Try to sign in with a new Thunderbird profile (see <a href="/en-US/kb/profile-manager-create-and-remove-thunderbird-profiles">Profile Manager - Create and remove Thunderbird profiles</a> for instructions on how to create a new profile).
</li><li> If a new Thunderbird profile works, we recommend continuing using the new profile.<strong>For experienced users who want to keep other changes made in the config editor:</strong> Open the Thunderbird profile manager to switch back to the old Thunderbird profile and use the <a href="/en-US/kb/config-editor">Config Editor</a> to filter for <strong>oauth2</strong>, find the appropriate server(s), and delete the entries for <code><strong>oauth2.issuer</strong></code> and <code><strong>auth2.scope</strong></code>.
</li><li> If that doesn’t work, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask for support</a>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_imappop3-works-but-smtp-does-not-work">IMAP/POP3 works, but SMTP does not work</h2>
<ul><li> If you have a Microsoft 365 business account, ensure that SMTP authentication is enabled or ask your IT administrator to check and turn it on if disabled. Read Microsoft’s article <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission">Enable or disable authenticated client SMTP submission (SMTP AUTH) in Exchange Online</a> for instructions.
</li><li> If you have a Microsoft 365/Hotmail/etc. <strong>personal</strong> account, use basic authentication (read Microsoft’s guide <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/pop-imap-and-smtp-settings-for-outlook-com-d088b986-291d-42b8-9564-9c414e2aa040">POP, IMAP, and SMTP settings for Outlook.com</a> for instructions).
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_calendar-does-not-work">Calendar does not work</h2>
<ul><li> Thunderbird does not support Exchange calendars. If you are using an add-on or other software to enable calendar, then you will need to seek support from the author of that add-on or software.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_where-to-get-help">Where to get help</h1>
<ul><li> If you are a user within a business or academic institution that provides Microsoft accounts, you should seek assistance from within your organization.
</li><li> If you have a personal account through one of Microsoft’s hosted services, Please follow our <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth Troubleshooting Checklist</a> before <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">asking a support question</a> on our forums.
</li></ul>
</div>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "335356", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 23, 2026, 12:12:34 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-23T00:12:34.345151-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fix typo;please don''t approve, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/214", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 23, 2026, 12:12:58 AM", "datetime": "2026-06-23T00:12:58.743044-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Jul 21, 2026, 10:03:56 AM", "datetime": "2026-07-21T10:03:56.641660-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:03+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (337824,'en-US','fix-missing-reply-all-button-152','Fix missing Reply All  Button in 152','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/fix-missing-reply-all-button-152','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/fix-missing-reply-all-button-152/revision/337824',NULL,'2026-06-30T19:58:27.131303-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip, pls do not approve, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/215',1,'2026-06-30T20:22:49.164612-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','In some emails on Exchange (EWS) accounts, Thunderbird may hide the {button Reply All} button in the message header and the ''''''Recipients'''''' column may not display all recipient information when using Table View — even when multiple recipients are in the ''''To'''' or ''''Cc'''' fields. This article explains why this happens and how to fix the affected messages.

{note}''''''Note:'''''' Only ''''''Thunderbird 152.0'''''' is affected and only for Microsoft Exchange accounts that use Exchange Web Services (EWS). IMAP and POP accounts are not affected, nor Thunderbird 151 or earlier. The underlying problem is corrected in ''''''Thunderbird 153''''''.{/note}

== Why the Reply All button is missing ==

When Thunderbird 152.0 downloaded certain messages from an Exchange (EWS) server, some message details were not stored correctly in your local copy, including the full list of recipients. Because Thunderbird does not find more than one recipient, it does not show the {button Reply All} button for those messages or display the correct information in the Recipients column.

Whether a message is affected, depends on the version of Thunderbird that originally downloaded it, which is why only some messages are missing the button and others are not.

Updating to Thunderbird 153 avoids the problem, but messages that were already downloaded with incomplete data need to be repaired manually using the steps below.

== Repair an affected folder ==

You must update to version 152.0.1 or newer, then repair the folder to rebuild its local index with the missing recipient information. After a repair, the {button Reply All} button appears again for all messages that have multiple recipients, and the full recipient list shows correctly in the message list.

# Update to version 152.0.1 or newer. Briefly: click the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Help} > {menu About Thunderbird}. If there is an update available, you will see the update download. When the download is finished,  click on {button Restart Thunderbird to Update} to update. Refer to [[Update Thunderbird to the latest version]].
# In the folder pane, find the Exchange (EWS) folder that contains messages with a missing {button Reply All} button.
# Right-click (control+click on macOS) the EWS folder > {menu Properties}.
# In the ''''''Folder Properties'''''' window, ''''''General information'''''' tab, click {button Repair Folder} and wait for the repair to finish.

Repeat these steps for other Exchange folders where the {button Reply All} button is missing.

== Still missing the Reply All button? ==

If the {button Reply All} button is still missing after updating Thunderbird and repairing the folder, confirm that the message actually has more than one recipient in the ''''To'''' or ''''Cc'''' fields. A message sent only to you will not show a {button Reply All} button.

Need help? [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form Ask for support] in our [https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird Thunderbird community forum] from our global community of volunteers who have been doing most Thunderbird support for over 20 years.','<p>In some emails on Exchange (EWS) accounts, Thunderbird may hide the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button in the message header and the <strong>Recipients</strong> column may not display all recipient information when using Table View — even when multiple recipients are in the <em>To</em> or <em>Cc</em> fields. This article explains why this happens and how to fix the affected messages.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> Only <strong>Thunderbird 152.0</strong> is affected and only for Microsoft Exchange accounts that use Exchange Web Services (EWS). IMAP and POP accounts are not affected, nor Thunderbird 151 or earlier. The underlying problem is corrected in <strong>Thunderbird 153</strong>.</div>
<h2 id="w_why-the-reply-all-button-is-missing">Why the Reply All button is missing</h2>
<p>When Thunderbird 152.0 downloaded certain messages from an Exchange (EWS) server, some message details were not stored correctly in your local copy, including the full list of recipients. Because Thunderbird does not find more than one recipient, it does not show the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button for those messages or display the correct information in the Recipients column.
</p><p>Whether a message is affected, depends on the version of Thunderbird that originally downloaded it, which is why only some messages are missing the button and others are not.
</p><p>Updating to Thunderbird 153 avoids the problem, but messages that were already downloaded with incomplete data need to be repaired manually using the steps below.
</p>
<h2 id="w_repair-an-affected-folder">Repair an affected folder</h2>
<p>You must update to version 152.0.1 or newer, then repair the folder to rebuild its local index with the missing recipient information. After a repair, the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button appears again for all messages that have multiple recipients, and the full recipient list shows correctly in the message list.
</p>
<ol><li> Update to version 152.0.1 or newer. Briefly: click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Help</span> &gt; <span class="menu">About Thunderbird</span>. If there is an update available, you will see the update download. When the download is finished,  click on <span class="button">Restart Thunderbird to Update</span> to update. Refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/updating-thunderbird">Update Thunderbird to the latest version</a>.
</li><li> In the folder pane, find the Exchange (EWS) folder that contains messages with a missing <span class="button">Reply All</span> button.
</li><li> Right-click (control+click on macOS) the EWS folder &gt; <span class="menu">Properties</span>.
</li><li> In the <strong>Folder Properties</strong> window, <strong>General information</strong> tab, click <span class="button">Repair Folder</span> and wait for the repair to finish.
</li></ol>
<p>Repeat these steps for other Exchange folders where the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button is missing.
</p>
<h2 id="w_still-missing-the-reply-all-button">Still missing the Reply All button?</h2>
<p>If the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button is still missing after updating Thunderbird and repairing the folder, confirm that the message actually has more than one recipient in the <em>To</em> or <em>Cc</em> fields. A message sent only to you will not show a <span class="button">Reply All</span> button.
</p><p>Need help? <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">Ask for support</a> in our <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird">Thunderbird community forum</a> from our global community of volunteers who have been doing most Thunderbird support for over 20 years.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "337824", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 30, 2026, 7:58:27 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-30T19:58:27.131303-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip, pls do not approve, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/215", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 30, 2026, 8:22:49 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-30T20:22:49.164612-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:19:00+00:00','MT');
