Need help? [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form Ask for support] in our [https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird Thunderbird community forum] from our global community of volunteers who have been doing most Thunderbird support for over 20 years.','<p>For some <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Exchange</a> (EWS) account emails with multiple recipients in <em>To</em> or <em>Cc</em> fields, Thunderbird may hide the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button in the message pane header and the message list''s columns Correspndents and Recipients may not display all recipient information in Table View. This article explains why this happens and how to fix the affected messages.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> Only <strong>Thunderbird 152.0</strong> is affected and only for Microsoft Exchange accounts that use Exchange Web Services (EWS). IMAP and POP accounts are not affected, nor Thunderbird 151 or earlier. The underlying problem is corrected in <strong>Thunderbird 152.0.1</strong>.</div>
<h2 id="w_why-the-reply-all-button-is-missing">Why the Reply All button is missing</h2>
<p>When Thunderbird 152.0 downloaded certain messages from an Exchange (EWS) server, some message details were not stored correctly in your local copy, including the full list of recipients. Because Thunderbird does not find more than one recipient, it does not show the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button for those messages nor display the correct information in the Recipients column.
</p><p>Whether a message is affected depends on the version of Thunderbird that last downloaded it, which is why only some messages are missing the button and others messages are not missing the button.
</p><p><strong>Updating to Thunderbird 152.0.1 or newer avoids incorrect message downloads, but messages already downloaded with incomplete data using 152.0 need to be repaired manually using the steps below.</strong>
</p>
<h2 id="w_repair-an-affected-folder">Repair an affected folder</h2>
<p>You must update to version 152.0.1 or newer, then repair the folder to rebuild its local index with the missing recipient information. After a repair, the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button appears again for all messages that have multiple recipients, and the full recipient list will display correctly in the message list.
</p>
<ol><li> Update to version 152.0.1 or newer. Briefly: click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Help</span> &gt; <span class="menu">About Thunderbird</span>. If an update is available, you will see an <span class="button">Update to &lt;version number&gt;</span> button. Click to update, and when the download is finished click on <span class="button">Restart Thunderbird to Update</span> to complete the update. Refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/updating-thunderbird">Update Thunderbird to the latest version</a> for details.
</li><li> In the folder pane, find the Exchange (EWS) folder that contains messages with a missing <span class="button">Reply All</span> button.
</li><li> Right-click (<span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">click</span> on macOS) the EWS folder &gt; <span class="menu">Properties</span>.
</li><li> In the <strong>Folder Properties</strong> window, <strong>General information</strong> tab, click <span class="button">Repair Folder</span> and wait for the repair to finish.
</li></ol>
<p>Repeat these steps for other Exchange folders where the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button is missing.
</p>
<h2 id="w_still-missing-the-reply-all-button">Still missing the Reply All button?</h2>
<p>If the <span class="button">Reply All</span> button is still missing after updating Thunderbird and repairing the folder, confirm that the message actually has more than one recipient in the <em>To</em> or <em>Cc</em> fields by examining the message headers by pressing <span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">U</span> (<span class="key">Command</span> + <span class="key">U</span> on macOS) to view the message source. A message sent only to you will not show a <span class="button">Reply All</span> button.
</p><p>Need help? <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">Ask for support</a> in our <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird">Thunderbird community forum</a> from our global community of volunteers who have been doing most Thunderbird support for over 20 years.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "337846", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 30, 2026, 10:00:01 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-30T22:00:01.225001-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "remove spaces before :-) others  do it, link to Thunderbird and Exchange discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/215", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 30, 2026, 10:00:44 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-30T22:00:44.437337-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Jun 30, 2026, 10:07:44 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-30T22:07:44.878036-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:18:56+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (337890,'en-US','close-tray-starting-154','Close to tray starting in 154','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/close-tray-starting-154','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/close-tray-starting-154/revision/337890',NULL,'2026-06-30T22:45:56.677509-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','do not approve or translate. discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/216',1,'2026-06-30T22:49:24.489571-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,1,'2026-10-14T09:47:44.197264-07:00','rtanglao','approved','__TOC__

If you use Thunderbird on Windows, you may be familiar with sending Thunderbird to the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taskbar#Notification_area system tray] (the notification area) so it keeps running in the background. Starting with Thunderbird 154, the way this works has changed – instead of minimizing to the tray, Thunderbird now moves to the tray when you close it. This article explains the change and shows you how to turn the feature on.

{note}''''''Note:'''''' This feature applies only to ''''''Thunderbird 154 and later'''''' on Windows 10 and 11. It is not available on macOS or Linux.{/note}

==“Close to tray” instead of “minimize to tray”==
In earlier versions, Thunderbird could be set to move to the system tray when you minimized the window. As of Thunderbird 154, that behavior has been replaced:

* The ''''''Minimize'''''' button now simply minimizes the Thunderbird window, like other applications.
* The ''''''Close'''''' (X) button can move Thunderbird to the system tray instead of quitting, so it keeps running in the background.

If you previously used the ''''''When Thunderbird is minimized, move it to the tray'''''' setting, Thunderbird 154 and newer automatically migrates you to this new “''''''close to tray''''''” behavior. This keeps Thunderbird consistent with how modern Windows applications use the system tray.

==Turn on “close to tray”==
# Click the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings}.
# Select the {menu General} tab and scroll down to the ''''''System Integration'''''' section.
# Check the box labeled ''''''When Thunderbird is closed, move it to the tray''''''. This option is off by default. Once this is enabled, clicking the ''''''Close'''''' (X) button moves Thunderbird to the system tray instead of quitting. Thunderbird keeps running in the background so you continue to receive new messages and notifications.

==Close Thunderbird from the system tray==
When “close to tray” is turned on, you can fully quit Thunderbird directly from the tray:

# In the system tray, right-click the Thunderbird icon.
# Select ''''''Exit Thunderbird''''''.

This lets you close Thunderbird without opening the window first.

==Start Thunderbird in the tray==
You can also have Thunderbird start directly in the system tray instead of opening a window when it launches.

# Click the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Settings}.
# Select the {menu General} tab and scroll down to the ''''''System Integration'''''' section.
# Check the box labeled ''''''Start Thunderbird in the tray''''''. This option is off by default. Once you check this option, you have to click on the Thunderbird icon in the tray to open a Thunderbird window.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_close-to-tray-instead-of-minimize-to-tray"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">“Close to tray” instead of “minimize to tray”</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_turn-on-close-to-tray"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Turn on “close to tray”</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_close-thunderbird-from-the-system-tray"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Close Thunderbird from the system tray</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_start-thunderbird-in-the-tray"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Start Thunderbird in the tray</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p>If you use Thunderbird on Windows, you may be familiar with sending Thunderbird to the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taskbar#Notification_area">system tray</a> (the notification area) so it keeps running in the background. Starting with Thunderbird 154, the way this works has changed – instead of minimizing to the tray, Thunderbird now moves to the tray when you close it. This article explains the change and shows you how to turn the feature on.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> This feature applies only to <strong>Thunderbird 154 and later</strong> on Windows 10 and 11. It is not available on macOS or Linux.</div>
<h2 id="w_close-to-tray-instead-of-minimize-to-tray">“Close to tray” instead of “minimize to tray”</h2>
<p>In earlier versions, Thunderbird could be set to move to the system tray when you minimized the window. As of Thunderbird 154, that behavior has been replaced:
</p>
<ul><li> The <strong>Minimize</strong> button now simply minimizes the Thunderbird window, like other applications.
</li><li> The <strong>Close</strong> (X) button can move Thunderbird to the system tray instead of quitting, so it keeps running in the background.
</li></ul>
<p>If you previously used the <strong>When Thunderbird is minimized, move it to the tray</strong> setting, Thunderbird 154 and newer automatically migrates you to this new “<strong>close to tray</strong>” behavior. This keeps Thunderbird consistent with how modern Windows applications use the system tray.
</p>
<h2 id="w_turn-on-close-to-tray">Turn on “close to tray”</h2>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">General</span> tab and scroll down to the <strong>System Integration</strong> section.
</li><li> Check the box labeled <strong>When Thunderbird is closed, move it to the tray</strong>. This option is off by default. Once this is enabled, clicking the <strong>Close</strong> (X) button moves Thunderbird to the system tray instead of quitting. Thunderbird keeps running in the background so you continue to receive new messages and notifications.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_close-thunderbird-from-the-system-tray">Close Thunderbird from the system tray</h2>
<p>When “close to tray” is turned on, you can fully quit Thunderbird directly from the tray:
</p>
<ol><li> In the system tray, right-click the Thunderbird icon.
</li><li> Select <strong>Exit Thunderbird</strong>.
</li></ol>
<p>This lets you close Thunderbird without opening the window first.
</p>
<h2 id="w_start-thunderbird-in-the-tray">Start Thunderbird in the tray</h2>
<p>You can also have Thunderbird start directly in the system tray instead of opening a window when it launches.
</p>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.
</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">General</span> tab and scroll down to the <strong>System Integration</strong> section.
</li><li> Check the box labeled <strong>Start Thunderbird in the tray</strong>. This option is off by default. Once you check this option, you have to click on the Thunderbird icon in the tray to open a Thunderbird window.
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "337890", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Jun 30, 2026, 10:45:56 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-30T22:45:56.677509-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "do not approve or translate. discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/216", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Jun 30, 2026, 10:49:24 PM", "datetime": "2026-06-30T22:49:24.489571-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Oct 14, 2026, 9:47:44 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-14T09:47:44.197264-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:18:53+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (340686,'en-US','tb-oauth','OAuth2 for Thunderbird','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-oauth','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-oauth/revision/340686',333928,'2026-07-16T08:47:14.634017-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','oauth runs on port 80, localhost aka 127.0.0.1',1,'2026-07-16T08:47:37.388378-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','=OAuth for Thunderbird=
{note}''''''NOTES'''''':
* For simplicity, we will use the term [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth ''''''OAuth''''''] instead of ''''''OAuth 2'''''' or ''''''OAuth 2.0''''''. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
* Prior to Thunderbird 153, the OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead.{/note}
__TOC__ 
=What is OAuth?=
[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth OAuth] is a web standard that allows you to authorize an application (such as Thunderbird) or service to sign in to a provider without divulging private information, such as the password to your provider’s account.

For less technical people, here is a "bank" analogy for OAuth: 
* Instead of giving Thunderbird your ''''''full access password'''''' (the one that allows you to access all of a provider’s services), you log in to your bank''s website (possibly with the bank''s ads) directly. 
* Once you are verified, the bank gives Thunderbird a temporary, laminated visitor badge (''''''Access Token'''''') with a limited photo ID (OpenID). Thunderbird never stores your passowrd, just this token.
* Thunderbird presents this badge to the vault guards (email servers) to fetch your messages (and sometimes your contacts and calendars) but nothing else in the bank, all done without your full access password.

[[UI:details_start]]
==More detailed OAuth explanation for less technical people==
The ''''''Bank'''''' analogy breaks down how this works:
* ''''''The Bank &amp; Vault (Your Email Provider)'''''': Google, Microsoft, etc., act as the secure bank holding your valuable assets (emails, contacts, calendar).
* ''''''Your All Access Password (Your Username/Password)'''''': This is the key to your entire safety deposit box. You only show this directly to the Bank during verification, never to random third-party apps.
* ''''''Thunderbird (The Courier)'''''': Thunderbird is a hired courier service you use to deliver and pick up your mail from the bank.
* ''''''The OAuth2 Token (The Temporary Badge)'''''': Instead of handing your password to the courier, the Bank issues Thunderbird a laminated, time-sensitive visitor badge.

===How the Process Works===
# ''''''Requesting Access'''''': When you set up a new email in Thunderbird, it asks the Bank for a badge.
# ''''''The Laminated Badge'''''': The Bank temporarily redirects you to their secure website (the Bank’s lobby), where you verify your identity with your full access password (e.g. your Google or Microsoft account password) and perhaps Two-Factor Authentication (2FA).
# ''''''Restricted Privileges'''''': Once verified, the Bank issues Thunderbird an Access Token. This token only grants the ability to read and send emails (and perhaps contacts and calendars) —it cannot be used for anything else (e.g. Google Photos or Microsoft’s One Drive), to change your account password, or lock you out.
# ''''''Revoking Access'''''': If you no longer trust Thunderbird, or if your device is lost, you can call the Bank (go to your Google or Microsoft account settings) and tell them to “cancel the badge.” Thunderbird instantly loses access, but your account (for example your Google or Microsoft account) remains untouched.
[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_start]]
==That’s not technical enough. Please explain OAuth in depth!==
OAuth is a web standard that allows you to authorize one application or service to sign in to another without divulging private information, such as passwords. ''''''Sign in with Google / Apple /Microsoft'''''' is an example of OAuth that you may have seen. Many email providers like Gmail and Outlook use OAuthto allow Thunderbird to sign in to their email services without sharing the entire email provider’s credentials with Thunderbird.

OAuth is the default authentication method for Gmail, Outlook, Yahoo Mail, and many more providers. The provider (not Thunderbird) opens your system’s web browser and presents a sequence of provider-branded dialogs asking for an account username and password, agreement to a terms of service, and authorization for the types of the provider’s data you wish Thunderbird to be permitted to access, such as mail, calendar, and contacts. Any or all of these dialogs could have ads. ''''''Anything displayed in these dialogues (including ads) is controlled by the email provider, not Thunderbird''''''.

===OAuth dialog sequence===
You will be shown dialogs for some or all of the following as part of the authentication process:
# Provide your username, email or mobile number. The username will be your email address.
# Provide your account password (do not use your ''''''app password'''''').
# Optionally, agree to the Terms of Service.
# Optionally, choose a method to confirm “Is it really you?” by sending a security code to your phone or account email address and then enter the code that was sent into a followup dialog.
# Grant permission to ''''''Thunderbird'''''' to access some or all of the following data: Mail, contacts, calendar.
At the end of the authentication process:
* An ''''''access token'''''' is sent from your provider, which is then stored by Thunderbird in its list of passwords to allow future access to the server. A token is a string of characters that allows access from a specific client on a specific device - it is not a password. Tokens eventually expire at a time determined by your provider, which requires you to again authenticate with your username and password. If you do not know your password, then you must use your provider’s account page in a web browser to create a new password, often called a ''''''password reset.''''''
* Thunderbird sets the account’s configuration according to the authentication settings you just provided.
[[UI:details_end]]
=How do I troubleshoot OAuth issues?=
If your email provider is using OAuth 2 and you see authentication or authorization errors, then please follow this checklist:
# ''''''OAuth requires JavaScript and cookies,'''''' which may not be enabled in your system web browser. They must be enabled in order for OAuth2 to work. Please double check they are enabled (consult your system browser’s documentation). For example, Firefox’s documentation can be found at [https://support.mozilla.org/products/firefox support.mozilla.org/products/firefox]
# OAuth runs a web server on your computer at port 80. If you have other software running on port 80 (for example a WebDAV client) on <code>localhost</code> or <code>127.0.0.1</code>, please disable it in order to make OAuth work.
# Since OAuth uses the system web browser, all the normal browser troubleshooting steps apply. Consult your system browser’s official documentation for troubleshooting. For example, here are steps for Firefox: [[Troubleshoot and diagnose Firefox problems]]. Briefly:
#* If your browser has something similar to [[Use Troubleshoot Mode in Firefox|Firefox Troubleshoot mode]], see if using it will resolve your OAuth 2 issue.
#* Disable Extensions.
#* Disable antivirus and other third party software.
#* If you are on Windows, try [https://support.microsoft.com/windows/windows-startup-settings-1af6ec8c-4d4a-4b23-adb7-e76eef0b847f Windows Safe Mode].
# Try changing your system web browser to another web browser. For example, change it from Chrome to Firefox.
# Ask for support from your email provider first - most of the time, OAuth issues are not caused by Thunderbird. Transitioning from traditional passwords to OAuth can result in issues that only your email provider can fix.
# Ask for support from our wonderful worldwide community of Mozilla support volunteers who, for over 20 years, have done the majority of Thunderbird support: [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form]','<h1 id="w_oauth-for-thunderbird">OAuth for Thunderbird</h1>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTES</strong>:
<ul><li> For simplicity, we will use the term <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth"><strong>OAuth</strong></a> instead of <strong>OAuth 2</strong> or <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
</li><li> Prior to Thunderbird 153, the OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead.</li></ul></div>

