#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>
#* Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only ''''you yourself'''' will be able to see your saved passwords.
# The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog]]
# To set your Primary Password, click {button OK}.
Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Change your Primary Password =
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Click the button {button Change Primary Password}.
# Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new Primary Password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Remove your Primary Password =
Follow the steps above to ''''Change your Primary Password'''', but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.

= Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it =
If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.{/warning}
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Tools} > {menu Developer Tools} > Click the {button OK} button in the ''''''Incoming Connection'''''' dialog (keyboard shortcut:{key Ctrl+Shift+J}, {key Command+Shift+J} on macOS) to open the error console.
# Copy the text given below and paste it into the console.<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
# Click the {button Evaluate} button. If you do not see an ''''Evaluate'''' button, press {key Return} or {key Enter} on your keyboard.
# When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the {button Reset} button.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog]]','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">What does the Primary Password protect?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_set-a-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Set a Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_change-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Change your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_remove-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Remove your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</span></a></li></ul></div>
<div class="warning">WARNING:<strong>We do not recommend using the Primary Password feature. It is not secure</strong>. Until this feature is removed, we will keep this article for legacy reasons. <strong>Your primary password protects only authentication information, such as saved passwords - it <em>does not</em> prevent others from seeing your messages, your calendars, your contacts, or other personal information in Thunderbird.</strong> To block access to locally stored (downloaded) messsages you should use traditional computer protections such as disk encryption, computer accounts, and file system permissions. Create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password, do not give  administrator capability and do not give access to your computer account''s file system. (Thunderbird stores mail accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.) </div>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect">What does the Primary Password protect?</h1>
<p>Thunderbird can save passwords and other authentication information for each account in the <a href="/en-US/kb/password-manager-remember-delete-change-tb">Password Manager</a>, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. 
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords and authentication informatio?  You can set a Primary Password, which must be entered to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. You only need to enter your Primary Password once per Thunderbird session.
</p><p><img alt="tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-12-29-50-749867.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<h1 id="w_set-a-primary-password">Set a Primary Password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Put a checkmark next to <strong>Use a Primary Password</strong>.<br><br><img alt="tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-09-05-dfd94c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> The <em>Change Primary Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( )
</li><li> Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only <em>you yourself</em> will be able to see your saved passwords.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br><br><img alt="tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-59-36-393ff3.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> To set your Primary Password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_change-your-primary-password">Change your Primary Password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Click the button <span class="button">Change Primary Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new Primary Password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_remove-your-primary-password">Remove your Primary Password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to <em>Change your Primary Password</em>, but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.
</p>
<h1 id="w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Developer Tools</span> &gt; Click the <span class="button">OK</span> button in the <strong>Incoming Connection</strong> dialog (keyboard shortcut:<span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span>, <span class="key">Command</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span> on macOS) to open the error console.
</li><li> Copy the text given below and paste it into the console.<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button. If you do not see an <em>Evaluate</em> button, press <span class="key">Return</span> or <span class="key">Enter</span> on your keyboard.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the <span class="button">Reset</span> button.<br><br><img alt="tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-15-48-30-8ed674.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "346021", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 7, 2026, 9:31:39 PM", "datetime": "2026-08-07T21:31:39.596699-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "the hamburger menu should be used instead of keyboard shortcut top open the console (keyboard shortcuts are often usurped :-) by tools like Notion)", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 7, 2026, 9:33:05 PM", "datetime": "2026-08-07T21:33:05.507011-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Aug 7, 2026, 9:33:05 PM", "datetime": "2026-08-07T21:33:05.507011-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:18:10+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (346087,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/346087',346021,'2026-08-08T08:36:23.000248-07:00','Wayne Mery','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/','backed out feature removal information, add warning about forgetting a primary password',1,'2026-08-08T08:47:52.618390-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,1,'2026-08-08T08:47:52.618390-07:00','wsmwk','approved','__TOC__

{warning}WARNING: ''''''Do not use the Primary Password feature for protecting data stored locally on your computer. A Primary Password protects only authentication information, such as saved passwords - it ''''does not'''' prevent others from seeing your messages, your calendars, your contacts, or other personal information in Thunderbird.''''''<br><br>To block access to locally stored (downloaded) messsages you should use traditional computer protections such as disk encryption, computer accounts, and file system permissions. Create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}macOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}macOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password, do not give  administrator capability and do not give access to your computer account''s file system. (Thunderbird stores mail accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.){/warning}
<!-- [Commented out: Not useful because it''s very easy to reverse this method]<br><br> A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True. This will prevent the display of email until the Primary Password is entered. It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file. Using an operating system account does address that.-->

= What does the Primary Password protect? =
Thunderbird can save passwords and other authentication information for each account in the [[Password Manager - Remember, delete and change passwords in Thunderbird|Password Manager]], so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. 

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords and authentication informatio?  You can set a Primary Password, which must be entered to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. You only need to enter your Primary Password once per Thunderbird session.

{warning}There is no way to recover a forgotten Primary Password. If you forget it, you must [[Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password#w_Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it|reset it]], which will cause stored credentials to be deleted from Thunderbird.{/warning}

[[Image:tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password]]

= Set a Primary Password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Put a checkmark next to ''''''Use a Primary Password''''''.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password]]
# The ''''Change Primary Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>
#* Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only ''''you yourself'''' will be able to see your saved passwords.
# The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog]]
# To set your Primary Password, click {button OK}.
Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Change your Primary Password =
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Click the button {button Change Primary Password}.
# Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new Primary Password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Remove your Primary Password =
Follow the steps above to ''''Change your Primary Password'''', but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.

= Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it =
If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.{/warning}
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Tools} > {menu Developer Tools} > Click the {button OK} button in the ''''''Incoming Connection'''''' dialog (keyboard shortcut:{key Ctrl+Shift+J}, {key Command+Shift+J} on macOS) to open the error console.
# Copy the text given below and paste it into the console.<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
# Click the {button Evaluate} button. If you do not see an ''''Evaluate'''' button, press {key Return} or {key Enter} on your keyboard.
# When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the {button Reset} button.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog]]','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">What does the Primary Password protect?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_set-a-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Set a Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_change-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Change your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_remove-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Remove your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</span></a></li></ul></div>
<div class="warning">WARNING: <strong>Do not use the Primary Password feature for protecting data stored locally on your computer. A Primary Password protects only authentication information, such as saved passwords - it <em>does not</em> prevent others from seeing your messages, your calendars, your contacts, or other personal information in Thunderbird.</strong><br><br>To block access to locally stored (downloaded) messsages you should use traditional computer protections such as disk encryption, computer accounts, and file system permissions. Create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password, do not give  administrator capability and do not give access to your computer account''s file system. (Thunderbird stores mail accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.)</div>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect">What does the Primary Password protect?</h1>
<p>Thunderbird can save passwords and other authentication information for each account in the <a href="/en-US/kb/password-manager-remember-delete-change-tb">Password Manager</a>, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. 
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords and authentication informatio?  You can set a Primary Password, which must be entered to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. You only need to enter your Primary Password once per Thunderbird session.
</p>
<div class="warning">There is no way to recover a forgotten Primary Password. If you forget it, you must <a href="/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password#w_Reset_your_Primary_Password_if_you_have_forgotten_it">reset it</a>, which will cause stored credentials to be deleted from Thunderbird.</div>
<p><img alt="tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-12-29-50-749867.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p>
<h1 id="w_set-a-primary-password">Set a Primary Password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Put a checkmark next to <strong>Use a Primary Password</strong>.<br><br><img alt="tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-09-05-dfd94c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> The <em>Change Primary Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( )
</li><li> Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only <em>you yourself</em> will be able to see your saved passwords.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br><br><img alt="tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-59-36-393ff3.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> To set your Primary Password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_change-your-primary-password">Change your Primary Password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Click the button <span class="button">Change Primary Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new Primary Password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_remove-your-primary-password">Remove your Primary Password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to <em>Change your Primary Password</em>, but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.
</p>
<h1 id="w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Developer Tools</span> &gt; Click the <span class="button">OK</span> button in the <strong>Incoming Connection</strong> dialog (keyboard shortcut:<span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span>, <span class="key">Command</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span> on macOS) to open the error console.
</li><li> Copy the text given below and paste it into the console.<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button. If you do not see an <em>Evaluate</em> button, press <span class="key">Return</span> or <span class="key">Enter</span> on your keyboard.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the <span class="button">Reset</span> button.<br><br><img alt="tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-15-48-30-8ed674.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "346087", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 8, 2026, 8:36:23 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-08T08:36:23.000248-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Wayne Mery", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "backed out feature removal information, add warning about forgetting a primary password", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 8, 2026, 8:47:52 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-08T08:47:52.618390-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Aug 8, 2026, 8:47:52 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-08T08:47:52.618390-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:06+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (346249,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/346249',346087,'2026-08-10T04:09:06.383064-07:00','Mark Heijl','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/','correcting wikilink and minor edits',1,'2026-08-10T04:15:54.979178-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__

{warning}WARNING: ''''''Do not use the Primary Password feature for protecting data stored locally on your computer. A Primary Password protects only authentication information, such as saved passwords - it ''''does not'''' prevent others from seeing your messages, your calendars, your contacts, or other personal information in Thunderbird.''''''<br><br>To block access to locally stored (downloaded) messsages you should use traditional computer protections such as disk encryption, computer accounts, and file system permissions. Create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}macOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}macOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password, do not give  administrator capability and do not give access to your computer account''s file system. (Thunderbird stores mail accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.){/warning}
<!-- [Commented out: Not useful because it''s very easy to reverse this method]<br><br> A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True. This will prevent the display of email until the Primary Password is entered. It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file. Using an operating system account does address that.-->

= What does the Primary Password protect? =
Thunderbird can save passwords and other authentication information for each account in the [[Password Manager - Remember, delete and change passwords in Thunderbird|Password Manager]], so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. 

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords and authentication informatio?  You can set a Primary Password, which must be entered to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. You only need to enter your Primary Password once per Thunderbird session.
{warning}There is no way to recover a forgotten Primary Password. If you forget it, you must [[#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it|reset it]], which will cause stored credentials to be deleted from Thunderbird.{/warning}
;[[Image:tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password]]

= Set a Primary Password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Put a checkmark next to ''''''Use a Primary Password''''''.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password]]
# The ''''Change Primary Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>
#* Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only ''''you yourself'''' will be able to see your saved passwords.
# The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog]]
# To set your Primary Password, click {button OK}.
Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Change your Primary Password =
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Click the button {button Change Primary Password}.
# Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new Primary Password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Remove your Primary Password =
Follow the steps above to ''''Change your Primary Password'''', but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.

= Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it =
If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.{/warning}
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Tools} > {menu Developer Tools} > Click the {button OK} button in the ''''''Incoming Connection'''''' dialog (keyboard shortcut:{key Ctrl+Shift+J}, {key Command+Shift+J} on macOS) to open the error console.
# Copy the text given below and paste it into the console.<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
# Click the {button Evaluate} button. If you do not see an ''''Evaluate'''' button, press {key Return} or {key Enter} on your keyboard.
# When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the {button Reset} button.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog]]','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">What does the Primary Password protect?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_set-a-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Set a Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_change-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Change your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_remove-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Remove your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</span></a></li></ul></div>
<div class="warning">WARNING: <strong>Do not use the Primary Password feature for protecting data stored locally on your computer. A Primary Password protects only authentication information, such as saved passwords - it <em>does not</em> prevent others from seeing your messages, your calendars, your contacts, or other personal information in Thunderbird.</strong><br><br>To block access to locally stored (downloaded) messsages you should use traditional computer protections such as disk encryption, computer accounts, and file system permissions. Create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password, do not give  administrator capability and do not give access to your computer account''s file system. (Thunderbird stores mail accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.)</div>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect">What does the Primary Password protect?</h1>
<p>Thunderbird can save passwords and other authentication information for each account in the <a href="/en-US/kb/password-manager-remember-delete-change-tb">Password Manager</a>, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. 
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords and authentication informatio?  You can set a Primary Password, which must be entered to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. You only need to enter your Primary Password once per Thunderbird session.
</p>
<div class="warning">There is no way to recover a forgotten Primary Password. If you forget it, you must <a href="#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it">reset it</a>, which will cause stored credentials to be deleted from Thunderbird.</div>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-12-29-50-749867.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<h1 id="w_set-a-primary-password">Set a Primary Password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Put a checkmark next to <strong>Use a Primary Password</strong>.<br><br><img alt="tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-09-05-dfd94c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> The <em>Change Primary Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( )
</li><li> Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only <em>you yourself</em> will be able to see your saved passwords.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br><br><img alt="tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-59-36-393ff3.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> To set your Primary Password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_change-your-primary-password">Change your Primary Password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Click the button <span class="button">Change Primary Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new Primary Password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_remove-your-primary-password">Remove your Primary Password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to <em>Change your Primary Password</em>, but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.
</p>
<h1 id="w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Developer Tools</span> &gt; Click the <span class="button">OK</span> button in the <strong>Incoming Connection</strong> dialog (keyboard shortcut:<span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span>, <span class="key">Command</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span> on macOS) to open the error console.
</li><li> Copy the text given below and paste it into the console.<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button. If you do not see an <em>Evaluate</em> button, press <span class="key">Return</span> or <span class="key">Enter</span> on your keyboard.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the <span class="button">Reset</span> button.<br><br><img alt="tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-15-48-30-8ed674.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "346249", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 10, 2026, 4:09:06 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-10T04:09:06.383064-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Mark Heijl", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "correcting wikilink and minor edits", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 10, 2026, 4:15:54 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-10T04:15:54.979178-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:04+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (347603,'en-US','tb-custom-oauth','Custom OAuth for Thunderbird','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth/revision/347603',340688,'2026-08-17T00:12:18.762542-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','WIP, do not approve or translate: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird. See [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird]] for an overview.

{note}NOTE: For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, please ignore this article.{/note}
__TOC__
[[UI:details_start]]
==OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations)==
Who is this for?
* Organizations
* Universities
* IT administrators
* Third-party provider developers
Thunderbird provides a WebExtension API that allows add-ons to register OAuth2 providers.
https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/oauthProvider.html
An add-on can provide OAuth2 configuration including:
*Client ID
*Authorization endpoint
*Token endpoint
*Scopes
*PKCE settings
*Browser flow settings
This allows organizations to package their OAuth2 configuration once and deploy it to many users.
The API now supports two deployment models:
#Registering OAuth2 providers for new mail services that Thunderbird doesn''t already know about.
#Overriding Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 configuration for selected email domains or entire hostnames. This allows an organization to replace Thunderbird''s built-in configuration only where required, while continuing to use the built-in provider for everyone else.
For example, an organization using Microsoft 365 with its own Azure application registration can transparently replace Thunderbird''s built-in Microsoft OAuth2 configuration for users in its own email domain, while all other Microsoft 365 users continue using Thunderbird''s built-in configuration.

Thunderbird also displays when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension, including the name of the extension, so users can easily identify where their configuration is coming from.
===Administrator workflow===
Organizations that wish to deploy a common OAuth2 configuration to many users can package it as a Thunderbird WebExtension.
====1. Create the extension manifest====
Create a ''''''manifest.json'''''' containing the OAuth provider registration and any required OAuth2 settings (client ID, authorization endpoint, token endpoint, scopes, PKCE settings, browser flow settings, hostname registrations, email domain overrides, etc.).
[[UI:details_start]]
====Example manifest.json====
<code>
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Example Microsoft 365 OAuth Provider",
  "version": "1.0",
  "applications": {
    "gecko": {
      "id": "oauth-provider@example.org",
      "strict_min_version": "155.0"
    }
  },
  "oauth_provider": {
    "issuer": "this.must.be.unique",
    "clientId": "your-client-id-here-0000-000000000000",
    "clientSecret": "",
    "issuerIdentifier": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/v2.0",
    "authorizationEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/authorize",
    "tokenEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/token",
    "redirectionEndpoint": "https://localhost",
    "usePKCE": true,
    "useExternalBrowser": true,
    "hostnames": [
      "office365.com",
      "outlook.com"
    ],
    "emailDomains": [
      "example.com"
    ],
    "scopes": "https://outlook.office.com/IMAP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/POP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/SMTP.Send https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All offline_access"
  }
}
</code>
[[UI:details_end]]
====2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file.====
* Simply zip the manifest.json file.
* Rename to change the file extension from ".zip" to ".xpi.
====3. Deploy the extension====
Deploy the extension to users using the organization''s preferred software deployment mechanism.
Possible deployment methods include:
* Enterprise Policy (policies.json)
* Windows Group Policy (ADMX templates)
* Microsoft Intune
* macOS Configuration Profiles
* Linux enterprise package deployment
* Manual installation, if appropriate
Thunderbird''s Enterprise Policies already support automatically installing extensions from either local files or URLs, as well as force-installing extensions so users cannot remove them. The newer ExtensionSettings policy also allows administrators to automatically install or force-install a specific extension by ID using an install_url.
<!--
 TODO: Link to the Thunderbird Enterprise Policy documentation for extension deployment.
-->
===4. Install / enable the extension===
Depending on the deployment method, the extension may:
* be installed automatically when Thunderbird starts,
* be force-installed by enterprise policy,
* or be installed manually by the user via the Add-ons Manager.
''''''Once installed, no additional account configuration is normally required.''''''
====User experience====
Once the extension is installed, Thunderbird automatically uses the registered OAuth2 provider whenever an account matches the configured hostname or email-domain rules.

When an extension is supplying the OAuth2 configuration, Thunderbird indicates this in Thunderbird App Menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings}, by displaying the name of the extension that is providing the OAuth2 settings. This allows users and administrators to see which extension is customizing their OAuth configuration.
<!--
TODO: Add screenshot
-->

[[UI:details_end]]

[[UI:details_start]]
==Per-account OAuth2 customization (recommended for advanced users)==
Who is this for?
*Advanced users
*Testing
*Individual account configuration
If only a single account needs custom OAuth2 settings, Thunderbird also supports configuring OAuth2 directly for an individual account.

Unlike the add-on approach, these settings affect only that account and are not intended for organization-wide deployment.

This is currently available for Microsoft Exchange accounts, with support for IMAP currently under development.
[[UI:details_start]]
===Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization===
====General Setup Instructions====
#Create a new account with a Microsoft 365 email address.
#Choose manual configuration.
#Choose the EWS or Graph protocol.
#For the Exchange URL enter:
#*For EWS: https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx
#*For Graph: https://graph.microsoft.com/
#Select OAuth for authentication.
#Username is your email address.
#Choose Advanced Configuration... and accept the resulting dialog.
#In server settings, click the Advanced... button.
#Check the "Override Office365 Settings" check box.
#In Application ID, enter the application ID your organization has set up. Sometimes, it will be called a "Client ID".
#Enter your organization''s Microsoft 365 Tenant ID.
#Optionally set the redirect URI. Default value is http://localhost
#Optionally set the endpoint host. Default value is https://login.microsoftonline.com
#Optionally set the OAuth scopes. Default values are:
#*For EWS: https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All offline_access
#*For Graph: https://graph.microsoft.com/User.Read https://graph.microsoft.com/MailboxFolder.ReadWrite https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.ReadWrite https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.Send offline_access
# Accept the Advanced Account Settings dialog and trigger an email update. Messages should download and the account should operate as normal.
====US Government Compute Cloud (GCC)====
GCC does not include the common Thunderbird application ID, so all GCC users must have an organization that registers a Thunderbird application ID for their tenant IDs. When following the instructions above, use the Application ID and Tenant ID provided to you by your administrator and the following values for the other fields:

*Exchange URL:
**For EWS: https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx
**For Graph: https://graph.microsoft.us/
*Redirect URI: http://localhost
*Endpoint Host: https://login.microsoftonline.us


[[UI:details_end]]
[[UI:details_end]]

[[UI:details_start]]
==Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configuration==
Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required including:
* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/ PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)]
* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/ Dynamic Client Registration (DCR)]
Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configuration automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.
[[UI:details_end]]','<p>This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird. See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth2 for Thunderbird</a> for an overview.
</p>
<div class="note">NOTE: For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, please ignore this article.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_oauth2-provider-add-ons-recommended-for-administrators-and-organizations"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations)</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_administrator-workflow"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Administrator workflow</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_1-create-the-extension-manifest"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">1. Create the extension manifest</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_example-manifest-json"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Example manifest.json</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_2-package-the-extension-into-a-standard-thunderbird-extension-xpi-file"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.3</span> <span class="toctext">2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file.</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_3-deploy-the-extension"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.4</span> <span class="toctext">3. Deploy the extension</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_4-install-enable-the-extension"><span class="tocnumber">1.2</span> <span class="toctext">4. Install / enable the extension</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_user-experience"><span class="tocnumber">1.2.1</span> <span class="toctext">User experience</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_per-account-oauth2-customization-recommended-for-advanced-users"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Per-account OAuth2 customization (recommended for advanced users)</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_per-account-oauth2-microsoft-exchange-customization"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_general-setup-instructions"><span class="tocnumber">2.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">General Setup Instructions</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_us-government-compute-cloud-gcc"><span class="tocnumber">2.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">US Government Compute Cloud (GCC)</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_future-standards-like-pacc-and-dcr-will-eliminate-most-oauth-custom-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configuration</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_oauth2-provider-add-ons-recommended-for-administrators-and-organizations" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations)</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p>Who is this for?
</p><ul><li> Organizations
</li><li> Universities
</li><li> IT administrators
</li><li> Third-party provider developers
</li></ul><p>Thunderbird provides a WebExtension API that allows add-ons to register OAuth2 providers.
<a href="https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/oauthProvider.html">https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/oauthProvider.html</a>
An add-on can provide OAuth2 configuration including:
</p><ul><li>Client ID
</li><li>Authorization endpoint
</li><li>Token endpoint
</li><li>Scopes
</li><li>PKCE settings
</li><li>Browser flow settings
</li></ul><p>This allows organizations to package their OAuth2 configuration once and deploy it to many users.
The API now supports two deployment models:
</p><ol><li>Registering OAuth2 providers for new mail services that Thunderbird doesn''t already know about.
</li><li>Overriding Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 configuration for selected email domains or entire hostnames. This allows an organization to replace Thunderbird''s built-in configuration only where required, while continuing to use the built-in provider for everyone else.
</li></ol><p>For example, an organization using Microsoft 365 with its own Azure application registration can transparently replace Thunderbird''s built-in Microsoft OAuth2 configuration for users in its own email domain, while all other Microsoft 365 users continue using Thunderbird''s built-in configuration.
</p><p>Thunderbird also displays when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension, including the name of the extension, so users can easily identify where their configuration is coming from.
</p><h3 id="w_administrator-workflow">Administrator workflow</h3><p>Organizations that wish to deploy a common OAuth2 configuration to many users can package it as a Thunderbird WebExtension.
</p><h4 id="w_1-create-the-extension-manifest">1. Create the extension manifest</h4><p>Create a <strong>manifest.json</strong> containing the OAuth provider registration and any required OAuth2 settings (client ID, authorization endpoint, token endpoint, scopes, PKCE settings, browser flow settings, hostname registrations, email domain overrides, etc.).
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h4 id="w_example-manifest-json" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-expanded="false">Example manifest.json</button></h4><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-hidden="true"><p><code>
{
</code></p><pre><code> "manifest_version": 2,
 "name": "Example Microsoft 365 OAuth Provider",
 "version": "1.0",
 "applications": {
   "gecko": {
     "id": "oauth-provider@example.org",
     "strict_min_version": "155.0"
   }
 },
 "oauth_provider": {
   "issuer": "this.must.be.unique",
   "clientId": "your-client-id-here-0000-000000000000",
   "clientSecret": "",
   "issuerIdentifier": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/v2.0",
   "authorizationEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/authorize",
   "tokenEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/token",
   "redirectionEndpoint": "https://localhost",
   "usePKCE": true,
   "useExternalBrowser": true,
   "hostnames": [
     "office365.com",
     "outlook.com"
   ],
   "emailDomains": [
     "example.com"
   ],
   "scopes": "https://outlook.office.com/IMAP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/POP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/SMTP.Send https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All offline_access"
 }
</code></pre><code>
</code><p><code>}
</code>
</p></div>
</section><p></p><h4 id="w_2-package-the-extension-into-a-standard-thunderbird-extension-xpi-file">2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file.</h4><ul><li> Simply zip the manifest.json file.
</li><li> Rename to change the file extension from ".zip" to ".xpi.
</li></ul><h4 id="w_3-deploy-the-extension">3. Deploy the extension</h4><p>Deploy the extension to users using the organization''s preferred software deployment mechanism.
Possible deployment methods include:
</p><ul><li> Enterprise Policy (policies.json)
</li><li> Windows Group Policy (ADMX templates)
</li><li> Microsoft Intune
</li><li> macOS Configuration Profiles
</li><li> Linux enterprise package deployment
</li><li> Manual installation, if appropriate
</li></ul><p>Thunderbird''s Enterprise Policies already support automatically installing extensions from either local files or URLs, as well as force-installing extensions so users cannot remove them. The newer ExtensionSettings policy also allows administrators to automatically install or force-install a specific extension by ID using an install_url.

</p><h3 id="w_4-install-enable-the-extension">4. Install / enable the extension</h3><p>Depending on the deployment method, the extension may:
</p><ul><li> be installed automatically when Thunderbird starts,
</li><li> be force-installed by enterprise policy,
</li><li> or be installed manually by the user via the Add-ons Manager.
</li></ul><p><strong>Once installed, no additional account configuration is normally required.</strong>
</p><h4 id="w_user-experience">User experience</h4><p>Once the extension is installed, Thunderbird automatically uses the registered OAuth2 provider whenever an account matches the configured hostname or email-domain rules.
</p><p>When an extension is supplying the OAuth2 configuration, Thunderbird indicates this in Thunderbird App Menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span>, by displaying the name of the extension that is providing the OAuth2 settings. This allows users and administrators to see which extension is customizing their OAuth configuration.

</p><p></p></div>























</section>
<p></p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_per-account-oauth2-customization-recommended-for-advanced-users" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Per-account OAuth2 customization (recommended for advanced users)</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>Who is this for?
</p><ul><li>Advanced users
</li><li>Testing
</li><li>Individual account configuration
</li></ul><p>If only a single account needs custom OAuth2 settings, Thunderbird also supports configuring OAuth2 directly for an individual account.
</p><p>Unlike the add-on approach, these settings affect only that account and are not intended for organization-wide deployment.
</p><p>This is currently available for Microsoft Exchange accounts, with support for IMAP currently under development.
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_per-account-oauth2-microsoft-exchange-customization" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-expanded="false">Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-hidden="true"><h4 id="w_general-setup-instructions">General Setup Instructions</h4><ol><li>Create a new account with a Microsoft 365 email address.
</li><li>Choose manual configuration.
</li><li>Choose the EWS or Graph protocol.
</li><li>For the Exchange URL enter:
<ul><li>For EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li>For Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/">https://graph.microsoft.com/</a>
</li></ul>
</li><li>Select OAuth for authentication.
</li><li>Username is your email address.
</li><li>Choose Advanced Configuration... and accept the resulting dialog.
</li><li>In server settings, click the Advanced... button.
</li><li>Check the "Override Office365 Settings" check box.
</li><li>In Application ID, enter the application ID your organization has set up. Sometimes, it will be called a "Client ID".
</li><li>Enter your organization''s Microsoft 365 Tenant ID.
</li><li>Optionally set the redirect URI. Default value is <a href="http://localhost">http://localhost</a>
</li><li>Optionally set the endpoint host. Default value is <a href="https://login.microsoftonline.com">https://login.microsoftonline.com</a>
</li><li>Optionally set the OAuth scopes. Default values are:
<ul><li>For EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All">https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All</a> offline_access
</li><li>For Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/User.Read">https://graph.microsoft.com/User.Read</a> <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/MailboxFolder.ReadWrite">https://graph.microsoft.com/MailboxFolder.ReadWrite</a> <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.ReadWrite">https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.ReadWrite</a> <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.Send">https://graph.microsoft.com/Mail.Send</a> offline_access
</li></ul>
</li><li> Accept the Advanced Account Settings dialog and trigger an email update. Messages should download and the account should operate as normal.
</li></ol><h4 id="w_us-government-compute-cloud-gcc">US Government Compute Cloud (GCC)</h4><p>GCC does not include the common Thunderbird application ID, so all GCC users must have an organization that registers a Thunderbird application ID for their tenant IDs. When following the instructions above, use the Application ID and Tenant ID provided to you by your administrator and the following values for the other fields:
</p><ul><li>Exchange URL:
<ul><li>For EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li>For Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.us/">https://graph.microsoft.us/</a>
</li></ul>
</li><li>Redirect URI: <a href="http://localhost">http://localhost</a>
</li><li>Endpoint Host: <a href="https://login.microsoftonline.us">https://login.microsoftonline.us</a>
</li></ul><p><br>
</p></div>





</section></div>



</section>
<p></p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_future-standards-like-pacc-and-dcr-will-eliminate-most-oauth-custom-configuration" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-expanded="false">Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configuration</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-hidden="true"><p>Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required including:
</p><ul><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/">PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/">Dynamic Client Registration (DCR)</a>
</li></ul><p>Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configuration automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.
</p></div>


</section>
