Here’s how to get started with Mozilla’s Thundermail service.
<p></p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configure-thundermail-in-thunderbird-for-android"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_automatic-setup-for-thunderbird-18-or-newer"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Automatic Setup for Thunderbird 18 or newer</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_qr-code-setup-for-thunderbird-19-or-newer"><span class="tocnumber">1.2</span> <span class="toctext">QR Code Setup for Thunderbird 19 or newer</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_manual-settings"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Manual Settings</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_receiving-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Receiving email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_sending-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Sending email</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configure-catch-all-thundermail-tb-pro-and-custom-domain-aliases-for-use-with-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, tb.pro and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_set-up-a-thunderbird-identity-for-each-alias-you-want-to-reply-from"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Set up a Thunderbird identity for each alias you want to reply from</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_send-email-with-the-identities-for-your-aliases"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Send email with the identities for your aliases</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_have-some-fun-now-that-email-setup-is-done"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Have some fun now that email setup is done!</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configure-thundermail-in-thunderbird-for-android">Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android</h1>
<h2 id="w_automatic-setup-for-thunderbird-18-or-newer">Automatic Setup for Thunderbird 18 or newer</h2>
<div class="note"> <strong>Note:</strong> Thunderbird Pro requires Thunderbird for Android version 18 or newer (also K-9). We recommend using the latest monthly release version documented in <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android">New in Thunderbird for Android</a>.</div>
<ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add account</span>.
</li><li> Enter your Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> The next screen will prompt you to <strong>Please specify the special folders for your account</strong>, and offer some suggested settings for your Thunderbird special folders. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click <span class="button">Next</span>. 
</li><li> On the next screen, configure your user name under <strong>Your name</strong> and optionally your signature and click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> The next screen has sync and notification options. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_qr-code-setup-for-thunderbird-19-or-newer">QR Code Setup for Thunderbird 19 or newer</h2>
<ul><li> This is faster because you don''t have to type your Thundermail email address.<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> Please ensure Thunderbird is allowed to use the camera in Android settings in order to make QR code scanning work.</div>
</li><li> Please follow the instructions at: <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=How+To+Import+from+Thundermail#w_how-to-import-from-thundermail" title="Page does not exist.">How to Import from Thundermail</a> to import your Thundermail settings into Thunderbird for Android using the QR code on the Thundermail accounts page.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_manual-settings">Manual Settings</h1>
<p>Thunderbird should automatically configure your Thundermail email settings to match your <a href="https://accounts.tb.pro/mail">TB Pro accounts page</a>. If it doesn’t, please manually change the receiving email and sending email settings according to the sections below. If manual setup fails, please create a support ticket using our form at: <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new">https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new</a>.
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_receiving-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Receiving email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</li><li> Under <strong>Accounts</strong>, tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Fetching mail</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Incoming Server</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Server:</strong> <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> <strong>Security:</strong> SSL/TLS <strong>Port:</strong> 993
</li><li><strong>Authentication:</strong> OAuth 2.0
</li><li><strong>Username:</strong> Your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li></ul></div>


<h2 id="w_sending-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Sending email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</li><li> Under <strong>Accounts</strong>, tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Sending mail</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Outgoing server</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li> <strong>Server Name:</strong> <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> <strong>Connection</strong> <strong>Security:</strong> SSL/TLS <strong>Port:</strong> 465
</li><li> <strong>Authentication:</strong> OAuth 2.0
</li><li> <strong>Username:</strong> Your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li></ul><p></p></div>


</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_configure-catch-all-thundermail-tb-pro-and-custom-domain-aliases-for-use-with-thunderbird">Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, <a href="http://tb.pro">tb.pro</a> and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird</h1>
<h2 id="w_set-up-a-thunderbird-identity-for-each-alias-you-want-to-reply-from">Set up a Thunderbird identity for each alias you want to reply from</h2>
<p>The steps are the same for <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias">catch-All</a>, <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases">tb.pro, Thundermail and custom domain aliases</a>, so we will just use the term <strong>alias</strong> to refer to all of these types of aliases in the instructions below:
</p><p>You will have to set up an <strong>identity</strong> in Thunderbird for every alias you want to send email from.
</p><p>For example, if your catch-all is for <code>glamroc.com</code> and you would like to reply as  <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code> in addition to the default alias for <code>glamroc.com</code>, you will have to setup identities for both <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code>.
</p><p>Here''s how:
</p>
<ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>.
</li><li> Tap on your primary Thundermail email account (the email address you use to log in to Thundermail), for example <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> &gt; <span class="menu">Sending mail</span>. 
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Manage identities</span> &gt; <span class="menu">⋮</span> (also known as the <em>kebab</em> menu because the three vertical dots evoke a kebab) &gt; <span class="menu">New identity</span>.
</li><li> Enter the desired alias in the <strong>Email address</strong> field. For example: <code>alice@glamroc.com</code><br>All other fields are optional, but we recommend you enter a memorable shorter name in the <strong>Your name</strong> field.
</li><li> Save the identity by tapping <span class="button">SAVE</span>. When replying, you can now change the <strong>From</strong> field to this identity by tapping on it.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_send-email-with-the-identities-for-your-aliases">Send email with the identities for your aliases</h2>
<ol><li> When composing or replying, tap on the email address in the  <strong>From</strong> field.
</li><li> Tap on the relevant identity for the alias you wish to send from.
</li><li> Write and send the email as usual.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_have-some-fun-now-that-email-setup-is-done">Have some fun now that email setup is done!</h1>
<p>Congratulations, you have successfully set up Thundermail with Thunderbird for Android! Next:
</p>
<ul><li> Send and receive email with Thundermail.
</li><li> New to Thunderbird for Android? Then check out <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-android">Getting started with Thunderbird for Android</a>.
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "348421", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 21, 2026, 6:34:36 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-21T06:34:36.725658-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Mozinet", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Mozinet/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Form-only edits, the removal of periods after email addresses to prevent ambiguity, Latin abbreviation (see guidelines), cosmetic changes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 21, 2026, 8:05:23 PM", "datetime": "2026-08-21T20:05:23.504563-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Aug 21, 2026, 8:05:32 PM", "datetime": "2026-08-21T20:05:32.610380-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:19:11+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (348669,'en-US','protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','Protect your Thunderbird passwords with a Primary Password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/protect-your-thunderbird-passwords-primary-password/revision/348669',346249,'2026-08-25T03:36:18.587073-07:00','Wayne Mery','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/','improve reset password instructions',1,'2026-08-27T11:25:38.200349-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__

{warning}WARNING: ''''''Do not use the Primary Password feature for protecting data stored locally on your computer. A Primary Password protects only authentication information, such as saved passwords - it ''''does not'''' prevent others from seeing your messages, your calendars, your contacts, or other personal information in Thunderbird.''''''<br><br>To block access to locally stored (downloaded) messsages you should use traditional computer protections such as disk encryption, computer accounts, and file system permissions. Create a separate {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}macOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} user account on the operating system for each person, make sure your {for win}Windows{/for}{for mac}macOS{/for}{for linux}OS{/for} account requires a password, do not give  administrator capability and do not give access to your computer account''s file system. (Thunderbird stores mail accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.){/warning}
<!-- [Commented out: Not useful because it''s very easy to reverse this method]<br><br> A weaker method is to change the hidden preference mail.password_protect_local_cache in the [[config editor]] to True. This will prevent the display of email until the Primary Password is entered. It does not however do anything about your mail being stored on the hard disk as a plain text file. Using an operating system account does address that.-->

= What does the Primary Password protect? =
Thunderbird can save passwords and other authentication information for each account in the [[Password Manager - Remember, delete and change passwords in Thunderbird|Password Manager]], so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. 

What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords and authentication informatio?  You can set a Primary Password, which must be entered to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. You only need to enter your Primary Password once per Thunderbird session.
{warning}There is no way to recover a forgotten Primary Password. If you forget it, you must [[#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it|reset it]], which will cause stored credentials to be deleted from Thunderbird.{/warning}
;[[Image:tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password]]

= Set a Primary Password =
By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Put a checkmark next to ''''''Use a Primary Password''''''.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password]]
# The ''''Change Primary Password'''' dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
#* Use at least one capital letter.
#* Use one or more digits.
#* Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: <nowiki>@ # $ % ^ & * ( )</nowiki>
#* Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only ''''you yourself'''' will be able to see your saved passwords.
# The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog]]
# To set your Primary Password, click {button OK}.
Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.

= Change your Primary Password =
# [[T:optionspreferences TB]]
# Select the {menu Privacy & Security} section.
# Go to the ''''Passwords'''' subsection.
# Click the button {button Change Primary Password}.
# Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
# Enter the new Primary Password twice.
# Click {button OK}.

= Remove your Primary Password =
Follow the steps above to ''''Change your Primary Password'''', but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.

= Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it =
If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
{warning}''''''Warning:'''''' This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.{/warning}
# Click Thunderbird app menu in the top right of Thunderbird {menu ☰} > {menu Tools} > {menu Developer Tools} (or instead of the menu use keyboard shortcut: {for not mac}{key Ctrl+Shift+J}{/for}{for mac}{key Command+Shift+J}{/for}) to open the error console.
# If you see an ''''''Incoming Connection'''''' dialog, click the {button OK} button.
# Copy the text given below and paste it into the console at ">>".<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
# If you do not see an {button Evaluate} button, press {key Return} or {key Enter} on your keyboard. Otherwise, click the {button Evaluate} button.
# When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the {button Reset} button.<br/><br/>[[Image:tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog]]','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">What does the Primary Password protect?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_set-a-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Set a Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_change-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Change your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_remove-your-primary-password"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Remove your Primary Password</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</span></a></li></ul></div>
<div class="warning">WARNING: <strong>Do not use the Primary Password feature for protecting data stored locally on your computer. A Primary Password protects only authentication information, such as saved passwords - it <em>does not</em> prevent others from seeing your messages, your calendars, your contacts, or other personal information in Thunderbird.</strong><br><br>To block access to locally stored (downloaded) messsages you should use traditional computer protections such as disk encryption, computer accounts, and file system permissions. Create a separate <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> user account on the operating system for each person, make sure your <span class="for" data-for="win" style="display: none;">Windows</span><span class="for" data-for="mac">macOS</span><span class="for" data-for="linux" style="display: none;">OS</span> account requires a password, do not give  administrator capability and do not give access to your computer account''s file system. (Thunderbird stores mail accounts, messages and password information separately for each operating system account.)</div>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_what-does-the-primary-password-protect">What does the Primary Password protect?</h1>
<p>Thunderbird can save passwords and other authentication information for each account in the <a href="/en-US/kb/password-manager-remember-delete-change-tb">Password Manager</a>, so you don''t have to re-enter them when you check for new messages. 
</p><p>What if you share a computer, and don''t want others to see your stored passwords and authentication informatio?  You can set a Primary Password, which must be entered to view or use the passwords you''ve told Thunderbird to remember. You only need to enter your Primary Password once per Thunderbird session.
</p>
<div class="warning">There is no way to recover a forgotten Primary Password. If you forget it, you must <a href="#w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it">reset it</a>, which will cause stored credentials to be deleted from Thunderbird.</div>
<dl><dt><img alt="tb91-password-required-mozilla-thunderbird-please-enter-your-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-12-29-50-749867.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<h1 id="w_set-a-primary-password">Set a Primary Password</h1>
<p>By default, Thunderbird does not use a Primary Password to protect stored credentials. To set a Primary Password:
</p>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Put a checkmark next to <strong>Use a Primary Password</strong>.<br><br><img alt="tb91-preferences-privacy-security-passwords-use-primary-password" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-09-05-dfd94c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> The <em>Change Primary Password</em> dialog will appear. As you enter your Primary Password, the password quality meter will indicate the difficulty of guessing the password. Here are some tips for creating better passwords:
<ul><li> Use at least one capital letter.
</li><li> Use one or more digits.
</li><li> Use at least one non-alphanumeric character, such as: @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( )
</li><li> Make your Primary Password something that is easy for you to remember, but difficult for others to guess, to ensure that only <em>you yourself</em> will be able to see your saved passwords.
</li></ul>
</li><li> The password must be entered twice to make sure that you have entered it correctly as intended.<br><br><img alt="tb91-use-primary-password-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-14-59-36-393ff3.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li><li> To set your Primary Password, click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now that your Primary Password is set, Thunderbird will prompt you to enter it the first time you need to view or use a password per session.
</p>
<h1 id="w_change-your-primary-password">Change your Primary Password</h1>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span>.

</li><li> Select the <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> section.
</li><li> Go to the <em>Passwords</em> subsection.
</li><li> Click the button <span class="button">Change Primary Password</span>.
</li><li> Enter your current Primary Password to confirm you are authorized to change it.
</li><li> Enter the new Primary Password twice.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">OK</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_remove-your-primary-password">Remove your Primary Password</h1>
<p>Follow the steps above to <em>Change your Primary Password</em>, but leave both of the new Primary Password fields empty. However, if you do not know your primary password, then you must use the reset instructions below.
</p>
<h1 id="w_reset-your-primary-password-if-you-have-forgotten-it">Reset your Primary Password if you have forgotten it</h1>
<p>If you''ve forgotten your Primary Password, you can reset it.
</p>
<div class="warning"><strong>Warning:</strong> This will remove all of your saved authentication information. You will need other information sources for your account usernames and passwords.</div>
<ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu in the top right of Thunderbird <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Developer Tools</span> (or instead of the menu use keyboard shortcut: <span class="for" data-for="not mac" style="display: none;"><span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span></span><span class="for" data-for="mac"><span class="key">Command</span> + <span class="key">Shift</span> + <span class="key">J</span></span>) to open the error console.
</li><li> If you see an <strong>Incoming Connection</strong> dialog, click the <span class="button">OK</span> button.
</li><li> Copy the text given below and paste it into the console at "&gt;&gt;".<br><code>openDialog("chrome://pippki/content/resetpassword.xhtml")</code>
</li><li> If you do not see an <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button, press <span class="key">Return</span> or <span class="key">Enter</span> on your keyboard. Otherwise, click the <span class="button">Evaluate</span> button.
</li><li> When prompted with the confirmation dialog box, click the <span class="button">Reset</span> button.<br><br><img alt="tb91-reset-primary-password-dialog" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2022-02-14-15-48-30-8ed674.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "348669", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Aug 25, 2026, 3:36:18 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-25T03:36:18.587073-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Wayne Mery", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "improve reset password instructions", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Aug 27, 2026, 11:25:38 AM", "datetime": "2026-08-27T11:25:38.200349-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:13:01+00:00','M');
