INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350447,'en-US','tb-custom-oauth','Custom OAuth for Thunderbird','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth/revision/350447',348171,'2026-09-02T23:07:45.128625-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','"split into four; the three child articles are submitted separately, please approve those first"  wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218',1,'2026-09-02T23:24:49.440635-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird 155 and newer. See [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird]] for an overview.

{note}''''''NOTE'''''': For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and, AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, you can safely ignore this article.{/note}
__TOC__
= Overview of custom OAuth =

Thunderbird provides several ways for organizations or individuals to use custom OAuth2 configurations, whether you need to configure a single account, deploy organization-specific settings across many users, or integrate with an identity provider that Thunderbird does not support by default.

Administrators can deploy OAuth2 settings centrally using an add-on, while advanced users can configure OAuth2 settings for individual accounts. This article explains the available options and helps you choose the approach that best fits your organization.

Custom OAuth configurations for IMAP and Exchange email accounts can be created with a Thunderbird add-on or by manual configuration without code changes starting in Thunderbird 155. It is possible to do this for any OAuth provider that supports Exchange or IMAP. POP3 support is in progress (currently scheduled for 156 - see [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2061466 bug 2061466]).

If you need help configuring OAuth2 for a specific provider, please contact the provider''s support team to get the configuration details (Thunderbird support cannot provide provider-specific configuration details). Use the information provided by the provider to configure Thunderbird manually or via an add-on.

Custom OAuth is new and there may be issues. If you need help, please do not hesitate to [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird ask for support in the Thunderbird support forum]. For Enterprise deployments, please ask in the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise Thunderbird Enterprise group].

For all the details, please read one or more of the following:

* [[Custom OAuth for end users]]
* [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]]
* [[Custom OAuth for administrators]]

= Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations =

Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required, including:

* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/ PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)]
* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/ DCR (Dynamic Client Registration)]

Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configurations automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.','<p>This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird 155 and newer. See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth2 for Thunderbird</a> for an overview.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTE</strong>: For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and, AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, you can safely ignore this article.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_overview-of-custom-oauth"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Overview of custom OAuth</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_future-standards-like-pacc-and-dcr-will-eliminate-most-oauth-custom-configurations"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_overview-of-custom-oauth">Overview of custom OAuth</h1>
<p>Thunderbird provides several ways for organizations or individuals to use custom OAuth2 configurations, whether you need to configure a single account, deploy organization-specific settings across many users, or integrate with an identity provider that Thunderbird does not support by default.
</p><p>Administrators can deploy OAuth2 settings centrally using an add-on, while advanced users can configure OAuth2 settings for individual accounts. This article explains the available options and helps you choose the approach that best fits your organization.
</p><p>Custom OAuth configurations for IMAP and Exchange email accounts can be created with a Thunderbird add-on or by manual configuration without code changes starting in Thunderbird 155. It is possible to do this for any OAuth provider that supports Exchange or IMAP. POP3 support is in progress (currently scheduled for 156 - see <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2061466">bug 2061466</a>).
</p><p>If you need help configuring OAuth2 for a specific provider, please contact the provider''s support team to get the configuration details (Thunderbird support cannot provide provider-specific configuration details). Use the information provided by the provider to configure Thunderbird manually or via an add-on.
</p><p>Custom OAuth is new and there may be issues. If you need help, please do not hesitate to <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird">ask for support in the Thunderbird support forum</a>. For Enterprise deployments, please ask in the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">Thunderbird Enterprise group</a>.
</p><p>For all the details, please read one or more of the following:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users">Custom OAuth for end users</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-administrators">Custom OAuth for administrators</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_future-standards-like-pacc-and-dcr-will-eliminate-most-oauth-custom-configurations">Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations</h1>
<p>Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required, including:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/">PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/">DCR (Dynamic Client Registration)</a>
</li></ul>
<p>Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configurations automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350447", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:07:45 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:07:45.128625-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "\"split into four; the three child articles are submitted separately, please approve those first\"  wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:24:49 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:24:49.440635-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:41+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350449,'en-US','thunderbird-desktop-and-thundermail','Thunderbird for Desktop and Thundermail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-desktop-and-thundermail','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-desktop-and-thundermail/revision/350449',330154,'2026-09-02T23:21:27.438512-07:00','Mark Heijl','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/','a few instances of Thunderbird Pro > Thundermail and some minor edits',1,'2026-09-04T18:10:20.052453-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,1,'2026-09-04T18:10:20.052453-07:00','rtanglao','approved','[[Template:TBproEarlyBirdInviteOnly]]

__TOC__

=Configure Thundermail for Thunderbird Desktop=
{note}''''''NOTE'''''': Thundermail requires the latest ESR or monthly release. We recommend the latest monthly release version of Thunderbird, which is documented in: [[New in Thunderbird Desktop]].{/note}
# Click the Thunderbird menu {menu ☰} >  {menu Add Account} ({menu New Account} for older versions of Thunderbird) > {menu Email}.
# Enter your Thundermail email address and follow the prompts to set up your Thundermail email account. For versions older than 150, please click the {button Connect} button next to ''''''Thundermail Address Book'''''' and the {button Connect} button next to ''''''Thundermail Calendar'''''' to ensure your Thundermail address book and calendar are added to Thunderbird.
Thunderbird should have automatically added your Thundermail address in [[Configuration Options for Accounts|account settings]], a CalDAV calendar called ''''''Thundermail Calendar'''''' in your Thunderbird calendar [[New in Thunderbird 102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar|space]] (press {for not mac}{key Alt+3}{/for}{for mac}{key Ctrl+3}{/for}), and a CardDAV address book called ''''''Thundermail Address Book'''''' in your address book [[New in Thunderbird 102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar|space]] (press {for not mac}{key Alt+2}{/for}{for mac}{key Ctrl+2}{/for}) to check). If any of those are missing, please try changing the settings to those in the manual settings sections below, and please [https://accounts.tb.pro/contact/ create a Thunderbird Pro support ticket] if you have any issues.

=Manual Settings=
If Thunderbird failed to add the expected items or you need to adjust them, please try manually changing the settings by following the instructions in the sections below. If manual setup fails, please [https://accounts.tb.pro/contact/ create a Thundermail support ticket].

[[UI:details_start]]
==Receiving Email==
# Switch to your Thundermail account: Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰}  > {menu Account Settings}.
# In the left sidebar, click on your Thundermail account. For example: ''''''nemo@thundermail<!---->.com''''''. Then, click on the chevron “&gt;” to expand the drop down menu. Finally, click on {menu Server Settings} and set the following values:
* ''''''Server Name:'''''' [http://mail.thundermail.com mail.thundermail.com]
* ''''''Server Port:'''''' 993
* ''''''Security:'''''' SSL/TLS
* ''''''Username:'''''' your full Thundermail address e.g. ''''''nemo@thundermail<!---->.com''''''
* ''''''Authentication method:'''''' OAuth2

==Sending Email==
# Switch to your Thundermail account: Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings}.
# In the left sidebar, click on your Thundermail account. For example ''''''nemo@thundermail<!---->.com''''''. Click on {button Edit outgoing server…} and set the following values:
* ''''''Server Name:'''''' mail.thundermail.com
* ''''''Server Port:'''''' 465
* ''''''Connection Security:'''''' SSL/TLS
* ''''''Username:'''''' your full Thundermail address For example: ''''''nemo@thundermail<!---->.com''''''
* ''''''Authentication method:'''''' OAuth2

==Thundermail Calendar==
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} ({menu New Account} for older versions of Thunderbird) > {menu Calendar} > {button On the Network}.
# For ''''''Username:'''''', enter your Thundermail address. For example ''''''nemo@thundermail<!---->.com''''''.
# For ''''''Location'''''', enter ''''''mail.thundermail.com''''''.
# Click {button Find Calendars}.
# In the ''''''Create New Calendar'''''' dialog, select your Thundermail Calendar and click {button Subscribe}.

==Thundermail Address Book==
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰}  > {menu Add Account} ({menu New Account} for older versions of Thunderbird) > {menu Address Book} ({menu New Address Book} for older versions of Thunderbird) > {menu Add remote Address Book} ({button New remote Address Book} for older versions of Thunderbird).
#  For ''''''Username:'''''', enter your Thundermail email address. For example ''''''nemo@thundermail<!---->.com''''''.
# For ''''''URL/Hostname'''''', enter ''''''https://mail.thundermail.com''''''.
# Click {button Continue}.
# Select your Thundermail Address Book and click {button Continue}.

[[UI:details_end]]

=Custom Domain catch-all and custom aliases=
How to setup Thunderbird Desktop for [https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51253809827347-Custom-Domains-and-DNS-Configuration custom domains] in Thundermail:

==Send and receive email with Thundermail custom domain aliases using Thunderbird identities==
For every [https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases alias] you create in Thundermail, you will have to create a new [[Using Identities|identity]] in Thunderbird in order to send email with that alias. Here''s how using our example custom domain <code>glamroc.com</code>, our example Thundermail address of <code>roc@thundermail.com</code>, and custom domain alias of <code>roc@glamroc.com</code>:
# Click the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > Click on the thundermail.com account in the left sidebar, which in our example is: <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> > {button Manage Identities…"} > {button Add…}.
# Add your alias e.g. <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by filling in the ''''''New Identity'''''' form, and then click {button OK} when you are done.
# If you want this to be the default identity when composing new email, click on the new identity, and then click on {button Set Default} and {button Close}.
Now the custom domain alias <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> is the default identity when writing new email, and you can send and receive email on both <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> and <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by selecting the appropriate identity.

==Send and reply to email using a Thundermail catch-all alias==
* If you have a [https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias catch-all alias configured in Thundermail for a custom domain], here''s how to use  Thunderbird ''''''custom from address'''''' feature to reply with whatever email address on your custom domain that you want:
*# When composing or replying to an email, click on the email address next to ''''''From''''''.
*# Click {button Customize From Address…}. 
*# Finally, type in an email address for the custom domain you created the catch-all in.

=Send Thundermail, use your Thundermail Calendar and Thundermail Address Book, and have some fun!=
* [[Send and receive messages in Thunderbird|Send and receive email]] with Thundermail. If you have large files, you can also use [https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235 ''''''Thunderbird Send'''''']. which is included with your Thundermail subscription.
* Enjoy your Thundermail Calendar: [https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird/email-and-messaging/calendar Create some events, invite people to invites, etc].
* Use your Thundermail Address Book for [[Addressing an Email|composing email]], managing your contacts, etc.
* New to Thunderbird? Check out [[Get started with Thunderbird]].','<p></p><div class="warning">NOTE: Thundermail (formerly known as Thunderbird Pro) is currently in its Early Bird release and available  by invitation only. To learn more and join the waitlist, visit <a href="https://tb.pro/">our website</a>. We’ll be opening Thundermail to the public once the Early Bird release concludes.</div>
<p></p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configure-thundermail-for-thunderbird-desktop"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Thundermail for Thunderbird Desktop</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_manual-settings"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Manual Settings</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_receiving-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Receiving Email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_sending-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Sending Email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thundermail-calendar"><span class="tocnumber">2.3</span> <span class="toctext">Thundermail Calendar</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_thundermail-address-book"><span class="tocnumber">2.4</span> <span class="toctext">Thundermail Address Book</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_custom-domain-catch-all-and-custom-aliases"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Custom Domain catch-all and custom aliases</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_send-and-receive-email-with-thundermail-custom-domain-aliases-using-thunderbird-identities"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Send and receive email with Thundermail custom domain aliases using Thunderbird identities</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_send-and-reply-to-email-using-a-thundermail-catch-all-alias"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Send and reply to email using a Thundermail catch-all alias</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_send-thundermail-use-your-thundermail-calendar-and-thundermail-address-book-and-have-some-fun"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Send Thundermail, use your Thundermail Calendar and Thundermail Address Book, and have some fun!</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configure-thundermail-for-thunderbird-desktop">Configure Thundermail for Thunderbird Desktop</h1>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTE</strong>: Thundermail requires the latest ESR or monthly release. We recommend the latest monthly release version of Thunderbird, which is documented in: <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop">New in Thunderbird Desktop</a>.</div>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt;  <span class="menu">Add Account</span> (<span class="menu">New Account</span> for older versions of Thunderbird) &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> Enter your Thundermail email address and follow the prompts to set up your Thundermail email account. For versions older than 150, please click the <span class="button">Connect</span> button next to <strong>Thundermail Address Book</strong> and the <span class="button">Connect</span> button next to <strong>Thundermail Calendar</strong> to ensure your Thundermail address book and calendar are added to Thunderbird.
</li></ol>
<p>Thunderbird should have automatically added your Thundermail address in <a href="/en-US/kb/configuration-options-accounts">account settings</a>, a CalDAV calendar called <strong>Thundermail Calendar</strong> in your Thunderbird calendar <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar">space</a> (press <span class="for" data-for="not mac" style="display: none;"><span class="key">Alt</span> + <span class="key">3</span></span><span class="for" data-for="mac"><span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">3</span></span>), and a CardDAV address book called <strong>Thundermail Address Book</strong> in your address book <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-102#w_take-control-of-your-personal-information-new-spaces-toolbar">space</a> (press <span class="for" data-for="not mac" style="display: none;"><span class="key">Alt</span> + <span class="key">2</span></span><span class="for" data-for="mac"><span class="key">Ctrl</span> + <span class="key">2</span></span>) to check). If any of those are missing, please try changing the settings to those in the manual settings sections below, and please <a href="https://accounts.tb.pro/contact/">create a Thunderbird Pro support ticket</a> if you have any issues.
</p>
<h1 id="w_manual-settings">Manual Settings</h1>
<p>If Thunderbird failed to add the expected items or you need to adjust them, please try manually changing the settings by following the instructions in the sections below. If manual setup fails, please <a href="https://accounts.tb.pro/contact/">create a Thundermail support ticket</a>.
</p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_receiving-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Receiving Email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Switch to your Thundermail account: Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span>  &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span>.
</li><li> In the left sidebar, click on your Thundermail account. For example: <strong>nemo@thundermail.com</strong>. Then, click on the chevron “&gt;” to expand the drop down menu. Finally, click on <span class="menu">Server Settings</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li> <strong>Server Name:</strong> <a href="http://mail.thundermail.com">mail.thundermail.com</a>
</li><li> <strong>Server Port:</strong> 993
</li><li> <strong>Security:</strong> SSL/TLS
</li><li> <strong>Username:</strong> your full Thundermail address e.g. <strong>nemo@thundermail.com</strong>
</li><li> <strong>Authentication method:</strong> OAuth2
</li></ul></div>


<h2 id="w_sending-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Sending Email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Switch to your Thundermail account: Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span>.
</li><li> In the left sidebar, click on your Thundermail account. For example <strong>nemo@thundermail.com</strong>. Click on <span class="button">Edit outgoing server…</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li> <strong>Server Name:</strong> <a href="http://mail.thundermail.com">mail.thundermail.com</a>
</li><li> <strong>Server Port:</strong> 465
</li><li> <strong>Connection Security:</strong> SSL/TLS
</li><li> <strong>Username:</strong> your full Thundermail address For example: <strong>nemo@thundermail.com</strong>
</li><li> <strong>Authentication method:</strong> OAuth2
</li></ul></div>


<h2 id="w_thundermail-calendar" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-expanded="false">Thundermail Calendar</button></h2>
<ol class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-hidden="true"><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> (<span class="menu">New Account</span> for older versions of Thunderbird) &gt; <span class="menu">Calendar</span> &gt; <span class="button">On the Network</span>.
</li><li> For <strong>Username:</strong>, enter your Thundermail address. For example <strong>nemo@thundermail.com</strong>.
</li><li> For <strong>Location</strong>, enter <strong><a href="http://mail.thundermail.com">mail.thundermail.com</a></strong>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Find Calendars</span>.
</li><li> In the <strong>Create New Calendar</strong> dialog, select your Thundermail Calendar and click <span class="button">Subscribe</span>.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_thundermail-address-book" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-expanded="false">Thundermail Address Book</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-3" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span>  &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> (<span class="menu">New Account</span> for older versions of Thunderbird) &gt; <span class="menu">Address Book</span> (<span class="menu">New Address Book</span> for older versions of Thunderbird) &gt; <span class="menu">Add remote Address Book</span> (<span class="button">New remote Address Book</span> for older versions of Thunderbird).
</li><li>  For <strong>Username:</strong>, enter your Thundermail email address. For example <strong>nemo@thundermail.com</strong>.
</li><li> For <strong>URL/Hostname</strong>, enter <strong><a href="https://mail.thundermail.com">https://mail.thundermail.com</a></strong>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Continue</span>.
</li><li> Select your Thundermail Address Book and click <span class="button">Continue</span>.
</li></ol><p></p></div>

</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_custom-domain-catch-all-and-custom-aliases">Custom Domain catch-all and custom aliases</h1>
<p>How to setup Thunderbird Desktop for <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51253809827347-Custom-Domains-and-DNS-Configuration">custom domains</a> in Thundermail:
</p>
<h2 id="w_send-and-receive-email-with-thundermail-custom-domain-aliases-using-thunderbird-identities">Send and receive email with Thundermail custom domain aliases using Thunderbird identities</h2>
<p>For every <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases">alias</a> you create in Thundermail, you will have to create a new <a href="/en-US/kb/using-identities">identity</a> in Thunderbird in order to send email with that alias. Here''s how using our example custom domain <code>glamroc.com</code>, our example Thundermail address of <code>roc@thundermail.com</code>, and custom domain alias of <code>roc@glamroc.com</code>:
</p>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; Click on the <a href="http://thundermail.com">thundermail.com</a> account in the left sidebar, which in our example is: <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> &gt; <span class="button">Manage Identities…"</span> &gt; <span class="button">Add…</span>.
</li><li> Add your alias e.g. <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by filling in the <strong>New Identity</strong> form, and then click <span class="button">OK</span> when you are done.
</li><li> If you want this to be the default identity when composing new email, click on the new identity, and then click on <span class="button">Set Default</span> and <span class="button">Close</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now the custom domain alias <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> is the default identity when writing new email, and you can send and receive email on both <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> and <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by selecting the appropriate identity.
</p>
<h2 id="w_send-and-reply-to-email-using-a-thundermail-catch-all-alias">Send and reply to email using a Thundermail catch-all alias</h2>
<ul><li> If you have a <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias">catch-all alias configured in Thundermail for a custom domain</a>, here''s how to use  Thunderbird <strong>custom from address</strong> feature to reply with whatever email address on your custom domain that you want:
<ol><li> When composing or replying to an email, click on the email address next to <strong>From</strong>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Customize From Address…</span>. 
</li><li> Finally, type in an email address for the custom domain you created the catch-all in.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_send-thundermail-use-your-thundermail-calendar-and-thundermail-address-book-and-have-some-fun">Send Thundermail, use your Thundermail Calendar and Thundermail Address Book, and have some fun!</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">Send and receive email</a> with Thundermail. If you have large files, you can also use <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235"><strong>Thunderbird Send</strong></a>. which is included with your Thundermail subscription.
</li><li> Enjoy your Thundermail Calendar: <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird/email-and-messaging/calendar">Create some events, invite people to invites, etc</a>.
</li><li> Use your Thundermail Address Book for <a href="/en-US/kb/addressing-email">composing email</a>, managing your contacts, etc.
</li><li> New to Thunderbird? Check out <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird">Get started with Thunderbird</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350449", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:21:27 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:21:27.438512-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Mark Heijl", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "a few instances of Thunderbird Pro > Thundermail and some minor edits", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 4, 2026, 6:10:20 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-04T18:10:20.052453-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 4, 2026, 6:10:20 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-04T18:10:20.052453-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:18:51+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350450,'en-US','custom-oauth-end-users','Custom OAuth for end users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users/revision/350450',NULL,'2026-09-02T23:22:25.520359-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218',1,'2026-09-02T23:22:49.027777-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__

End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are two examples:

= Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange =

When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:

# Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email}.
# Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
# Click ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom left. (''''''NOTE'''''': Do not click {button Continue}, because if you do, your system’s web browser will open, which is not what you want. If you do this, please start over again at step 1.)
# In the middle, click  {button Microsoft} > {button Set up account}.
# Type your service URL in the ''''''Enter the service URL'''''' field and then click on {button Find settings}. Here are some common service URLs:
#* [https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx]
#* for Government High GCC, ask your administrator. It may be, for EWS: [https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx] and for Graph: https://graph.microsoft.us/
#* For China, ask your administrator.
# Select ''''''Authentication Method''''''  {menu OAuth2}.
# Uncheck ''''''Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings''''''.
# Enter your custom ''''''Tenant ID'''''' and ''''''AppID'''''' in the relevant fields and then click {button Connect}.

= Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration? =

The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.

If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.

This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.

See [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]] for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.

See [[Custom OAuth for Thunderbird]] for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p>End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are two examples:
</p>
<h1 id="w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</h1>
<p>When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:
</p>
<ol><li> Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
</li><li> Click <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom left. (<strong>NOTE</strong>: Do not click <span class="button">Continue</span>, because if you do, your system’s web browser will open, which is not what you want. If you do this, please start over again at step 1.)
</li><li> In the middle, click  <span class="button">Microsoft</span> &gt; <span class="button">Set up account</span>.
</li><li> Type your service URL in the <strong>Enter the service URL</strong> field and then click on <span class="button">Find settings</span>. Here are some common service URLs:
<ul><li> <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> for Government High GCC, ask your administrator. It may be, for EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a> and for Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.us/">https://graph.microsoft.us/</a>
</li><li> For China, ask your administrator.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Select <strong>Authentication Method</strong>  <span class="menu">OAuth2</span>.
</li><li> Uncheck <strong>Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings</strong>.
</li><li> Enter your custom <strong>Tenant ID</strong> and <strong>AppID</strong> in the relevant fields and then click <span class="button">Connect</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</h1>
<p>The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.
</p><p>If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.
</p><p>This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a> for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth">Custom OAuth for Thunderbird</a> for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350450", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:22:25 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:22:25.520359-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:22:49 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:22:49.027777-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:38+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350451,'en-US','custom-oauth-administrators','Custom OAuth for administrators','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-administrators','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-administrators/revision/350451',NULL,'2026-09-02T23:29:24.800407-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip! do not translate please! discussion at: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/1218',1,'2026-09-02T23:30:00.559837-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__
= OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations) =

Who is this for?

* Organizations
* Universities
* IT administrators
* Third-party provider developers

Thunderbird provides a WebExtension API that allows add-ons to register OAuth2 providers. Please see: [https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/oauthProvider.html oauthProvider API].

An add-on can provide OAuth2 configuration including:

* Client ID
* Authorization endpoint
* Token endpoint
* Scopes
* PKCE settings
* Browser flow settings

This allows organizations to package their OAuth2 configuration once and deploy it to many users. The API now supports two deployment models:

# Registering OAuth2 providers for new mail services that Thunderbird doesn''t already know about.
# Overriding Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 configuration for selected email domains or entire hostnames. This allows an organization to replace Thunderbird''s built-in configuration only where required while continuing to use the built-in provider for everyone else.

For example, an organization using Microsoft 365 with its own Azure application registration can transparently replace Thunderbird''s built-in Microsoft OAuth2 configuration for users in its own email domain while all other Microsoft 365 users continue using Thunderbird''s built-in configuration.

Thunderbird also displays when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension, including the name of the extension, so users can easily identify where their configuration is coming from.

== Administrator workflow ==

Organizations that wish to deploy a common OAuth2 configuration to many users can package it as a Thunderbird WebExtension:

=== 1. Create the extension manifest ===

Create a {filepath manifest.json} file containing the OAuth provider registration and any required OAuth2 settings (client ID, authorization endpoint, token endpoint, scopes, PKCE settings, browser flow settings, hostname registrations, email domain overrides, etc.).
[[UI:details_start]]
=== Example manifest.json: ===
<code>
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Example Microsoft 365 OAuth Provider",
  "version": "1.0",
  "applications": {
    "gecko": {
      "id": "oauth-provider@example.org",
      "strict_min_version": "155.0"
    }
  },
  "oauth_provider": {
    "issuer": "this.must.be.unique",
    "clientId": "your-client-id-here-0000-000000000000",
    "clientSecret": "",
    "issuerIdentifier": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/v2.0",
    "authorizationEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/authorize",
    "tokenEndpoint": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/your-tenant-id-0000-0000-000000000000/oauth2/v2.0/token",
    "redirectionEndpoint": "https://localhost",
    "usePKCE": true,
    "useExternalBrowser": true,
    "hostnames": [
      "office365.com",
      "outlook.com"
    ],
    "emailDomains": [
      "example.com"
    ],
    "scopes": "https://outlook.office.com/IMAP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/POP.AccessAsUser.All https://outlook.office.com/SMTP.Send https://outlook.office.com/EWS.AccessAsUser.All offline_access"
  }
}
</code>
[[UI:details_end]]
=== 2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file ===

* Zip the manifest.json file.
* Rename the file extension from ".zip" to ".xpi.

=== 3. Deploy the extension ===

Deploy the extension to users using the organization''s preferred software deployment mechanism. Possible deployment methods include:

* Enterprise Policy ({filepath policies.json})
* Windows Group Policy (ADMX templates)
* Microsoft Intune
* macOS configuration profiles
* Linux enterprise package deployment
* Manual installation, if appropriate

Thunderbird''s Enterprise Policies already support automatically installing extensions from either local files or URLs, as well as force-installing extensions so users cannot remove them. The newer ''''''ExtensionSettings'''''' policy also allows administrators to automatically install or force-install a specific extension by ID using an install_url.
<!--
 TODO: Link to the Thunderbird Enterprise Policy documentation for extension deployment.
-->
=== 4. Install / enable the extension ===

Depending on the deployment method, the extension may be:

* installed automatically when Thunderbird starts
* force-installed by enterprise policy
* installed manually by the user via the Add-ons Manager

''''''Once installed, no additional account configuration is normally required.''''''

=== User experience ===

Once the extension is installed, Thunderbird automatically uses the registered OAuth2 provider whenever an account matches the configured hostname or email/domain rules.

Thunderbird displays an indicator when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension. The indicator includes the name of the extension being used so users can easily identify which extension, if any, is supplying their configuration. This allows users and administrators to see which extension is customizing their OAuth configuration.
<!--
TODO: Add screenshot of the indicator
-->

See [[Custom OAuth for Thunderbird]] for other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_oauth2-provider-add-ons-recommended-for-administrators-and-organizations"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations)</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_administrator-workflow"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Administrator workflow</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_1-create-the-extension-manifest"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">1. Create the extension manifest</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_example-manifest-json"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Example manifest.json:</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_2-package-the-extension-into-a-standard-thunderbird-extension-xpi-file"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.3</span> <span class="toctext">2. Package the extension into a standard Thunderbird extension (.xpi) file</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_3-deploy-the-extension"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.4</span> <span class="toctext">3. Deploy the extension</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_4-install-enable-the-extension"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.5</span> <span class="toctext">4. Install / enable the extension</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_user-experience"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.6</span> <span class="toctext">User experience</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_oauth2-provider-add-ons-recommended-for-administrators-and-organizations">OAuth2 provider add-ons (recommended for administrators and organizations)</h1>
<p>Who is this for?
</p>
<ul><li> Organizations
</li><li> Universities
</li><li> IT administrators
</li><li> Third-party provider developers
</li></ul>
<p>Thunderbird provides a WebExtension API that allows add-ons to register OAuth2 providers. Please see: <a href="https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/oauthProvider.html">oauthProvider API</a>.
</p><p>An add-on can provide OAuth2 configuration including:
</p>
<ul><li> Client ID
</li><li> Authorization endpoint
</li><li> Token endpoint
</li><li> Scopes
</li><li> PKCE settings
</li><li> Browser flow settings
</li></ul>
<p>This allows organizations to package their OAuth2 configuration once and deploy it to many users. The API now supports two deployment models:
</p>
<ol><li> Registering OAuth2 providers for new mail services that Thunderbird doesn''t already know about.
</li><li> Overriding Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 configuration for selected email domains or entire hostnames. This allows an organization to replace Thunderbird''s built-in configuration only where required while continuing to use the built-in provider for everyone else.
</li></ol>
<p>For example, an organization using Microsoft 365 with its own Azure application registration can transparently replace Thunderbird''s built-in Microsoft OAuth2 configuration for users in its own email domain while all other Microsoft 365 users continue using Thunderbird''s built-in configuration.
</p><p>Thunderbird also displays when OAuth2 settings are being provided by an extension, including the name of the extension, so users can easily identify where their configuration is coming from.
</p>
<h2 id="w_administrator-workflow">Administrator workflow</h2>
<p>Organizations that wish to deploy a common OAuth2 configuration to many users can package it as a Thunderbird WebExtension:
</p>
<h3 id="w_1-create-the-extension-manifest">1. Create the extension manifest</h3>
<p>Create a <span class="filepath">manifest.json</span> file containing the OAuth provider registration and any required OAuth2 settings (client ID, authorization endpoint, token endpoint, scopes, PKCE settings, browser flow settings, hostname registrations, email domain overrides, etc.).
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_example-manifest-json" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Example manifest.json:</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p><code>
{
</code></p><pre><code> "manifest_version": 2,
 "name": "Example Microsoft 365 OAuth Provider",
