INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350456,'en-US','tb-custom-oauth','Custom OAuth for Thunderbird','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth/revision/350456',350447,'2026-09-02T23:50:58.610058-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218',1,'2026-09-02T23:51:10.192624-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird 155 and newer. See [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird]] for an overview.

{note}''''''NOTE'''''': For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and, AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, you can safely ignore this article.{/note}
__TOC__
= Overview of custom OAuth =

Thunderbird provides several ways for organizations or individuals to use custom OAuth2 configurations, whether you need to configure a single account, deploy organization-specific settings across many users, or integrate with an identity provider that Thunderbird does not support by default.

Administrators can deploy OAuth2 settings centrally using an add-on, while advanced users can configure OAuth2 settings for individual accounts. This article explains the available options and helps you choose the approach that best fits your organization.

Custom OAuth configurations for IMAP and Exchange email accounts can be created with a Thunderbird add-on or by manual configuration without code changes starting in Thunderbird 155. It is possible to do this for any OAuth provider that supports Exchange or IMAP. POP3 support is in progress (currently scheduled for 156 - see [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2061466 bug 2061466]).

If you need help configuring OAuth2 for a specific provider, please contact the provider''s support team to get the configuration details (Thunderbird support cannot provide provider-specific configuration details). Use the information provided by the provider to configure Thunderbird manually or via an add-on.

Custom OAuth is new and there may be issues. If you need help, please do not hesitate to [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird ask for support in the Thunderbird support forum]. For Enterprise deployments, please ask in the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise Thunderbird Enterprise group].

For all the details, please read one or more of the following:
* [[Custom OAuth for end users]]
* [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]]
* [[Custom OAuth for administrators]]

= Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations =

Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required, including:

* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/ PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)]
* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/ DCR (Dynamic Client Registration)]

Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configurations automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.','<p>This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird 155 and newer. See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth2 for Thunderbird</a> for an overview.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTE</strong>: For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and, AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, you can safely ignore this article.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_overview-of-custom-oauth"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Overview of custom OAuth</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_future-standards-like-pacc-and-dcr-will-eliminate-most-oauth-custom-configurations"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_overview-of-custom-oauth">Overview of custom OAuth</h1>
<p>Thunderbird provides several ways for organizations or individuals to use custom OAuth2 configurations, whether you need to configure a single account, deploy organization-specific settings across many users, or integrate with an identity provider that Thunderbird does not support by default.
</p><p>Administrators can deploy OAuth2 settings centrally using an add-on, while advanced users can configure OAuth2 settings for individual accounts. This article explains the available options and helps you choose the approach that best fits your organization.
</p><p>Custom OAuth configurations for IMAP and Exchange email accounts can be created with a Thunderbird add-on or by manual configuration without code changes starting in Thunderbird 155. It is possible to do this for any OAuth provider that supports Exchange or IMAP. POP3 support is in progress (currently scheduled for 156 - see <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2061466">bug 2061466</a>).
</p><p>If you need help configuring OAuth2 for a specific provider, please contact the provider''s support team to get the configuration details (Thunderbird support cannot provide provider-specific configuration details). Use the information provided by the provider to configure Thunderbird manually or via an add-on.
</p><p>Custom OAuth is new and there may be issues. If you need help, please do not hesitate to <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird">ask for support in the Thunderbird support forum</a>. For Enterprise deployments, please ask in the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">Thunderbird Enterprise group</a>.
</p><p>For all the details, please read one or more of the following:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users">Custom OAuth for end users</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a>
</li><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-administrators">Custom OAuth for administrators</a>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_future-standards-like-pacc-and-dcr-will-eliminate-most-oauth-custom-configurations">Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations</h1>
<p>Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required, including:
</p>
<ul><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/">PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)</a>
</li><li> <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/">DCR (Dynamic Client Registration)</a>
</li></ul>
<p>Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configurations automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350456", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:50:58 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:50:58.610058-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip do not translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:51:10 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:51:10.192624-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:29+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350457,'en-US','custom-oauth-end-users','Custom OAuth for end users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-end-users/revision/350457',350450,'2026-09-02T23:54:00.328039-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip! do not translate please! discussion at: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218 some examples',1,'2026-09-02T23:54:09.016233-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','__TOC__

End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are some examples:

= Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange =

When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:

# Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email}.
# Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
# Click ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom left. (''''''NOTE'''''': Do not click {button Continue}, because if you do, your system’s web browser will open, which is not what you want. If you do this, please start over again at step 1.)
# In the middle, click  {button Microsoft} > {button Set up account}.
# Type your service URL in the ''''''Enter the service URL'''''' field and then click on {button Find settings}. Here are some common service URLs:
#* [https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx]
#* for Government High GCC, ask your administrator. It may be, for EWS: [https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx] and for Graph: https://graph.microsoft.us/
#* For China, ask your administrator.
# Select ''''''Authentication Method''''''  {menu OAuth2}.
# Uncheck ''''''Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings''''''.
# Enter your custom ''''''Tenant ID'''''' and ''''''AppID'''''' in the relevant fields and then click {button Connect}.

= Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration? =

The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.

If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.

This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.

See [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]] for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.

See [[Custom OAuth for Thunderbird]] for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.','<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</span></a></li></ul></div>
<p>End users have some OAuth configuration possibilities. Here are some examples:
</p>
<h1 id="w_custom-tenantid-and-appid-for-microsoft-exchange">Custom TenantID and AppID for Microsoft Exchange</h1>
<p>When creating an Exchange account in Thunderbird, end users can override their TenantID and AppID as follows:
</p>
<ol><li> Create your Exchange account as normal on the first run or Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> Enter your full name and any valid Exchange email address.
</li><li> Click <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom left. (<strong>NOTE</strong>: Do not click <span class="button">Continue</span>, because if you do, your system’s web browser will open, which is not what you want. If you do this, please start over again at step 1.)
</li><li> In the middle, click  <span class="button">Microsoft</span> &gt; <span class="button">Set up account</span>.
</li><li> Type your service URL in the <strong>Enter the service URL</strong> field and then click on <span class="button">Find settings</span>. Here are some common service URLs:
<ul><li> <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> for Government High GCC, ask your administrator. It may be, for EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.us/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a> and for Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.us/">https://graph.microsoft.us/</a>
</li><li> For China, ask your administrator.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Select <strong>Authentication Method</strong>  <span class="menu">OAuth2</span>.
</li><li> Uncheck <strong>Use default Microsoft 365 Copilot authentication settings</strong>.
</li><li> Enter your custom <strong>Tenant ID</strong> and <strong>AppID</strong> in the relevant fields and then click <span class="button">Connect</span>.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_need-more-control-over-your-oauth2-configuration">Need more control over your OAuth2 configuration?</h1>
<p>The Tenant ID and AppID options above are intended to make common Microsoft 365 configurations easy to set up.
</p><p>If you need to customize additional OAuth2 settings for Exchange, IMAP or POP accounts, you can instead create the account using OAuth2 and then configure its OAuth2 settings through Advanced Account Settings.
</p><p>This allows advanced users to customize settings such as the authorization and token endpoints, scopes, redirect URI, PKCE, and browser authentication method.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a> for per-account advanced configuration and other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p><p>See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth">Custom OAuth for Thunderbird</a> for the other ways to customize OAuth2 in Thunderbird.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350457", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:54:00 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:54:00.328039-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip! do not translate please! discussion at: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218 some examples", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 2, 2026, 11:54:09 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-02T23:54:09.016233-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:26+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350467,'en-US','thundermail-and-thunderbird-android','Thundermail and Thunderbird for Android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thundermail-and-thunderbird-android','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thundermail-and-thunderbird-android/revision/350467',348421,'2026-09-03T03:20:52.905832-07:00','Michele Rodaro','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/michro/','Note: Thunderbird Pro > Thundermail',1,'2026-09-29T04:19:11.740872-07:00','AliceWyman',1,1,1,'2026-09-29T04:19:11.740872-07:00','AliceWyman','approved','[[Template:TBproEarlyBirdInviteOnly]]
Here’s how to get started with Mozilla’s Thundermail service.

__TOC__

=Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android=
== Automatic Setup for Thunderbird 18 or newer==
{note} ''''''Note:'''''' Thundermail requires Thunderbird for Android version 18 or newer (also K-9). We recommend using the latest monthly release version documented in [[New in Thunderbird for Android]].{/note}
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]} > {menu Add account}.
# Enter your Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
# Click {button Next}.
# The next screen will prompt you to ''''''Please specify the special folders for your account'''''', and offer some suggested settings for your Thunderbird special folders. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click {button Next}. 
# On the next screen, configure your user name under ''''''Your name'''''' and optionally your signature and click {button Next}.
# The next screen has sync and notification options. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click {button Next}.

==QR Code Setup for Thunderbird 19 or newer==
* This is faster because you don''t have to type your Thundermail email address.{note}''''''Note:'''''' Please ensure Thunderbird is allowed to use the camera in Android settings in order to make QR code scanning work.{/note}
* Please follow the instructions at: [[How To Import from Thundermail#w_how-to-import-from-thundermail|How to Import from Thundermail]] to import your Thundermail settings into Thunderbird for Android using the QR code on the Thundermail accounts page.

=Manual Settings=
Thunderbird should automatically configure your Thundermail email settings to match your [https://accounts.tb.pro/mail TB Pro accounts page]. If it doesn’t, please manually change the receiving email and sending email settings according to the sections below. If manual setup fails, please create a support ticket using our form at: https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new.
[[UI:details_start]]

==Receiving email==
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]}
# Under ''''''Accounts'''''', tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
# Tap {menu Fetching mail} > {menu Incoming Server} and set the following values:
*''''''Server:'''''' <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> ''''''Security:'''''' SSL/TLS ''''''Port:'''''' 993
*''''''Authentication:'''''' OAuth 2.0
*''''''Username:'''''' Your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>

==Sending email==
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]}
# Under ''''''Accounts'''''', tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
# Tap {menu Sending mail} > {menu Outgoing server} and set the following values:
* ''''''Server Name:'''''' <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> ''''''Connection'''''' ''''''Security:'''''' SSL/TLS ''''''Port:'''''' 465
* ''''''Authentication:'''''' OAuth 2.0
* ''''''Username:'''''' Your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
[[UI:details_end]]

=Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, tb.pro and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird=
==Set up a Thunderbird identity for each alias you want to reply from==
The steps are the same for [https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias catch-All], [https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases tb.pro, Thundermail and custom domain aliases], so we will just use the term ''''''alias'''''' to refer to all of these types of aliases in the instructions below:

You will have to set up an ''''''identity'''''' in Thunderbird for every alias you want to send email from.

For example, if your catch-all is for <code>glamroc.com</code> and you would like to reply as  <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code> in addition to the default alias for <code>glamroc.com</code>, you will have to setup identities for both <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code>.

Here''s how:
# Thunderbird app menu {menu ≡} > Settings gear icon {button [[Image:gear icon]]}.
# Tap on your primary Thundermail email account (the email address you use to log in to Thundermail), for example <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> > {menu Sending mail}. 
# Tap {menu Manage identities} > {menu ⋮} (also known as the ''''kebab'''' menu because the three vertical dots evoke a kebab) > {menu New identity}.
# Enter the desired alias in the ''''''Email address'''''' field. For example: <code>alice@glamroc.com</code><br>All other fields are optional, but we recommend you enter a memorable shorter name in the ''''''Your name'''''' field.
# Save the identity by tapping {button SAVE}. When replying, you can now change the ''''''From'''''' field to this identity by tapping on it.

==Send email with the identities for your aliases==
# When composing or replying, tap on the email address in the  ''''''From'''''' field.
# Tap on the relevant identity for the alias you wish to send from.
# Write and send the email as usual.

=Have some fun now that email setup is done!=
Congratulations, you have successfully set up Thundermail with Thunderbird for Android! Next:
* Send and receive email with Thundermail.
* New to Thunderbird for Android? Then check out [[Getting started with Thunderbird for Android]].
<!--
* If you have large files, you can send them with FIXME because Send on Mobile needs some polish: add a link to Thunderbird Send, which is included with your Thunderbird Pro subscription.
-->','<p></p><div class="warning">NOTE: Thundermail (formerly known as Thunderbird Pro) is currently in its Early Bird release and available  by invitation only. To learn more and join the waitlist, visit <a href="https://tb.pro/">our website</a>. We’ll be opening Thundermail to the public once the Early Bird release concludes.</div>
Here’s how to get started with Mozilla’s Thundermail service.
<p></p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configure-thundermail-in-thunderbird-for-android"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_automatic-setup-for-thunderbird-18-or-newer"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Automatic Setup for Thunderbird 18 or newer</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_qr-code-setup-for-thunderbird-19-or-newer"><span class="tocnumber">1.2</span> <span class="toctext">QR Code Setup for Thunderbird 19 or newer</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_manual-settings"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Manual Settings</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_receiving-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class="toctext">Receiving email</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_sending-email"><span class="tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class="toctext">Sending email</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_configure-catch-all-thundermail-tb-pro-and-custom-domain-aliases-for-use-with-thunderbird"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, tb.pro and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_set-up-a-thunderbird-identity-for-each-alias-you-want-to-reply-from"><span class="tocnumber">3.1</span> <span class="toctext">Set up a Thunderbird identity for each alias you want to reply from</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_send-email-with-the-identities-for-your-aliases"><span class="tocnumber">3.2</span> <span class="toctext">Send email with the identities for your aliases</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_have-some-fun-now-that-email-setup-is-done"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Have some fun now that email setup is done!</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_configure-thundermail-in-thunderbird-for-android">Configure Thundermail in Thunderbird for Android</h1>
<h2 id="w_automatic-setup-for-thunderbird-18-or-newer">Automatic Setup for Thunderbird 18 or newer</h2>
<div class="note"> <strong>Note:</strong> Thundermail requires Thunderbird for Android version 18 or newer (also K-9). We recommend using the latest monthly release version documented in <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-android">New in Thunderbird for Android</a>.</div>
<ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add account</span>.
</li><li> Enter your Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> The next screen will prompt you to <strong>Please specify the special folders for your account</strong>, and offer some suggested settings for your Thunderbird special folders. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click <span class="button">Next</span>. 
</li><li> On the next screen, configure your user name under <strong>Your name</strong> and optionally your signature and click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li><li> The next screen has sync and notification options. For most people, the defaults are fine. Configure them to your needs and click <span class="button">Next</span>.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_qr-code-setup-for-thunderbird-19-or-newer">QR Code Setup for Thunderbird 19 or newer</h2>
<ul><li> This is faster because you don''t have to type your Thundermail email address.<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> Please ensure Thunderbird is allowed to use the camera in Android settings in order to make QR code scanning work.</div>
</li><li> Please follow the instructions at: <a class="new" href="/en-US/kb/new?title=How+To+Import+from+Thundermail#w_how-to-import-from-thundermail" title="Page does not exist.">How to Import from Thundermail</a> to import your Thundermail settings into Thunderbird for Android using the QR code on the Thundermail accounts page.
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_manual-settings">Manual Settings</h1>
<p>Thunderbird should automatically configure your Thundermail email settings to match your <a href="https://accounts.tb.pro/mail">TB Pro accounts page</a>. If it doesn’t, please manually change the receiving email and sending email settings according to the sections below. If manual setup fails, please create a support ticket using our form at: <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new">https://support.tb.pro/hc/en-us/requests/new</a>.
</p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_receiving-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Receiving email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</li><li> Under <strong>Accounts</strong>, tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Fetching mail</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Incoming Server</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Server:</strong> <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> <strong>Security:</strong> SSL/TLS <strong>Port:</strong> 993
</li><li><strong>Authentication:</strong> OAuth 2.0
</li><li><strong>Username:</strong> Your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li></ul></div>


<h2 id="w_sending-email" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Sending email</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>
</li><li> Under <strong>Accounts</strong>, tap on your Thundermail email account. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Sending mail</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Outgoing server</span> and set the following values:
</li></ol><ul><li> <strong>Server Name:</strong> <code>mail.thundermail.com</code> <strong>Connection</strong> <strong>Security:</strong> SSL/TLS <strong>Port:</strong> 465
</li><li> <strong>Authentication:</strong> OAuth 2.0
</li><li> <strong>Username:</strong> Your full Thundermail email address. For example: <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>
</li></ul><p></p></div>


</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_configure-catch-all-thundermail-tb-pro-and-custom-domain-aliases-for-use-with-thunderbird">Configure Catch-All, Thundermail, <a href="http://tb.pro">tb.pro</a> and Custom Domain Aliases for use with Thunderbird</h1>
<h2 id="w_set-up-a-thunderbird-identity-for-each-alias-you-want-to-reply-from">Set up a Thunderbird identity for each alias you want to reply from</h2>
<p>The steps are the same for <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias">catch-All</a>, <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases">tb.pro, Thundermail and custom domain aliases</a>, so we will just use the term <strong>alias</strong> to refer to all of these types of aliases in the instructions below:
</p><p>You will have to set up an <strong>identity</strong> in Thunderbird for every alias you want to send email from.
</p><p>For example, if your catch-all is for <code>glamroc.com</code> and you would like to reply as  <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code> in addition to the default alias for <code>glamroc.com</code>, you will have to setup identities for both <code>alice@glamroc.com</code> and <code>slade@glamroc.com</code>.
</p><p>Here''s how:
</p>
<ol><li> Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; Settings gear icon <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span>.
</li><li> Tap on your primary Thundermail email account (the email address you use to log in to Thundermail), for example <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> &gt; <span class="menu">Sending mail</span>. 
</li><li> Tap <span class="menu">Manage identities</span> &gt; <span class="menu">⋮</span> (also known as the <em>kebab</em> menu because the three vertical dots evoke a kebab) &gt; <span class="menu">New identity</span>.
</li><li> Enter the desired alias in the <strong>Email address</strong> field. For example: <code>alice@glamroc.com</code><br>All other fields are optional, but we recommend you enter a memorable shorter name in the <strong>Your name</strong> field.
</li><li> Save the identity by tapping <span class="button">SAVE</span>. When replying, you can now change the <strong>From</strong> field to this identity by tapping on it.
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_send-email-with-the-identities-for-your-aliases">Send email with the identities for your aliases</h2>
<ol><li> When composing or replying, tap on the email address in the  <strong>From</strong> field.
</li><li> Tap on the relevant identity for the alias you wish to send from.
</li><li> Write and send the email as usual.
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_have-some-fun-now-that-email-setup-is-done">Have some fun now that email setup is done!</h1>
<p>Congratulations, you have successfully set up Thundermail with Thunderbird for Android! Next:
</p>
<ul><li> Send and receive email with Thundermail.
</li><li> New to Thunderbird for Android? Then check out <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-android">Getting started with Thunderbird for Android</a>.
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350467", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 3, 2026, 3:20:52 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-03T03:20:52.905832-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Michele Rodaro", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/michro/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Note: Thunderbird Pro > Thundermail", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 4:19:11 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T04:19:11.740872-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "AliceWyman", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 4:19:11 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T04:19:11.740872-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "AliceWyman", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:19:08+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (350715,'en-US','account-hub-thunderbird-desktop','Account Hub for Thunderbird Desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/account-hub-thunderbird-desktop','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/account-hub-thunderbird-desktop/revision/350715',333958,'2026-09-04T03:58:47.454489-07:00','Michele Rodaro','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/michro/','Fixed a broken link ;)',1,'2026-09-04T05:27:31.112643-07:00','AliceWyman',1,1,1,'2026-09-04T05:27:31.112643-07:00','AliceWyman','approved','= Account Hub for Thunderbird Desktop =

The Account Hub is our new streamlined, unified, and modern experience for adding email accounts, address books, and calendars.
{note}''''''NOTES''''''
* Prior to Thunderbird 150, the legacy Account Setup was the default. For versions older than 150, please refer to these articles: [[Automatic Account Configuration]] and [[Manual Account Configuration]].
*For simplicity, we will use the term [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth ''''''OAuth''''''] instead of ''''''OAuth 2'''''' or ''''''OAuth 2.0''''''. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
* Prior to Thunderbird 153, the OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead. We will refer to versions 152 and lower as ''''''older versions'''''' in this article.{/note}
== Automatic email account setup ==
# ''''''Start'''''': When you first run Thunderbird after installing it, the Account Hub will open into the email setup flow. After the first run, you can start the email setup flow by clicking the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} ({menu New Account} in older versions) > {menu Email}.
# ''''''Name and Email address'''''': Fill in your ''''''Full name'''''' and ''''''Email address'''''' and click {button Continue}. For example, <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>:
#;[[Image:TB153 Account Hub enter email address]] 
# ''''''Configuration lookup'''''': Thunderbird will look in its configuration database for email settings based on your email address - most email providers are in our mail provider database. If they aren’t, you can manually configure your settings. Here are the settings that Thunderbird automatically finds for [[Thunderbird for Desktop and Thundermail|Thundermail]] (other providers that use OAuth like Gmail and Microsoft email are very similar to Thundermail):
#;[[Image:tb153 account hub thundermail settings]] 
# ''''''Confirm settings'''''': The default settings are suitable for most folks, so click {button Continue} to finish the setup. For email providers that use OAuth (like ''''''Thundermail''''''), a branded tab will be provided by the provider. This OAuth dialog will pop up in the system browser (for older versions, this opens a tab in Thunderbird). In our example below, Thundermail uses OAuth, so the tab is branded with Thundermail colours and graphics:
#;[[Image:tb153 opening the system browser for OAuth]] 
# ''''''Enter the password in your email provider’s branded system browser tab:'''''' In this step, you will enter your password and then click {button SIGN IN}. Some providers may require you to do multi-factor authentication during this step. When done, you will receive a notification that your account has been connected to Thunderbird and that you can close the system browser tab:
#;[[Image:tb153 oauth web browser securely connected]] 
# ''''''Add other services like Address Books and Calendars:'''''' Back in Thunderbird, if the email provider supports other services, you are prompted to add them. For most people, clicking {button Continue} to add the default other services is sensible:
#;[[Image:tb153 additional services like calendar and address book]] 
# ''''''Done!'''''' At this stage, your email account and other connected services are set up. You can click {button Finish} to end or click {button Add another email} to add another email account:
#;[[Image:tb153 account hub done!]] 
# ''''''Need help?'''''' If your email provider uses OAuth, please follow our [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird|OAuth Troubleshooting Checklist]] first. If you are still stuck, please [http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form ask a support question] in our community support forum.','<h1 id="w_account-hub-for-thunderbird-desktop">Account Hub for Thunderbird Desktop</h1>
<p>The Account Hub is our new streamlined, unified, and modern experience for adding email accounts, address books, and calendars.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTES</strong>
<ul><li> Prior to Thunderbird 150, the legacy Account Setup was the default. For versions older than 150, please refer to these articles: <a href="/en-US/kb/automatic-account-configuration">Automatic Account Configuration</a> and <a href="/en-US/kb/manual-account-configuration">Manual Account Configuration</a>.
</li><li>For simplicity, we will use the term <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth"><strong>OAuth</strong></a> instead of <strong>OAuth 2</strong> or <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Thunderbird’s current implementation of OAuth is based on OAuth 2.0, the most common version of OAuth.
</li><li> Prior to Thunderbird 153, the OAuth dialogues are presented in a tab inside Thunderbird. Versions 153 and higher display the OAuth dialogues externally in your system’s default web browser (Firefox, Chrome, etc.) instead. We will refer to versions 152 and lower as <strong>older versions</strong> in this article.</li></ul></div>

<h2 id="w_automatic-email-account-setup">Automatic email account setup</h2>
<ol><li> <strong>Start</strong>: When you first run Thunderbird after installing it, the Account Hub will open into the email setup flow. After the first run, you can start the email setup flow by clicking the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> (<span class="menu">New Account</span> in older versions) &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>.
</li><li> <strong>Name and Email address</strong>: Fill in your <strong>Full name</strong> and <strong>Email address</strong> and click <span class="button">Continue</span>. For example, <code>nemo@thundermail.com</code>:
<dl><dt><img alt="TB153 Account Hub enter email address" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-33-21-80fd75.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Configuration lookup</strong>: Thunderbird will look in its configuration database for email settings based on your email address - most email providers are in our mail provider database. If they aren’t, you can manually configure your settings. Here are the settings that Thunderbird automatically finds for <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-desktop-and-thundermail">Thundermail</a> (other providers that use OAuth like Gmail and Microsoft email are very similar to Thundermail):
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 account hub thundermail settings" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-36-47-226006.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Confirm settings</strong>: The default settings are suitable for most folks, so click <span class="button">Continue</span> to finish the setup. For email providers that use OAuth (like <strong>Thundermail</strong>), a branded tab will be provided by the provider. This OAuth dialog will pop up in the system browser (for older versions, this opens a tab in Thunderbird). In our example below, Thundermail uses OAuth, so the tab is branded with Thundermail colours and graphics:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 opening the system browser for OAuth" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-43-30-1cb019.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Enter the password in your email provider’s branded system browser tab:</strong> In this step, you will enter your password and then click <span class="button">SIGN IN</span>. Some providers may require you to do multi-factor authentication during this step. When done, you will receive a notification that your account has been connected to Thunderbird and that you can close the system browser tab:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 oauth web browser securely connected" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-47-06-840125.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Add other services like Address Books and Calendars:</strong> Back in Thunderbird, if the email provider supports other services, you are prompted to add them. For most people, clicking <span class="button">Continue</span> to add the default other services is sensible:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 additional services like calendar and address book" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-51-20-e6913b.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Done!</strong> At this stage, your email account and other connected services are set up. You can click <span class="button">Finish</span> to end or click <span class="button">Add another email</span> to add another email account:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb153 account hub done!" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-06-18-00-55-10-940bc7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""> 
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>Need help?</strong> If your email provider uses OAuth, please follow our <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth Troubleshooting Checklist</a> first. If you are still stuck, please <a href="http://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">ask a support question</a> in our community support forum.
</li></ol>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "350715", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 4, 2026, 3:58:47 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-04T03:58:47.454489-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Michele Rodaro", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/michro/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Fixed a broken link ;)", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 4, 2026, 5:27:31 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-04T05:27:31.112643-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "AliceWyman", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 4, 2026, 5:27:31 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-04T05:27:31.112643-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "AliceWyman", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:03:34+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (351487,'en-US','tb-custom-oauth','Custom OAuth for Thunderbird','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tb-custom-oauth/revision/351487',350456,'2026-09-07T22:33:43.197284-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip do not translate please add toby''s edits suggested in https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/218#issuecomment-5579685387',1,'2026-09-07T22:34:03.195026-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird 155 and newer. See [[OAuth2 for Thunderbird]] for an overview.

{note}''''''NOTE'''''': For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and, AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, you can safely ignore this article.{/note}
__TOC__
= Overview of custom OAuth =

Thunderbird provides several ways for organizations or individuals to use custom OAuth2 configurations, whether you need to configure a single account, deploy organization-specific settings across many users, or integrate with an identity provider that Thunderbird does not support by default.

Custom OAuth configurations for IMAP, POP and Exchange email accounts can be created with a Thunderbird add-on or by manual configuration without code changes starting in Thunderbird 156. It is possible to do this for any OAuth provider that supports Exchange, POP or IMAP. 

If you need help configuring OAuth2 for a specific provider, please contact the provider''s support team to get the configuration details (Thunderbird support cannot provide provider-specific configuration details). Use the information provided by the provider to configure Thunderbird manually or via an add-on.

Administrators can deploy OAuth2 settings centrally using an add-on, while advanced users can configure OAuth2 settings for individual accounts. This article explains the available options and helps you choose the approach that best fits your organization.

Custom OAuth is new and there may be issues. If you need help, please do not hesitate to [https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird ask for support in the Thunderbird support forum]. For Enterprise deployments, please ask in the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise Thunderbird Enterprise group].

For all the details, please read one or more of the following:
* [[Custom OAuth for end users]]
* [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]]
* [[Custom OAuth for administrators]]

= Future standards, like PACC and DCR, will eliminate most OAuth custom configurations =

Thunderbird is considering implementing emerging standards intended to reduce the amount of manual OAuth2 configuration required, including:

* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-pacc/ PACC (Provisioning and Configuration Capabilities)]
* [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-mailmaint-dcr/ DCR (Dynamic Client Registration)]

Over time, these standards should allow Thunderbird to discover or negotiate OAuth2 configurations automatically with compatible servers, reducing (or potentially eliminating) the need to distribute client IDs or manually configure OAuth settings.','<p>This article covers custom OAuth configuration for Thunderbird 155 and newer. See <a href="/en-US/kb/tb-oauth">OAuth2 for Thunderbird</a> for an overview.
</p>
<div class="note"><strong>NOTE</strong>: For most Thunderbird users, Thunderbird''s built-in OAuth2 support for many common providers (like Google, Microsoft, Yahoo and, AOL) is sufficient. If you don''t need to customize your OAuth configuration, you can safely ignore this article.</div>
