<h1 id="w_custom-domain-catch-all-and-custom-aliases">Custom Domain catch-all and custom aliases</h1>
<p>How to setup Thunderbird Desktop for <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51253809827347-Custom-Domains-and-DNS-Configuration">custom domains</a> in Thundermail:
</p>
<h2 id="w_send-and-receive-email-with-thundermail-custom-domain-aliases-using-thunderbird-identities">Send and receive email with Thundermail custom domain aliases using Thunderbird identities</h2>
<p>For every <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/46804820453907-Setting-Up-Email-Aliases">alias</a> you create in Thundermail, you will have to create a new <a href="/en-US/kb/using-identities">identity</a> in Thunderbird in order to send email with that alias. Here''s how using our example custom domain <code>glamroc.com</code>, our example Thundermail address of <code>roc@thundermail.com</code>, and custom domain alias of <code>roc@glamroc.com</code>:
</p>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; Click on the <a href="http://thundermail.com">thundermail.com</a> account in the left sidebar, which in our example is: <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> &gt; <span class="button">Manage Identities…</span> &gt; <span class="button">Add…</span>.
</li><li> Add your alias e.g. <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by filling in the <strong>New Identity</strong> form, and then click <span class="button">OK</span> when you are done.
</li><li> If you want this to be the default identity when composing new email, click on the new identity, and then click on <span class="button">Set Default</span> and <span class="button">Close</span>.
</li></ol>
<p>Now the custom domain alias <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> is the default identity when writing new email, and you can send and receive email on both <code>roc@thundermail.com</code> and <code>roc@glamroc.com</code> by selecting the appropriate identity.
</p>
<h2 id="w_send-and-reply-to-email-using-a-thundermail-catch-all-alias">Send and reply to email using a Thundermail catch-all alias</h2>
<ul><li> If you have a <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/articles/51338950680467-Setting-Up-a-Catch-All-Alias">catch-all alias configured in Thundermail for a custom domain</a>, here''s how to use  Thunderbird <strong>custom from address</strong> feature to reply with whatever email address on your custom domain that you want:
<ol><li> When composing or replying to an email, click on the email address next to <strong>From</strong>.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Customize From Address…</span>. 
</li><li> Finally, type in an email address for the custom domain you created the catch-all in.
</li></ol>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_send-thundermail-use-your-thundermail-calendar-and-thundermail-address-book-and-have-some-fun">Send Thundermail, use your Thundermail Calendar and Thundermail Address Book, and have some fun!</h1>
<ul><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">Send and receive email</a> with Thundermail. If you have large files, you can also use <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235"><strong>Thunderbird Send</strong></a>. which is included with your Thundermail subscription.
</li><li> Enjoy your Thundermail Calendar: <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/products/thunderbird/email-and-messaging/calendar">Create some events, invite people to invites, etc</a>.
</li><li> Use your Thundermail Address Book for <a href="/en-US/kb/addressing-email">composing email</a>, managing your contacts, etc.
</li><li> New to Thunderbird? Check out <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird">Get started with Thunderbird</a>.
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "353211", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 17, 2026, 9:31:05 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-17T09:31:05.673469-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fix typo i.e. remove extra \"", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 17, 2026, 9:31:18 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-17T09:31:18.459744-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:07+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (355747,'en-US','thunderbird-and-exchange','Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange/revision/355747',314835,'2026-09-25T10:30:25.631414-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','filters, folder quotas, folder subscriptions, message retention policies, message download sync, message tagging, ntlm authentication, star message have been implemented. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/210',1,'2026-09-26T10:09:09.004597-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,1,'2026-09-26T10:09:09.004597-07:00','wsmwk','approved','{warning}
For the best Exchange experience on desktops, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services), ''''''currently limited to email''''''.

Calendar and address book using EWS on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is a) Exchange via ''''''Microsoft Graph'''''' and b) Exchange support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.

Support for Exchange on desktop is new, so we very much welcome your [[#w_feedback-and-bug-reports|feedback and bug reports]]. For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].{/warning}

__TOC__

=Setup=
When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the ''''''Exchange Web Services'''''' option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, ''''''Exchange'''''' should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first ''''''Exchange'''''' option.

==Configure your Exchange account==
Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird ''''''Account Hub''''''.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Hub'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account type selection dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-exchange-support-button|width=450]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}

====Manual EWS Configuration====
There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
# In [[New in Thunderbird Desktop|145]] and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking {menu MANUAL CONFIGURATION}. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
# Under ''''''Protocol'''''', select {menu EWS}.
# Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
[[UI:details_end]]

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profile with no accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with no accounts, you will see the older ''''Add account'''' wizard.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Setup'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account settings dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange Web Services'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange Web Services} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}
[[UI:details_end]]

=Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account=
Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to [[Send and receive messages in Thunderbird|send and receive emails]] just like an IMAP or POP account.

=Limitations=
Not yet supported:
* Address book
* Calendar
* OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
* Shared mailboxes 
<!--
"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&hide_resolved=1 this bug list].
-->
=Feedback and Bug Reports=
To report a problem when using version 145 or newer, please [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&component=Networking:%20Exchange&short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&blocked=1847846&bug_type=defect file a bug report].

To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].

You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta beta group] or the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise enterprise group].

=Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers=
Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].','<div class="warning">
<p>For the best Exchange experience on desktops, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services), <strong>currently limited to email</strong>.
</p><p>Calendar and address book using EWS on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is a) Exchange via <strong>Microsoft Graph</strong> and b) Exchange support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.
</p>
Support for Exchange on desktop is new, so we very much welcome your <a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports">feedback and bug reports</a>. For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_setup"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Setup</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configure-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure your Exchange account</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-4"><a href="#w_manual-ews-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Manual EWS Configuration</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_limitations"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Limitations</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Feedback and Bug Reports</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_setup">Setup</h1>
<p>When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the <strong>Exchange Web Services</strong> option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, <strong>Exchange</strong> should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first <strong>Exchange</strong> option.
</p>
<h2 id="w_configure-your-exchange-account">Configure your Exchange account</h2>
<p>Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 145 or newer.
</p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird <strong>Account Hub</strong>.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Hub</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account type selection dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-exchange-support-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-03-20-2802fd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><h4 id="w_manual-ews-configuration">Manual EWS Configuration</h4><p>There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
</p><ol><li> In <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop">145</a> and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking <span class="menu">MANUAL CONFIGURATION</span>. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
</li><li> Under <strong>Protocol</strong>, select <span class="menu">EWS</span>.
</li><li> Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
</li></ol><p></p></div>






</section>
<p></p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with no accounts, you will see the older <em>Add account</em> wizard.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Setup</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account settings dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-23-12-9340ff.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange Web Services</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange Web Services</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><p></p></div>



</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</h1>
<p>Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">send and receive emails</a> just like an IMAP or POP account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_limitations">Limitations</h1>
<p>Not yet supported:
</p>
<ul><li> Address book
</li><li> Calendar
</li><li> OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
</li><li> Shared mailboxes 
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_feedback-and-bug-reports">Feedback and Bug Reports</h1>
<p>To report a problem when using version 145 or newer, please <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&amp;component=Networking:%20Exchange&amp;short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&amp;blocked=1847846&amp;bug_type=defect">file a bug report</a>.
</p><p>To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p><p>You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta">beta group</a> or the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">enterprise group</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</h1>
<p>Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "355747", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 25, 2026, 10:30:25 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-25T10:30:25.631414-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "filters, folder quotas, folder subscriptions, message retention policies, message download sync, message tagging, ntlm authentication, star message have been implemented. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/210", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 26, 2026, 10:09:09 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-26T10:09:09.004597-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 26, 2026, 10:09:09 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-26T10:09:09.004597-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:05+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (355813,'en-US','microsoft-ews-graph-migration','Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration/revision/355813',NULL,'2026-09-25T13:08:23.373463-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','needs review by Wayne, do not localize. Discussion: https://tbpro.zendesk.com/agent/tickets/9081',1,'2026-09-25T13:09:57.792461-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{note}''''''NOTE'''''': If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email, and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.{/note}

Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in Thunderbird versions 154 and higher.

Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > click on your EWS account in the left sidebar > {button Delete}.

''''''Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.''''''

Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use Account Hub to set up a new account. In Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email} enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account. Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the {button Microsoft Graph API} button when presented with the list of available protocols.','<div class="note"><strong>NOTE</strong>: If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email, and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.</div>
<p>Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in Thunderbird versions 154 and higher.
</p><p>Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; click on your EWS account in the left sidebar &gt; <span class="button">Delete</span>.
</p><p><strong>Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.</strong>
</p><p>Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use Account Hub to set up a new account. In Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span> enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account. Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> button when presented with the list of available protocols.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "355813", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 25, 2026, 1:08:23 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-25T13:08:23.373463-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "needs review by Wayne, do not localize. Discussion: https://tbpro.zendesk.com/agent/tickets/9081", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 25, 2026, 1:09:57 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-25T13:09:57.792461-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:02+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (356350,'en-US','microsoft-ews-graph-migration','Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration/revision/356350',355813,'2026-09-28T09:54:57.512421-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','fix typos change to warning. Discusion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/232',1,'2026-09-28T09:55:14.147655-07:00','rtanglao',1,0,1,'2026-09-28T10:00:11.373117-07:00','rtanglao','approved','{warning}''''''NOTE'''''': If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email, and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.{/warning}

Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in Thunderbird versions 154 and higher.

Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > click on your EWS account in the left sidebar > {button Delete}.

''''''Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.''''''

Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the Account Hub to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu :
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email} enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account. 
Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the {button Microsoft Graph API} button when presented with the list of available protocols.','<div class="warning"><strong>NOTE</strong>: If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email, and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.</div>
<p>Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in Thunderbird versions 154 and higher.
</p><p>Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; click on your EWS account in the left sidebar &gt; <span class="button">Delete</span>.
</li></ul>
<p><strong>Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.</strong>
</p><p>Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the Account Hub to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu&nbsp;:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span> enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account. 
</li></ul>
<p>Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> button when presented with the list of available protocols.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "356350", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 9:54:57 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T09:54:57.512421-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "fix typos change to warning. Discusion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/232", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 9:55:14 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T09:55:14.147655-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 10:00:11 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T10:00:11.373117-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:17:00+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (356655,'en-US','thunderbird-and-exchange-redirect-1','Thunderbird and Exchange','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange-redirect-1','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange-redirect-1/revision/356655',NULL,'2026-09-28T20:08:45.467387-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','REDIRECT [[Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)]]','<p>REDIRECT <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a>
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "356655", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 8:08:45 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T20:08:45.467387-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:16:57+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (356656,'en-US','thunderbird-and-exchange','Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange/revision/356656',355747,'2026-09-28T20:15:23.078670-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip do not approve, or translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/210',1,'2026-09-28T20:21:32.654122-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,1,'2026-09-28T20:21:32.654122-07:00','wsmwk','approved','{warning}
For the best Microsoft Exchange and Microsoft Graph experience on desktop, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.

Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services) and Microsoft Graph. Support for Exchange and Graph is ''''''currently limited to email''''''.

Calendar and address book using EWS and Graph on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is Exchange support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.

For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].{/warning}

__TOC__

=Setup=
When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the ''''''Exchange Web Services'''''' option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, ''''''Exchange'''''' should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first ''''''Exchange'''''' option.

==Configure your Exchange account==
Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird ''''''Account Hub''''''.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Hub'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account type selection dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-exchange-support-button|width=450]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}

====Manual EWS Configuration====
There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
# In [[New in Thunderbird Desktop|145]] and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking {menu MANUAL CONFIGURATION}. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
# Under ''''''Protocol'''''', select {menu EWS}.
# Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
[[UI:details_end]]

[[UI:details_start]]
===Thunderbird profile with no accounts===
If you have a [[Profiles - Where Thunderbird stores your messages and other user data|Thunderbird profile]] with no accounts, you will see the older ''''Add account'''' wizard.
# Click Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Setup'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account settings dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] ''''Exchange Web Services'''' radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange Web Services} button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.{/note}
[[UI:details_end]]

=Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account=
Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to [[Send and receive messages in Thunderbird|send and receive emails]] just like an IMAP or POP account.

=Limitations=
Not yet supported:
* Address book
* Calendar
* OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
* Shared mailboxes 
<!--
"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&hide_resolved=1 this bug list].
-->
=Feedback and Bug Reports=
To report a problem when using version 157 or newer, please [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&component=Networking:%20Exchange&short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&blocked=1847846&bug_type=defect file a bug report].

To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].

You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta beta group] or the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise enterprise group].

=Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers=
Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].','<div class="warning">
<p>For the best Microsoft Exchange and Microsoft Graph experience on desktop, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.
</p><p>Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services) and Microsoft Graph. Support for Exchange and Graph is <strong>currently limited to email</strong>.
</p><p>Calendar and address book using EWS and Graph on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is Exchange support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.
</p>
For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_setup"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Setup</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configure-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure your Exchange account</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-4"><a href="#w_manual-ews-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Manual EWS Configuration</span></a></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.2</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_limitations"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Limitations</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Feedback and Bug Reports</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_setup">Setup</h1>
<p>When creating a new account using classic account creation, choose the <strong>Exchange Web Services</strong> option. Or if using the new Account Hub, when providing an exchange email address, <strong>Exchange</strong> should be automatically detected. If Exchange is not detected, choose the first <strong>Exchange</strong> option.
</p>
<h2 id="w_configure-your-exchange-account">Configure your Exchange account</h2>
<p>Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.
</p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profiles-with-1-or-more-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profiles with 1 or more accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with one or more accounts, you will see the new Thunderbird <strong>Account Hub</strong>.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Hub</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account type selection dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-exchange-support-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-03-20-2802fd.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><h4 id="w_manual-ews-configuration">Manual EWS Configuration</h4><p>There are a diversity of EWS servers which could cause auto configuration of your EWS email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
</p><ol><li> In <a href="/en-US/kb/new-thunderbird-desktop">145</a> and newer once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking <span class="menu">MANUAL CONFIGURATION</span>. Please consult your Exchange administrator for the exact manual settings.
</li><li> Under <strong>Protocol</strong>, select <span class="menu">EWS</span>.
</li><li> Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange administrator.
</li></ol><p></p></div>






</section>
<p></p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_thunderbird-profile-with-no-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird profile with no accounts</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you have a <a href="/en-US/kb/profiles-where-thunderbird-stores-user-data">Thunderbird profile</a> with no accounts, you will see the older <em>Add account</em> wizard.
</p><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Setup</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account settings dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb142-beta-old-pre-account-hub-flow-exchange-webservices-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-21-23-12-9340ff.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <em>Exchange Web Services</em> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange account.
</li></ol><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange Web Services</span> button only appears if the email you entered is an Exchange account.</div><p></p></div>



</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-account">Send and receive emails with your Exchange Account</h1>
<p>Once you have configured your Exchange Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">send and receive emails</a> just like an IMAP or POP account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_limitations">Limitations</h1>
<p>Not yet supported:
</p>
<ul><li> Address book
</li><li> Calendar
</li><li> OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
</li><li> Shared mailboxes 
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_feedback-and-bug-reports">Feedback and Bug Reports</h1>
<p>To report a problem when using version 157 or newer, please <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&amp;component=Networking:%20Exchange&amp;short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&amp;blocked=1847846&amp;bug_type=defect">file a bug report</a>.
</p><p>To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p><p>You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta">beta group</a> or the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">enterprise group</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-servers">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted exchange servers</h1>
<p>Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "356656", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 8:15:23 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T20:15:23.078670-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip do not approve, or translate, discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/210", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 8:21:32 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T20:21:32.654122-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 8:21:32 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T20:21:32.654122-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:16:55+00:00','M');
