</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-151">Thunderbird 151</h1>
<h2 id="w_thundermail-configuration" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-6" aria-expanded="false">Thundermail configuration</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-6" aria-hidden="true"><li> <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-desktop-and-thundermail">Thundermail</a> configuration is now built-in to Thunderbird.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_ews-oauth-improvements" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-7" aria-expanded="false">EWS OAuth improvements</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-7" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> You can now override OAuth provider details for EWS accounts.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-150">Thunderbird 150</h1>
<h2 id="w_account-hub-is-now-the-default" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-8" aria-expanded="false">Account hub is now the default</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-8" aria-hidden="true"><li> Account hub is opened on the first run of Thunderbird. It is now the default across mail, calendar and address book.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_alphabetical-sorting-in-recent-destinations" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-9" aria-expanded="false">Alphabetical sorting in Recent Destinations</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-9" aria-hidden="true"><li> <strong>Recent Destinations</strong> in General Settings now supports alphabetical sorting. See: Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> &gt; Scroll down to <strong>Sort order:</strong> under <strong>Folder Management Options</strong> and choose <span class="menu">Alphabetical</span> or <span class="menu">Most recent</span>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_custom-accent-color" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-10" aria-expanded="false">Custom Accent color</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-10" aria-hidden="true"><ol><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Appearance</span>.
</li><li> Next to <strong>Define the color used for primary buttons and highlighted elements:</strong>, select one of the following buttons: <span class="button">Follow operating system</span>, <span class="button">Thunderbird blue</span>, <span class="button">Purple</span>, <span class="button">Orange</span>, <span class="button">Pink</span>, <span class="button">Ink</span> or <span class="button">Teal</span>.
</li></ol><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-149">Thunderbird 149</h1>
<h2 id="w_exchange-improvements" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-11" aria-expanded="false">Exchange Improvements</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-11" aria-hidden="true"><li> Starred Exchange messages now persist across devices.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_account-hub-on-for-address-books" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-12" aria-expanded="false">Account Hub on for Address Books</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-12" aria-hidden="true"><li> Address books are now created in Account Hub.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_dark-mode-link-color-fixed" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-13" aria-expanded="false">Dark Mode link color fixed</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-13" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Dark mode link colors are once more readable.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-148">Thunderbird 148</h1>
<h2 id="w_ntlm-support" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-14" aria-expanded="false">NTLM support</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-14" aria-hidden="true"><li> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NTLM">NTLM authentication</a> is now supported.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_yahoo-aol-att-oauth2-authentication-update" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-15" aria-expanded="false">Yahoo, AOL, ATT OAuth2 authentication update</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-15" aria-hidden="true"><li> Thunderbird switched to a more secure authorization protocol called <strong>PKCE</strong> for Yahoo, AT&amp;T and AOL accounts. This switch adds support for Yahoo/AOL calendars and address books. For full details, see <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-yahoo">OAuth2 Authentication for Yahoo, AOL and ATT</a>.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_dark-mode-link-color-issue" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-16" aria-expanded="false">Dark Mode link color issue</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-16" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> <strong>Known issue:</strong> Dark mode link colors might be hard to read. <strong>Workaround:</strong> Switch to a different theme. A fix has been developed for a future release; you can follow along in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2018015">bug 2018015</a>.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-147">Thunderbird 147</h1>
<h2 id="w_reset-folder-order" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-17" aria-expanded="false">Reset Folder Order</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-17" aria-hidden="true"><li> <span class="menu">Reset Folder Order</span> has been fixed (see the <a href="#w_thunderbird-142">142 section</a>). It now restores the original location of a folder that was moved inside another parent folder.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_localization-of-folder-names" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-18" aria-expanded="false">Localization of folder names</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-18" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> All outstanding issues with localization of folder names have been fixed. If you notice any other issues, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird">file a Thunderbird desktop support request</a>.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-146">Thunderbird 146</h1>
<h2 id="w_drag-and-drop-on-macos" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-19" aria-expanded="false">Drag and Drop on macOS</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-19" aria-hidden="true"><li> Drag and Drop for attachments on macOS is working again.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_openpgp-keyserver" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-20" aria-expanded="false">OpenPGP Keyserver</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-20" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> You can now add your own OpenPGP Keyserver:
<ul><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Privacy &amp; Security</span> &gt; Under <strong>OpenPGP Keyservers</strong> <span class="menu">Add…</span> Screenshot:
</li></ul>
</li></ul><p><img alt="tb146-add-open-pgp-keyserver" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-12-12-11-07-38-4c0f65.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-145">Thunderbird 145</h1>
<h2 id="w_dns-over-https" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-21" aria-expanded="false">DNS over HTTPS</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-21" aria-hidden="true"><li> Thunderbird now supports DNS over HTTPS.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_account-hub-supports-manual-configuration-for-ews-imap-and-pop3-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-22" aria-expanded="false">Account Hub supports manual configuration for EWS, IMAP and POP3 accounts</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-22" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> In the <strong>Account Hub</strong>, once you enter a valid email address, you can now manually configure your email settings by clicking <span class="menu">MANUAL CONFIGURATION</span>.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb145-account-hub-manual-configuration" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-12-23-06-35-21b46c.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> in the <strong>Account Hub</strong> supports manual configuration of <span class="menu">EWS</span>, <span class="menu">IMAP</span> and <span class="menu">POP3</span> accounts.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb145-ews-imap-pop-account-hub-manual-configuration" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-12-23-55-15-82277a.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Manual configuration of EWS accounts is now supported as per the screenshots in the above two bullet points. For more details, see the <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange#w_manual-ews-configuration">Manual EWS configuration section of our Thunderbird and Exchange article</a>.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-144">Thunderbird 144</h1>
<h2 id="w_exchange-enhancements-and-bug-fixes" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-23" aria-expanded="false">Exchange enhancements and bug fixes</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-23" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Another bug fix release.
</li><li> Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions. We are planning a promotional splash in one of the next few releases. Getting closer!
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-143">Thunderbird 143</h1>
<h2 id="w_exchange-support-enhancements-and-bug-fixes" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-24" aria-expanded="false">Exchange Support enhancements and bug fixes</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-24" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Bug fixes.
</li><li> Various Exchange Support fixes. This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-142">Thunderbird 142</h1>
<h2 id="w_switch-to-the-old-account-setup-flow-to-fix-issues-with-hotmail-and-outlookx2ecom-accounts" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-25" aria-expanded="false">Switch to the old Account Setup flow to fix issues with Hotmail and <a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a> accounts</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-25" aria-hidden="true"><p>If you see: <strong>Exchange auth error</strong> or <strong>Authentication failed. Please check the username and password</strong> when adding a <strong>Hotmail</strong> or <strong><a href="http://outlook.com">outlook.com</a></strong> account, here''s how to switch to the old <strong>Account Setup</strong> flow to fix the problem:
</p><ul><li> Click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> and untick <strong>Create accounts in the new Account Hub</strong>:
<dl><dt><img alt="tb142-disable-account-hub" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-27-10-54-39-2636ed.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="350">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> Please try adding the email account again. If you still have issues, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/questions/new/thunderbird/form">file a support request on SUMO</a>.
</li></ul><p>This will be fixed in a future Thunderbird release.
</p></div>



<h2 id="w_reset-manual-folder-sorting-order" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-26" aria-expanded="false">Reset manual folder sorting order</button></h2>
<ol class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-26" aria-hidden="true"><li> Right-click (<span class="key">Control</span> + click on macOS) on the account row, for example <strong><a href="mailto:nemo@thunderbird.net">nemo@thunderbird.net</a></strong> in the <em>Folder Pane</em> (more details: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-main-window-supernova">Getting Started with the Thunderbird main window (Supernova and newer)</a>) and then:
</li><li> Click the menu item <span class="menu">Reset Folder Order</span> to clear any previously defined manual sorting. <strong>Note:</strong> This feature doesn’t restore the original location of a folder that was moved into another parent folder; this will be fixed in a future release. You can follow along in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979880">bug 1979880</a>.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb142-reset-folder-order" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-19-00-23-24-8d62b7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="350">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_add-a-visual-signature-to-a-pdf" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-27" aria-expanded="false">Add a visual signature to a PDF</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-27" aria-hidden="true">Just like Firefox, you can now insert your signature into a PDF by typing, drawing or adding an image. Please refer to the Firefox article for full details: <a href="/en-US/kb/view-pdf-files-firefox-or-choose-another-viewer#w_add-a-signature-to-a-pdf">View and edit PDF files using Firefox’s built-in viewer</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-28" aria-expanded="false">Folders erroneously renamed to ‘Junk’ will be renamed back to ‘Spam’ and ‘Bulk’ in a post 142 release</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-28" aria-hidden="true">See the <a href="#w_thunderbird-141">141 section</a> for more details.
</p>
<h2 id="w_more-stable-exchange-support" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-29" aria-expanded="false">More stable Exchange support</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-29" aria-hidden="true"><p>This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
</p><p>Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange">Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)</a> and our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p></div>
</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-141">Thunderbird 141</h1>
<h2 id="w_archive-action-added-to-new-email-notifications" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-30" aria-expanded="false">Archive action added to new email notifications</button></h2>
<ul class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-30" aria-hidden="true"><li> You can now <span class="button">Archive</span> email from your operating system''s new email native notifications (in addition to <span class="button">Mark as Read</span>, <span class="button">Delete</span>, <span class="button">Mark as Starred</span> and <span class="button">Mark as Spam</span>). Please see <a href="/en-US/kb/new-email-notifications-thunderbird">New email notifications in Thunderbird</a> for further information.
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_better-exchange-support" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-31" aria-expanded="false">Better Exchange support</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-31" aria-hidden="true"><p>Now, anyone can try the Exchange support in Thunderbird. For most, we recommend waiting for a future release, which will be more stable and include additional features, such as on-premises OAuth authentication (password authentication for on-premises is supported in this release).
</p><p>This release''s implementation is still limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
</p><p>Please refer to our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p></div>

<h2 id="w_31-day-warning-for-pgp-key-expiry" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-32" aria-expanded="false">31-day warning for PGP Key expiry</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-32" aria-hidden="true"><p>If your PGP key expires in the next 31 days, you will receive a warning when you are composing an email. For extra security, you can choose to let it expire or you can extend your key''s certificate.
</p><p>More info on PGP encrypted email can be found in <a href="/en-US/kb/introduction-to-e2e-encryption">Introduction to End-to-end encryption in Thunderbird</a> and <a href="/en-US/kb/openpgp-thunderbird-howto-and-faq">OpenPGP in Thunderbird - HOWTO and FAQ</a>.
</p></div>

<h2 id="w_folders-erroneously-renamed-to-junk-will-be-renamed-back-to-spam-and-bulk-in-a-post-142-release_2" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-33" aria-expanded="false">Folders erroneously renamed to ‘Junk’ will be renamed back to ‘Spam’ and ‘Bulk’ in a post 142 release</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-33" aria-hidden="true">This is primarily a problem in the English language locales. It was discovered a few days after the release of 141. You can follow the progress of the fix in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1979511">bug 1979511</a>.
</p></section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-140-esr-140-eclipse">Thunderbird 140 / ESR 140 Eclipse</h1>
<h2 id="w_dark-message-mode" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-34" aria-expanded="false">Dark Message Mode</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-34" aria-hidden="true"><p>Thunderbird now automatically displays your messages in dark mode when enabled in your operating system settings. If there are display issues with a message in dark mode, you can disable dark mode for that message by sliding the toggle to the right to disable dark message mode.
</p><dl><dt><img alt="tb140-dark-mode-toggle" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-06-29-22-50-16-bf91b7.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl></div>


<h2 id="w_new-appearance-panel-in-settings" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-35" aria-expanded="false">New Appearance Panel in Settings</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-35" aria-hidden="true">The new Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Appearance</span>, allows you to toggle your message list layout between Card and Table view, adjust your Cards view, and set your default folder sorting order and threading options. For more details, refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/appearance-panel-layout-message-list-card-view-tab">Appearance Panel - Layout, Message List, Card View, Table View, Sorting and Threading settings</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_native-os-notifications" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-36" aria-expanded="false">Native OS Notifications</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-36" aria-hidden="true">Thunderbird now uses your operating system''s built-in notifications, whether you’re on Windows, Linux or macOS. You can now quickly delete, archive or use customizable actions directly from your notifications.
</p>
<h2 id="w_redesigned-account-hub" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-37" aria-expanded="false">Redesigned Account hub</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-37" aria-hidden="true">The newly redesigned Account hub makes it easier to add an account. Connect all of your emails, address books and calendars in a few easy steps. The Account hub is currently not enabled for your first account (you will see it when you add a second account). It is also not enabled by default for your address book. You can change the defaults in your Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">General</span> &gt; search for <strong>Account hub</strong> (not enabled in ESR; we recommend the latest monthly release for the best experience).
</p>
<h2 id="w_manual-folder-sorting" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-38" aria-expanded="false">Manual Folder Sorting</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-38" aria-hidden="true">Drag and drop your folders to get the order you want.
</p>
<h2 id="w_experimental-exchange-support-for-advanced-users" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-39" aria-expanded="false">Experimental Exchange Support for advanced users</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-39" aria-hidden="true"><p>Preliminary Microsoft Exchange support is available for you to try. Our current, initial implementation of Exchange is limited to email – account setup, folder management and writing, receiving and reading email.
</p><p>We recommend you try this in a test <a href="/en-US/kb/using-multiple-profiles">Thunderbird profile</a> in <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-beta">Thunderbird Beta</a> (please ensure you back up your profile before starting). Please refer to our <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> for full instructions.
</p></div>

<h2 id="w_export-for-mobile" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-40" aria-expanded="false">Export for Mobile</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-40" aria-hidden="true">Generate a QR code to quickly transfer your account settings and credentials to your Thunderbird for Android app. Please refer to <a href="/en-US/kb/thunderbird-android-import">Import settings and data into Thunderbird for Android</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="w_horizontal-scroll-for-table-view" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-41" aria-expanded="false">Horizontal Scroll for Table View</button></h2>
<p class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-41" aria-hidden="true">Table view now supports horizontal scrolling. This allows you to view wide messages – for example, data in a table with many columns.
</p></section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_older-releases">Older Releases</h1>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-139" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-42" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 139</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-42" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> You can now manually sort folders by dragging and dropping them in the folder pane.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-138" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-43" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 138</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-43" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Dark mode and high contrast mode improvements.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-137" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-44" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 137</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-44" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> Fixed Two-factor authentication via text or email for Office 365 using OAuth2.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_thunderbird-136" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-45" aria-expanded="false">Thunderbird 136</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-45" aria-hidden="true"><ul><li> New Appearance panel, Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Appearance</span>.
</li></ul><p></p></div>

</section>
<p></p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "356683", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 10:26:15 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T22:26:15.935836-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "wip, do not translate, needs Wayne''s review. Discussion https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/234", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 10:27:10 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T22:27:10.384420-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 6:45:22 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T06:45:22.846414-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:16:39+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (356692,'en-US','microsoft-ews-graph-migration','Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration/revision/356692',356350,'2026-09-28T23:22:45.870275-07:00','Mark Heijl','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/','minor edits',1,'2026-09-29T07:16:24.752567-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,1,'2026-09-29T07:16:24.752567-07:00','wsmwk','approved','{warning}''''''NOTE:'''''' If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email, and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.{/warning}
Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in Thunderbird versions 154 and higher.

Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > click your EWS account in the left sidebar > {button Delete}.
''''''Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.''''''

Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the Account Hub to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu:
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email} and enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account.
Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the {button Microsoft Graph API} button when presented with the list of available protocols.','<div class="warning"><strong>NOTE:</strong> If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email, and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.</div>
<p>Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in Thunderbird versions 154 and higher.
</p><p>Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; click your EWS account in the left sidebar &gt; <span class="button">Delete</span>.
</li></ul>
<p><strong>Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.</strong>
</p><p>Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the Account Hub to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span> and enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account.
</li></ul>
<p>Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> button when presented with the list of available protocols.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "356692", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 28, 2026, 11:22:45 PM", "datetime": "2026-09-28T23:22:45.870275-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Mark Heijl", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "minor edits", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 7:16:24 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T07:16:24.752567-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 7:16:24 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T07:16:24.752567-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:11:34+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (356806,'en-US','thunderbird-and-exchange','Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange/revision/356806',356681,'2026-09-29T06:38:27.269033-07:00','Wayne Mery','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/','add wiki link for graph',1,'2026-09-29T06:39:19.277992-07:00','wsmwk',1,0,1,'2026-09-29T06:39:19.277992-07:00','wsmwk','approved','{warning}Please read [[Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026]] for details on the upcoming migration to Graph from Exchange via EWS.{/warning}
{warning}
For the best Microsoft Exchange and Microsoft Graph experience on desktop, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.

Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services) and Microsoft Graph. Support for Exchange and Graph is ''''''currently limited to email''''''.

Calendar and address book using Exchange and Graph on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is Exchange and Graph support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.

For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].{/warning}

__TOC__

=Setup=
When creating a new Exchange or Graph account, once an Exchange or Graph account is detected, Thunderbird will give you options for both Exchange and Graph.
==Configure your Exchange or Graph account==
Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.

# Click the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Hub'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange or Graph email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear in your system web browser.<br><br>{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account type selection dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb157-beta-exchange-graph-button|width=450]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] {button Exchange} or the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] {button Microsoft Graph API} radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange or Graph account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange} and {button Microsoft Graph API} radio buttons only appear if the email you entered is an Exchange or Graph account.{/note}

===Manual Exchange and Graph configuration===
There are a diversity of Exchange and Graph servers which could cause auto configuration of your Exchange or Graph email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
# Add a new email account: Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email} and enter a Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) and then click ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom left of the Account hub.
# Click {button Microsoft Exchange or Microsoft 365} and then click {button Set up account}.
# In the ''''''Exchange settings'''''' dialog, type one of the following URLs into the ''''''Enter the service URL'''''' field:
#* For EWS: https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx
#* For Graph: https://graph.microsoft.com/
# Click {button Find settings}. For EWS, ''''''Exchange Web Services'''''' will automatically be selected. For Graph, ''''''Microsoft Graph'''''' will be selected.
# Your Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) you used in step 1 should be automatically entered into the ''''''Username'''''' field (if not, then correct it).
# Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange or Graph administrator. Full details at [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]] (scroll down to ''''''Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization'''''').

=Send and receive emails with your Exchange or Graph Account=
Once you have configured your Exchange or Graph Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to [[Send and receive messages in Thunderbird|send and receive emails]] just like an IMAP or POP account.

=Limitations=
Not yet supported:
* Address book
* Calendar
* OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
* Shared mailboxes 
<!--
"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&hide_resolved=1 this bug list].
-->
=Feedback and Bug Reports=
To report a problem when using version 157 or newer, please [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&component=Networking:%20Exchange&short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&blocked=1847846&bug_type=defect file a bug report].

To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].

You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta beta group] or the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise enterprise group].

=Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted Exchange and Graph servers=
Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS and Graph instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] and [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API wiki page].','<div class="warning">Please read <a href="/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration">Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026</a> for details on the upcoming migration to Graph from Exchange via EWS.</div>
<div class="warning">
<p>For the best Microsoft Exchange and Microsoft Graph experience on desktop, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.
</p><p>Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services) and Microsoft Graph. Support for Exchange and Graph is <strong>currently limited to email</strong>.
</p><p>Calendar and address book using Exchange and Graph on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is Exchange and Graph support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.
</p>
For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_setup"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Setup</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configure-your-exchange-or-graph-account"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure your Exchange or Graph account</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_manual-exchange-and-graph-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Manual Exchange and Graph configuration</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-or-graph-account"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Send and receive emails with your Exchange or Graph Account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_limitations"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Limitations</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Feedback and Bug Reports</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-and-graph-servers"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted Exchange and Graph servers</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_setup">Setup</h1>
<p>When creating a new Exchange or Graph account, once an Exchange or Graph account is detected, Thunderbird will give you options for both Exchange and Graph.
</p>
<h2 id="w_configure-your-exchange-or-graph-account">Configure your Exchange or Graph account</h2>
<p>Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.
</p>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Hub</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange or Graph email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear in your system web browser.<br><br><div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account type selection dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb157-beta-exchange-graph-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-09-28-21-05-35-ae6b32.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <span class="button">Exchange</span> or the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange or Graph account.
</li></ol>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange</span> and <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> radio buttons only appear if the email you entered is an Exchange or Graph account.</div>
<h3 id="w_manual-exchange-and-graph-configuration">Manual Exchange and Graph configuration</h3>
<p>There are a diversity of Exchange and Graph servers which could cause auto configuration of your Exchange or Graph email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
</p>
<ol><li> Add a new email account: Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span> and enter a Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) and then click <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom left of the Account hub.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Microsoft Exchange or Microsoft 365</span> and then click <span class="button">Set up account</span>.
</li><li> In the <strong>Exchange settings</strong> dialog, type one of the following URLs into the <strong>Enter the service URL</strong> field:
<ul><li> For EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> For Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/">https://graph.microsoft.com/</a>
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Find settings</span>. For EWS, <strong>Exchange Web Services</strong> will automatically be selected. For Graph, <strong>Microsoft Graph</strong> will be selected.
</li><li> Your Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) you used in step 1 should be automatically entered into the <strong>Username</strong> field (if not, then correct it).
</li><li> Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange or Graph administrator. Full details at <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a> (scroll down to <strong>Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization</strong>).
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-or-graph-account">Send and receive emails with your Exchange or Graph Account</h1>
<p>Once you have configured your Exchange or Graph Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">send and receive emails</a> just like an IMAP or POP account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_limitations">Limitations</h1>
<p>Not yet supported:
</p>
<ul><li> Address book
</li><li> Calendar
</li><li> OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
</li><li> Shared mailboxes 
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_feedback-and-bug-reports">Feedback and Bug Reports</h1>
<p>To report a problem when using version 157 or newer, please <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&amp;component=Networking:%20Exchange&amp;short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&amp;blocked=1847846&amp;bug_type=defect">file a bug report</a>.
</p><p>To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p><p>You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta">beta group</a> or the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">enterprise group</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-and-graph-servers">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted Exchange and Graph servers</h1>
<p>Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS and Graph instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> and <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API">Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API wiki page</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "356806", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 6:38:27 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T06:38:27.269033-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Wayne Mery", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/wsmwk/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "add wiki link for graph", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 6:39:19 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T06:39:19.277992-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 29, 2026, 6:39:19 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-29T06:39:19.277992-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:15:41+00:00','MT');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (357201,'en-US','thunderbird-and-exchange','Thunderbird and Microsoft protocols (Exchange and Graph)','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/thunderbird-and-exchange/revision/357201',356806,'2026-09-30T01:56:25.340416-07:00','Mark Heijl','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/','minor edits',1,'2026-09-30T04:43:05.263376-07:00','wsmwk',1,1,1,'2026-09-30T04:43:05.263376-07:00','wsmwk','approved','{warning}Please read [[Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026]] for details on the upcoming migration to Graph from Exchange via EWS.{/warning}
{warning}
For the best Microsoft Exchange and Microsoft Graph experience on desktop, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.

Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services) and Microsoft Graph. Support for Exchange and Graph is ''''''currently limited to email''''''.

Calendar and address book using Exchange and Graph on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is Exchange and Graph support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.

For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].{/warning}

__TOC__

=Setup=
When creating a new Exchange or Graph account, once an Exchange or Graph account is detected, Thunderbird will give you options for both Exchange and Graph.

==Configure your Exchange or Graph account==
Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.
# Click the Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {button New Account} > {menu Email}. The ''''Account Hub'''' dialog will appear.
# Enter your name, email address, and if required, your [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication two factor authentication code] for your Exchange or Graph email account.
# Click the {button Continue} button. The password entry dialog will appear in your system web browser.{note}''''''Note:'''''' In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.{/note} Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
#:[[Image:tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11|width=450]]
# Enter your password and click {button Sign in}. The account type selection dialog will appear.
#:[[Image:tb157-beta-exchange-graph-button|width=450]]
# Select the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] {button Exchange} or the [[Image:radio_button|width=20]] {button Microsoft Graph API} radio button and click {button Continue} to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
# Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange or Graph account.
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The {button Exchange} and {button Microsoft Graph API} radio buttons only appear if the email you entered is an Exchange or Graph account.{/note}

===Manual Exchange and Graph configuration===
There are a diversity of Exchange and Graph servers which could cause auto configuration of your Exchange or Graph email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
# Add a new email account: Thunderbird app menu {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email}, enter a Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) and then click ''''''MANUAL CONFIGURATION'''''' on the bottom left of the Account hub.
# Click {button Microsoft Exchange or Microsoft 365} and then click {button Set up account}.
# In the ''''''Exchange settings'''''' dialog, type one of the following URLs into the ''''''Enter the service URL'''''' field:
#* For EWS: https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx
#* For Graph: https://graph.microsoft.com/
# Click {button Find settings}. For EWS, ''''''Exchange Web Services'''''' will automatically be selected. For Graph, ''''''Microsoft Graph'''''' will be selected.
# Your Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) you used in step 1 should be automatically entered into the ''''''Username'''''' field (if not, then correct it).
# Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange or Graph administrator. Full details at [[Custom OAuth for advanced users]] (scroll down to ''''''Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization'''''').

=Send and receive emails with your Exchange or Graph Account=
Once you have configured your Exchange or Graph Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to [[Send and receive messages in Thunderbird|send and receive emails]] just like an IMAP or POP account.

=Limitations=
Not yet supported:
* Address book
* Calendar
* OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
* Shared mailboxes 
<!--
"Phase 5" items are on the near term road map, expected to by available/resolved by 1Q2026. The full list of phase 5 is [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/showdependencytree.cgi?id=1995377&hide_resolved=1 this bug list].
-->
=Feedback and Bug Reports=
To report a problem when using version 157 or newer, please [https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&component=Networking:%20Exchange&short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&blocked=1847846&bug_type=defect file a bug report].

To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page].

You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta beta group] or the [https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise enterprise group].

=Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted Exchange and Graph servers=
Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS and Graph instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page] and [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API wiki page].','<div class="warning">Please read <a href="/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration">Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026</a> for details on the upcoming migration to Graph from Exchange via EWS.</div>
<div class="warning">
<p>For the best Microsoft Exchange and Microsoft Graph experience on desktop, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.
</p><p>Thunderbird on desktop implements Exchange via EWS (Exchange Web Services) and Microsoft Graph. Support for Exchange and Graph is <strong>currently limited to email</strong>.
</p><p>Calendar and address book using Exchange and Graph on desktop will follow at a later date. Also in the future is Exchange and Graph support on Android, so please do not file bug reports for these items at this time.
</p>
For more technical detail, as well as things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.</div>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_setup"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Setup</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_configure-your-exchange-or-graph-account"><span class="tocnumber">1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Configure your Exchange or Graph account</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-3"><a href="#w_manual-exchange-and-graph-configuration"><span class="tocnumber">1.1.1</span> <span class="toctext">Manual Exchange and Graph configuration</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-or-graph-account"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Send and receive emails with your Exchange or Graph Account</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_limitations"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Limitations</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_feedback-and-bug-reports"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Feedback and Bug Reports</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-and-graph-servers"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted Exchange and Graph servers</span></a></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_setup">Setup</h1>
<p>When creating a new Exchange or Graph account, once an Exchange or Graph account is detected, Thunderbird will give you options for both Exchange and Graph.
</p>
<h2 id="w_configure-your-exchange-or-graph-account">Configure your Exchange or Graph account</h2>
<p>Again, for the best experience, we recommend Thunderbird 157 or newer.
</p>
<ol><li> Click the Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="button">New Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>. The <em>Account Hub</em> dialog will appear.
</li><li> Enter your name, email address, and if required, your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication">two factor authentication code</a> for your Exchange or Graph email account.
</li><li> Click the <span class="button">Continue</span> button. The password entry dialog will appear in your system web browser.<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> In the first dialog box, you do not enter your account password. You will enter your password in a separate, branded panel provided by Microsoft (not Mozilla) as part of their OAuth implementation.</div> Screenshot of that panel provided by Microsoft:
<dl><dd><img alt="tb-141-oauth-microsoft-enter-password-win11" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-08-25-21-26-20-034f89.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Enter your password and click <span class="button">Sign in</span>. The account type selection dialog will appear.
<dl><dd><img alt="tb157-beta-exchange-graph-button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-09-28-21-05-35-ae6b32.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="450">
</dd></dl>
</li><li> Select the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <span class="button">Exchange</span> or the <img alt="radio_button" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2025-11-19-10-27-42-00d9fc.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" width="20"> <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> radio button and click <span class="button">Continue</span> to use Thunderbird''s built-in Exchange code.
</li><li> Follow the prompts to finish adding your Exchange or Graph account.
</li></ol>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The <span class="button">Exchange</span> and <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> radio buttons only appear if the email you entered is an Exchange or Graph account.</div>
<h3 id="w_manual-exchange-and-graph-configuration">Manual Exchange and Graph configuration</h3>
<p>There are a diversity of Exchange and Graph servers which could cause auto configuration of your Exchange or Graph email account to fail. Here''s a potential workaround for that situation:
</p>
<ol><li> Add a new email account: Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span>, enter a Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) and then click <strong>MANUAL CONFIGURATION</strong> on the bottom left of the Account hub.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Microsoft Exchange or Microsoft 365</span> and then click <span class="button">Set up account</span>.
</li><li> In the <strong>Exchange settings</strong> dialog, type one of the following URLs into the <strong>Enter the service URL</strong> field:
<ul><li> For EWS: <a href="https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx">https://outlook.office365.com/EWS/Exchange.asmx</a>
</li><li> For Graph: <a href="https://graph.microsoft.com/">https://graph.microsoft.com/</a>
</li></ul>
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Find settings</span>. For EWS, <strong>Exchange Web Services</strong> will automatically be selected. For Graph, <strong>Microsoft Graph</strong> will be selected.
</li><li> Your Microsoft 365 email address (e.g. <code>nemo@o365.thunderbird.net</code>) you used in step 1 should be automatically entered into the <strong>Username</strong> field (if not, then correct it).
</li><li> Follow the prompts and fill in the settings from your Exchange or Graph administrator. Full details at <a href="/en-US/kb/custom-oauth-advanced-users">Custom OAuth for advanced users</a> (scroll down to <strong>Per-account OAuth2 Microsoft Exchange customization</strong>).
</li></ol>
<h1 id="w_send-and-receive-emails-with-your-exchange-or-graph-account">Send and receive emails with your Exchange or Graph Account</h1>
<p>Once you have configured your Exchange or Graph Account in Thunderbird, you can use it to <a href="/en-US/kb/sending-and-receiving-messages-thunderbird">send and receive emails</a> just like an IMAP or POP account.
</p>
<h1 id="w_limitations">Limitations</h1>
<p>Not yet supported:
</p>
<ul><li> Address book
</li><li> Calendar
</li><li> OAuth authentication with on-premise instances 
</li><li> Shared mailboxes 
</li></ul>
<p>
</p>
<h1 id="w_feedback-and-bug-reports">Feedback and Bug Reports</h1>
<p>To report a problem when using version 157 or newer, please <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=MailNews%20Core&amp;component=Networking:%20Exchange&amp;short_desc=%5BEWS%5D%20xxxxxxxxx&amp;blocked=1847846&amp;bug_type=defect">file a bug report</a>.
</p><p>To see open bug reports and more technical detail about bug reporting, tracking bug activity, plus things to test and look for, see the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a>.
</p><p>You may use Topicbox to discuss or ask questions about Exchange, either the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/beta">beta group</a> or the <a href="https://thunderbird.topicbox.com/groups/enterprise">enterprise group</a>.
</p>
<h1 id="w_microsoft-hosted-vs-self-hosted-exchange-and-graph-servers">Microsoft hosted vs. self hosted Exchange and Graph servers</h1>
<p>Administrators and others interested in the details of EWS and Graph instances (Microsoft hosted, Microsoft hosted as tenant-specific, and self hosted) will want to refer to the <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Exchange">Thunderbird:Exchange wiki page</a> and <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API">Thunderbird:Microsoft_Graph_API wiki page</a>.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "357201", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Sep 30, 2026, 1:56:25 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-30T01:56:25.340416-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Mark Heijl", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/markh2/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "minor edits", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Sep 30, 2026, 4:43:05 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-30T04:43:05.263376-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Sep 30, 2026, 4:43:05 AM", "datetime": "2026-09-30T04:43:05.263376-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "wsmwk", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:15:39+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (359085,'en-US','filelink-large-attachments','Filelink for Large Attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/filelink-large-attachments/revision/359085',333667,'2026-10-05T00:06:53.086879-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','wip do not translate',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','[[Template:TBproEarlyBirdInviteOnly]]
{note}If you have a Thundermail invite, you can use Send as a Filelink provider. Please refer to the following for more details: [[Getting Started with Thunderbird Pro]] and [https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235 our Thunderbird Send Articles].{/note}
With Thunderbird''s FileLink feature, you can upload large attachments to the cloud and send a link instead of attaching them directly to your email message. This feature is provided by [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons].

__TOC__

= Thunderbird''s Filelink feature =
Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.

Thunderbird’s Filelink feature eliminates this problem by providing support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service, and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link to that online storage. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.

Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to [[How to Use Attachments|regular attachments]]. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message.

The Thunderbird community has made available a number of [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons] for various providers ([[#w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported|more details below]]).

= Adding Filelink =
To use this feature, you need to install one of the [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons] first, as described below. For some online storage service providers, you may have to first create a new account or configure an existing account with them.
* Click Thunderbird app menu  {menu ≡} > {menu Settings} > {menu Composition} > scroll down to the ''''''Attachments''''''  section and then click on ''''''Find more providers...''''''.
* Choose a Filelink Provider add-on, and click {button ＋ Add to Thunderbird}.
* Confirm add-on permissions from the popup panel by clicking the {button Add} button.
* In the ''''''Attachments'''''' section of the ''''Settings'''' (see above), click on the button {button Add [Your Filelink provider]}.
* Add information as requested by the addon. For example, {button Add WebDAV} if you added the ''''''WebDAV'''''' provider.
* Thunderbird will suggest using Filelink for attachments larger than 5 MB (you can change the threshold in the same section), or you can right-click on a regular attachment to convert it.
* 5MB or larger is the default threshold for FileLink to automatically offer to share an attachment with one of its providers. Change the size by clicking the up and down arrows or un-check to turn off the automatic offer
*;[[Image:tb157-macos-up-down-arrows-filelink]]]]
= Using Filelink =
When you attach a file to a message, and the file size exceeds the size you have specified above, Thunderbird will automatically offer to use Filelink:
;[[Image:Proton-FileLink-LargeFileNotification]]
* Click the {button Link} button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.
* If you prefer to attach the file as a [[How to Use Attachments|regular attachment]], choose {button Ignore}.
After choosing {button Link}, your file will be uploaded in the background and the displayed message will update to:
;[[Image:Proton-FileLink-UploadingFileNotification]]
{note} Regardless of the file size and the Filelink notification, you can always force a file to be attached using Filelink:
* Choose the down arrow of the {button Attach | v} button on the composition toolbar to access the {menu Filelink} submenu.
* Right-click on a regular attachment and from the context menu, choose {menu Convert to...} > {menu [Your FileLink provider]}. You can choose from a list of your enabled providers.{/note}
When the linking is complete – that is, your attachment has been uploaded to the service provider – Thunderbird will automatically add a section in the message with a link to the online copy of your file  as shown below:
;[[Image:TB78-filelink-linked-anonymous]]
{note}''''''Note:'''''' The link section will appear in plain text, if ''''''Compose messages in HTML format'''''' is turned off in Account Settings.{/note}
When your recipients receive the message, they will see the same information and clicking the link in the message will take them to a page where they can download the attachment.

----

= Filelink FAQ =
Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Thunderbird servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.

== Does Thunderbird know which provider the user has picked? ==
No. Thunderbird is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.

== What storage services are currently supported? ==
* Some popular file storage providers are supported through [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-ons]. The ''''''Send'''''' Filelink provider that is part of Thundermail is part of Thunderbird 153 and newer. No need to install it.
* You can also use Filelink with your preferred WebDAV server with the [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/filelink-provider-for-webdav/ Filelink provider for WebDAV] add-on.
<!-- [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/addon/dl-for-thunderbird DL for Thunderbird] may be the best if you prefer using your own server instead of relying on third-party providers.-->

== Can the storage service view my attachments? ==
Unless you encrypt the file before uploading, the storage services will be able to view the file, as will anyone who obtains the link to the attachment. Users must decide on their own which service provider they trust with that responsibility. Service providers will generally explain your privacy rights in their terms of service. 

Note that standard attachment functionality is also not encrypted. When you send an attachment the "normal" way, without using Thunderbird’s OpenPGP or SMIME features, the file can be accessed by anyone that can access the message between your system and the recipients system (such as your email service provider). Filelink adds some security as file uploads to the storage service provider are sent via HTTPS, a secure protocol. We recommend using Thunderbird''s OpenPGP or SMIME features for maximum security of your communications.

== How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site? ==
Depending on the terms and conditions of your third-party storage service provider and your service agreement with them, the file may remain available on the provider’s site for a limited period, a certain number of downloads, or until you explicitly delete it. You will need to log in to the provider’s website to view and delete the files in your personal storage space.

== Can you support service provider X or protocol Y? ==
Please check if the Thunderbird community has provided a [https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink Filelink provider add-on] for the service provider you are looking for.
[https://webextension-api.thunderbird.net/en/mv3/cloudFile.html Developer documentation for the Filelink API] is available.

== I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work? ==
Regular email attachments still work the same as they always have. We are just offering you an option to upload large attachments somewhere else if you wish. Thunderbird''s notion of a large file defaults to 5 MB, but can be customized in Thunderbird’s Settings:
# Click Thunderbird app menu  {menu ≡} > {menu Settings} > {menu Composition} and scroll down to the ''''''Attachments'''''' section.
# ''''''[✓] Offer to share for files larger than [5] MB''''''
Removing the checkmark will prevent the automatical Filelink notification when you attach big files, but the Filelink feature will still be available from the {button 📎 Attach | v} button dropdown as well as other attachment menus and context menus.','<p></p><div class="warning">NOTE: Thundermail (formerly known as Thunderbird Pro) is currently in its Early Bird release and available  by invitation only. To learn more and join the waitlist, visit <a href="https://tb.pro/">our website</a>. We’ll be opening Thundermail to the public once the Early Bird release concludes.</div>
<p></p>
<div class="note">If you have a Thundermail invite, you can use Send as a Filelink provider. Please refer to the following for more details: <a href="/en-US/kb/getting-started-thunderbird-pro">Getting Started with Thunderbird Pro</a> and <a href="https://support.tb.pro/hc/sections/43937747865235">our Thunderbird Send Articles</a>.</div>
<p>With Thunderbird''s FileLink feature, you can upload large attachments to the cloud and send a link instead of attaching them directly to your email message. This feature is provided by <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a>.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbirds-filelink-feature"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird''s Filelink feature</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_adding-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Adding Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_using-filelink"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Using Filelink</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_filelink-faq"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Filelink FAQ</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_does-thunderbird-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked"><span class="tocnumber">4.1</span> <span class="toctext">Does Thunderbird know which provider the user has picked?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported"><span class="tocnumber">4.2</span> <span class="toctext">What storage services are currently supported?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_can-the-storage-service-view-my-attachments"><span class="tocnumber">4.3</span> <span class="toctext">Can the storage service view my attachments?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_how-long-will-my-file-be-available-on-the-storage-service-providers-site"><span class="tocnumber">4.4</span> <span class="toctext">How long will my file be available on the storage service provider''s site?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_can-you-support-service-provider-x-or-protocol-y"><span class="tocnumber">4.5</span> <span class="toctext">Can you support service provider X or protocol Y?</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_i-dont-like-the-idea-of-storing-my-files-with-third-parties-like-this-do-regular-attachments-still-work"><span class="tocnumber">4.6</span> <span class="toctext">I don''t like the idea of storing my files with third-parties like this. Do regular attachments still work?</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_thunderbirds-filelink-feature">Thunderbird''s Filelink feature</h1>
<p>Many email servers do not accept messages with large file attachments. The file size restriction varies depending on the mail server configuration. While you might be able to send a message with a large attachment, the receiving mail server might refuse to accept a message with a large attachment. This is all beyond the control of Thunderbird.
</p><p>Thunderbird’s Filelink feature eliminates this problem by providing support for online storage services. It allows you to upload attachments to an online storage service, and then replaces the attachment in the message with a link to that online storage. The message recipient clicks the link to download the attachment. As additional benefits, sending and receiving large files is much faster and both you and the recipient will save disk space.
</p><p>Note that you can use Thunderbird Filelink in addition to <a href="/en-US/kb/how-use-attachments">regular attachments</a>. For example, you can attach small files directly to a message and use Filelink for large attachments in the same message.
</p><p>The Thunderbird community has made available a number of <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a> for various providers (<a href="#w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported">more details below</a>).
</p>
<h1 id="w_adding-filelink">Adding Filelink</h1>
<p>To use this feature, you need to install one of the <a href="https://addons.thunderbird.net/thunderbird/tag/filelink">Filelink provider add-ons</a> first, as described below. For some online storage service providers, you may have to first create a new account or configure an existing account with them.
</p>
<ul><li> Click Thunderbird app menu  <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Settings</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Composition</span> &gt; scroll down to the <strong>Attachments</strong>  section and then click on <strong>Find more providers...</strong>.
</li><li> Choose a Filelink Provider add-on, and click <span class="button">＋ Add to Thunderbird</span>.
</li><li> Confirm add-on permissions from the popup panel by clicking the <span class="button">Add</span> button.
</li><li> In the <strong>Attachments</strong> section of the <em>Settings</em> (see above), click on the button <span class="button">Add [Your Filelink provider]</span>.
</li><li> Add information as requested by the addon. For example, <span class="button">Add WebDAV</span> if you added the <strong>WebDAV</strong> provider.
</li><li> Thunderbird will suggest using Filelink for attachments larger than 5 MB (you can change the threshold in the same section), or you can right-click on a regular attachment to convert it.
</li><li> 5MB or larger is the default threshold for FileLink to automatically offer to share an attachment with one of its providers. Change the size by clicking the up and down arrows or un-check to turn off the automatic offer
<dl><dt><img alt="tb157-macos-up-down-arrows-filelink" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2026-10-05-00-01-07-2e8862.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">]]
</dt></dl>
</li></ul>
<h1 id="w_using-filelink">Using Filelink</h1>
<p>When you attach a file to a message, and the file size exceeds the size you have specified above, Thunderbird will automatically offer to use Filelink:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="Proton-FileLink-LargeFileNotification" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-10-14-54-16-912351.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<ul><li> Click the <span class="button">Link</span> button to upload the file to the online storage service provider.
</li><li> If you prefer to attach the file as a <a href="/en-US/kb/how-use-attachments">regular attachment</a>, choose <span class="button">Ignore</span>.
</li></ul>
<p>After choosing <span class="button">Link</span>, your file will be uploaded in the background and the displayed message will update to:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="Proton-FileLink-UploadingFileNotification" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-05-10-14-51-18-5d2e96.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<div class="note"> Regardless of the file size and the Filelink notification, you can always force a file to be attached using Filelink:
<ul><li> Choose the down arrow of the <span class="button">Attach | v</span> button on the composition toolbar to access the <span class="menu">Filelink</span> submenu.
</li><li> Right-click on a regular attachment and from the context menu, choose <span class="menu">Convert to...</span> &gt; <span class="menu">[Your FileLink provider]</span>. You can choose from a list of your enabled providers.</li></ul></div>

<p>When the linking is complete – that is, your attachment has been uploaded to the service provider – Thunderbird will automatically add a section in the message with a link to the online copy of your file  as shown below:
</p>
<dl><dt><img alt="TB78-filelink-linked-anonymous" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2021-08-29-10-09-54-3855ee.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
<div class="note"><strong>Note:</strong> The link section will appear in plain text, if <strong>Compose messages in HTML format</strong> is turned off in Account Settings.</div>
<p>When your recipients receive the message, they will see the same information and clicking the link in the message will take them to a page where they can download the attachment.
</p>
<hr>
<h1 id="w_filelink-faq">Filelink FAQ</h1>
<p>Attachments that you send via Filelink are not stored on Thunderbird servers. Each file storage service provider has its own privacy policy and terms of service. The file storage service provider has reviewed the Filelink feature to ensure that it is consistent with their policies.
</p>
<h2 id="w_does-thunderbird-know-which-provider-the-user-has-picked">Does Thunderbird know which provider the user has picked?</h2>
<p>No. Thunderbird is unaware of which provider has been selected by a user. Provider configuration is stored on the local machine.
</p>
<h2 id="w_what-storage-services-are-currently-supported">What storage services are currently supported?</h2>
