<h1 id="w_how-to-import-from-thunderbird-desktop">How To Import from Thunderbird Desktop</h1>
<div class="warning"><em>Export for Mobile</em> is available starting with Thunderbird Desktop 128.4.0 or Thunderbird beta 132 or newer (this  feature is not available in older versions of Thunderbird). We recommend Thunderbird Desktop 153 or newer for the best experience.</div>
<h2 id="w_export-thunderbird-desktop-settings">Export Thunderbird Desktop settings</h2>
<ol><li> Open Thunderbird Desktop and click Thunderbird app menu <span class="menu">≡</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Tools</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Export for Mobile</span>.
</li><li> Check <strong>✓</strong> the Email accounts you wish to export to Thunderbird for Android.
<dl><dt><img alt="Thunderbird 128 Export accounts to Thunderbird Mobile" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-10-22-21-02-15-d056ee.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li><li> If you have accounts that are not using <a href="https://wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth">OAuth</a> and you wish to export your passwords, you will see a <strong>Security</strong> section. Check <strong>✓</strong> <strong>Include all accounts passwords</strong> under <strong>Security</strong> to export your passwords in the QR code.
</li><li> Click <span class="button">Export</span>. (You may be prompted for a PIN number. For Windows, that would be the Windows signin password.) A QR code will be displayed. Follow the steps below on your Android phone or tablet to scan the Thunderbird Desktop settings QR code. If you have many email accounts, multiple QR codes will be displayed when you scan from your Android device in the next step.
<dl><dt><img alt="tb-128-qr-code" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2024-10-23-21-48-15-38e86e.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title="">
</dt></dl>
</li></ol>
<h2 id="w_import-the-desktop-settings-into-thunderbird-for-android">Import the Desktop settings into Thunderbird for Android</h2>
<p>Open Thunderbird for Android on your phone or tablet and follow the steps below.
</p>
<h3 id="w_first-run_2">First run</h3>
<p>After you install Thunderbird for Android and run it for the first time, here''s how to import settings from Desktop:
</p>
<ol><li> Tap <span class="button">Get started</span> on the initial screen.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Import settings</span>. Thunderbird for Android will start the camera and display <em>Align the QR code provided from Thunderbird Desktop</em>. Scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop.
<ul><li> If you have many email accounts, you will scan multiple QR codes, but only one QR code is displayed at a time by Thunderbird Desktop. After scanning the first QR code, Thunderbird for Android will display a confirmation of the form: <em>Scanned X of Y</em> where <em>X</em> is the count of QR codes you have scanned so far out of a total of <em>Y</em> QR codes (for example <em>Scanned 1 of 2</em>). In Thunderbird Desktop, click <span class="button">Next</span> to manually advance to the next QR code to be scanned. Repeat until all QR codes have been scanned.
</li></ul>
</li><li> Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all checked <strong>✓</strong>. Tap <span class="button">Import</span>.
</li><li> If your account uses <a href="https://wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth">OAuth</a> instead of password authentication or you have chosen not to import your passwords, you will be prompted for your passwords. Follow the prompts to complete your import.
</li></ol>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h3 id="w_subsequent-runs_2" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-expanded="false">Subsequent runs</button></h3><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-4" aria-hidden="true"><p>Once you have set up an email account, the next time you import email settings, the process will be slightly different:
</p><ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the gear icon: <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> at the bottom left.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Import settings</span> &gt; <span class="button">Scan QR code</span>. Scan the QR code from Thunderbird Desktop. If you have many email accounts, after scanning the first QR code, another code will be displayed and scanned until all account settings have been set up.
</li><li> Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all checked <strong>✓</strong>. Tap <span class="button">Import</span>.
</li><li> If your account uses <a href="https://wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth">OAuth</a> instead of password authentication or you have chosen not to import your passwords, you will be prompted for your passwords. Follow the prompts to complete your import.
</li></ol><p></p></div>


</section>
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_how-to-import-from-k-9-or-other-thunderbird-for-android-apps">How To Import from K-9 or other Thunderbird for Android apps</h1>
<div class="warning">Importing from K-9 Mail requires K-9 Mail 8.0 or later.</div>
<p>Open Thunderbird for Android on your phone or tablet and follow the steps below.
</p>
<h2 id="w_first-run_3">First run</h2>
<p>After you install Thunderbird for Android and run it for the first time, here''s how to import settings from <strong>K-9</strong> or Thunderbird from Android:
</p>
<ol><li> Tap <span class="button">Get started</span> on the initial screen.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Import your account settings now</span> &gt; tap <span class="button">Import from app</span>.
</li><li> Tap the email app you wish to import your email account settings from (either <strong>K-9 Mail</strong> or another release of Thunderbird for Android, for example Beta).
</li><li> Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all checked <strong>✓</strong>. Tap <span class="button">Import</span>.
</li><li> If your account uses <a href="https://wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth">OAuth</a> instead of password authentication or you have chosen not to import your passwords, you will be prompted for your passwords. Follow the prompts to complete your import.
</li></ol>
<p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h2 id="w_subsequent-runs_3" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-expanded="false">Subsequent runs</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-0" aria-hidden="true"><p>Once you have set up an email account, the next time you import email settings, the process will be slightly different:
</p><ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the <em>gear</em> icon: <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> at the bottom left.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Import settings</span> &gt; tap <span class="button">Import from app</span>.
</li><li> Tap the email app you wish to import your email account settings from (either <strong>K-9 Mail</strong> or another release of Thunderbird for Android, for example Beta).
</li><li> Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all checked <strong>✓</strong>. Tap <span class="button">Import</span>.
</li><li> If your account uses <a href="https://wikipedia.org/wiki/OAuth">OAuth</a> instead of password authentication or you have chosen not to import your passwords, you will be prompted for your passwords. Follow the prompts to complete your import.
</li></ol><p></p></div>


</section>
<p></p><p></p><section class="mzp-c-details is-details">
<p></p>
<h1 id="w_how-to-import-email-account-settings-using-a-settings-file-from-thunderbird-or-k-9">How To Import email account settings using a settings file from Thunderbird or K-9</h1>
<div class="warning"> It is not possible to import a settings file from apps other than Thunderbird for Android or <em>K-9</em>.</div>
<h2 id="w_1-export-thunderbird-or-k-9-settings-to-a-file" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-expanded="false">1. Export Thunderbird or K-9 settings to a file</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-1" aria-hidden="true"><p>Open the app, K-9 or Thunderbird for Android, whose settings you wish to export.
</p><ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the <em>gear</em> icon: <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> at the bottom left.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Export settings</span>.
</li><li> Check <strong>✓</strong> the Email accounts you wish to export to Thunderbird for Android.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Export</span>.
</li><li> Change the directory and the filename as you see fit and tap <span class="button">Save</span>. Take note of the filename and directory, for example a directory in <em>Google Drive</em>, the <strong>Downloads</strong> directory, etc. You will need the filename and directory in order to import the settings in <em>Step 2</em>.
</li></ol></div>


<h2 id="w_2-import-the-exported-settings-file-into-thunderbird-for-android" class="is-summary"><button type="button" aria-controls="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-expanded="false">2. Import the exported settings file into Thunderbird for Android</button></h2><div class="mzp-js-details-wrapper is-closed" id="expand-mzpcdetailshmzpcdetailsdivforh-2" aria-hidden="true"><p>Open Thunderbird for Android on your phone or tablet and follow the steps below.
</p><h3 id="w_first-run_4">First run</h3><p>Here''s how to import settings from the file you created in <em>Step 1</em>:
</p><ol><li> Tap <span class="button">Get started</span> on the initial screen.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Import your account settings now</span> &gt; <span class="button">Select file</span>. Select the file you saved from <em>Step 1.</em>
</li><li> Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all checked <strong>✓</strong>. Tap <span class="button">Import</span>.
</li><li> You will be prompted for your passwords. Follow the prompts to complete your import.
</li></ol><h3 id="w_subsequent-runs_4">Subsequent runs</h3><p>Once you have set up an email account, the next time you import email settings, the process will be slightly different:
</p><ol><li> Tap <span class="button">≡</span> at the top left &gt; tap the <em>gear</em> icon: <span class="button"><img alt="gear icon" class="wiki-image frameless lazy" data-original-src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/media/uploads/gallery/images/2014-06-13-01-42-16-c1469d.png" src="https://assets-prod.sumo.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net/static/placeholder.cb78bf81388965e3.gif" title=""></span> at the bottom left.
</li><li> Tap <span class="button">Import settings</span> &gt; tap <span class="button">Select file</span>. Select the file you saved from the <em>Step 1.</em>
</li><li> Ensure the email accounts you want to import are all checked <strong>✓</strong>. Tap <span class="button">Import</span>.
</li><li> You will be prompted for your passwords. Follow the prompts to complete your import.
</li></ol><p></p></div>







</section>
<p></p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "360861", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 7, 2026, 5:14:55 PM", "datetime": "2026-10-07T17:14:55.745081-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "Please do not translate. Discussion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/235 needs review by Wayne and/or Monica.", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 7, 2026, 5:15:06 PM", "datetime": "2026-10-07T17:15:06.554050-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization", "text": "Oct 17, 2026, 11:56:29 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-17T11:56:29.510128-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Readied for localization by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:14:51+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (361030,'en-US','compacting-folders-potential-complications','Compacting folders - Potential complications','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/compacting-folders-potential-complications','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/compacting-folders-potential-complications/revision/361030',280487,'2026-10-08T08:35:31.246221-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','do not translate please. Discussion https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/237',1,'2026-10-08T08:36:20.356510-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','Occasionally, when a folder is [[Compacting folders|compacted]], the mail messages in the folder seem to disappear. In some cases, there is an interruption or failure to display messages or message lists, which is typically recoverable. In some cases, there may be real data loss, which is not recoverable. Some of the observed behaviours are described and explained below. Please review these items, and if you are still having difficulty, please [https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/questions/new/thunderbird/form post your issue in support].

__TOC__
=Selecting a folder while compact is in progress=
Thunderbird can not read the index for the file while compacting is in progress, so when you select a folder, the mail in the folder will simply not be displayed until compact completes. When it does complete, the mail will reappear.

=Software interruption to the process=
Sometimes after the compact, no messages display in the folder due to an interruption of the compact process. This causes an empty index file (.msf) to be created. Some possible causes of interruption are:
* Controlling or not allowing access to the Thunderbird profile folder for backup scans
* Cloud syncing (which is not recommended)
* [https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Testing:Antivirus_Related_Performance_Issues Antivirus software scans] (which is not recommended).
Here''s how you can cure the problem of an empty index file:
* Right click (control click on macOS) on the folder
* Select {menu Properties} from folder context menu
* Use the {button Repair Folder} button
This will force a new index to be built and should recover from this situation without data loss.

=Mail file has previously been deleted=
Compact occurs and displays mail since the deletion, so the missing mail is all before a certain point in time. Whilst there have not been any known instances in the recent past, historically, antivirus products regularly scanned the mail storage file and if malware was found, deleted or quarantined the file. Due to Thunderbird using an index to fill the user interface, these types of deletions really only become obvious if an old mail is selected or a complete reindex occurs as in a compact process.

=Thunderbird using too much disk space=
See the next heading. 

=Mail file corruption=
If the underlying MBOX file is corrupt, then compact will write a new MBOX file that can at best be described as truncated.  In this case, the mail list often retains the earliest entries, but later ones are lost from the point where the MBOX file became corrupt. Some of the more common causes are:
* Hard disk failure
* Memory failure
* Software or malware corrupting memory where the data to be written to file is stored

==Other symptoms of mail file corruption==
One or more of these events may occur when the MBOX file is corrupt: 
* Previously deleted messages reappearing
* Messages with a date of 1969
* Message bodies containing bits of other messages
* Messages in the message list not matching what is displayed when clicked
* Thunderbird may stop responding before messages in a folder are displayed','<p>Occasionally, when a folder is <a href="/en-US/kb/compacting-folders">compacted</a>, the mail messages in the folder seem to disappear. In some cases, there is an interruption or failure to display messages or message lists, which is typically recoverable. In some cases, there may be real data loss, which is not recoverable. Some of the observed behaviours are described and explained below. Please review these items, and if you are still having difficulty, please <a href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/questions/new/thunderbird/form">post your issue in support</a>.
</p>
<div id="toc"><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_selecting-a-folder-while-compact-is-in-progress"><span class="tocnumber">1</span> <span class="toctext">Selecting a folder while compact is in progress</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_software-interruption-to-the-process"><span class="tocnumber">2</span> <span class="toctext">Software interruption to the process</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_mail-file-has-previously-been-deleted"><span class="tocnumber">3</span> <span class="toctext">Mail file has previously been deleted</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_thunderbird-using-too-much-disk-space"><span class="tocnumber">4</span> <span class="toctext">Thunderbird using too much disk space</span></a></li><li class="toclevel-1"><a href="#w_mail-file-corruption"><span class="tocnumber">5</span> <span class="toctext">Mail file corruption</span></a><ul><li class="toclevel-2"><a href="#w_other-symptoms-of-mail-file-corruption"><span class="tocnumber">5.1</span> <span class="toctext">Other symptoms of mail file corruption</span></a></li></ul></li></ul></div>
<h1 id="w_selecting-a-folder-while-compact-is-in-progress">Selecting a folder while compact is in progress</h1>
<p>Thunderbird can not read the index for the file while compacting is in progress, so when you select a folder, the mail in the folder will simply not be displayed until compact completes. When it does complete, the mail will reappear.
</p>
<h1 id="w_software-interruption-to-the-process">Software interruption to the process</h1>
<p>Sometimes after the compact, no messages display in the folder due to an interruption of the compact process. This causes an empty index file (.msf) to be created. Some possible causes of interruption are:
</p>
<ul><li> Controlling or not allowing access to the Thunderbird profile folder for backup scans
</li><li> Cloud syncing (which is not recommended)
</li><li> <a href="https://wiki.mozilla.org/Thunderbird:Testing:Antivirus_Related_Performance_Issues">Antivirus software scans</a> (which is not recommended).
</li></ul>
<p>Here''s how you can cure the problem of an empty index file:
</p>
<ul><li> Right click (control click on macOS) on the folder
</li><li> Select <span class="menu">Properties</span> from folder context menu
</li><li> Use the <span class="button">Repair Folder</span> button
</li></ul>
<p>This will force a new index to be built and should recover from this situation without data loss.
</p>
<h1 id="w_mail-file-has-previously-been-deleted">Mail file has previously been deleted</h1>
<p>Compact occurs and displays mail since the deletion, so the missing mail is all before a certain point in time. Whilst there have not been any known instances in the recent past, historically, antivirus products regularly scanned the mail storage file and if malware was found, deleted or quarantined the file. Due to Thunderbird using an index to fill the user interface, these types of deletions really only become obvious if an old mail is selected or a complete reindex occurs as in a compact process.
</p>
<h1 id="w_thunderbird-using-too-much-disk-space">Thunderbird using too much disk space</h1>
<p>See the next heading. 
</p>
<h1 id="w_mail-file-corruption">Mail file corruption</h1>
<p>If the underlying MBOX file is corrupt, then compact will write a new MBOX file that can at best be described as truncated.  In this case, the mail list often retains the earliest entries, but later ones are lost from the point where the MBOX file became corrupt. Some of the more common causes are:
</p>
<ul><li> Hard disk failure
</li><li> Memory failure
</li><li> Software or malware corrupting memory where the data to be written to file is stored
</li></ul>
<h2 id="w_other-symptoms-of-mail-file-corruption">Other symptoms of mail file corruption</h2>
<p>One or more of these events may occur when the MBOX file is corrupt: 
</p>
<ul><li> Previously deleted messages reappearing
</li><li> Messages with a date of 1969
</li><li> Message bodies containing bits of other messages
</li><li> Messages in the message list not matching what is displayed when clicked
</li><li> Thunderbird may stop responding before messages in a folder are displayed
</li></ul>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "361030", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 8, 2026, 8:35:31 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-08T08:35:31.246221-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "do not translate please. Discussion https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/237", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 8, 2026, 8:36:20 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-08T08:36:20.356510-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:14:49+00:00','M');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (361419,'en-US','microsoft-ews-graph-migration','Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration/revision/361419',359103,'2026-10-08T12:59:19.770855-07:00','Mozinet','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Mozinet/','added ‘Find what version of Thunderbird you are using’ article and made 2 corrections',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'unreviewed','{warning}''''''Note:'''''' If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.{/warning}
Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in [[Find what version of Firefox you are using|Thunderbird versions]]  154 and higher.

Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > click your EWS account in the left sidebar > {button Delete}.
''''''Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.''''''

Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the [[Account Hub for Thunderbird Desktop|Account Hub]] to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu:
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email} and enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account.
Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the {button Microsoft Graph API} button when presented with the list of available protocols.','<div class="warning"><strong>Note:</strong> If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.</div>
<p>Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in <a href="/en-US/kb/find-what-version-firefox-you-are-using">Thunderbird versions</a>  154 and higher.
</p><p>Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; click your EWS account in the left sidebar &gt; <span class="button">Delete</span>.
</li></ul>
<p><strong>Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.</strong>
</p><p>Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the <a href="/en-US/kb/account-hub-thunderbird-desktop">Account Hub</a> to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span> and enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account.
</li></ul>
<p>Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> button when presented with the list of available protocols.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "361419", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 8, 2026, 12:59:19 PM", "datetime": "2026-10-08T12:59:19.770855-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Mozinet", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/Mozinet/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "added ‘Find what version of Thunderbird you are using’ article and made 2 corrections", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T20:11:29+00:00','');
INSERT INTO article_revisions (revision_id, locale, article_slug, article_title, article_url, revision_url, previous_revision_id, created, creator_name, creator_url, comment, reviewed, reviewed_date, reviewed_by, is_approved, is_current, ready_for_l10n, readied_for_l10n_date, readied_for_l10n_by, status, source, content_html, info_json, scraped_at, significance) VALUES (363141,'en-US','microsoft-ews-graph-migration','Microsoft EWS to Graph Migration October 2026','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/microsoft-ews-graph-migration/revision/363141',361419,'2026-10-09T09:17:38.132291-07:00','Roland Tanglao','https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/','link to thunderbird articles. do not seet to RFL until Friday October 16, 2026 or later, Discussion: fix typos change to warning. Discusion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/232',1,'2026-10-09T09:18:58.401424-07:00','rtanglao',1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'approved','{warning}''''''Note:'''''' If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.{/warning}
Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in [[Find what version of Thunderbird you are using|Thunderbird versions]]  154 and higher.

Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Account Settings} > click your EWS account in the left sidebar > {button Delete}.
''''''Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.''''''

Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the [[Account Hub for Thunderbird Desktop|Account Hub]] to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu:
* Click {menu ☰} > {menu Add Account} > {menu Email} and enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account.
Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the {button Microsoft Graph API} button when presented with the list of available protocols.','<div class="warning"><strong>Note:</strong> If your organization uses Microsoft 365 for email and you’re currently using EWS to connect to that account, then you need to take action before October 2026. Note that on-premises Microsoft Exchange accounts can continue to use EWS. This applies only to users on Microsoft 365.</div>
<p>Earlier this year, Microsoft announced that they would begin a phased shutdown of EWS on Microsoft 365/Exchange Online starting in October of 2026 with the goal of a complete shutdown in 2027. If you are currently using Thunderbird’s EWS support to connect to a Microsoft 365/Exchange Online email account, then you will need to switch to the Microsoft Graph API support to keep using Thunderbird beyond these dates. Microsoft Graph support is available in <a href="/en-US/kb/find-what-version-thunderbird-you-are-using">Thunderbird versions</a>  154 and higher.
</p><p>Thunderbird does not support migrating accounts to different protocols. Instead, you will need to set up a new account. If you have an existing EWS-connected Microsoft 365 account, first you’ll need to delete that account in Thunderbird. You can do this from the Thunderbird app menu:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Account Settings</span> &gt; click your EWS account in the left sidebar &gt; <span class="button">Delete</span>.
</li></ul>
<p><strong>Deleting the account locally in Thunderbird will not affect any data stored on Microsoft 365, so your data is safe.</strong>
</p><p>Once your previous EWS account has been deleted, you can use the <a href="/en-US/kb/account-hub-thunderbird-desktop">Account Hub</a> to set up a new account. In the Thunderbird app menu:
</p>
<ul><li> Click <span class="menu">☰</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Add Account</span> &gt; <span class="menu">Email</span> and enter your Microsoft 365 hosted email account.
</li></ul>
<p>Thunderbird should automatically detect that your email is hosted on Microsoft 365 and will give you a few options for getting connected. Click the <span class="button">Microsoft Graph API</span> button when presented with the list of available protocols.
</p>','[{"label": "Revision id", "text": "363141", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Created", "text": "Oct 9, 2026, 9:17:38 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-09T09:17:38.132291-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Creator", "text": "Roland Tanglao", "datetime": null, "href": "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/user/rtanglao/"}, {"label": "Comment", "text": "link to thunderbird articles. do not seet to RFL until Friday October 16, 2026 or later, Discussion: fix typos change to warning. Discusion: https://github.com/thunderbird/knowledgebase-issues/issues/232", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed", "text": "Oct 9, 2026, 9:18:58 AM", "datetime": "2026-10-09T09:18:58.401424-07:00", "href": null}, {"label": "Reviewed by", "text": "rtanglao", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is approved?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Is current revision?", "text": "Yes", "datetime": null, "href": null}, {"label": "Ready for localization", "text": "No", "datetime": null, "href": null}]','2026-11-03T18:14:47+00:00','M');
